var Q_PEDS_EP = [
{
  id:'pedep-gp-1', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'When evaluating a child for stunting, which growth chart you will use?',
  options:['height for age charts','weight for age charts','weight for length charts','body mass index charts','mid arm circumference measurements'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Stunting reflects chronic malnutrition, which primarily impairs linear growth. Therefore, height-for-age is the correct indicator, not weight-based charts.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — the vocabulary the examiner is testing.** Three anthropometric words are constantly confused, and each one is tied to a different chart:\n\n| Term | What it means | The chart that shows it | Time course |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Stunting** | Low **height (length) for age** | Height-for-age | **Chronic** undernutrition — months to years |\n| **Wasting** | Low **weight for height** | Weight-for-height | **Acute** undernutrition — weeks |\n| **Underweight** | Low **weight for age** | Weight-for-age | Mixed — cannot separate the two |\n\nLinear growth is the slow variable. A child who is short for age has been undernourished, or chronically ill, long enough for the skeleton to fall behind — which is exactly why height-for-age is the marker of chronicity. Weight moves quickly in both directions and therefore reports the present, not the past.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.** *Weight-for-age charts* detect underweight but cannot tell you whether a light child is short-and-proportionate (stunted) or tall-and-thin (wasted), so they answer a different question. *Weight-for-length charts* are the wasting chart — the opposite end of the same axis, and the correct answer to a question about acute malnutrition. *Body mass index charts* are used from about 2 years upward mainly to classify overweight and obesity; `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` states that “Body mass index (BMI) is the most reliable and most commonly used clinical indicator of obesity”, with obese above the 95th centile, overweight 85th–95th and healthy 5th–85th. *Mid-arm circumference* is a rapid field screen for acute severe malnutrition — `16) P.E.M.txt` lists it under anthropometric data with a cut-off of 11.5 cm — and again reports wasting, not stunting.\n\n**Grounding.** `13) Short stature.txt` sets out which chart to use for whom: the WHO chart is “Recommended for 0-24 months”, the CDC chart “Recommended for 2-19 years”, both age- and sex-specific, with disease-specific charts for conditions such as Down syndrome. It also insists that “A single measurement is less important than growth trend”, so the answer is not merely *which chart* but *plotted serially*.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Short-for-age means look back — nutrition over years, chronic disease, an endocrine cause. Thin-for-height means look at the last few weeks — intake, losses, acute illness.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Stunting is low height-for-age and marks chronic undernutrition; wasting is low weight-for-height and marks acute undernutrition.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.30'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-2', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'A 4-year-old girl with Down syndrome, Saima, attends her routine annual follow-up with her mother. She is generally well, except for a history of constipation. On plotting her growth on a Down syndrome-specific chart, her height has dropped from the 75th to the 25th centile, while her weight has increased from the 50th to the 75th centile. Which of the following investigations is the most appropriate to perform?',
  options:['Lipid profile','Abdominal ultrasound','Full blood count','Thyroid function tests','Vitamin D levels'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Children with Down syndrome are at high risk for hypothyroidism. The combination of growth faltering, weight gain, and constipation is classic for thyroid dysfunction.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — the single rule that decides this question.** `13) Short stature.txt` prints the discriminator as a two-line rule, and it is worth memorising exactly as written:\n\n- “Weight affected > Height → Systemic disease”\n- “Height affected, Weight normal/↑ → Endocrine”\n\nSaima’s height has fallen two centile bands while her weight has *risen* one. That is the endocrine pattern, not the systemic one. The same slide adds the corollary under obesity: nutritional obesity gives a child who is “Obese + Tall”, whereas endocrine causes give “Obese + Short/poor height velocity”. A child growing fatter and shorter at the same time has a hormone problem until proved otherwise.\n\n**Why hypothyroidism specifically.** Two things converge. First, `6) Genetically determined disease_.txt` lists the later problems of Down syndrome, which include short stature and a broad susceptibility to associated disease; autoimmune thyroid disease is the commonest acquired endocrinopathy in this group and is the reason Down syndrome children are screened for it periodically *(the routine-screening interval itself is not stated in the cached decks — not taken from the course material)*. Second, the accompanying symptom fits: `52) hypothyrodism.txt` lists constipation, lethargy and poor feeding among the presenting features, and describes the hypothyroid child as “Short with infantile proportions” with coarse features, a large tongue and an umbilical hernia.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Lipid profile** — dyslipidaemia is a *consequence* of untreated hypothyroidism, not the test that diagnoses it, and it changes nothing in a 4-year-old.\n- **Abdominal ultrasound** — reasonable if you suspected an obstructive or structural cause for constipation, but it cannot explain falling height with rising weight.\n- **Full blood count** — would be the move if she were losing weight or looked systemically unwell; anaemia and chronic disease depress weight *more* than height, which is the opposite pattern.\n- **Vitamin D levels** — rickets impairs linear growth, but it does not raise weight, and it is not the discriminating test here.\n\n**A second Down-syndrome question sits in this section and keys differently.** The bank prints a near-identical vignette in which a Down syndrome child drops **one** centile in height but **two** in weight, and there the key is a coeliac screen. The direction of the weight line is the entire difference: weight falling faster than height sends you to gut and systemic disease, weight rising while height falls sends you to the thyroid. Read the weight before you answer.\n\n**Filing note.** Filed under growth and puberty because the question is answered by reading a growth chart; the secondary chapter is **endocrine**.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Height falling while weight rises is the endocrine growth pattern — in Down syndrome, check thyroid function first.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.32'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-3', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'Which is a characteristic of constitutional growth delay?',
  options:['Adequate bone age','More common in girls','Anal height is affected','A delayed onset of puberty','Presentation is in infancy'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Constitutional growth delay presents with normal health, slowed growth, delayed bone age, and late puberty. It is more common in boys and improves with age.\n\n---\n\n**⚠️ Transcription note — the printed option C.** The bank prints option C as **“Anal height is affected”**, which is plainly a typo for **“Final height is affected”**; it is printed identically again later in the same section, so it is the source’s own error rather than a scan artefact. The option is reproduced here exactly as printed — a printed typo survives verbatim in this bank — and the meaning is unchanged: final height is *not* affected in constitutional delay, which is why C is wrong.\n\n**Expansion — constitutional delay of growth and puberty (CDGP) in one table.** This is the single most examined comparison in the chapter, and `13) Short stature.txt` devotes a slide to it:\n\n| | **Familial short stature** | **Constitutional delay (CDGP)** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Parents | Short — height sits within the mid-parental range | Normal height, but often a “late bloomer” parent |\n| Bone age | **Equal to chronological age** | **Delayed** (often by 2 years or more) |\n| Puberty | Normal timing | **Delayed** |\n| Growth velocity | Normal | Normal but low, then a late catch-up |\n| Final adult height | Short — as predicted by the parents | **Normal** |\n| Sex | Either | **Commoner in boys** |\n\nThe lecture’s Case 2 is exactly this question in clinical form — a 13-year-old boy, no puberty yet, father a “late bloomer”, Tanner stage 1, bone age delayed 2 years, normal labs — and the answer given is “Constitutional delay of growth and puberty”, managed by “Reassurance ± puberty induction, 3-6 months testosterone injections to stimulate puberty at 13-14 years”.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Adequate bone age** — the reverse of the truth. `13) Short stature.txt` states the bone-age rule outright: “A. Delayed → Constitutional delay of growth and puberty/ Endocrine. B. Normal → Familial.” A normal bone age argues *against* CDGP.\n- **More common in girls** — it is commoner in boys, which is also why the classic vignette is a teenage boy who is the shortest in his class.\n- **Final height is affected** — the defining reassurance of CDGP is that adult height is normal. Growth is postponed, not lost.\n- **Presentation is in infancy** — CDGP presents in **late childhood and adolescence**, when peers pull ahead. A growth problem dating from infancy points to a small-for-gestational-age baby, a syndrome, or chronic disease.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Delayed bone age + delayed puberty + normal parents + normal labs = wait, reassure, and offer short-course testosterone only if the psychological burden warrants it. Do not investigate for growth hormone deficiency first; `13) Short stature.txt` warns in bold: “Do not jump to GH tests early !!”\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Constitutional delay = delayed bone age and delayed puberty in a boy with normal parents, and a normal final adult height.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.34 (the same question is printed again on p.161)'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-4', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'What would be the shape of the head if craniosynostosis affects the lambdoid suture?',
  options:['Wide and broad','Long and narrow','Prominent forehead','Flattening of the skull'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Localized Craniosynostosis: Sagittal suture fusion -> Long, narrow skull (scaphocephaly). Coronal suture fusion -> Asymmetrical skull (anterior plagiocephaly). Lambdoid suture fusion -> Posterior flattening of the skull (posterior plagiocephaly).\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — the whole map, because the examiner rotates the suture.** Craniosynostosis is premature fusion of a cranial suture. The skull cannot grow *across* a fused suture, so it compensates by growing *along* it — that single principle generates every named shape, and it is worth reasoning from rather than memorising:\n\n| Suture fused | Growth blocked | Resulting shape | Name |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Sagittal** (front-to-back, midline) | Side-to-side widening | Long and narrow | **Scaphocephaly** (dolichocephaly) |\n| **Coronal**, one side | Forward growth on that side | Asymmetrical, flattened forehead on the fused side | **Anterior plagiocephaly** |\n| **Coronal**, both sides | Forward growth altogether | Short, wide, tall skull with a prominent forehead | **Brachycephaly** |\n| **Lambdoid** | Backward growth | **Flattening of the occiput** | **Posterior plagiocephaly** |\n| **Metopic** | Forehead widening | Triangular, keel-shaped forehead | **Trigonocephaly** |\n\n*(The metopic and bilateral-coronal rows are filled from general medical knowledge — not taken from the course material; the endpoint file’s own box covers only the sagittal, coronal and lambdoid rows.)*\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.** *Wide and broad* is bilateral coronal fusion (brachycephaly). *Long and narrow* is sagittal fusion (scaphocephaly) — by far the commonest single-suture synostosis, and the trap answer because it is the shape most students remember. *Prominent forehead* belongs to brachycephaly and to metopic fusion, not to the lambdoid suture, which sits at the back of the skull.\n\n**The clinical discrimination that matters more than the names.** True lambdoid synostosis is **rare**; the overwhelming majority of flat occiputs in infancy are **positional (deformational) plagiocephaly** from lying supine, which is benign, needs no surgery, and resolves with repositioning and tummy time. The distinction is made on the shape of the whole head from above: positional flattening pushes the ipsilateral ear and forehead *forward* (a parallelogram), while true lambdoid synostosis pulls the ipsilateral ear *backward and down* with a ridge over the suture (a trapezoid) *(not taken from the course material)*. `1) Normal development.txt` illustrates “TOTAL CRANIOSTENOSIS” among the conditions recognisable from the face, so the syllabus expects the entity to be recognised, but it prints no suture-by-suture table.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Fused suture → skull grows parallel to it. Lambdoid is posterior, so the deficit is posterior — a flat occiput.\n\n**⚠️ The bank reprints this question twice more, and the p.197 printing is defective.** p.167 repeats it unchanged; p.197 prints five options instead of four, inserting *Plagiocephalic* in third place while still keying *Flattening of the skull*. Those two are the same finding — the box above calls lambdoid fusion “posterior plagiocephaly” — so that printing carries two correct answers. Per the standing rule the key is recorded as the bank printed it and is not disputed; the clean four-option printing from p.36 is the one held here, and the reprints are cited in `source`.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'A fused suture blocks growth across it, so lambdoid synostosis flattens the occiput (posterior plagiocephaly).',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.36 (the same question is printed again on p.167, and on p.197 with a fifth option added)'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-5', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'How is a very low birth weight infant defined?',
  options:['Birth weight less than 2500 g','Birth weight less than 1500 g','Birth weight less than 1750 g','Birth weight less than 1000 g','Birth weight less than 500'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Classification: Low Birth Weight (LBW): < 2500 g. Very Low Birth Weight (VLBW): < 1500 g. Extremely Low Birth Weight (ELBW): < 1000 g.\n\n---\n\n**⚠️ Transcription note.** On this printing option E reads “Birth weight less than 500” with no unit; the bank reprints the same question later in the section with “less than 500 g”, and the complete form is used here.\n\n**Expansion — the three definitions, and the two other axes they are always confused with.** `7) Intro to neonatology.txt` prints the birth-weight classification exactly as the box gives it:\n\n- “LBW - < 2500 grams · VLBW - < 1500 grams · ELBW - < 1000 grams”\n\nThe examiner’s favourite manoeuvre is to mix this axis with the other two ways a newborn is classified, so hold all three apart:\n\n| Axis | Categories |\n|---|---|\n| **Birth weight** | LBW < 2500 g · VLBW < 1500 g · ELBW < 1000 g |\n| **Gestational age** | Preterm < 37 weeks · Term 37–42 weeks · Post-term > 42 weeks |\n| **Weight for gestational age** | SGA · AGA · LGA |\n\nThese are independent. A 2200 g baby born at 40 weeks is LBW **and** term **and** SGA. A 2200 g baby born at 32 weeks is LBW **and** preterm **and** appropriate for gestational age. Answering a birth-weight question with a gestational-age category is the commonest error in this topic.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.** *< 2500 g* is plain LBW — the umbrella term, and the trap for anyone who has learned only one number. *< 1750 g* is not a recognised category at all; it is a manufactured distractor sitting between the two real ones. *< 1000 g* is ELBW. *< 500 g* is below the usual threshold of viability and is not a defined class.\n\n**Why the categories exist.** They are risk strata, not labels. `7) Intro to neonatology.txt` sets out what falls on the small preterm infant: respiratory distress syndrome type I (“Lung is the last organ to mature into a functional state”), apnoea of prematurity, patent ductus arteriosus, necrotising enterocolitis (“Incidence inversely related to GA”), intraventricular haemorrhage and periventricular leukomalacia — “Both are usually silent in presentation” — hypothermia from a large surface area and thin skin, hypoglycaemia from low glycogen stores, hypocalcaemia from an immature parathyroid, retinopathy of prematurity, anaemia and hyperbilirubinaemia. The lower the weight band, the more of that list applies.\n\n**Filing note.** Kept in growth and puberty because it is a growth-measurement definition and the bank prints it here; the secondary chapter is **neonatal**.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'LBW < 2500 g, VLBW < 1500 g, ELBW < 1000 g — a weight axis independent of gestational age and of SGA/AGA/LGA.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.38 (the same question is printed again on p.169)'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-6', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'A 2-year-old girl is brought to the clinic for a routine check-up. Her mother states that she does not like to eat but prefers eating only a few spoons of pasta each day. What is the best method to check her nutritional status?',
  options:['Head circumference','Height','Weight','Weight-for-height','Skinfold thickness'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Weight-for-height reflects acute malnutrition (wasting), unlike height-for-age (chronic). It is the best tool to assess current nutritional status in toddlers.\n\n---\n\n**⚠️ Transcription note.** On this page the explanation box is laid over the end of the last line and option E prints as “Skinfold thicknes”. The bank reprints the same question later in the section clear of the box, where the option reads “Skinfold thickness”; the complete form is used here.\n\n**Expansion — why a *ratio* beats any single measurement.** A single number cannot separate a small-but-healthy child from a starving one. A 2-year-old on the 3rd centile for weight may be perfectly nourished if she is also on the 3rd centile for height. Weight-for-height removes body size from the equation and asks the only question that matters acutely: **is there enough flesh on this skeleton?**\n\n| Index | What it detects | What it misses |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Weight alone** | Nothing on its own — needs a reference | Cannot separate small from thin |\n| **Height alone** | Stunting, i.e. chronic undernutrition | Blind to acute wasting |\n| **Weight-for-height** | **Wasting — acute undernutrition** | Blind to chronicity (a stunted child can be proportionate) |\n| **Head circumference** | Brain growth | Nutrition, except in prolonged severe deprivation |\n| **Skinfold thickness** | Body fat reserve | Poor reproducibility; observer-dependent |\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.** *Head circumference* answers a different question altogether — the endpoint file’s own summary slide states that occipitofrontal circumference “Reflects brain growth” (`Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf` p.8), and it is preserved until malnutrition is severe and prolonged. *Height* is the stunting measure and moves far too slowly to report a few weeks of poor intake. *Weight* on its own is uninterpretable without a reference for size. *Skinfold thickness* is a genuine measure of fat stores and `16) P.E.M.txt` lists it under anthropometric data (“Others: skin fold thickness, etc....”), but it needs calipers, training and an experienced observer, so it is a research and specialist tool rather than the best clinic method.\n\n**Where this sits in the malnutrition framework.** `16) P.E.M.txt` grades protein–energy malnutrition by the Wellcome classification on weight as a percentage of the 50th centile: 60–80% without oedema is underweight (mild to moderate), below 60% without oedema is **marasmus**, 60–80% *with* oedema is **kwashiorkor**, and below 60% with oedema is **marasmic-kwashiorkor**. The same lecture lists the anthropometric panel as “Length / Height (use percentile charts), Weight for age & weight for height, Head, chest & abdominal circumf., Mid-arm circumference (11.5 cm)”.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** A fussy toddler with normal weight-for-height is a feeding-behaviour problem, not a nutritional one — and that distinction changes the consultation from investigation to reassurance and dietary advice.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Weight-for-height is the clinic measure of acute nutritional status; height-for-age reports chronicity instead.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.40 (the same question is printed again on p.173)'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-7', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'When evaluating a child for stunting, which growth chart will you use?',
  options:['Height-for-age charts','Weight-for-age charts','Weight-for-length charts','Body mass index charts','Mid-arm circumference measurements'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Stunting reflects chronic undernutrition, which primarily affects linear growth (height) rather than just weight.\n\n---\n\n**⚠️ Within-bank reprint.** The endpoint file prints this question twice in the same section — once near the start and again here — with the options in Title Case and the stem reworded from “which growth chart you will use” to “which growth chart will you use”. Same five options, same key, a differently worded box. Both printings are staged and held as separate entries in this bank.\n\n**Expansion — the four-cell grid the examiner draws from.** Every anthropometric question in this section can be answered from one grid: pick the numerator, pick the denominator.\n\n| Index | Numerator ÷ denominator | Names the condition | Time course |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Height-for-age | height ÷ age | **Stunting** | Chronic |\n| Weight-for-height | weight ÷ height | **Wasting** | Acute |\n| Weight-for-age | weight ÷ age | **Underweight** | Mixed, non-specific |\n| BMI-for-age | weight ÷ height² ÷ age | **Overweight / obesity** | Chronic |\n\n“Stunting” names the *height* axis, so the denominator must be age and the numerator must be height. No other option can be right by definition.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.** *Weight-for-age* is the underweight chart and cannot distinguish short from thin — the Wellcome classification in `16) P.E.M.txt` is built on it precisely because it is a blunt population screen. *Weight-for-length* is the wasting chart, the acute counterpart. *BMI charts* are the obesity instrument; `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` states that BMI “is the most reliable and most commonly used clinical indicator of obesity”, that it is “age- and sex-specific” in children, and gives the cut-offs — obese above the 95th centile, overweight 85th–95th, healthy 5th–85th. *Mid-arm circumference* is the rapid screen for acute severe malnutrition (`16) P.E.M.txt`, cut-off 11.5 cm) and again reports wasting.\n\n**A practical point the lecture insists on.** `13) Short stature.txt` requires that the right chart also be the right *reference*: WHO charts for 0–24 months (based on breast-fed infants), CDC charts for 2–19 years, and disease-specific charts where they exist — a Down syndrome child must be plotted on a Down syndrome chart, or normal syndromic short stature will be read as pathology. It also states the interpretive rule that outranks any single plot: “A single measurement is less important than growth trend.”\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Short for age = look for months or years of undernutrition, chronic disease, or an endocrine cause. One height on one visit tells you very little; two heights six months apart tell you almost everything.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Stunting is assessed on height-for-age charts, plotted serially and against the right reference for the child.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.42'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-8', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'A 7-year-old boy is brought by his grandmother to the pediatrician due to concerns about his growth. According to her, the boy’s mother is of average height, while the father is slightly shorter than the mother. On examination, the boy is happy, playful, and shows no dysmorphic features. His height is 110 cm, just below the 3rd centile, and his weight is on the 3rd centile. Physical examination is otherwise normal. What is the most likely cause of his short stature?',
  options:['Achondroplasia','Constitutional delay of growth and puberty','Familial short stature','Growth hormone deficiency','Vitamin D deficiency'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> He has proportionate height and weight both at low centiles, no dysmorphism, and a short parent -> consistent with familial short stature.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — this vignette is the lecture’s own Case 1.** `13) Short stature.txt` prints it almost word for word: an 11-year-old boy, “Height 3rd centile, Normal growth velocity, Parents short, MPH= 165 cm, Normal proportions, Normal puberty stage, Bone age = chronological age”, and the answer given is **“Familial short stature”**, with the accompanying questions — is this pathological? do we investigate? — both answered no.\n\nFour features in this boy’s history do the work:\n\n1. **Height and weight are on the same low centile.** Proportionate smallness means the child is a small *normal* child, not a sick one. The lecture’s discriminator is explicit: “Weight affected > Height → Systemic disease; Height affected, Weight normal/↑ → Endocrine.” Neither applies here.\n2. **No dysmorphic features.** Examination in short stature is a search for syndromes; `13) Short stature.txt` lists “Dysmorphic features” and “Skeletal abnormalities” as specific things to look for, and names Turner, Russell–Silver, Down and Noonan as the syndromic causes.\n3. **The parents are short.** Short parents give a low mid-parental height, and a child tracking within about two centile spaces of it is growing to plan.\n4. **He is well.** Happy, playful, normal examination — chronic disease and psychosocial deprivation both leave marks that are absent.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Achondroplasia** — a *disproportionate* short stature: short limbs with a relatively long trunk, macrocephaly, frontal bossing and a depressed nasal bridge. `13) Short stature.txt` gives it its own slide under “Disproportionate short stature”. This boy is proportionate.\n- **Constitutional delay of growth and puberty** — possible in a boy, but CDGP is defined by a **delayed bone age** and **delayed puberty**, and it typically declares itself in adolescence when peers begin their growth spurt. At 7 with short parents, familial is the better fit. The two are separated by one X-ray: “A. Delayed → Constitutional delay of growth and puberty/ Endocrine. B. Normal → Familial.”\n- **Growth hormone deficiency** — would show *falling* centiles and a poor height velocity. `13) Short stature.txt` gives the threshold for even investigating it: “Severe short stature (height >3 SD below the mean), Height affected > Weight, Height >1.5 SD below MPH, Poor growth velocity (< 4cm per year).” None is met.\n- **Vitamin D deficiency** — rickets is a systemic disease with bone signs (widened wrists, rachitic rosary, bowing) and a poor velocity, not a well child tracking a low centile.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Short but proportionate + well + short parents + tracking the centile = a normal variant. `13) Short stature.txt` closes with the same message: “Most short children are normal variants.”\n\n**⚠️ Within-bank reprint — p.175.** The endpoint file prints this vignette a second time, reworded, with the same five options and the same key. It is held once in the app. The p.175 printing carries the fuller box, and this is the clause it adds:\n\n> The child’s growth follows the 3rd centile, both height and weight proportionate, and parents are short consistent with familial short stature. **Constitutional delay would show delayed puberty signs.**\n\n**Familial short stature against constitutional delay — the discrimination that box raises, and the one the exam always tests.** `13) Short stature.txt` teaches it as two cases rather than as a table: Case 1 is this boy (short parents, normal proportions, normal puberty stage, bone age = chronological age -> familial short stature), and Case 2 is a 13-year-old with no puberty yet, a father who was a late bloomer, Tanner stage 1, a bone age delayed 2 years and normal labs -> constitutional delay, managed by reassurance ± 3-6 months of testosterone to induce puberty at 13-14 years.\n\n| | **Familial short stature** | **Constitutional delay (CDGP)** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Parents | **Short** | Normal height, often a late-developing parent |\n| Bone age | **= chronological age** | **Delayed** |\n| Puberty | **Normal timing** | **Delayed** |\n| Age at presentation | Often school entry | **Adolescence** |\n| Sex | Either | **Boys** |\n| Final height | **Short**, as predicted | **Normal** |\n\nThe parents, the bone age and the puberty stage are the lecture’s own discriminators; the last two rows — age at presentation and final height — are standard teaching and are *not taken from the course material*. At 7 there are no pubertal signs to read in either direction, which is why the parents’ heights and the wrist film carry the diagnosis: “A. Delayed → Constitutional delay of growth and puberty/ Endocrine. B. Normal → Familial.”\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Proportionate short stature in a well child with short parents, tracking a low centile, is familial short stature.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.44 (the same vignette is printed again on p.175, reworded, with the same options and the same key; the p.175 box is the fuller one and is quoted in the explanation)'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-9', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'What is the most important factor affecting growth during the "infantile phase" in humans?',
  options:['Maternal nutrition','Infant nutrition','Size at birth','Vitamins','Parental size'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> During infancy, nutrition is the main driver of growth, unlike later childhood (GH-driven) or fetal life (placental/maternal factors).\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — the four phases of growth and what drives each.** This is the backbone of the whole chapter, and the bank returns to it repeatedly with different option sets, so learn the drivers rather than any one answer:\n\n| Phase | Timing | Main driver | Share of final height |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Fetal** | Conception to birth | Placental function, maternal nutrition and uterine environment | **30%** — and the fastest growth velocity of life |\n| **Infantile** | Birth to about 2 years | **Nutrition** (with thyroid hormone and insulin permissive) | **15%** |\n| **Childhood** | ~2 years to puberty | **Growth hormone / IGF-1**, with thyroid hormone | ~40% |\n| **Pubertal** | Puberty to epiphyseal fusion | **Sex steroids** — testosterone and oestradiol | **15%** |\n\nThe endpoint file’s own summary slides give two of these figures directly: “Fetal phase (30% of final height, Fastest period of Growth)” and “Infantile phase (15% of final height)” (`Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf` p.7). `14) Puberty.txt` supplies the pubertal share: the growth spurt is “Mediated by GONADAL STEROIDS (testosterone and estradiol)”, with “Estrogen more than androgens”, and it “Add[s] 15% to final adult height” before “Sex steroids cause fusion of epiphyseal growth plates and cessation of growth”. `13) Short stature.txt` lists the hormonal set for childhood growth as GH/IGF-1, thyroid hormone, insulin, sex steroids and glucocorticoids.\n\n**Why the answer is *infant* nutrition and not *maternal* nutrition.** The distinction is the whole question. Maternal nutrition governs the **fetal** phase, through the placenta. Once the cord is cut the determinant becomes what actually reaches the baby’s gut — breast milk or formula, in adequate volume, absorbed. That is why growth faltering clusters in infancy, and why the first response to a faltering infant is a feeding history rather than a hormone assay.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.** *Maternal nutrition* is the fetal-phase driver. *Size at birth* sets the starting point and predicts catch-up (or its absence — `13) Short stature.txt` notes that “10% intrauterine growth restriction do not catch-up growth”), but it does not drive the *rate* of infant growth. *Vitamins* matter only where a specific deficiency exists, such as vitamin D; they are not the main determinant of normal infant growth. *Parental size* becomes visible during the childhood phase, when a baby “finds” the centile predicted by mid-parental height, and it is the reason infants legitimately cross centiles early on.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** A faltering infant is a nutritional problem until proved otherwise. A faltering 6-year-old with a normal appetite is far more likely to be endocrine.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Growth is driven by maternal/placental factors in fetal life, nutrition in infancy, GH in childhood and sex steroids in puberty.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.46 (the same question is printed again on p.177)'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-10', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'What is the main drive for growth in the childhood phase?',
  options:['Nutrition','Growth hormone','Testosterone','Thyroid hormones','Testosterone and estrogen'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> From 2 years to puberty, growth depends mostly on GH. Pubertal hormones dominate later.\n\n---\n\n**⚠️ The bank prints this question three times in this section**, always with the same five options and the same key, once with “drive” and once with “driver” in the stem. It is held once in the app.\n\n**Expansion — the childhood phase belongs to the GH/IGF-1 axis.** From about 2 years until the pubertal growth spurt, height accrues steadily at roughly 5–7 cm a year, and the pacemaker is growth hormone acting largely through hepatic IGF-1. `13) Short stature.txt` devotes a slide to the “GH/IGF-1 axis” and lists the hormonal influences on childhood growth as “GH/IGF-1, Thyroid hormone, Insulin, Sex steroids, Glucocorticoids”. It also tells you how the axis is interrogated, and in what order:\n\n| Step | Tests |\n|---|---|\n| **Step One** | CBC, ESR, renal functions, electrolytes, LFT |\n| **Step Two** | TSH, fT4, coeliac screen, **IGF-1 and IGFBP-3**, **bone age**, **karyotyping (all girls)** |\n| **Step Three** | **GH testing** — provocation |\n\nwith the warning printed across the slide: “Do not jump to GH tests early !!”\n\n**Why growth hormone and not the other four.** *Nutrition* is the infantile driver; by school age a well child in a food-secure home is no longer nutrition-limited, which is why an isolated fall in height with a preserved weight in this age group points at hormones rather than intake. *Testosterone* and *testosterone and estrogen* belong to the **pubertal** phase — `14) Puberty.txt` states that the pubertal spurt is “Mediated by GONADAL STEROIDS (testosterone and estradiol)”, that oestrogen contributes “more than androgens”, and that these steroids ultimately close the epiphyses. Offering them for the childhood phase is a phase error. *Thyroid hormones* are the strongest distractor and are genuinely required — hypothyroidism is a classic cause of childhood growth failure, and `52) hypothyrodism.txt` opens by emphasising “linear growth and brain” development — but thyroid hormone is **permissive**: it is needed for GH to work, and its deficiency causes growth failure, yet it does not set the childhood growth rate. GH does.\n\n**How GH deficiency actually presents.** `13) Short stature.txt` gives the profile: severe short stature more than 3 SD below the mean, height affected more than weight, a poor growth velocity below 4 cm per year, low IGF-1 and IGFBP-3, a delayed bone age, and confirmation by GH provocation — because “No use of basal GH level ! GH secreted in pulses”, requiring two provocation tests (glucagon, insulin, arginine or clonidine). It also lists the red flags for an underlying cause: neonatal hypoglycaemia, prolonged jaundice and microphallus; craniofacial abnormalities; cranial irradiation, head trauma or CNS infection; consanguinity or an affected relative — with craniopharyngioma, “a developmental tumor arising from squamous remnant of Rathke’s pouch”, accounting for about 4%.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Phase tells you which hormone to suspect. Infancy → food. Childhood → GH (and thyroid). Puberty → sex steroids.\n\n**⚠️ The third printing — p.195** rewords “drive” into “driver” and prints a fuller box than p.48 does:\n\n> During childhood (after infancy, before puberty), growth is largely regulated by growth hormone rather than nutrition or sex hormones.\n\n**The phases, taken from that box’s own boundaries.** The childhood phase runs after infancy and before puberty — roughly 2 years to the onset of the pubertal spurt. It is the longest of the four phases, and although its velocity is the lowest of the growing years, its sheer length makes it the largest single contributor to final height.\n\n| Phase | Boundaries | Driver |\n|---|---|---|\n| Fetal | conception → birth | Placenta and maternal nutrition |\n| Infantile | birth → about 2 y | **Nutrition** |\n| **Childhood** | **about 2 y → puberty** | **GH / IGF-1**, thyroid hormone permissive |\n| Pubertal | puberty → epiphyseal fusion | **Sex steroids** |\n\n⚠️ The lectures put a number on only one phase: `14) Puberty.txt` says the pubertal spurt adds “15% to final adult height”. The shares usually quoted for the other three — about 30% fetal, 15% infantile, 40% childhood — are *not taken from the course material*.\n\nWhy the length of the phase matters clinically: a small, sustained deficit over eight childhood years costs more height than a dramatic one over eight months. That is why `13) Short stature.txt` makes growth **velocity** a criterion in its own right — “Height velocity <1.5 SD for age”, and “Poor growth velocity (< 4cm per year)” as a trigger to investigate GH — and why it prints across a slide that “A single measurement is less important than growth trend”.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Childhood-phase growth is driven by growth hormone acting through IGF-1, with thyroid hormone permissive.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.48 (the same question is printed again on p.179, and on p.195 with “drive” reworded to “driver”; the p.195 box is the fuller one and is quoted in the explanation)'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-11', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'Which of the following statements regarding measuring and plotting children’s growth is true?',
  options:['Correction for prematurity should be made up to the age of 6 months','Final height usually approximates the father’s height','Once a child is 5 years old, height does not usually deviate from the centile line','It is normal for an infant’s weight to cross centiles in the first months of life'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Early infant growth is variable, so centile crossing is common. By age 5, growth usually stabilizes along a centile.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — each of the four statements, taken in turn.**\n\n**D is true — early centile crossing is normal.** Birth weight is set largely by the intrauterine environment; the centile a baby will actually track is set by genetics. In the first months the infant therefore migrates from one to the other, upward if the baby was constitutionally small in utero (catch-up) or downward if the baby was constitutionally large (catch-down). This is why a single crossing in early infancy in a well, feeding baby is not growth faltering. `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` puts a threshold on when crossing *does* matter: faltering growth is “present if an infant’s weight falls across two centile spaces” — and it insists that faltering growth is a “Description, not a diagnosis”.\n\n**A is false — but only just, and the qualifier is what the examiner wants.** `13) Short stature.txt` prints the rule for correcting for gestational age as: “Continued for the first year of life. In extremely preterm infants, until 2 years of age.” `Lec 1 Normal development_.txt` states the developmental counterpart plainly: “Correction is not required after age of 2 years.” Six months is too short on either statement.\n\n**B is false — final height follows the *mid-parental* height, not the father’s.** Mid-parental height is calculated from both parents (`13) Short stature.txt` lists “Mid-parental height calculation” under history, and defines short stature partly as “Height >2 SD below mid-parental height”). Taking the father alone would systematically over-predict a daughter’s height and mis-predict any child of parents with dissimilar heights.\n\n**C is false as an absolute, and it is the most tempting distractor** because it is *nearly* true. After the early infant migration, a child does normally track a centile — but growth can and does deviate after 5 years, and when it does it is a red flag, not a normal event. That is exactly the situation the rest of this chapter tests: a child falling from the 50th to below the 3rd centile between 2 and 3 years, or a 7-year-old dropping from the 15th to the 5th. `13) Short stature.txt` includes “Serial measurements cross growth centile lines” among the *definitions* of short stature, which is only meaningful because crossing is abnormal at that age rather than impossible.\n\n**The interpretive rule underneath all four.** “A single measurement is less important than growth trend” (`13) Short stature.txt`). Every statement here is really a statement about the trend line, and the discipline being taught is to plot serially on the correct chart — WHO for 0–24 months, CDC for 2–19 years, and a disease-specific chart where one exists.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Crossing centiles in the first few months: watch. Crossing centiles after the toddler years: investigate.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Centile crossing is normal in early infancy and abnormal in later childhood; final height follows mid-parental, not paternal, height.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.50 (the same question is printed again on p.183)'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-12', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'Which of the following is a character of familial short stature?',
  options:['Height falls within 2 centile spaces of mid-parental height','More common in girls','Delayed onset of puberty','Presentation is in adolescence'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Children with familial short stature track a low centile consistent with genetic potential. Puberty is normal and not delayed.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — familial short stature is a *diagnosis of concordance*.** The child is short, and the child is short **in proportion to the parents**. That is the entire concept, and the mid-parental height is the instrument that measures it. `13) Short stature.txt` lists “Mid-parental height calculation” as a required part of the history, and defines short stature in part as “Height >2 SD below mid-parental height” — the mirror image of this option, which says the height falls *within* the expected band.\n\n| | **Familial short stature** | **Constitutional delay (CDGP)** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Parental height | **Short** | Usually normal, often a late-developing parent |\n| Height vs mid-parental height | **Concordant** | Discordant, then catches up |\n| Bone age | **Equal to chronological age** | **Delayed** |\n| Puberty | **Normal timing** | **Delayed** |\n| Age at presentation | Any — usually pre-school or school entry | **Adolescence** |\n| Sex | Either | **Boys** |\n| Final adult height | **Short**, as predicted | **Normal** |\n\nThe lecture’s Case 1 is the clinical form of the correct option: an 11-year-old boy on the 3rd centile with normal growth velocity, short parents, MPH 165 cm, normal proportions, normal puberty stage and a bone age equal to chronological age — answered as “Familial short stature”, with no investigation required.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.** *More common in girls* — familial short stature has no sex predilection; it is **constitutional delay** that is commoner in boys, and this option is lifted straight from the CDGP column to catch a student who has learned the two lists as one. *Delayed onset of puberty* is the defining feature of CDGP and is precisely what is **not** seen in familial short stature; a short child who enters puberty on time and remains short is short because that is the family’s height. *Presentation is in adolescence* is again CDGP: a short child with short parents is usually noticed early, often at school entry when height is compared with a whole class, whereas the constitutionally delayed child looks unremarkable until peers begin their pubertal spurt and pull away.\n\n**The single investigation that separates them.** A bone age on an X-ray of the left wrist and hand, read against Greulich and Pyle. `13) Short stature.txt` gives the rule in two lines: “A. Delayed → Constitutional delay of growth and puberty/ Endocrine. B. Normal → Familial.” Note that a delayed bone age does not by itself mean CDGP — endocrine disease delays it too, which is why the thyroid and the GH/IGF-1 axis sit in the same investigation step.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Short parents + normal bone age + normal puberty + tracking the centile = a normal variant that needs plotting, not testing.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Familial short stature is height concordant with mid-parental height, with a normal bone age and normally timed puberty.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.52 (the same question is printed again on p.185)'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-13', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'A 13-year-old girl is brought to the clinic for concerns about delayed puberty. She is in the 10th percentile for height and 25th percentile for weight. Physical examination reveals no signs of puberty, and her bone age is mildly delayed. Her mother also had a past history of delayed puberty. What is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Turner syndrome','Constitutional growth delay','Growth hormone deficiency','Hypothyroidism','Familial short stature'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Delayed puberty + mildly delayed bone age + family history = constitutional growth delay, a benign variant.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — first, is this delayed puberty at all?** `14) Puberty.txt` gives the definition to apply before anything else: delayed puberty is “Failure of development of any pubertal feature by 13 yrs of age in females or by 14 yrs of age in males.” At 13 with no pubertal signs, this girl has just crossed that line, which is what makes the consultation legitimate.\n\n**Three findings then converge on constitutional delay.**\n\n1. **A delayed bone age.** `13) Short stature.txt`: “A. Delayed → Constitutional delay of growth and puberty/ Endocrine. B. Normal → Familial.” The delay tells you her skeleton is younger than her birth certificate — she has more growing time left than her age suggests, which is the whole reason her final height will be normal.\n2. **A family history of delayed puberty.** CDGP runs in families; the lecture’s Case 2 makes the point with a father who was a “late bloomer”.\n3. **Her height and weight are only modestly reduced and are proportionate** — 10th and 25th centile. She is not severely short, and weight is not falling behind height, so neither the systemic nor the endocrine pattern of `13) Short stature.txt` (“Weight affected > Height → Systemic disease; Height affected, Weight normal/↑ → Endocrine”) applies.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Turner syndrome** — the most important thing to exclude in *any* short girl, and `13) Short stature.txt` puts “Karyotyping (All girls)” in its investigation ladder for that reason. But Turner is **hypergonadotropic** hypogonadism: gonadotrophins are raised, and there is usually severe short stature well below the 3rd centile plus stigmata — `6) Genetically determined disease_.txt` lists neck webbing, widely spaced nipples, a wide carrying angle, lymphoedema of the hands and feet, spoon-shaped nails and coarctation of the aorta. A girl on the 10th centile with no stigmata and a *delayed* bone age fits CDGP far better.\n- **Growth hormone deficiency** — would give severe short stature (more than 3 SD below the mean by the lecture’s own threshold), height affected more than weight, and a growth velocity under 4 cm a year. It also does not classically run in the family as delayed *puberty*.\n- **Hypothyroidism** — gives the endocrine pattern with weight normal or rising while height falls, plus constipation, lethargy and cold intolerance (`52) hypothyrodism.txt`). This girl’s weight centile sits *above* her height centile only modestly and she is otherwise well.\n- **Familial short stature** — would give a **normal** bone age and **normally timed** puberty. The delayed bone age alone excludes it.\n\n**Management, because the examiner asks it next.** `13) Short stature.txt` answers the Case 2 management question directly: “Reassurance ± puberty induction, 3-6 months testosterone injections to stimulate puberty at 13-14 years.” In girls the equivalent is a short course of low-dose oestrogen where the psychological burden justifies it *(the female regimen is not stated in the cached decks — not taken from the course material)*.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Delayed puberty with a delayed bone age and a family history of late puberty is constitutional delay, and needs reassurance not investigation.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.54 (the same question is printed again on p.187)'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-14', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'A 4-month-old infant is brought in for concerns about small size. The infant was born at 32 weeks gestation and has been primarily formula-fed. Which of the following is the first most important factor to consider in managing this infant’s growth?',
  options:['Adjusting for corrected gestational age','Introducing solid foods early','Switching to a higher-calorie formula','Recommending breastfeeding'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> For preterm babies, growth assessment must use corrected age (chronological age – weeks of prematurity) until about 2 years.\n\n---\n\n**⚠️ Note on the box and the lectures.** The box states the correction applies “until about 2 years”. `13) Short stature.txt` is more precise and slightly narrower: correction for gestational age is “Continued for the first year of life. In extremely preterm infants, until 2 years of age.” `Lec 1 Normal development_.txt` gives the developmental counterpart as “Correction is not required after age of 2 years.” The bank states the wider rule without the qualifier; the lectures’ version is the one to reproduce in an examination that asks for the duration, and the box is not wrong so much as unqualified.\n\n**Expansion — do the arithmetic before you do anything else.** This baby was born 8 weeks early. At a chronological age of 4 months (about 17 weeks), the corrected age is roughly **2 months**. Plotting a 2-month-old on a 4-month-old’s chart manufactures growth failure out of nothing, and the intervention that follows — extra calories, early solids, investigation — is then a response to an artefact. `Lec 1 Normal development_.txt` makes the same point for development: “The anticipated developmental skills of a 9-month-old baby (chronological age) born 3 months early at 28 weeks’ gestation are more like those of a 6-month-old baby (corrected age).”\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong, and why they are wrong in this *order*.**\n\n- **Introducing solid foods early** — actively harmful here. `15.2) Infant Feeding.txt` places complementary feeding “after 6 months”, with pureed, mashed and semisolid foods at 6 months, finger foods at 8 months and family foods at 12 months. At a corrected age of 2 months this infant is neurologically nowhere near able to handle solids, and early introduction risks choking and displaces milk calories.\n- **Switching to a higher-calorie formula** — a legitimate tool in a genuinely faltering preterm infant, but it is a treatment, and you have not yet established that there is anything to treat. Doing this before correcting for gestational age is treating the chart rather than the child.\n- **Recommending breastfeeding** — breast milk is preferred, and the lecture material devotes a whole deck to it, but at 4 months of formula feeding this is neither the immediate step nor a realistic single intervention, and it does not address the question asked.\n\n**When correction has been applied and the baby is *still* small.** Then the standard faltering-growth approach begins. `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` defines faltering as a weight fall “across two centile spaces”, lists the red flags — dysmorphic features, failure to gain weight despite adequate caloric intake, recurrent vomiting, diarrhoea or dehydration, organomegaly or lymphadenopathy, signs of chronic respiratory disease, developmental delay — and gives management as “Dietary and behavioural modification designed to increase energy intake and monitoring growth”, with “Investigations should be guided by history and examination.”\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Correct the age, re-plot, then decide. Most “small preterms” are the right size for their corrected age.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Plot preterm infants at corrected age before calling growth abnormal — through the first year, and to 2 years if extremely preterm.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.56 (the same question is printed again on p.191)'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-15', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'What is the fastest period of growth in a normal human?',
  options:['Fetal','Infantile','Childhood','Prepubertal','Pubertal'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> The fetal period has the highest growth velocity, with rapid cell division and tissue growth. Postnatal phases are slower.\n\n---\n\n**⚠️ The bank contradicts itself on this fact, and the contradiction is worth knowing.** Elsewhere in this same section the endpoint file asks “Which is the fastest period of growth throughout life?” over a four-option list that **does not offer *Fetal* at all** (Infancy / Childhood / Puberty / Adolescence), keys **Infancy**, and prints a box claiming infancy surpasses the fetal period. That claim is wrong on the file’s own evidence: the present question keys **Fetal**, the bank prints this same Fetal-keyed question a second time later in the section, and the file’s own summary slide states “Fetal phase (30% of final height, **Fastest period of Growth**)” (`Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf` p.7). Both keys are reproduced as printed, per the rule that the bank’s printed key is what the entry carries; the discrepancy is recorded here so that a learner meeting the other question knows why its key differs. In an examination, answer **fetal** whenever fetal is on the list, and **infancy** only when it is not.\n\n**Expansion — the velocity curve, in numbers.** Growth velocity is highest before birth and falls almost continuously afterwards, with one interruption:\n\n| Phase | Approximate velocity | Share of final height |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Fetal** | Fastest of life — the second trimester carries the peak linear velocity | **30%** |\n| **Infantile** (0–2 y) | Very rapid but decelerating: length rises steeply in year one, then slows | **15%** |\n| **Childhood** (2 y–puberty) | Slowest and steadiest, roughly 5–7 cm/year | ~40% |\n| **Pubertal** | A second peak — the growth spurt — then epiphyseal fusion | **15%** |\n\n*(The centimetre-per-year figures are filled from general medical knowledge — not taken from the course material; the cached decks give the percentage shares and the drivers, not the velocities.)*\n\n**The pubertal spurt is a *second* peak, not the highest one.** `14) Puberty.txt` states that the spurt is “Mediated by GONADAL STEROIDS (testosterone and estradiol)”, that oestrogen contributes “more than androgens”, and that it “Add[s] 15% to final adult height” before “Sex steroids cause fusion of epiphyseal growth plates and cessation of growth”. It also notes that “Girls have peak height velocity average 2 years before boys” — which is why girls are temporarily taller in early adolescence and why boys, given two extra pre-pubertal years of childhood growth, end taller.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.** *Infantile* is the fastest **postnatal** phase and is the answer only when fetal is not offered. *Childhood* is the slowest phase. *Prepubertal* is the tail of childhood — slower still, immediately before the spurt. *Pubertal* is the second peak, and it is the answer to “fastest **postnatal**” only in the sense of an acceleration, never in absolute terms against fetal life.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'The fetal period has the highest growth velocity of life and supplies 30% of final height; puberty is a second, smaller peak.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.58 (the same question is printed again on p.193)'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-16', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'A 3-year-old boy’s height has fallen from the 50th percentile to below the 3rd percentile over the past year. What is the most likely cause?',
  options:['Familial short stature','Constitutional growth delay','Growth hormone deficiency','Turner syndrome'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Crossing down centiles is always abnormal. A significant drop in height (not weight) is most suggestive of GH deficiency.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — the discriminator is *crossing*, not *being short*.** Two of the four options describe children who are short but who have always been short; only the pathological causes make a child *become* short. `13) Short stature.txt` lists “Serial measurements cross growth centile lines” among the definitions of short stature, alongside “Height velocity <1.5 SD for age and pubertal staging” — both of which are statements about the slope of the line rather than its position. A drop from the 50th to below the 3rd centile in one year is a catastrophic loss of velocity and cannot be a normal variant.\n\n**Why growth hormone deficiency.** The lecture gives the indications for suspecting it, and this boy meets three of the four: “Severe short stature (height >3 SD below the mean); **Height affected > Weight**; Height >1.5 SD below MPH; **Poor growth velocity (< 4cm per year)**.” The height-not-weight pattern is the same rule that runs through the whole chapter — “Weight affected > Height → Systemic disease; Height affected, Weight normal/↑ → Endocrine.”\n\n**How it would be worked up.** Step One screening bloods (CBC, ESR, renal function, electrolytes, LFT), then Step Two — TSH and fT4, coeliac screen, **IGF-1 and IGFBP-3**, bone age, and karyotype in any girl — and only then Step Three, GH provocation testing. `13) Short stature.txt` is emphatic that a random GH level is useless: “No use of basal GH level ! GH secreted in pulses”, and “2 GH provocation tests (sequential or on separate days) are required”, using glucagon, insulin, arginine or clonidine. In deficiency you expect “Low levels of IGF-1 and IGFBP-3” and a “Delayed bone age”. Once confirmed, look for a cause: “MRI brain (pituitary view), Fundus: papilledema, Visual field assessment” — bitemporal hemianopia points at the optic chiasm and a craniopharyngioma, which accounts for about 4% and is “Not truly malignant, but locally invasive”.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Familial short stature** — a child who has *always* tracked a low centile in line with short parents. It cannot produce a fall from the 50th centile.\n- **Constitutional growth delay** — declares itself in **adolescence**, not at 3, and its hallmark is delayed puberty with a delayed bone age; the growth line runs low but roughly parallel rather than plunging.\n- **Turner syndrome** — a real cause of falling height, and the option to take seriously in a **girl**. This patient is a boy, so it is excluded on sex alone. (Note how the bank sets the same trap in reverse elsewhere: “karyotyping (All girls)”.)\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Crossing centiles downward in a young child is pathological until proved otherwise. Ask first whether it is the weight line or the height line that is falling — that single observation splits systemic disease from endocrine disease.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'A falling height centile with a preserved weight centile is the endocrine pattern — in a young child, suspect GH deficiency.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.60 (the same question is printed again on p.199)'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-17', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'Which of the following statements is FALSE as regards growth in males?',
  options:['Adult males are taller than adult females','Males have a longer childhood growth phase than females','The peak height at puberty is higher in males than in females','Growth stops earlier in males'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Growth stops later in males because puberty occurs later and lasts longer.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — the whole sex difference in adult height comes from two facts, and this question tests both.**\n\n**Fact one: boys start puberty later.** `14) Puberty.txt` states it directly — “Girls have peak height velocity average 2 years before boys.” Because the childhood phase runs from about 2 years until the pubertal spurt begins, a later start means a **longer childhood phase** — which is option B, and it is true. Those extra pre-pubertal years are spent growing at 5–7 cm a year, and they are the larger half of the male height advantage.\n\n**Fact two: the male spurt is bigger.** The pubertal growth spurt is “Mediated by GONADAL STEROIDS (testosterone and estradiol)” and adds about 15% to final adult height (`14) Puberty.txt`). In boys the peak height velocity is higher than in girls — option C, also true.\n\nPut together: boys grow for longer *and* spurt harder, so adult males are taller than adult females — option A, true. And because puberty starts later and takes longer to complete, **epiphyseal fusion happens later in boys**, so growth stops **later**, not earlier. Option D is therefore the false statement and the correct answer to a question that asks which statement is FALSE.\n\n| | **Girls** | **Boys** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Puberty onset | Earlier | **About 2 years later** |\n| Childhood phase | Shorter | **Longer** |\n| Peak height velocity | Lower | **Higher** |\n| Epiphyseal fusion | Earlier | **Later** |\n| Final adult height | Shorter | **Taller** |\n\n**The mechanism of stopping, because it is counter-intuitive.** Growth ends when the epiphyseal growth plates fuse, and `14) Puberty.txt` attributes that to the sex steroids themselves: “Sex steroids cause fusion of epiphyseal growth plates and cessation of growth.” The same hormones that drive the spurt also end it — which is exactly why untreated precocious puberty produces a tall child who becomes a short adult, and why GnRH agonists preserve final height by postponing that fusion.\n\n**Read the stem twice.** This is a negatively worded question (“FALSE”), and three of the four options are true statements. Marking a true statement because it *looks* right is the commonest way this item is lost.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** In boys, later start + bigger spurt + later fusion = taller adults. Anything that brings puberty forward steals height by closing the plates early.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Boys enter puberty about two years later, grow for longer and fuse their epiphyses later — so male growth stops later, not earlier.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.62 (the same question is printed again on p.203)'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-18', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'You are giving a lecture to medical students about the treatment of precocious puberty and its importance in preserving height. You discuss certain population segments where treatment would be most indicated and beneficial. Which of the following patient populations would benefit most from GnRH agonist therapy?',
  options:['Younger children with rapidly progressing noncentral precocious puberty','Younger children with rapidly progressing central precocious puberty','Older children with rapidly progressing noncentral precocious puberty','Older children with rapidly progressing central precocious puberty','Younger boys with slowly progressing central precocious puberty'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> GnRH agonists are most effective in young children with fast-progressing central precocious puberty, as treatment delays bone maturation and helps preserve adult height.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — three separate decisions are hidden in this one option list.** Work through them in order and only one option survives.\n\n**Decision 1: central or peripheral?** A GnRH agonist works by continuously stimulating the pituitary GnRH receptor, which paradoxically **downregulates** it and switches off LH and FSH release. It therefore acts *only* on disease driven by the hypothalamic–pituitary–gonadal axis. `14) Puberty.txt` defines central precocious puberty as “a result of the early activation of the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis”. Peripheral (noncentral, gonadotrophin-independent) precocity is driven by sex steroid produced **downstream** of the pituitary — an adrenal or gonadal tumour, congenital adrenal hyperplasia, McCune–Albright syndrome, or an exogenous source such as a parent’s testosterone gel — and in that setting the gonadotrophins are already suppressed, so there is nothing for a GnRH agonist to switch off. **Options A and C are eliminated.**\n\n**Decision 2: younger or older?** The purpose of treatment is to preserve adult height by preventing early epiphyseal fusion — `14) Puberty.txt`: “Sex steroids cause fusion of epiphyseal growth plates and cessation of growth.” A younger child has far more growth remaining to protect, and a bone age that has not yet run away. In an older child close to fusion the height is largely already spent and treatment buys little. **Option D is eliminated.**\n\n**Decision 3: rapidly or slowly progressing?** Slowly progressing central precocity often behaves benignly, with a bone age that keeps pace with height and a normal predicted adult height; these children are observed rather than treated. **Option E is eliminated** — and note it is doubly wrong, being both slowly progressing and, in a boy, the group in which a structural cause must be hunted rather than a benign course assumed.\n\nWhat is left is **B: younger children with rapidly progressing central precocious puberty.**\n\n**Why boys deserve a second thought.** `14) Puberty.txt` gives the epidemiology that changes practice: central precocious puberty “occurs 5-10 folds more frequently in girls than in boys because ovaries are very sensitive to gonadotrophin secretion”; “A high prevalence of idiopathic central precocious puberty has been reported in girls (90%)”, whereas “structural central nervous system (CNS) abnormality can be demonstrated in up to 75% of boys”. So in a boy, central precocity is a brain scan until proved otherwise — the deck lists “MRI brain with contrast for pituitary gland in gonadotropin-dependent PP”, and warns that with brain tumours “The only sign is precocious puberty”, with neuroendocrine features such as diabetes insipidus, hyperthermia, gelastic seizures, obesity and cachexia, and visual field defects.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** GnRH agonist = central disease only, and the younger and faster the child, the more height there is to save.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'GnRH agonists work only in central precocious puberty, and benefit most the young child whose disease is progressing rapidly.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.64'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-19', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'Which of the following is NOT associated with short stature?',
  options:['Chronic renal failure','Hypothyroidism','PEM','Thalassemia major','G6PD deficiency'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Conditions like chronic renal failure, hypothyroidism, PEM, and thalassemia major impair growth. G6PD deficiency only causes hemolysis during oxidative stress but does not affect linear growth.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — the principle: growth fails when a disease is *continuous*.** Any illness that imposes a sustained metabolic, nutritional or endocrine burden costs height. G6PD deficiency is the exception on this list because it is **episodic**: the enzyme defect is silent until an oxidant challenge — fava beans, certain drugs, infection — precipitates an acute haemolytic crisis, after which the child returns to baseline. Between crises there is no chronic anaemia, no chronic inflammation, and therefore no growth cost.\n\n**Why each of the other four does impair growth.**\n\n- **Chronic renal failure** — a classic multi-mechanism cause: acidosis, renal osteodystrophy from disordered vitamin D and phosphate handling, anorexia and poor intake, anaemia, and GH resistance. `13) Short stature.txt` places “Renal functions” and “Electrolytes” in **Step One** of the investigation of short stature, which is exactly why: renal disease is common, silent, and treatable.\n- **Hypothyroidism** — the endocrine cause the chapter returns to repeatedly. `52) hypothyrodism.txt` opens with an emphasis on “linear growth and brain” development and describes the child as “Short with infantile proportions”, with constipation, lethargy, coarse features, a large tongue and an umbilical hernia. It produces the signature endocrine pattern of falling height with preserved or rising weight.\n- **PEM (protein–energy malnutrition)** — the commonest cause of poor growth worldwide. `16) P.E.M.txt` lists “Growth failure” as the first clinical feature of both marasmus (weight below 60% of the 50th centile, loss of subcutaneous fat, muscle wasting, no oedema) and kwashiorkor (weight 60–80% with oedema, hair and skin changes, hepatomegaly), and records among the consequences “Permanent delay in intellectual developement”.\n- **Thalassaemia major** — chronic severe anaemia with tissue hypoxia, a huge marrow expansion and metabolic demand, and — the part most often forgotten — **transfusional iron overload damaging the pituitary**, producing GH deficiency and hypogonadotropic hypogonadism, so growth fails both from the anaemia and from an acquired endocrinopathy *(the iron-overload mechanism is filled from general medical knowledge — not taken from the course material)*.\n\n**How to attack an “NOT associated” stem.** Do not look for the odd one out by feel. Ask of each option: *is this disease continuously present, and does it cost energy, oxygen or hormone?* Four here answer yes. G6PD deficiency answers no.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Chronic disease of any organ system is a cause of short stature, which is why the first tier of investigation is a systemic screen — CBC, ESR, renal function, electrolytes, LFT — before any hormone is measured.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Continuous chronic disease costs height; an episodic disorder such as G6PD deficiency does not impair linear growth.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.66'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-20', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'A 6-month-old infant has an upper-to-lower segment ratio of 1.7:1. What does this indicate?',
  options:['Normal','Long trunk','Short trunk','Disproportionate limbs'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> At birth, the ratio is 1.7:1, gradually decreasing with age as limbs grow faster than the trunk. Thus, 1.7:1 at 6 months is expected and normal.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — what the segments are, and why the ratio moves.** The **lower segment** is measured from the top of the pubic symphysis to the floor; the **upper segment** is the total height (or length) minus the lower segment, and represents head plus trunk. A newborn is built head- and trunk-heavy — an expression of the cephalocaudal direction of growth that `1) Normal development.txt` lists as the first principle of development (“Directional Pattern: Cephalocaudal Pattern (Head to Tail)”). Through infancy and childhood the **limbs grow faster than the trunk**, so the ratio falls steadily:\n\n| Age | Approximate upper : lower segment ratio |\n|---|---|\n| Birth | **1.7 : 1** |\n| 3 years | About 1.3 : 1 |\n| 7–10 years | About **1 : 1** |\n| Adult | Slightly below 1 : 1 |\n\n*(The numeric ratios other than the one printed in the bank’s own box are filled from general medical knowledge — not taken from the course material. `13) Short stature.txt` requires the measurement but prints no table: under Examination it lists “Proportions (upper/lower segment, arm span)” without giving values.)*\n\n**Why the measurement is taken at all — it splits short stature in two.** Every child with short stature is first classified as **proportionate** or **disproportionate**, and that single division reroutes the whole work-up:\n\n| | **Proportionate short stature** | **Disproportionate short stature** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Segments | Ratio appropriate for age | Ratio abnormal for age |\n| Typical causes | Familial, constitutional delay, endocrine (GH deficiency, hypothyroidism), chronic disease, malnutrition, Turner, SGA without catch-up | **Skeletal dysplasias** — achondroplasia and its relatives; rickets; spinal disease |\n| Next step | Growth chart, bone age, systemic screen, hormones | Skeletal survey and genetic assessment |\n\n`13) Short stature.txt` gives “Disproportionate short stature — Achondroplasia” its own slide, which is the entity this measurement exists to catch: short limbs against a relatively preserved trunk, giving a **persistently high** upper-to-lower ratio well past the age at which it should have fallen.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.** *Long trunk* and *short trunk* both misread the ratio as a statement about the trunk in isolation; the ratio is a comparison, and at 6 months the value given is simply the expected one. *Disproportionate limbs* would be the answer if this ratio were found in an older child — 1.7:1 at seven years old would be strongly abnormal and would send you looking for a skeletal dysplasia — but in a 6-month-old it is normal, which is precisely the point of the question: **a segment ratio is only interpretable against age.**\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Measure the segments in every short child. A normal ratio for age keeps you on the common path; an abnormal one moves you straight to the skeleton.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'The upper-to-lower segment ratio is about 1.7:1 at birth and falls to 1:1 by school age; it separates proportionate from disproportionate short stature.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.68'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-21', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'Janine, a 9-month-old female infant, is seen by her family doctor because of concern that she is not growing fast enough. She is on the 5th centile for height and 2nd centile for weight. What is the greatest influence on her growth rate at this age?',
  options:['Genes','Growth hormone','Nutrition','Oestrogen','Testosterone'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> At 9 months of age, nutrition is the most important factor influencing growth. Poor intake or malabsorption can lead to weight and height centile drop despite normal hormones and genes.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — the age in the stem is the answer.** Nine months places Janine squarely in the **infantile phase**, which runs from birth to about two years and is nutrition-driven. The endpoint file’s own summary slide gives it 15% of final height (`Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf` p.7), and the driver is what reaches the gut, not what the pituitary secretes.\n\n| Phase | Age | Driver |\n|---|---|---|\n| Fetal | to birth | Placenta, maternal nutrition, uterine environment |\n| **Infantile** | **birth to ~2 y** | **Nutrition** |\n| Childhood | ~2 y to puberty | Growth hormone / IGF-1 (thyroid permissive) |\n| Pubertal | puberty to fusion | Sex steroids — oestradiol and testosterone |\n\n**Note also which centile is lower.** Her weight (2nd) has fallen below her height (5th). `13) Short stature.txt` gives the rule: “Weight affected > Height → Systemic disease; Height affected, Weight normal/↑ → Endocrine.” Weight leading the fall points to intake, losses or systemic illness — consistent with a nutritional cause and *against* an endocrine one, which reinforces the same answer from a second direction.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Genes** — genetic potential is real and becomes visible during the **childhood** phase, when an infant settles onto the centile predicted by mid-parental height. It is also precisely why early centile crossing is normal. But it does not set the *rate* of growth at 9 months, and it cannot be modified.\n- **Growth hormone** — the childhood driver. GH-dependent growth becomes established from around the second half of the first year onwards and dominates from about 2 years; congenital GH deficiency causes remarkably little growth failure at birth. `13) Short stature.txt` sets a high bar before GH is even investigated — severe short stature more than 3 SD below the mean, height affected more than weight, growth velocity under 4 cm a year — and warns “Do not jump to GH tests early !!”\n- **Oestrogen** and **testosterone** — pubertal hormones. `14) Puberty.txt` attributes the growth spurt to “GONADAL STEROIDS (testosterone and estradiol)”, adding 15% to adult height and then closing the epiphyses. In a 9-month-old they are quiescent apart from the brief neonatal “mini-puberty” the same deck illustrates, which does not drive somatic growth.\n\n**What to do about Janine.** `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` frames the assessment: faltering growth is “Description, not a diagnosis”, present when weight falls “across two centile spaces”; ensure “accurate serial measuring and plotting of height and weight on appropriate growth chart”; screen for the red flags — dysmorphic features, failure to gain weight despite adequate caloric intake, recurrent vomiting, diarrhoea or dehydration, organomegaly or lymphadenopathy, chronic respiratory signs, developmental delay; and manage with “Dietary and behavioural modification designed to increase energy intake and monitoring growth”, with investigations “guided by history and examination” rather than fired off as a panel.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** In an infant, take a feeding history before you take blood.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Growth at 9 months is nutrition-driven; genes and growth hormone dominate later, and sex steroids only at puberty.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.70'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-22', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'John, a 3-year-old boy, is referred to paediatric outpatients because of concern about the development of pubic and axillary hair. His testes are 1.5 mL in size (prepubertal). His blood pressure is 100/75 mmHg. Gonadotrophin levels are undetectable (prepubertal), but his bone age is 5 years. What is the most likely cause of his early pubertal development?',
  options:['Adrenal tumor','Brain tumor','Idiopathic precocious puberty','Prader-Willi syndrome','Testicular tumor'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Early pubic hair, advanced bone age, and undetectable gonadotropins suggest peripheral (gonadotropin-independent) precocious puberty. An adrenal tumor can produce androgens causing early virilization without testicular enlargement.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — the testes are the switch, and everything else follows from them.** In a boy, testicular volume tells you whether the gonad is being *driven* or *bypassed*. `14) Puberty.txt` includes a slide on “Testicular examination in precocious puberty” for exactly this reason, and describes measurement by orchidometer; the deck’s Tanner description of genital stage II is “enlargement of the scrotum and testes”, so testicular growth is the **first** event of true male puberty.\n\n| | **Central (gonadotrophin-dependent)** | **Peripheral (gonadotrophin-independent)** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Mechanism | Early activation of the HPG axis | Sex steroid made **downstream** of the pituitary, or given exogenously |\n| LH / FSH | **Detectable / pubertal** | **Suppressed / undetectable** |\n| Testes in a boy | **Enlarged** (> 4 mL) | **Prepubertal** (small) — the androgen is not coming from them |\n| Sequence | Follows normal pubertal sequence | Out of sequence — virilisation without testicular growth |\n| Typical causes | Idiopathic (especially girls), CNS lesion (up to 75% of boys), prolonged untreated hypothyroidism | Adrenal tumour, CAH, testicular/ovarian tumour, McCune–Albright, **exogenous steroid** |\n| Responds to GnRH agonist | **Yes** | **No** |\n\nJohn has **pubic and axillary hair with 1.5 mL testes and undetectable gonadotrophins**. The androgen is therefore adrenal or exogenous, and among the options only an adrenal tumour supplies it. His bone age of 5 years against a chronological age of 3 confirms that the androgen exposure is real and sustained, because androgens accelerate skeletal maturation — the reason untreated precocity costs adult height.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Brain tumor** — would cause **central** precocity with **raised** gonadotrophins and **enlarged** testes. `14) Puberty.txt` warns that with brain tumours “The only sign is precocious puberty” and that “structural central nervous system (CNS) abnormality can be demonstrated in up to 75% of boys with central precocious puberty” — so this is the right instinct for the wrong biochemistry. Undetectable gonadotrophins exclude it.\n- **Idiopathic precocious puberty** — a diagnosis of exclusion within the **central** category, and one that is far commoner in girls (“A high prevalence of idiopathic central precocious puberty has been reported in girls (90%)”). It, too, would give detectable gonadotrophins and large testes.\n- **Prader-Willi syndrome** — the opposite phenotype: neonatal hypotonia and poor feeding, then hyperphagia and obesity, with **hypogonadism** and delayed or absent puberty. `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` lists it under syndromic obesity.\n- **Testicular tumor** — would produce androgen from the gonad itself and give **asymmetrical testicular enlargement**, usually a palpable unilateral mass. Symmetrically small testes exclude it.\n\n**The investigation that follows.** Adrenal androgens and a 17-hydroxyprogesterone to exclude congenital adrenal hyperplasia, then adrenal imaging. `14) Puberty.txt` notes that atypical premature adrenarche — with marked growth acceleration, phallic enlargement, cystic acne or a bone age more than 2 SD advanced — requires an “ACTH STIMULATION TEST to exclude CAH”. John’s advanced bone age and true virilisation put him firmly in that atypical group rather than in benign premature adrenarche.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Virilisation with small testes means the androgen is not testicular. Look at the adrenal, at the medicine cabinet, and at 17-hydroxyprogesterone.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Virilisation with prepubertal testes and suppressed gonadotrophins is peripheral precocity — an adrenal or exogenous androgen source.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.72'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-23', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'Chelsea, a 7-year-old girl, is brought by her mother to the paediatric clinic. Her mother is concerned about early puberty. Chelsea has developed breasts and recently some pubic hair. On examination, she has breast development stage 3 and pubic hair stage 2. She has not started her periods but has had a growth spurt. A pelvic ultrasound shows multicystic ovaries and an enlarging uterus. What is the most likely cause of her early pubertal development?',
  options:['Brain tumor','Congenital adrenal hyperplasia','Idiopathic precocious puberty','Ovarian tumor','Turner syndrome'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> True central precocious puberty is suggested by breast development, growth spurt, and estrogen-sensitive organ changes like multicystic ovaries and enlarged uterus. In girls, this is most commonly idiopathic.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — read the ultrasound, then read the sequence.** Two findings establish that this is **central** precocity, and one epidemiological fact then names it.\n\n**1. The pelvic ultrasound is the giveaway.** `14) Puberty.txt` sets out exactly what to look for: “Ultrasound examination of the ovaries and uterus helps us to assess the progress of puberty. Uterus: Infantile: tubular. Pubertal: pear-shaped. Endometrial lining identification.” **Multicystic ovaries** mean multiple follicles are being recruited — the signature of pulsatile gonadotrophin drive — and an **enlarging uterus** means sustained oestrogen exposure. An ovary driven by FSH is a central process by definition.\n\n**2. The sequence is normal, just early.** Breast development first, then pubic hair, then a growth spurt, with menarche still to come. `14) Puberty.txt` states that central precocious puberty “follows the sequence observed in normal puberty”. Peripheral causes characteristically break the sequence — virilisation without gonadal enlargement, or rapid isolated development.\n\n**3. In girls, central precocity is usually idiopathic.** The same deck: central precocious puberty “occurs 5-10 folds more frequently in girls than in boys because ovaries are very sensitive to gonadotrophin secretion”, and “A high prevalence of idiopathic central precocious puberty has been reported in girls (90%)”, against a structural CNS abnormality demonstrable “in up to 75% of boys”.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Brain tumor** — the 10% that must still be excluded, and the deck insists on it: “MRI brain with contrast for pituitary gland in gonadotropin-dependent PP.” It would be the answer if there were headache, papilloedema, a visual field defect, diabetes insipidus, gelastic seizures, hyperthermia or unnatural crying or laughing. Chelsea has none, and in a girl of 7 the prior probability is against it — but *most likely* is not *certain*, and she still gets the scan.\n- **Congenital adrenal hyperplasia** — an adrenal androgen excess, so it virilises: pubic hair, acne, clitoromegaly, accelerated growth and bone age, **without** breast development and **without** ovarian follicular activity. Breast stage 3 is oestrogen, not androgen.\n- **Ovarian tumor** — an oestrogen-secreting tumour would suppress gonadotrophins and typically present with a **unilateral adnexal mass**, not bilateral multicystic ovaries, and often with rapid isolated breast development out of sequence.\n- **Turner syndrome** — the opposite problem. `6) Genetically determined disease_.txt` describes it as “a chromosomal monosomy syndrome of short female with sexual infantilism”: gonadal dysgenesis gives **absent or delayed** puberty with **raised** gonadotrophins, plus short stature, neck webbing, widely spaced nipples and coarctation.\n\n**Management, and why it matters here.** Chelsea is young and progressing with a growth spurt already under way, so she is in exactly the group that benefits from a **GnRH agonist**: continuous receptor stimulation downregulates the axis, halting progression and — the point of the treatment — postponing epiphyseal fusion, since “Sex steroids cause fusion of epiphyseal growth plates and cessation of growth”.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Multicystic ovaries with an enlarging uterus and a normal pubertal sequence mean central precocity — idiopathic in about 90% of girls, but still image the brain.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.74'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-24', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'Sophia, an 18-month-old girl, is brought to outpatients by her mother, who is concerned as Sophia has developed breasts. She is otherwise well and growing normally. On examination, she has breast development stage 3 (BIII), but no pubic or axillary hair. Her bone age is 20 months. What is the most likely cause of her early pubertal development?',
  options:['Brain tumor','Congenital adrenal hyperplasia','Idiopathic precocious puberty','Premature thelarche','Premature puberty'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Sophia has isolated breast development without pubic or axillary hair and with normal growth and bone age. This is characteristic of premature thelarche, a benign and self-limiting condition in infants and toddlers. There are no signs to suggest true precocious puberty or hormonal pathology.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — the word *isolated* is doing all the work.** `14) Puberty.txt` groups premature thelarche among the **“Benign incomplete variants”** of premature sexual development. The defining feature of an incomplete variant is that **one** pubertal sign appears **alone**, without the systemic consequences of true sex-steroid exposure. Three checks confirm it in Sophia:\n\n1. **No androgen signs** — no pubic or axillary hair, so the adrenal axis is quiet.\n2. **Normal growth** — no growth acceleration. True precocity produces a growth spurt.\n3. **Bone age 20 months at a chronological age of 18 months** — that is normal. Sustained oestrogen or androgen exposure **advances** bone age, and its absence is the strongest single piece of evidence that no meaningful hormone excess exists.\n\n**The three benign incomplete variants, side by side.**\n\n| | **Premature thelarche** | **Premature adrenarche / pubarche** | **Premature menarche** |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Sign | Isolated **breast** development | Isolated **sexual hair** (± body odour, mild acne) | Isolated cyclical **vaginal bleeding** |\n| Typical age | Infants and toddlers (often < 2 y) | Before 8 y in girls, 9 y in boys | Any, uncommon |\n| Growth & bone age | **Normal** | Normal or “slight increase of growth rate and bone age” | Normal |\n| Course | **Self-limiting**, often regresses | **Self-limited** | Self-limited |\n| Caveat | Reassess if it progresses or growth accelerates | Girls have “higher risk for polycystic ovarian syndrome later on” | Exclude local causes and abuse |\n\nThe adrenarche column is quoted from `14) Puberty.txt`, which defines premature adrenarche as “the appearance of sexual hair before the age of 8 yr in girls or 9 yr in boys without other evidence of maturation or growth acceleration”, notes it is “more frequent in girls than in boys”, and attributes it to “sensitivity to androgen production by adrenal gland (6-8 yrs)”.\n\n**A relevant neonatal aside from the same deck.** “High maternal level of prolactin can cause neoborn babies to be born with breast buds and even to lactate (self-limited).” Transplacental maternal hormones make breast tissue in a newborn an expected finding, and Sophia’s presentation at 18 months is the toddler version of the same benign phenomenon.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Brain tumor** — would drive the **whole** axis, giving a growth spurt, an advanced bone age and usually pubic hair, plus neurological or neuroendocrine features.\n- **Congenital adrenal hyperplasia** — an androgen excess: pubic hair, clitoromegaly, accelerated growth and a markedly advanced bone age. Sophia has none of these, and no breast development would be expected from adrenal androgen.\n- **Idiopathic precocious puberty** and **Premature puberty** — both name **true, complete** precocious puberty, which requires progression through the pubertal sequence with growth acceleration and bone-age advance. A normal bone age refutes both. (Note that the bank offers two labels for essentially the same wrong idea; neither is the answer, and recognising that they are the same answer twice is itself a clue.)\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Isolated breast tissue + normal growth + normal bone age = observe and re-measure in a few months. What would change the diagnosis is *progression*: a rising growth velocity, an advancing bone age, or pubic hair appearing.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Isolated breast development with normal growth and a normal bone age is premature thelarche — a benign incomplete variant, not precocious puberty.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.76'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-25', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'Tom, a 7-year-old boy, is referred by his GP due to concerns about his growth. He is adopted, with no details available about his biological father; his biological mother was of average height. He is a happy, playful boy with no dysmorphic features. His height is 110 cm (just below the 0.4th centile), and his weight is also on the 0.4th centile. Physical examination is normal. What is the most likely cause of his short stature?',
  options:['Achondroplasia','Constitutional delay of growth and puberty','Familial short stature','Growth hormone deficiency','Vitamin D deficiency'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Proportionate short stature with normal development and growth tracking suggests a familial pattern, especially in the absence of concerning features. Adopted children without parental data make this harder to confirm but still likely.\n\n---\n\n**Shared option menu — see `pedep-gp-8`.** The bank prints the same option list there; the questions are paired by the menu, not folded.\n\n**Expansion — this is a harder version of the familial short stature question, and the difficulty is deliberate.** The bank prints a near-identical stem elsewhere in this section (a 7-year-old boy, 110 cm, just below the 3rd centile, short father) with the same five options and the same key. **This vignette is not that one**: Tom is **adopted**, so the mid-parental height — normally the single most useful number in this consultation — is unavailable, and he sits at the **0.4th** centile rather than the 3rd, which is considerably shorter. The examiner is asking whether you can still reach the same conclusion when the easiest evidence has been removed.\n\n**What still points to a normal variant:**\n\n1. **Height and weight are on the same centile.** Proportionate smallness. `13) Short stature.txt`: “Weight affected > Height → Systemic disease; Height affected, Weight normal/↑ → Endocrine.” Neither pattern is present.\n2. **He is well.** Happy, playful, normal examination — no chronic disease, no dysmorphism, no skeletal abnormality among the things the lecture lists for examination.\n3. **He is *tracking*, not falling.** The vignette gives no fall in centile. Growth failure is a change in slope; a low but parallel line is a variant.\n\n**What is genuinely missing, and should be said.** Without parental heights there is no mid-parental height, and `13) Short stature.txt` uses that value twice — in the history (“Mid-parental height calculation”) and in the definition of short stature (“Height >2 SD below mid-parental height”). A biological mother “of average height” is a weak surrogate. In practice this child warrants documented **serial** measurements to establish the height velocity, since velocity is the one thing that does not depend on knowing the parents.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Achondroplasia** — disproportionate, with short limbs, macrocephaly and frontal bossing. `13) Short stature.txt` gives it as the example of “Disproportionate short stature”. Tom is proportionate and has no dysmorphic features.\n- **Constitutional delay of growth and puberty** — needs a **delayed bone age** and **delayed puberty**, and characteristically presents in adolescence. At 7, with nothing pointing to delayed maturation, it is not the best answer — though it is the option that cannot be fully excluded without an X-ray, and a bone age is the reasonable next investigation.\n- **Growth hormone deficiency** — the lecture’s trigger list for suspecting it is “Severe short stature (height >3 SD below the mean); Height affected > Weight; Height >1.5 SD below MPH; Poor growth velocity (< 4cm per year).” The 0.4th centile is roughly −2.6 SD, so he is short but not severely so by that threshold, and crucially his weight is equally affected and his velocity is not stated to be poor.\n- **Vitamin D deficiency** — rickets gives bone signs and a poor velocity, not a thriving child on a low tracking centile.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** When parental data are missing, growth **velocity** and a **bone age** replace them. `13) Short stature.txt` closes with “Most short children are normal variants” — but the way you prove it is a second measurement six months later, not a single reassuring visit.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Proportionate, well, tracking a low centile = familial short stature; where parental heights are unavailable, growth velocity and bone age carry the diagnosis.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.78'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-26', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'May, a 14-year-old girl, is referred by her school nurse because she is below the 0.4th centile for height. Her mother is 167 cm tall, and her father is 175 cm tall. She was born at term, birth weight 3.2 kg. She has always been the shortest in her class. On examination, she appears in early puberty. Her blood pressure is 135/65 mmHg. Which investigation is most likely to lead to a correct diagnosis?',
  options:['Coeliac screen','Thyroid stimulating hormone (TSH)','Full blood count','MRI scan of the brain','Karyotype'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Turner syndrome needs to be excluded as she has always been short. The elevated blood pressure may be because of an undetected coarctation of the aorta which is associated with the syndrome\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — three separate clues all point at one karyotype.**\n\n**1. Severe short stature against tall parents.** With a mother of 167 cm and a father of 175 cm, May’s mid-parental height is well above average, yet she is below the 0.4th centile. `13) Short stature.txt` defines short stature partly as “Height >2 SD below mid-parental height”; a child this discordant with her family is not a familial variant.\n\n**2. The blood pressure.** 135/65 in a 14-year-old is hypertensive, and the wide pulse pressure with a high systolic is exactly what an unrepaired coarctation of the aorta produces in the arm. `6) Genetically determined disease_.txt` lists “Congenital heart defects (coarctation of the aorta)” among the clinical features of Turner syndrome. This is the clue that converts a plausible diagnosis into the likely one.\n\n**3. She has always been short, and puberty is only just beginning at 14.** Turner short stature dates from early childhood and does not fluctuate.\n\n**The lecture is categorical about the test.** `13) Short stature.txt` places **“Karyotyping (All girls)”** in its investigation ladder for short stature — not as an option to consider, but as a standing instruction. Turner syndrome is easy to miss because, as the same lecture’s Case 3 shows, **mosaic** Turner may have “No, or minimal manifestations”, since “Only a small percentage of cells have a chromosomal abnormality 45,XO” while the rest are normal 46,XX.\n\n**What Turner looks like when it does show itself.** `6) Genetically determined disease_.txt`: “a chromosomal monosomy syndrome of short female with sexual infantilism and certain physical abnormalities”, incidence 1:2500 live-born girls (“More than 95% of 45X0 fetuses abort spontaneously”), with lymphoedema of the hands and feet in the neonate, spoon-shaped nails, short stature treated with growth hormone therapy, neck webbing, a wide carrying angle, widely spaced nipples, and coarctation. Cytogenetically about half are 45,X0, the remainder having a structural X abnormality or mosaicism such as 46XX/45X0 — and “The presence of Y chromosome sequence may increase the risk of gonadoblastoma, so gonadectomy is indicated.”\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.** The bank offers this same five-option menu three times in this section with three different keys, so the menu itself carries no information — the vignette must decide.\n\n- **Coeliac screen** — the answer when the **weight** line falls faster than the height line, or with gut symptoms, or in a high-risk group such as Down syndrome or type 1 diabetes. `13) Short stature.txt` puts the coeliac screen in Step Two beside TSH.\n- **TSH** — the answer when height falls while **weight is preserved or rising**, with lethargy, constipation and declining school performance (`52) hypothyrodism.txt`). May is not described that way, and hypothyroidism does not cause hypertension with a wide pulse pressure.\n- **Full blood count** — a Step One systemic screen, appropriate for suspected chronic disease or anaemia, but it cannot diagnose the condition the blood pressure is pointing at.\n- **MRI brain** — the answer when a pituitary or hypothalamic lesion is suspected: severe growth failure with a poor velocity, headache, visual field loss or papilloedema. Nothing here suggests it.\n\n**Filing note.** Kept in growth and puberty because the question is a short-stature work-up; the secondary chapter is **genetics**.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Karyotype every short girl — Turner syndrome may show only short stature, and a raised blood pressure suggests the associated coarctation.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.80'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-27', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'Eleanor, a 7-year-old girl, is referred for short stature. She is on the 5th centile for height and 25th centile for weight. Nine months ago, her height and weight were on the 15th centile. Her mother notes that she has become lethargic, her school performance has worsened, and she refuses to participate in sports. Which investigation is most likely to lead to a correct diagnosis?',
  options:['Coeliac screen','Thyroid stimulating hormone (TSH)','Full blood count','MRI scan of the brain','Karyotype'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Falling height with preserved or increased weight, lethargy, and cognitive decline are classic for hypothyroidism. TSH testing will help confirm the diagnosis.\n\n---\n\n**Shared option menu — see `pedep-gp-26`.** The bank prints the same option list there; the questions are paired by the menu, not folded.\n\n**Expansion — do the arithmetic on the two centile lines before anything else.** Nine months ago Eleanor was on the 15th centile for **both** height and weight. Now her height has fallen to the 5th while her weight has **risen** to the 25th. The two lines have moved in **opposite directions**, and `13) Short stature.txt` prints the interpretation as a rule:\n\n- “Weight affected > Height → Systemic disease”\n- “Height affected, Weight normal/↑ → Endocrine”\n\nThe same lecture makes the point again from the other direction under obesity: nutritional obesity gives a child who is “Obese + Tall”; endocrine causes give “Obese + Short/poor height velocity”. A child getting shorter relative to her peers while getting heavier has a hormone problem.\n\n**Then read the symptoms, which name the hormone.** Lethargy, declining school performance and refusal to exercise are the classic constitutional features of hypothyroidism. `52) hypothyrodism.txt` lists lethargy and poor feeding, constipation, cold extremities, dry thick skin, coarse facies and a hoarse cry among its features, describes the affected child as “Short with infantile proportions”, and opens by stating the lecture’s emphasis on “linear growth and brain” — the two things Eleanor has lost.\n\n**Why TSH is the right single test.** In acquired primary hypothyroidism the thyroid fails and the pituitary shouts: TSH rises before free T4 falls, so TSH is the most sensitive single screen. `13) Short stature.txt` places “TSH, fT4” together with the coeliac screen in **Step Two** of the investigation ladder — after the Step One systemic screen and, importantly, *before* any growth hormone testing, since “Do not jump to GH tests early !!”. The same deck lists hypothyroidism first among “Endocrinal causes of Short stature”, beside Cushing syndrome, GH deficiency and IGF-1 deficiency.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.** This is the same five-option menu the bank uses twice more in this section with different keys, so the menu is uninformative and the vignette must decide.\n\n- **Coeliac screen** — the answer when the **weight** line leads the fall, or with diarrhoea, abdominal distension or anaemia. Eleanor’s weight is rising, which argues against malabsorption.\n- **Full blood count** — Step One screening for chronic disease and anaemia; it would not explain a rising weight and does not diagnose thyroid disease.\n- **MRI brain** — reserved for suspected hypothalamic–pituitary pathology: very severe growth failure, headache, visual field defects, papilloedema. `13) Short stature.txt` lists it under “Considering potential causes of GHD”, alongside fundoscopy and visual field assessment looking for bitemporal hemianopia from a craniopharyngioma. Nothing in Eleanor’s story requires it yet.\n- **Karyotype** — mandatory in short girls per the lecture (“Karyotyping (All girls)”), and it would eventually be done, but Turner syndrome does not produce lethargy, weight gain and cognitive decline over nine months. The acute change of pattern is what makes TSH the higher-yield first test.\n\n**Filing note.** Secondary chapter **endocrine**.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Falling height with rising weight plus lethargy and poor school performance is acquired hypothyroidism — check TSH.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.82'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-28', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'Jake, a 5-year-old boy with Down syndrome, presents for a routine check-up. He has dropped one centile in height and two centile lines in weight over the past 6 months. His mother reports increased irritability and poor appetite. Which investigation is most likely to lead to a correct diagnosis?',
  options:['Coeliac screen','Thyroid stimulating hormone (TSH)','Full blood count','MRI scan of the brain','Karyotype'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Children with Down syndrome are at increased risk for coeliac disease, which can present with growth faltering, irritability, and poor appetite. A coeliac screen is warranted.\n\n---\n\n**Shared option menu — see `pedep-gp-26`.** The bank prints the same option list there; the questions are paired by the menu, not folded.\n\n**Expansion — the direction of the weight line is the whole question.** This section contains **two** Down syndrome growth vignettes, they carry **different keys**, and the only thing that separates them is which centile falls faster:\n\n| | **This boy (Jake)** | **The other Down syndrome vignette** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Height | Down **one** centile | Down **two** centiles (75th → 25th) |\n| Weight | Down **two** centiles | **Up** one centile (50th → 75th) |\n| Pattern | **Weight affected > Height** | **Height affected, weight ↑** |\n| Rule (`13) Short stature.txt`) | → **Systemic disease** | → **Endocrine** |\n| Symptom | Irritability, poor appetite | Constipation |\n| Answer | **Coeliac screen** | **Thyroid function tests** |\n\nThe lecture states the rule in two lines — “Weight affected > Height → Systemic disease; Height affected, Weight normal/↑ → Endocrine” — and this pair of questions is the cleanest possible demonstration of it. If you answer both by reflex (“Down syndrome, therefore thyroid”), you get one of them wrong.\n\n**Why coeliac disease in particular.** Down syndrome carries a raised risk of several autoimmune conditions, coeliac disease among them, which is why coeliac serology sits in periodic health surveillance for these children *(the surveillance schedule itself is not stated in the cached decks — not taken from the course material)*. `6) Genetically determined disease_.txt` documents the syndrome’s broad medical burden — congenital heart defects in 40%, duodenal atresia, Hirschsprung disease, conductive hearing loss in 75%, visual impairment, hypothyroidism-prone endocrine risk, atlantoaxial instability, obstructive sleep apnoea in 50–75% and an increased risk of leukaemia — establishing the principle that a Down syndrome child with a new symptom needs an active search, not reassurance.\n\nClinically, coeliac disease in a young child presents as **weight faltering with irritability and anorexia**, often with abdominal distension, loose or foul stools and iron deficiency. That is Jake’s story almost word for word. The screen is IgA tissue transglutaminase antibodies with a total IgA (to detect IgA deficiency, which causes false negatives), and diagnosis is confirmed on biopsy — **while the child is still eating gluten** *(the serology and biopsy detail is filled from general medical knowledge — not taken from the course material; `13) Short stature.txt` names the “Celiac screen” as a Step Two investigation without specifying the assay)*.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **TSH** — the right answer for the *other* Down syndrome vignette, where weight rises as height falls. Here weight is falling faster, which is the systemic pattern.\n- **Full blood count** — will very likely be abnormal (iron deficiency anaemia is common in coeliac disease) but it is non-specific and does not make the diagnosis.\n- **MRI brain** — no neurological or visual features, and no severe isolated height failure.\n- **Karyotype** — already known. He has a diagnosis of Down syndrome; repeating the karyotype adds nothing. This option is only correct in a short **girl** with no diagnosis.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Plot both lines. Weight falling faster than height sends you to the gut and to systemic disease; height falling while weight holds or rises sends you to the endocrine system.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Weight falling faster than height is the systemic pattern — in a Down syndrome child with irritability and anorexia, screen for coeliac disease.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.84'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-29', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'A 12-year-old girl is referred to the paediatric endocrinology clinic for evaluation of delayed puberty. She is diagnosed with constitutional delay of growth. What investigation is most likely to confirm this diagnosis?',
  options:['MRI brain','Thyroid function tests','Growth hormone level','Bone age','Karyotype'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Constitutional delay is confirmed by a bone age that is delayed relative to chronological age. It helps differentiate from pathological causes of delayed puberty.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — what a bone age actually is, and why it settles this.** `13) Short stature.txt` gives the method in three lines: “Most use: Greulich and Pyle”, “X-ray left wrist and hand”, and then the interpretation that answers this question:\n\n- “A. Delayed → Constitutional delay of growth and puberty/ Endocrine.”\n- “B. Normal → Familial.”\n\nThe radiograph compares the appearance and fusion of the ossification centres in the hand and wrist against a standard atlas. Skeletal maturation is driven by sex steroids and thyroid hormone, so it is a biological clock rather than a calendar one. In constitutional delay the whole clock is set back: the child is short **and immature**, and because the epiphyses are still open she has more growing time than her age implies — which is precisely why her final height will be normal.\n\n**Where bone age sits in the ladder.** Step Two of `13) Short stature.txt`, alongside TSH and fT4, the coeliac screen, IGF-1 and IGFBP-3, and karyotyping in all girls — after the Step One systemic bloods and **before** Step Three GH provocation testing.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **MRI brain** — reserved for suspected hypothalamic–pituitary disease. The lecture places it under “Considering potential causes of GHD” together with fundoscopy for papilloedema and visual field assessment for a bitemporal hemianopia, hunting a craniopharyngioma. A normal, well girl with delayed puberty does not need a scan first.\n- **Thyroid function tests** — genuinely part of the work-up, and `14) Puberty.txt` lists “Acquired hypothyroidism” among the causes of hypogonadotropic hypogonadism, so they must be done. But a normal TSH **excludes** an alternative; it does not **confirm** constitutional delay. The stem asks which test confirms the diagnosis.\n- **Growth hormone level** — actively misleading, and the lecture says so twice: “GH testing: No use of basal GH level !” because “GH secreted in pulses”, and confirmation requires “2 GH provocation tests (sequential or on separate days)” with glucagon, insulin, arginine or clonidine. A single random GH tells you nothing whatever.\n- **Karyotype** — mandatory in short girls per the same lecture (“Karyotyping (All girls)”), and it is the test that would confirm **Turner syndrome**, the main pathological alternative here. But Turner is hypergonadotropic hypogonadism with a **normal or advanced** bone age relative to the degree of pubertal failure and with characteristic stigmata; it is excluded rather than confirmed by this stem, which has already given the diagnosis as constitutional delay.\n\n**A note on the definition, because it should be checked first.** `14) Puberty.txt`: delayed puberty is “Failure of development of any pubertal feature by 13 yrs of age in females or by 14 yrs of age in males.” At 12 this girl has not formally reached that threshold — so the referral is early, and a bone age plus reassurance is the entire appropriate response.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'A bone age (left wrist and hand, Greulich and Pyle) delayed relative to chronological age is what confirms constitutional delay.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.86'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-30', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'A 15-year-old girl is referred to the paediatric endocrinology clinic for delayed puberty. Her height is on the 0.4th centile. She has an ejection systolic murmur and widely spaced nipples. Gonadotrophin levels are raised. What is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Turner syndrome','Klinefelter syndrome','McCune-Albright syndrome','Hypothyroidism','Silver-Russell syndrome'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Short stature, delayed puberty, heart murmur, and raised gonadotropins point to Turner syndrome. It is caused by gonadal dysgenesis and confirmed with karyotyping.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — “raised gonadotrophins” localises the lesion before you name the syndrome.** Delayed puberty splits into two biochemical halves, and `14) Puberty.txt` gives both:\n\n| | **Hypogonadotropic hypogonadism** | **Hypergonadotropic hypogonadism** |\n|---|---|---|\n| LH / FSH | **Low or normal** | **Raised** |\n| Lesion | Hypothalamus or pituitary — the drive is missing | **The gonad itself** has failed; no feedback, so the pituitary shouts |\n| Causes (per the deck) | Systemic disorders / any chronic illness; acquired hypothyroidism; hypothalamic-pituitary disorder, isolated or part of panhypopituitarism from tumour or radiation; **Kallmann syndrome, associated with absent sense of smell** | Gonadal dysgenesis — **Turner syndrome** in girls, Klinefelter in boys; chemotherapy or radiotherapy; surgical or torsion loss |\n\nRaised gonadotrophins therefore place this girl in the right-hand column before any physical sign is considered. Add **severe short stature** and **widely spaced nipples**, and the diagnosis is made.\n\n**The murmur is not incidental.** `6) Genetically determined disease_.txt` lists “Congenital heart defects (coarctation of the aorta)” as a feature of Turner syndrome; a bicuspid aortic valve is the other common lesion and is a classic source of an ejection systolic murmur *(the bicuspid valve is filled from general medical knowledge — not taken from the course material)*. Either finding mandates cardiac assessment, because aortic complications are the main threat to life in Turner syndrome.\n\n**The full Turner picture from the genetics deck.** “A chromosomal monosomy syndrome of short female with sexual infantilism and certain physical abnormalities”; incidence 1:2500 live-born girls, with more than 95% of 45,X0 fetuses aborting spontaneously; increasingly detected prenatally when “fetal edema of the neck, hands or feet or a cystic hygroma may be identified”. Clinical features: lymphoedema of the hands and feet in the neonate, spoon-shaped nails, **short stature (growth hormone therapy)**, neck webbing, a wide carrying angle, widely spaced nipples, and coarctation. Cytogenetics: about 50% are 45,X0, the rest having a short-arm deletion, an isochromosome, another structural abnormality, or mosaicism such as 46XX/45X0 — and where Y-chromosome sequence is present, gonadectomy is indicated because of gonadoblastoma risk.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Klinefelter syndrome** — 47,XXY, a **male** condition. It is also hypergonadotropic, and boys are typically **tall** with small firm testes and gynaecomastia. It cannot apply to a girl.\n- **McCune-Albright syndrome** — causes **peripheral precocious puberty**, not delayed puberty, with café-au-lait macules with irregular “coast of Maine” borders and polyostotic fibrous dysplasia *(the triad detail is not taken from the course material)*. Gonadotrophins would be **suppressed**.\n- **Hypothyroidism** — a cause of short stature and delayed puberty, but it is **hypogonadotropic** (`14) Puberty.txt` lists “Acquired hypothyroidism” under that heading), and it produces no murmur or widely spaced nipples.\n- **Silver-Russell syndrome** — severe intrauterine growth restriction with postnatal short stature, a relatively large head, a triangular face and body asymmetry; `13) Short stature.txt` names “Russel-Silver syndrome” among the syndromic causes of short stature. It does not cause gonadal failure with raised gonadotrophins.\n\n**Filing note.** Secondary chapter **genetics**.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Raised gonadotrophins mean primary gonadal failure — with short stature, widely spaced nipples and a murmur, that is Turner syndrome.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.88'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-31', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'A 5-year-old boy is referred to a paediatric specialist for tall stature and headaches. On examination, he has testicular enlargement and bilateral papilloedema. What investigation is most likely to confirm the diagnosis?',
  options:['CT or MRI brain scan','Karyotype','ACTH level','17-Hydroxyprogesterone','Bone age'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Precocious puberty and neurological symptoms like headache and papilledema suggest a central brain lesion (e.g., hypothalamic hamartoma). Brain imaging is the key investigation.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — two findings make this an emergency, and both point the same way.**\n\n**1. Testicular enlargement means the puberty is CENTRAL.** In a boy, the testes are the switch that separates the two kinds of precocity. Enlarged testes mean the gonad is being **driven** by pituitary gonadotrophins, so the lesion lies at or above the pituitary. `14) Puberty.txt` defines central precocious puberty as “a result of the early activation of the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis”, and includes a slide on “Testicular examination in precocious puberty” precisely because this single sign redirects the whole work-up. Had the testes been **small** with virilisation, the androgen would be adrenal or exogenous and the answer would be 17-hydroxyprogesterone or an adrenal study.\n\n**2. Papilloedema and headache mean raised intracranial pressure.** Together with tall stature for age — the growth acceleration of early sex-steroid exposure — this is a structural intracranial lesion until proved otherwise.\n\n**The epidemiology makes the scan compulsory in a boy.** `14) Puberty.txt` is explicit: central precocious puberty “occurs 5-10 folds more frequently in girls than in boys”; “A high prevalence of idiopathic central precocious puberty has been reported in girls (90%)”, whereas “structural central nervous system (CNS) abnormality can be demonstrated in up to **75% of boys** with central precocious puberty.” The same deck warns about presentation: with brain tumours “The only sign is precocious puberty”, and lists the accompanying features to hunt for — neurological signs; neuroendocrine features such as diabetes insipidus, hyperthermia, “unnatural crying or laughing (gelastic seizures)”, obesity and cachexia; and visual manifestations such as visual field defect, proptosis and decreased visual acuity. Under investigations it names “MRI brain with contrast for pituitary gland in gonadotropin-dependent PP.”\n\nThe gelastic-seizure line is worth remembering here, because **hypothalamic hamartoma** — the lesion named in the bank’s own box — is the classic cause of central precocity with gelastic seizures.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Karyotype** — the test for Turner syndrome in a short girl, or Klinefelter in a tall boy with small firm testes and delayed puberty. It has no role in a boy with an obviously acquired, progressive process and raised intracranial pressure.\n- **ACTH level** — points at adrenal disease, which causes **peripheral** precocity with **small** testes. Testicular enlargement rules that pathway out.\n- **17-Hydroxyprogesterone** — the screening test for congenital adrenal hyperplasia, again peripheral virilisation with prepubertal testes. `14) Puberty.txt` reserves adrenal testing (“ACTH STIMULATION TEST to exclude CAH”) for **atypical premature adrenarche** — marked growth acceleration, clitoral or phallic enlargement, cystic acne, or a bone age more than 2 SD advanced.\n- **Bone age** — will certainly be advanced and is part of the assessment, but it only quantifies how much sex-steroid exposure has occurred. It cannot find a tumour, and it would delay the scan in a child with papilloedema.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Central precocious puberty in a **boy** = image the brain. Add headache and papilloedema and the imaging is urgent.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Testicular enlargement means central precocity; in a boy — and especially with headache and papilloedema — that demands brain imaging.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.90'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-32', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'There are many causes of short stature in children. Which of the following does not cause short stature?',
  options:['Turner syndrome','Constitutional delay of growth','Rickets','Thyrotoxicosis','Cystic fibrosis'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Thyrotoxicosis typically causes growth acceleration, not short stature. The others are recognized causes of growth failure.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — thyroid hormone works in both directions, and that is the trap.** Too little thyroid hormone slows linear growth and skeletal maturation; too much **accelerates** both. `52) hypothyrodism.txt` sets hypothyroidism against thyrotoxicosis and lists “Rapid growth in height” among the features of the hyperthyroid child, alongside lid retraction. A thyrotoxic child is therefore typically **tall for age with an advanced bone age** — and the sting in the tail is that, because sex steroids and thyroid hormone drive epiphyseal fusion, prolonged untreated thyrotoxicosis can still shorten *adult* height even while the child looks tall *(the final-height consequence is filled from general medical knowledge — not taken from the course material)*. The question asks about short stature in childhood, and there thyrotoxicosis is the odd one out.\n\n**Why each of the other four does cause short stature.**\n\n- **Turner syndrome** — one of the two commonest genetic causes of short stature in girls. `6) Genetically determined disease_.txt` lists “Short stature. (Growth hormone therapy)” among its clinical features, and `13) Short stature.txt` names Turner first among “Syndromes causing Short stature”, alongside Russell–Silver, Down and Noonan. It is also the reason that lecture instructs “Karyotyping (All girls)”.\n- **Constitutional delay of growth** — causes short stature **during childhood and adolescence**, with a delayed bone age and delayed puberty, even though final adult height is normal. Note the distinction the examiner may exploit: it causes short stature *now*, not permanently.\n- **Rickets** — vitamin D deficiency impairs mineralisation of the growth plate, producing bowing, widened wrists, a rachitic rosary and stunted linear growth. The syllabus devotes two decks to it (`17) Vitamin D Defficiency Rickets(1).txt` and `Vitamin deficiency  Rickets.txt`).\n- **Cystic fibrosis** — the model of growth failure from chronic disease: fat malabsorption from pancreatic insufficiency, a high energy cost from chronic suppurative lung disease, and recurrent infection. It produces the systemic pattern of `13) Short stature.txt` — “Weight affected > Height → Systemic disease.”\n\n**The framework the lecture wants you to carry into any short-stature question.**\n\n| Category | Examples |\n|---|---|\n| **Normal variants** — commonest | Familial short stature; constitutional delay of growth and puberty |\n| **Systemic / chronic disease** | Coeliac disease, cystic fibrosis, chronic renal failure, chronic anaemia, inflammatory bowel disease, PEM |\n| **Endocrine** | GH deficiency, IGF-1 deficiency, **hypo**thyroidism, Cushing syndrome |\n| **Syndromic / genetic** | Turner, Down, Noonan, Russell–Silver |\n| **Skeletal (disproportionate)** | Achondroplasia and other dysplasias, rickets |\n| **Prenatal** | SGA without catch-up — “10% intrauterine growth restriction do not catch-up growth” |\n\n(Categories and named entities from `13) Short stature.txt`.)\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Any question containing a thyroid word should be answered by asking *which direction*: hypothyroidism shortens and delays, thyrotoxicosis lengthens and advances.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Hypothyroidism stunts growth and delays bone age; thyrotoxicosis accelerates both, so it is not a cause of childhood short stature.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.92'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-33', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'A 5-year-old boy is referred by the school nurse because his height and weight are both on the 99th centile at school entry. He appears healthy, with tall parents, no birthmarks, and normal blood pressure. What is the most appropriate next step?',
  options:['Thyroid function tests','9 a.m. cortisol level','Calculate mid-parental height','Dietary assessment','Genetic studies for Prader-Willi syndrome'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> A child growing along the 99th centile with tall parents and no red flags likely has familial tall stature. Calculating mid-parental height is appropriate to assess genetic potential.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — tall stature is assessed exactly like short stature, in reverse.** The same instrument answers both: plot the child, plot the parents, and see whether the two agree. `13) Short stature.txt` lists “Mid-parental height calculation” as a routine part of the growth history and defines abnormality partly by discordance from it (“Height >2 SD below mid-parental height”). A tall child of tall parents is **concordant** and needs no investigation; a tall child of average parents is **discordant** and does.\n\n**The three details in this stem that are all negatives, and each excludes something.**\n\n| Detail given | What it excludes |\n|---|---|\n| **Tall parents** | Pathological tall stature — the child is growing to plan |\n| **“No birthmarks”** | **McCune–Albright syndrome** (café-au-lait macules) and **neurofibromatosis type 1** — both associated with endocrine and growth abnormality *(not taken from the course material)* |\n| **Normal blood pressure** | Glucocorticoid or catecholamine excess |\n| **Height AND weight both on the 99th** | Proportionate — no obesity-with-short-stature pattern, no gigantism-with-normal-weight pattern |\n\nThe “no birthmarks” clause is quietly load-bearing and is easy to read past; the examiner put it there.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Thyroid function tests** — appropriate for suspected **thyrotoxicosis**, which `52) hypothyrodism.txt` associates with “Rapid growth in height” and lid retraction. This boy has none of the accompanying features (tachycardia, weight loss, tremor, heat intolerance, goitre), and he is heavy as well as tall, which is against it.\n- **9 a.m. cortisol level** — measures for Cushing syndrome, which `13) Short stature.txt` lists among the **endocrinal causes of short stature**. Cushing makes children **fat and short**, not tall and proportionate, and would be accompanied by hypertension, striae and a moon face. The stem’s normal blood pressure argues against it.\n- **Dietary assessment** — the move for **nutritional** obesity. But `13) Short stature.txt` gives the discriminator: “A. Nutritional Obesity: Obese + Tall. B. Endocrinal causes: Obese + Short/poor height velocity.” This boy is tall *and* heavy in proportion, i.e. large rather than obese; without a BMI above the 95th centile — `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` gives obese >95th, overweight 85th–95th, healthy 5th–85th — there is nothing to assess.\n- **Genetic studies for Prader-Willi syndrome** — Prader–Willi causes **obesity with short stature**, hypotonia and poor feeding in infancy followed by hyperphagia, hypogonadism and learning difficulty. `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` lists it under syndromic obesity. It is the opposite phenotype to this child.\n\n**When tall stature *would* need investigating.** A tall child **discordant** with the parents, with a growth velocity that is accelerating, or with any of: disproportionate long limbs and an arm span exceeding height (Marfan syndrome), a tall boy with small firm testes and learning difficulties (Klinefelter, 47,XXY), café-au-lait macules with precocious puberty (McCune–Albright), or coarse features with acral enlargement (a GH-secreting pituitary lesion — gigantism before epiphyseal fusion) *(this list is filled from general medical knowledge — not taken from the course material; the cached decks address short stature and precocious puberty rather than tall stature as a topic)*.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Do the arithmetic before ordering any test. Most tall children are tall for the same reason most short children are short — their parents.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Assess tall stature by comparing the child with the mid-parental height; concordance with tall parents in a well child needs no investigation.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.94'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-34', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'A 14-month-old baby girl is brought to the clinic due to concerns about her size. She is on the 2nd centile for weight, height, and head circumference. She was born on the 2nd centile. She is taking cow’s milk, fully weaned, with normal stools and unremarkable examination. Her parents are short and are concerned she may also be small as she grows. What is the most likely cause of her size?',
  options:['Constitutionally small','Cow’s milk protein enteropathy','Hypothyroidism','Growth hormone deficiency','Skeletal dysplasia'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Consistent growth along low centiles since birth in a healthy child with short parents suggests constitutional smallness rather than pathological causes.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — four features, and every one of them says “normal small child”.**\n\n1. **She was born on the 2nd centile and is still on the 2nd centile.** No centile crossing. `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` sets the threshold for concern at a weight falling “across two centile spaces”; nothing has fallen at all. `13) Short stature.txt` likewise defines abnormality by “Serial measurements cross growth centile lines”, not by a low position.\n2. **All three parameters are on the same centile** — weight, height **and head circumference**. She is a small child, uniformly scaled. Pathology almost always disturbs the proportions: malnutrition takes weight first, endocrine disease takes height while sparing weight, and head circumference is preserved longest of all.\n3. **Her parents are short.** Her size is concordant with her genetic potential, which is the definition of constitutional (familial) smallness.\n4. **She is well.** Normal stools, unremarkable examination, fully weaned and feeding. None of the red flags in `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` — dysmorphic features, failure to gain weight despite adequate caloric intake, recurrent vomiting, diarrhoea or dehydration, organomegaly or lymphadenopathy, signs of chronic respiratory disease, developmental delay — is present.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Cow’s milk protein enteropathy** — would give gastrointestinal symptoms: loose, mucoid or blood-streaked stools, vomiting, abdominal distension, often eczema or a family history of atopy, and characteristically **weight falling faster than height**. Her stools are normal and she has not crossed centiles.\n- **Hypothyroidism** — produces the endocrine pattern, with **height falling while weight is preserved or rises**, plus constipation, lethargy, coarse facies, a large tongue, dry skin, cold extremities and an umbilical hernia (`52) hypothyrodism.txt`, which also describes the affected child as “Short with infantile proportions”). Congenital hypothyroidism is detected on newborn screening; the same deck notes that “Before neonatal screening, less than 5 %” were picked up early. Nothing here fits.\n- **Growth hormone deficiency** — needs falling centiles and a poor velocity. `13) Short stature.txt` sets the bar for even suspecting it: “Severe short stature (height >3 SD below the mean); Height affected > Weight; Height >1.5 SD below MPH; Poor growth velocity (< 4cm per year).” None is met, and GH-dependent growth is barely established at 14 months in any case.\n- **Skeletal dysplasia** — would give **disproportionate** short stature. `13) Short stature.txt` gives achondroplasia its own slide under “Disproportionate short stature”, and requires that “Proportions (upper/lower segment, arm span)” be measured in every short child. This girl is proportionate on all three parameters and her examination is unremarkable.\n\n**What to do, since the parents are asking.** Plot her again in three to six months on the WHO chart (`13) Short stature.txt`: “Recommended for 0-24 months”). If she stays on the 2nd centile she is a small, healthy child and needs nothing. If she falls off it, the work-up begins — and the lecture’s own summary is the message for the parents: “Most short children are normal variants.”\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'A child born on a low centile who is still on it, proportionate, well, and with short parents is constitutionally small — reassure and re-plot.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.96'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-35', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'perinatal',
  stem:'Which of the following is the most important cause of failure to establish breathing and the requirement of resuscitation at birth?',
  options:['Congenital lung anomalies','Birth trauma','Birth asphyxia','Retained lung fluid'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Birth asphyxia is the leading cause of neonatal respiratory failure and need for resuscitation. Congenital anomalies or retained fluid are less common causes.\n\n---\n\n**⚠️ Filing note.** This is a **perinatal** question printed inside the Growth and Puberty section of the endpoint file. It is filed under `perinatal` because it concerns the transition at delivery and resuscitation in the delivery room; the secondary chapter is **neonatal**.\n\n**Expansion — why asphyxia dominates the list.** The first breath has to do three things at once: clear lung liquid, establish a functional residual capacity, and drop pulmonary vascular resistance so that blood diverts from the ductus into the lungs. Anything that impairs the drive to breathe interrupts all three, and the commonest such insult is **intrapartum hypoxia–ischaemia**. Hypoxia first causes rapid breathing, then primary apnoea (which responds to stimulation), then gasping, then **terminal apnoea**, which responds only to positive-pressure ventilation *(the primary/terminal apnoea sequence is filled from general medical knowledge — not taken from the course material)*. That is why the single most effective manoeuvre in newborn resuscitation is inflation of the lungs, not chest compressions or drugs.\n\n**The syllabus treats asphyxia as a major topic in its own right.** `12) HIE.txt` is devoted to hypoxic-ischaemic encephalopathy, and `58) cerebral palsy.txt` and `9) Respiratory distress in newborn.txt` both list birth asphyxia among their causes — the recurrence across three decks is itself a statement of weight.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Congenital lung anomalies** — congenital diaphragmatic hernia, pulmonary hypoplasia, congenital cystic adenomatoid malformation. Each is a genuine cause of failure to establish breathing, and each is **rare**. The question asks for the most important cause, meaning the commonest and most consequential.\n- **Birth trauma** — a real contributor, and the material covers it (`10) new Seizures . Birth injuries , Hpoglycemia 2.txt`). Severe trauma such as cervical spinal injury or a subgaleal haemorrhage can compromise a newborn, but it accounts for a small fraction of resuscitations compared with asphyxia.\n- **Retained lung fluid** — transient tachypnoea of the newborn, commonest after elective caesarean section. It is very common but usually **mild and self-limiting**: these babies are tachypnoeic and may need oxygen, but they breathe. It is a cause of respiratory distress, not of failure to breathe at all.\n\n**The distinction the examiner is drawing.** *Respiratory distress* (the baby is breathing, badly) and *failure to establish respiration* (the baby is not breathing) are different problems with different differentials. `9) Respiratory distress in newborn.txt` handles the first; this question is about the second, and there the answer is asphyxia.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Anticipate. Risk factors for intrapartum asphyxia — fetal distress, meconium-stained liquor, abruption, cord prolapse, prolonged or obstructed labour — should bring a trained resuscitator to the delivery before the baby is born, because the intervention that works is effective lung inflation in the first minute.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Birth asphyxia is the leading reason a newborn fails to establish breathing and needs resuscitation; effective lung inflation is the key intervention.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.98'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-36', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'What is the most important determinant of growth in the infant phase?',
  options:['Nutrition','Genetic factors','Hormonal influence','Physical activity'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Infant growth relies primarily on adequate nutrient intake. Even with normal genes and hormones, poor nutrition can slow growth significantly.\n\n---\n\n**⚠️ A near-twin, held separately.** The endpoint file asks this same fact elsewhere in the section over a **different, five-option list** (Maternal nutrition / Infant nutrition / Size at birth / Vitamins / Parental size), and again over a third list. The option sets are **replaced**, not merely trimmed, so by the standard folding test these are separate questions rather than reprints, and each is held. The underlying fact is one fact — but the discriminations being tested differ: the five-option version tests *infant* nutrition against *maternal* nutrition, while this four-option version tests nutrition against the other three broad determinants.\n\n**Expansion — the four determinants, ranked by phase.**\n\n| Determinant | When it dominates | Why not in infancy |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Nutrition** | **Birth to ~2 years** | — it *is* the answer |\n| Genetic factors | Childhood onwards | Sets the destination centile, not the early rate; this is why infants normally cross centiles as they migrate from their birth size toward their genetic size |\n| Hormonal influence | Childhood (GH/IGF-1) and puberty (sex steroids) | GH-dependent growth is barely established in the first months; a GH-deficient newborn is of normal length |\n| Physical activity | Never a primary determinant of linear growth | Affects muscle and bone density, not height |\n\nThe endpoint file’s own summary slide assigns the infantile phase **15% of final height** (`Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf` p.7), and `13) Short stature.txt` lists the influences on growth as genetics, systemic health, hormones (GH/IGF-1, thyroid hormone, insulin, sex steroids, glucocorticoids) and “Nutrition, Sleep, Exercise” — a set that must then be sequenced by age, which is what this question tests.\n\n**Why this matters clinically rather than as trivia.** Because nutrition is the infant driver, **growth faltering clusters in infancy**, and the first response is a feeding history rather than a hormone assay. `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` calls faltering growth a “Description, not a diagnosis”, present when weight falls “across two centile spaces”, and gives management as “Dietary and behavioural modification designed to increase energy intake and monitoring growth”, with “Investigations should be guided by history and examination” — not a reflex panel. It also lists the red flags that shift the suspicion off pure intake: dysmorphic features, failure to gain weight **despite adequate caloric intake**, recurrent vomiting, diarrhoea or dehydration, organomegaly or lymphadenopathy, signs of chronic respiratory disease, and developmental delay.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.** *Genetic factors* set the target, not the trajectory, in the first two years. *Hormonal influence* is the childhood and pubertal answer — offering it for infancy is a phase error, and the same bank tests that error directly with a question keying growth hormone for the childhood phase. *Physical activity* has no meaningful role in infant linear growth and is a filler option.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Infant not growing → look at what is going into the baby, and how much of it stays in.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Nutrition is the dominant determinant of growth in infancy; genes and hormones take over from the childhood phase onward.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.100'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-37', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'Laila, an 11-month-old girl, is being monitored by her health provider. Her birthweight was 2.4 kg (2nd centile) and she has remained on the 2nd centile, now weighing 7.0 kg. Her mother is on the 5th centile and her father is on the 40th centile for height. She is well, has a good appetite and has never needed to visit her doctor. She has no abnormal signs on examination and her development is normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Cow milk protein allergy','Stunting','Marasmus','Normal','Vitamin D deficiency'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Laila’s growth follows her familial pattern (parents’ heights), and she is healthy and developing normally, indicating constitutional smallness rather than pathology.\n\n---\n\n**⚠️ Transcription note.** The stem is printed with a double space and a space before the question mark (“What is  the most likely diagnosis ?”); the spacing has been tidied and nothing else altered.\n\n**Expansion — “Normal” is the hardest answer to choose, so justify it explicitly.** Five things have to be true, and all five are given:\n\n1. **She has never crossed a centile.** Born on the 2nd, still on the 2nd. `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` defines faltering growth as present when weight falls “across two centile spaces” — Laila has fallen across none. Position on a chart is not a diagnosis; **movement** is.\n2. **Her size is concordant with her family.** Her mother is on the 5th centile. A small child of a small mother is growing to plan; `13) Short stature.txt` requires the mid-parental height precisely so this judgement can be made.\n3. **She is well and has a good appetite.** No feeding difficulty, no illness, never needed a doctor.\n4. **Examination is normal.** None of the red flags in `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` — dysmorphic features, failure to gain weight despite adequate caloric intake, recurrent vomiting, diarrhoea or dehydration, organomegaly or lymphadenopathy, chronic respiratory signs, developmental delay.\n5. **Development is normal.** A malnourished infant loses developmental ground; `16) P.E.M.txt` lists “Retarded development” in the underweight child and “Permanent delay in intellectual developement” among the consequences of PEM.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Cow milk protein allergy** — needs symptoms: loose, mucoid or blood-streaked stools, vomiting, poor feeding, eczema, or weight falling faster than height. Laila has a good appetite, no signs, and a stable centile.\n- **Stunting** — low **height-for-age** from chronic undernutrition. Laila is small, but she is small **as expected for her family** and has not decelerated; stunting describes a child whose linear growth has been suppressed below her potential, which is a different claim.\n- **Marasmus** — a severe form of PEM. `16) P.E.M.txt` defines it by the Wellcome classification as weight **below 60%** of the 50th centile without oedema, occurring “during first 3 years of life (Usually 1st year)”, with loss of subcutaneous fat, muscle wasting of the limbs and abdomen, and psychic changes — anxious, irritable, excessively crying, little sleeping. At 7.0 kg on the 2nd centile Laila is nowhere near that, and she is thriving in every other respect.\n- **Vitamin D deficiency** — rickets gives bone signs (widened wrists, rachitic rosary, delayed fontanelle closure, bowing once weight-bearing), often with irritability, and a decelerating growth line. Her examination is normal.\n\n**The teaching point.** A low centile is a **position**; growth faltering is a **direction**. Two measurements six months apart carry more information than any single number — which is exactly what `13) Short stature.txt` means by “A single measurement is less important than growth trend.” The correct management of Laila is to keep plotting her, and to say clearly to her parents that she is normal.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'A well infant tracking her own low centile, concordant with a small parent, is normal — faltering growth is a fall, not a low position.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.102'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-38', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'You are examining the growth chart of an individual who presented as a teenager complaining of short stature and delayed puberty. He has had X-rays for bone age assessment done twice at the age of 5 and at the age of 11 years and both were somewhat delayed. Finally, he ended with a height that is compatible with his mid-parental height. Which type of short stature did he suffer from?',
  options:['Genetic short stature','Growth hormone deficiency','Chromosomal disorder','Constitutional short stature','Nutritional short stature'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Delayed bone age and puberty with eventual normal adult height is characteristic of constitutional growth delay.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — this question is answered by the *ending*, not the beginning.** The stem is written retrospectively on purpose: it gives you the whole natural history, and the diagnostic weight sits in the last sentence. **He reached a final height compatible with his mid-parental height.** That single outcome excludes every pathological option, because pathology costs adult height. Combined with a **delayed bone age on two separate occasions** and **delayed puberty**, only constitutional delay of growth and puberty fits.\n\n| Feature in the stem | What it tells you |\n|---|---|\n| Presented **as a teenager** | CDGP declares itself when peers begin their pubertal spurt — not in infancy |\n| **Delayed puberty** | Sets the diagnosis in the delayed-maturation family, not the familial one |\n| Bone age delayed at **5 and again at 11** | Consistently immature skeleton — `13) Short stature.txt`: “A. Delayed → Constitutional delay of growth and puberty/ Endocrine” |\n| **Final height = mid-parental height** | Nothing was lost. Growth was postponed, not destroyed |\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Genetic short stature** (familial short stature) — would show a **normal** bone age (“B. Normal → Familial”) and **normally timed** puberty, and the final height, while normal *for the family*, would be reached on schedule rather than late. The two bone ages exclude it.\n- **Growth hormone deficiency** — the “Endocrine” half of the delayed-bone-age rule, and the reason a delayed bone age alone is not diagnostic. But GH deficiency untreated does **not** end at mid-parental height: `13) Short stature.txt` describes low IGF-1 and IGFBP-3, a growth velocity under 4 cm a year, severe short stature more than 3 SD below the mean, and treatment with daily subcutaneous recombinant human GH — the whole point of which is that final height is otherwise lost.\n- **Chromosomal disorder** — Turner syndrome in a girl or Down syndrome in either sex both produce **permanently** reduced adult height (`6) Genetically determined disease_.txt` lists short stature under both), and Turner needs growth hormone therapy for that reason. Reaching mid-parental height excludes them.\n- **Nutritional short stature** — PEM causes growth failure with weight affected more than height (`16) P.E.M.txt`), and severe or prolonged malnutrition leaves permanent deficits, including “Permanent delay in intellectual developement”. Catch-up after treatment can be substantial, but the stem gives no nutritional history and gives delayed puberty and a delayed bone age instead.\n\n**Management, which the examiner often asks next.** `13) Short stature.txt` answers it in its Case 2: “Reassurance ± puberty induction, 3-6 months testosterone injections to stimulate puberty at 13-14 years.” The treatment is offered for the psychological burden of being the smallest boy in the year, not to increase final height — which is normal either way.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Delayed bone age + delayed puberty + normal final height = constitutional delay. Delayed bone age + a final height *below* the mid-parental range = something pathological was missed.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Constitutional delay is recognised retrospectively by a persistently delayed bone age, late puberty, and a final height matching mid-parental height.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.104'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-39', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'A 2-year-old boy presents to the office with his father due to concerns over the appearance of his son’s genitals. He reports that over the last 2 months he has noticed his penis has gotten longer, frequent erections, and what appears to be a few dark strands of hair. On examination, you confirm that the phallus is long for stated age, pubic hair that is curly, and testicles that are less than 2.5 cm. His father denies any past medical history for his son and even states that he had imaging of his head during an ER visit for a fall 2 weeks prior which was normal. When you question the father about his onset of puberty, he claims to have noticed changes at 14 years old. He denies any other family history of illness. The child lives with his mother and father. Medications in the home include maternal intake of daily multivitamins and paternal gel for decreased libido. Which of the following statements is likely an explanation for this child’s presentation?',
  options:['Noncentral precocious puberty hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis early activation','Noncentral precocious puberty from exogenous source of sex hormone','Central precocious puberty hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis early activation','Central precocious puberty from exogenous source of sex hormone','Incomplete precocious puberty based on history and early thelarche'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> This 2-year-old boy shows signs of virilization (penile growth, pubic hair) but prepubertal testicular size (<2.5 cm), which suggests peripheral (gonadotropin-independent) precocious puberty, rather than central (which would have testicular enlargement). The history of paternal testosterone gel at home raises concern for exogenous androgen exposure, which is a common cause of noncentral precocious puberty in young children. Central activation of the HPG axis is unlikely given the very young age and small testes.\n\n---\n\n**⚠️ Note on the printed options.** Options A and C are internally contradictory as written: “noncentral precocious puberty **hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis early activation**” describes two mutually exclusive things, since noncentral (peripheral) disease is by definition **not** driven by the HPG axis. They are deliberate nonsense pairings, and recognising that A cannot exist is itself a route to the answer. The options are reproduced exactly as printed.\n\n**Expansion — the answer is a 2×2 grid, and only one cell is possible.**\n\n| | **HPG axis activation** | **Exogenous / peripheral source** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Central** | The real entity — enlarged testes, detectable LH/FSH | Impossible by definition |\n| **Noncentral (peripheral)** | Impossible by definition | **The real entity — small testes, suppressed LH/FSH** ✔ |\n\n**The clinical evidence.** *Testicles less than 2.5 cm* is prepubertal. In a boy, true central puberty **begins** with testicular enlargement — `14) Puberty.txt` describes genital stage II as “enlargement of the scrotum and testes and thinning and redness of the scrotal skin”, and includes a slide on testicular examination in precocious puberty for exactly this reason. Virilisation (penile growth, erections, curly pubic hair) **without** testicular growth therefore means the androgen is arriving from somewhere other than the testes.\n\n**The source is named in the last line of the stem.** “Paternal gel for decreased libido” is a transdermal testosterone preparation. Skin-to-skin contact transfers it, and a toddler carried against a treated shoulder or chest receives a proportionally enormous dose. This is a **reversible** cause: the virilisation stops and partially regresses once the exposure ends *(the reversibility and the transfer mechanism are filled from general medical knowledge — not taken from the course material; `14) Puberty.txt` classifies precocity into central and peripheral without discussing transdermal androgen transfer)*.\n\n**The distractor details the examiner planted.** *“Imaging of his head... was normal”* pre-empts the central answer. *“He had changes at 14 years old”* gives a father whose own puberty was, if anything, late, weakening any familial argument. Both are there to close off the alternatives before you reach the medication list.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.** *A* — impossible construction (see above); noncentral disease is gonadotrophin-independent. *C* — central activation would give **enlarged testes**, and the head imaging was normal; the deck notes that up to 75% of boys with central precocity have a demonstrable structural CNS abnormality, making a normal scan additional evidence against it. *D* — impossible construction; an exogenous hormone suppresses the axis rather than activating it. *E* — “thelarche” is **breast** development, which this boy does not have; the option is describing a girl’s benign incomplete variant and does not apply.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** In any virilised toddler, examine the testes and then take a **household medication history**. Small testes plus an androgen in the home is the diagnosis, and the treatment is removing the exposure.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Virilisation with prepubertal testes is peripheral precocity — in a toddler, look for exogenous androgen such as a parent’s testosterone gel.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.106, box on p.107'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-40', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'What is the most likely cause of faltering growth in a 9-month-old infant?',
  options:['Small atrial septal defect (ASD)','Persistent diarrhea','Physiological gastroesophageal reflux','Frequent minor upper respiratory infections'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Chronic or frequent diarrhea leads to poor nutrient absorption and weight/height faltering; minor infections or reflux are less impactful.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — faltering growth is an energy-balance problem, and there are only four ways to fail.** Every cause fits into one of these boxes, and the strategy for answering any faltering-growth question is to ask which box the option belongs in:\n\n| Mechanism | Examples |\n|---|---|\n| **Not enough in** | Inadequate intake, poor feeding technique, insufficient breast milk, neglect, poverty, an aversive feeding relationship |\n| **Not enough absorbed** | **Persistent diarrhoea**, coeliac disease, cow’s milk protein enteropathy, cystic fibrosis, chronic infection or parasitosis |\n| **Too much lost** | Recurrent vomiting, severe reflux, protein-losing states |\n| **Too much used** | Congenital heart disease with a significant shunt, chronic lung disease, chronic infection, hyperthyroidism |\n\n**Persistent diarrhoea sits in the second box and is by far the highest-yield answer in this population**, particularly in a setting where infectious diarrhoea is common; the syllabus gives it a full deck (`23).1) Acute diarrhea.txt`). It causes malabsorption of the nutrients that *are* taken in, is often accompanied by anorexia during illness, and recurs, so the child never gets a period of catch-up. The endpoint file’s own summary slide states the single commonest cause overall as “Most common cause of faltering growth: Inadequate calorie intake” (`Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf` p.27) — but of the four options offered here, only persistent diarrhoea produces a comparable sustained negative energy balance.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong — note that each is qualified to make it trivial.**\n\n- **Small atrial septal defect** — the word “small” is doing the work. A **large** left-to-right shunt causes faltering growth by raising energy expenditure and causing breathlessness on feeding, and the syllabus covers this in `33)Congenital acyanotic heart disease_.txt`. A **small** ASD is haemodynamically insignificant, often asymptomatic, and frequently closes spontaneously.\n- **Physiological gastroesophageal reflux** — again the qualifier decides it. Physiological reflux (“happy posseting”) occurs in a large proportion of infants and by definition does **not** affect growth. Pathological reflux with volume loss, feed refusal or aspiration does.\n- **Frequent minor upper respiratory infections** — common in infancy, especially once a child enters day care, and they cause brief dips in intake with prompt recovery. They are **minor** and self-limiting, so they do not produce a sustained deficit.\n\n**How this is assessed in practice.** `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` reminds you that faltering growth is a “Description, not a diagnosis”, present when weight falls “across two centile spaces”; that you must “Ensure accurate serial measuring and plotting of height and weight on appropriate growth chart”; that “Investigations should be guided by history and examination”; and that “Simple behaviral advice can improve quantity and quality of nutritional intake”. Its red flags — dysmorphic features, failure to gain weight despite adequate caloric intake, recurrent vomiting, diarrhoea or dehydration, organomegaly or lymphadenopathy, signs of chronic respiratory disease, developmental delay — are what separate a behavioural feeding problem from organic disease.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Read the adjectives. “Small”, “physiological” and “minor” are all instructions to discount the option.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Faltering growth is an energy-balance failure; persistent diarrhoea causes sustained malabsorption, whereas minor or physiological conditions do not.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.109'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-41', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'During which phase of growth is faltering most commonly seen?',
  options:['Infantile','Childhood','Pubertal','Fetal'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Infancy is a high-risk period for growth faltering due to dependence on nutrition and vulnerability to illness.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — three properties of infancy converge to make it the vulnerable phase.**\n\n1. **Growth is fastest postnatally, so the nutritional demand is highest.** A baby roughly triples birth weight and grows about 25 cm in the first year; the endpoint file’s own summary slide assigns the infantile phase 15% of final height (`Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf` p.7). A high growth rate means a small shortfall in intake shows up quickly on the chart.\n2. **The driver of growth in this phase is nutrition itself** — not hormones, not genes. Anything that reduces intake or absorption translates directly into lost growth, which is not true of the childhood phase, where GH keeps a well-nourished child growing through minor illnesses.\n3. **The infant is entirely dependent on someone else to feed her**, and the feeding method changes twice in the first year — breast or formula, then complementary feeding from 6 months (`15.2) Infant Feeding.txt`: pureed, mashed and semisolid at 6 months, finger foods at 8 months, family foods at 12 months). Each transition is an opportunity for intake to fall. Infection rates also rise as maternal antibody wanes and weaning foods introduce enteric pathogens.\n\n**What faltering growth actually means.** `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` is emphatic that it is a “Description, not a diagnosis”, and gives the threshold: “present if an infant’s weight falls across two centile spaces.” Management is “Dietary and behavioural modification designed to increase energy intake and monitoring growth”, with the reminder that “Investigations should be guided by history and examination” and that “Simple behaviral advice can improve quantity and quality of nutritional intake”. Its red-flag list — dysmorphic features, failure to gain weight despite adequate caloric intake, recurrent vomiting, diarrhoea or dehydration, organomegaly or lymphadenopathy, signs of chronic respiratory disease, developmental delay — is what separates the common behavioural picture from organic disease. The endpoint file’s own summary slide names the commonest cause: “Most common cause of faltering growth: Inadequate calorie intake” (`Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf` p.27).\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Childhood** — growth is slowest and steadiest here, GH-driven, and the child eats independently. Growth failure in this phase is *less common* but *more sinister*: it is where endocrine disease and chronic illness declare themselves, and a falling centile after age 5 should always be investigated.\n- **Pubertal** — the growth spurt is a period of high demand, and undernutrition in adolescence can delay puberty and blunt the spurt, but the adolescent controls her own intake and has reserves an infant does not.\n- **Fetal** — growth restriction in utero certainly happens (IUGR/SGA), but it is not called “faltering growth”. That term is reserved for postnatal weight crossing centiles. `13) Short stature.txt` handles the fetal equivalent under small for gestational age, noting that “10% intrauterine growth restriction do not catch-up growth”.\n\n**A related question in this same section asks the same fact over a five-option list** that adds “Fetal phase” and “Prepubertal phase”; the answer is the same, and both are held because the option sets differ.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Faltering growth is an infant diagnosis. In an older child, a falling centile is not “faltering growth” — it is a symptom that needs a cause.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Faltering growth clusters in infancy, where growth is fast, nutrition-dependent and entirely reliant on the carer.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.111'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-42', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'Which is the fastest period of growth throughout life?',
  options:['Infancy','Childhood','Puberty','Adolescence'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Growth velocity is highest during infancy, surpassing fetal, childhood, and pubertal phases.\n\n---\n\n**⚠️ DEFECT — the printed box contradicts the bank’s own answer elsewhere, and the bank is wrong here.** The key (**Infancy**) is defensible **because *Fetal* is not among the four options**, and among what is offered infancy genuinely has the highest velocity. But the box’s further claim that infancy *surpasses* the fetal period is wrong on the endpoint file’s own evidence:\n\n- Twice in this same section the file asks “What is the fastest period of growth in a normal human?” over a five-option list that **does** include *Fetal*, and keys **Fetal** both times.\n- Its own summary slide states: “Fetal phase (30% of final height, **Fastest period of Growth**)” (`Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf` p.7).\n\nThe key is left exactly as the bank prints it, per the rule that the printed key is what the entry carries, and the discrepancy is recorded here so the learner is not confused when the other printing keys *Fetal*. **In an examination: answer *fetal* whenever fetal is offered, and *infancy* only when it is not.**\n\n**Expansion — the velocity curve.** Growth velocity is at its maximum before birth and declines almost continuously afterwards, with a single interruption at puberty:\n\n| Phase | Velocity | Share of final height | Driver |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Fetal** | **Highest of life** (peak linear velocity in the second trimester) | **30%** | Placenta, maternal nutrition, uterine environment |\n| **Infancy** (0–2 y) | Highest **postnatal** velocity, decelerating steeply | **15%** | **Nutrition** |\n| **Childhood** (2 y – puberty) | Slowest and steadiest | ~40% | **GH / IGF-1**, thyroid permissive |\n| **Puberty** | A **second peak**, lower than fetal | **15%** | **Sex steroids** |\n\nThe pubertal figures come from `14) Puberty.txt`: the spurt is “Mediated by GONADAL STEROIDS (testosterone and estradiol)”, oestrogen contributing “more than androgens”, adding “15% to final adult height”, after which “Sex steroids cause fusion of epiphyseal growth plates and cessation of growth”. The same deck notes that “Girls have peak height velocity average 2 years before boys”.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.** *Childhood* is the **slowest** phase — steady, GH-driven, roughly 5–7 cm a year *(the centimetre figure is filled from general medical knowledge — not taken from the course material)*. *Puberty* is the second peak, real but smaller than infancy and far smaller than fetal life. *Adolescence* overlaps puberty and is, if anything, the tail of it — by late adolescence the epiphyses are fusing and growth is ending, so it is the weakest option on the list.\n\n**A useful sanity check on the numbers.** A term newborn is about 50 cm and a 1-year-old about 75 cm — roughly 25 cm in the first year. A 5-year-old grows 5–7 cm a year. A boy at peak height velocity manages 9–10 cm a year. Nothing postnatal comes close to the fetal rate, in which a whole 50 cm human is built in 40 weeks *(these velocity figures are filled from general medical knowledge — not taken from the course material)*.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Fetal life has the highest growth velocity, infancy the highest postnatal velocity — answer fetal whenever it is offered.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.113'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-43', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'Which factor mainly determines the size of a newborn at birth?',
  options:['Maternal size','Paternal size','Growth hormone','Nutrition after birth'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Maternal genetics and uterine environment primarily influence birth size, more than paternal genetics or postnatal nutrition.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — birth size is a statement about the *uterus*, not about the baby’s genes.** The fetus grows inside a maternal environment that constrains it, supplies it, and sets a ceiling on it. Three separate maternal factors combine:\n\n1. **Uterine capacity and maternal size** — a small mother constrains fetal growth regardless of the father’s height. This is the biological reason a first baby of a small mother is not born large even when the father is tall.\n2. **Placental function** — the delivery system for every nutrient and every molecule of oxygen. Placental insufficiency is the mechanism of most asymmetrical growth restriction.\n3. **Maternal nutrition and health** — `7) Intro to neonatology.txt` lists the causes of small for gestational age in two columns: **asymmetrical IUGR** (“Head spare”, onset after the 18th week of gestation, chronic hypertension, severe malnutrition, genetic mutation) and **symmetrical IUGR** (“Proportionate to the rest of the body”, TORCH infections, chromosomal anomalies, anaemia, substance abuse including fetal alcohol syndrome). Almost every entry on that list is a maternal or placental factor.\n\n**And birth size does not predict adult size.** This is the key clinical corollary, and it is why the question matters. Because birth size reflects the intrauterine environment while later size reflects genetic potential, infants **normally cross centiles** in the first months — upward if they were constrained in utero, downward if the environment was generous. `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` therefore sets the threshold for concern at a weight falling “across two centile spaces” rather than at any single crossing.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Paternal size** — contributes to the child’s **genetic** height potential, which becomes visible during the childhood phase, and it enters the mid-parental height calculation that `13) Short stature.txt` requires. But it has little influence on birth size, because the father contributes no uterus.\n- **Growth hormone** — a striking negative that is worth remembering: **fetal growth is essentially GH-independent**. A baby with congenital GH deficiency is born of normal length; the growth failure only becomes apparent through the first year or two. Fetal growth is driven far more by insulin and IGF-2 *(the specific fetal growth factors are filled from general medical knowledge — not taken from the course material)*. The insulin point is directly illustrated in the syllabus by the infant of a diabetic mother: `7) Intro to neonatology.txt` gives the mechanism as “Maternal Hyperglycemia, Fetal Hyperglycemia & Hyperinsulinemia”, producing **macrosomia** — fetal insulin acting as a growth factor.\n- **Nutrition after birth** — by definition cannot determine size **at** birth. It is the determinant of growth *during* the infantile phase, which is a different question the bank asks separately.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** A small newborn prompts a question about the pregnancy, not about the family’s height. A small 2-year-old prompts a question about the parents.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Birth size is set by maternal size, placental function and maternal health — not by paternal genes or growth hormone.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.115'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-44', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'Which hormone is least related to growth in early life?',
  options:['Growth hormone','Insulin','Thyroid hormone','IGF-1'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Early infancy growth is largely driven by nutrition, insulin, and thyroid hormones, while GH plays a minor role initially.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — this is counter-intuitive and correct, and the counter-intuition is the point.** “Growth hormone” is named for what it does in **childhood**, not in fetal life or early infancy. GH-dependent growth becomes established from roughly the second half of the first year and dominates from about 2 years until puberty. Before that, the growth of a well-fed infant is driven by nutrition, with insulin, thyroid hormone and IGF-1 as the effectors.\n\n| Hormone | Role in **fetal life and early infancy** | Role later |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Growth hormone** | **Minimal** — a GH-deficient newborn is of normal length | **The** childhood driver, via IGF-1 |\n| **Insulin** | **Major fetal growth factor** — drives tissue accretion in response to substrate | Metabolic; permissive for growth |\n| **Thyroid hormone** | **Essential** — for skeletal maturation and, critically, brain development | Permissive throughout; deficiency causes growth failure |\n| **IGF-1** | Active from fetal life, but in infancy it is largely **nutrition-driven rather than GH-driven** | GH-driven and the main mediator of childhood growth |\n\n**Two lines of evidence from the syllabus support each column.**\n\n*For insulin as the fetal growth factor*, `7) Intro to neonatology.txt` gives the infant of a diabetic mother: the aetiology is “Maternal Hyperglycemia, Fetal Hyperglycemia & Hyperinsulinemia”, and the consequence heading the problem list is **macrosomia**. High fetal insulin makes a big baby — a natural experiment proving insulin’s growth role before birth.\n\n*For thyroid hormone*, `52) hypothyrodism.txt` opens by stating its emphasis on “linear growth and brain” development, describes the untreated child as “Short with infantile proportions” with delayed bone age, and devotes several slides to neonatal screening — “Analyzed in central lab. For TSH” — because the damage of untreated congenital hypothyroidism in the first weeks is irreversible. `13) Short stature.txt` lists thyroid hormone among the hormonal influences on growth, and puts “TSH, fT4” in **Step Two** of investigation, ahead of any GH testing.\n\n*For GH’s late arrival*, `13) Short stature.txt` sets a demanding threshold before GH is even investigated — “Severe short stature (height >3 SD below the mean); Height affected > Weight; Height >1.5 SD below MPH; Poor growth velocity (< 4cm per year)” — and warns “Do not jump to GH tests early !!”. It also lists the neonatal clues that GH deficiency *is* present, and note that none of them is short length: “Neonate: Hypoglycemia, Prolonged jaundice, microphallus”. GH deficiency in a newborn shows itself metabolically, not by size.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.** *Insulin*, *thyroid hormone* and *IGF-1* are all directly involved in fetal and early infant growth, as above. Only GH is the outlier — and the question is asked in the negative (“least related”), so the outlier is the answer.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** A newborn of normal size does not exclude GH deficiency, and a GH assay in a small infant is the wrong first test. Think nutrition, thyroid, and the systemic screen first.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Fetal and early infant growth is driven by nutrition, insulin, thyroid hormone and IGF-1; growth hormone only takes over in the childhood phase.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.117'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-45', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'Low birth weight in infants may predispose to which later condition?',
  options:['Childhood obesity','Tall stature','Early puberty','Reduced IQ'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The key as printed is D, reduced IQ, and it is defensible — but note that A is also a recognised consequence, so read the list before answering.**\n\n**Why reduced IQ follows low birth weight.** Low birth weight arises by two routes, and both threaten the brain. A **preterm** infant is born before brain growth is complete and then faces the complications `7) Intro to neonatology.txt` lists as the frequent prematurity-related problems: “Periventricular-Intraventricular Hemorrhage (IVH)” and “Periventricular Leukomalacia (PVL)”, of which it warns “Both are usually silent in presentation”, plus hypoglycaemia from low glycogen stores, hypothermia, sepsis, and the consequences of prolonged ventilation. A **growth-restricted** term infant has been chronically undersupplied in utero; `7) Intro to neonatology.txt` divides SGA into asymmetrical (“Head spare”, onset after the 18th week, chronic hypertension, severe malnutrition) and symmetrical (“Proportionate to the rest of the body”, TORCH, chromosomal anomalies, anaemia, fetal alcohol syndrome) — and the symmetrical group, in which the head has **not** been spared, carries the worse neurodevelopmental outlook. The association between low birth weight and reduced cognitive scores is graded: the lower the birth weight and the earlier the gestation, the larger the average deficit *(the graded dose-response is not taken from the course material)*.\n\n**⚠️ DEFECT — option A is also correct, and arguably more famous.** Low birth weight is the classic antecedent in the **developmental origins of health and disease** (Barker) hypothesis: a fetus undersupplied in utero adapts its metabolism to scarcity, and when it later meets an abundant food supply it gains fat disproportionately, giving central obesity, insulin resistance, type 2 diabetes and hypertension in adult life. Rapid postnatal catch-up growth is the step that converts the risk into disease *(this entire paragraph is not taken from the course material — none of the cached Pediatrics decks covers the Barker hypothesis or metabolic programming)*. **Option C, early puberty, is likewise a recognised association** in girls born small who show rapid catch-up. The bank keys D and D is left as printed, per the rule that the printed key stands; the alternatives are recorded here so a learner who chose A knows why.\n\n**Why option B is unambiguously wrong.** Low birth weight predisposes to **short**, not tall, stature. `13) Short stature.txt` states it directly under small for gestational age: “10% intrauterine growth restriction do not catch-up growth” — that is, one in ten SGA infants never regains the centile they should have occupied, and SGA without catch-up is a standing cause of short stature in the lecture’s own list.\n\n**The clinical consequence — surveillance, not prediction.** A low birth weight infant needs: growth plotted at **corrected** age (`13) Short stature.txt`: “Continued for the first year of life. In extremely preterm infants, until 2 years of age”); developmental surveillance, since `Lec 1 Normal development_.txt` requires that skills also be judged at corrected age and gives the worked example of a 9-month-old born at 28 weeks whose skills resemble a 6-month-old’s; hearing and vision checks, given retinopathy of prematurity; and attention to feeding, because catch-up growth needs calories. What must be avoided is the opposite error — assuming a poor outcome. Most low birth weight children develop normally, and the purpose of follow-up is early intervention, which `1) Normal development.txt` places in the “first 2- 3 ys of age (critical period)”.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Low birth weight is a marker of risk across three domains at once — cognitive, growth, and later metabolic — and the answer to a single-best-answer question depends on which of them is offered.\n\nWritten for this bank — Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Low birth weight predisposes to reduced IQ, to short stature where catch-up fails, and — via metabolic programming — to later obesity.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.119'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-46', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'Which type of scale provides the most accurate measurement of infant weight?',
  options:['Electronic scale','Spring balance','Bathroom scale','Manual balance'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Electronic scales are precise for small weight changes, unlike spring, bathroom, or manual balances.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — the measurement is the diagnosis, so the instrument matters.** Everything in this chapter rests on serial weights: `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` defines faltering growth as a weight falling “across two centile spaces” and instructs you to “Ensure accurate serial measuring and plotting of height and weight on appropriate growth chart”; `13) Short stature.txt` states that “A single measurement is less important than growth trend” and that “Serial measurements show pattern of growth”. A trend can only be trusted if each point on it is trustworthy — and in an infant the changes being tracked are tens of grams, so a scale that reads to the nearest 100 g cannot detect what you are looking for.\n\n| Instrument | Typical resolution | Why it fails for an infant |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Electronic (digital) infant scale** | **To the gram or 5–10 g**, with a tare function and often a damping/averaging mode for a moving baby | — it is the answer |\n| Spring balance | Coarse; the spring **stretches and drifts** with age and load | Loses calibration silently; poor reproducibility |\n| Bathroom scale | 100–500 g, designed for an adult’s weight range | Resolution far too coarse; a baby cannot stand still on it |\n| Manual (beam) balance | Good when correctly calibrated and read | Slow, operator-dependent reading, and hard to use with a squirming infant |\n\n*(The resolution figures are filled from general medical knowledge — not taken from the course material; the cached decks require accurate serial measurement without specifying instruments.)*\n\n**The technique matters as much as the device.** Weigh the infant **naked or in a clean nappy of known weight**, on the same scale, at a similar time relative to feeds, with the scale zeroed (tared) immediately beforehand and standing on a hard level surface. Using a different scale at each visit introduces a step change in the growth line that can be mistaken for faltering or for catch-up. The same discipline applies to length: `13) Short stature.txt` devotes two consecutive slides to “How to measure Height?” and “How to accurately measure Height?”, and the corresponding rule is that under about 3 years the child is measured **recumbent** with two observers, and standing thereafter — never interchangeably, since the two methods differ by about a centimetre.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.** *Spring balance* — the archetype of a drifting instrument; springs fatigue, so the error is systematic, not random, and it grows over exactly the months you are trying to compare. *Bathroom scale* — the wrong resolution and the wrong weight range, and unusable for a baby who cannot stand. *Manual balance* — genuinely accurate when calibrated and read properly, which is why it is the strongest distractor, but it is slower, needs a still subject, and its reading is observer-dependent.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Before you diagnose faltering growth, check the plot: same scale, tared, naked baby, correct chart, correct (or corrected) age. A surprising proportion of apparent growth failure is a measurement artefact.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Serial infant weights need a tared electronic scale reading to the gram; coarse or drifting instruments manufacture false growth trends.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.121'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-47', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'How should height be measured in children below 3 years of age?',
  options:['Recumbent length (lying down)','Standing height','Sitting height','Arm span'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Children under 3 cannot reliably stand straight; recumbent measurement is standard.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — why the method changes at about 2–3 years, and why it must not be mixed.** A toddler cannot stand still, straight, with heels down, buttocks and shoulders against a stadiometer, looking ahead in the Frankfurt plane. **Recumbent length** removes all of that: the child lies on a length board, one observer holds the head against the fixed headboard with the eyes vertical, and a second observer straightens the legs and brings the footboard to the soles. It needs two people, which is precisely why it is abandoned as soon as the child can cooperate.\n\nThe practical consequence is the part examiners test: **recumbent length reads about 0.5–1 cm greater than standing height in the same child**, because the spine is not compressed by gravity and the joints are fully extended. Switching methods therefore produces an artefactual **drop** in the plotted line that can be mistaken for growth failure *(the magnitude of the difference is filled from general medical knowledge — not taken from the course material)*. This is also why the growth charts themselves change: `13) Short stature.txt` gives the WHO chart as “Recommended for 0-24 months” and the CDC chart as “Recommended for 2-19 years”, and the WHO chart is built on **length** while the CDC chart is built on **height**. Use the chart that matches the method.\n\n**The lecture treats measurement as a skill in its own right**, devoting consecutive slides to “How to measure Height?” and “How to accurately measure Height?”, and building every definition on top of it: short stature is “Height >2 SDs below the mean for age and sex (<3rd percentile)”, “Height >2 SD below mid-parental height”, and “Height velocity <1.5 SD for age and pubertal staging”. A velocity is the difference between two measurements, so a 1 cm method error in either one can double or erase an apparent year’s growth. Hence “A single measurement is less important than growth trend” — but only if the trend is measured the same way each time.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Standing height** — the correct method from about 2–3 years onward, once the child can cooperate. Attempting it in a 1-year-old produces an unreliable, usually underestimated, number.\n- **Sitting height** — a real and useful measurement, but for a different purpose: it is part of assessing **body proportions**. `13) Short stature.txt` includes a slide on “Sitting height” and lists “Proportions (upper/lower segment, arm span)” under examination, because proportion is what separates a skeletal dysplasia such as achondroplasia from proportionate short stature. It is an adjunct, never the primary measure of stature.\n- **Arm span** — likewise a proportion measurement, used to detect disproportionate limb length (long limbs in Marfan syndrome, short limbs in achondroplasia). It approximates height in an adult but is not a substitute for measuring it, least of all in a toddler.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Under 3, lie the child down and use a length board and two people. Over 3, stand the child up. Record which method was used, and never compare one with the other.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Measure recumbent length under about 3 years and standing height thereafter — and never mix the two, since length reads ~1 cm greater.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.123'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-48', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'What does head circumference mainly reflect?',
  options:['Brain growth','Skull bone growth','Body weight','Muscle mass'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Brain growth drives skull expansion in infancy; weight or muscle mass has minimal impact.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — the skull is a passive container, and the brain inflates it.** The infant calvarium is a set of separate bones joined by open sutures and fontanelles. It expands **because the brain expands**, not the other way round, so occipitofrontal circumference (OFC) is a non-invasive proxy for brain growth. The endpoint file’s own summary slide states it directly: “Head circumference (OFC) ... Reflects brain growth” (`Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf` p.8).\n\nThe converse gives the two disorders that flank the normal range:\n\n| | **Microcephaly** | **Macrocephaly** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Definition | OFC well below the expected range for age and sex | OFC well above it |\n| Mechanism | The brain has failed to grow, or has been injured | The brain, CSF or blood inside the skull has increased |\n| Typical causes | Congenital infection (TORCH), perinatal asphyxia and HIE, severe prenatal insults, genetic and metabolic syndromes | **Raised intracranial pressure — hydrocephalus, subdural haematoma, brain tumour**; benign familial macrocephaly; some metabolic and storage disorders |\n\nThe macrocephaly causes are from the endpoint file’s own summary slide (`Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf` p.20: “Macrocephaly ... Causes: ... Raised ICP (hydrocephalus, subdural hematoma, brain tumor)”). `1) Normal development.txt` includes both **MICROCEPHALY** and **SEVERE HYDROCEPHALUS** among the conditions recognisable from the face in a child with intellectual disability, and lists “TOTAL CRANIOSTENOSIS” beside them — the third mechanism, in which the skull itself restricts the brain rather than following it.\n\n**Why the crossing of centiles is what matters, not the absolute value.** A big-headed baby with big-headed parents whose OFC tracks the 97th centile is normal (benign familial macrocephaly). A baby whose OFC **crosses upward** through centiles while the anterior fontanelle bulges, the sutures separate and the sun-setting sign appears has progressive hydrocephalus and needs urgent imaging. Equally, an OFC that **falls away** from its centile in the first year signals acquired brain injury or a failure of brain growth. Plot it at every visit alongside weight and length — a triad of measurements, as `16) P.E.M.txt` requires under anthropometric data (“Head, chest & abdominal circumf.”).\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Skull bone growth** — the tempting answer, and precisely backwards. The bones grow *at the sutures, in response to* brain expansion. The proof is craniosynostosis: fuse a suture and the skull deforms rather than restricting the brain, because the brain still has to go somewhere; and in total craniosynostosis, where the brain genuinely is restricted, the result is raised intracranial pressure, not a small brain.\n- **Body weight** — head circumference is remarkably **independent** of nutritional state. In malnutrition the head is spared longest, which is exactly why the asymmetrical growth-restricted fetus in `7) Intro to neonatology.txt` is described as “Head spare”. An infant whose head circumference is falling with her weight has a far more serious problem than one whose weight alone is falling.\n- **Muscle mass** — no relationship; muscle bulk is assessed by mid-arm circumference and by inspection, and `16) P.E.M.txt` uses “Mid-arm circumference (11.5 cm)” for exactly that.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Measure the head at every infant visit and plot it. A head circumference crossing centiles in either direction is a neurological finding.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Head circumference reflects brain growth, is spared in malnutrition, and must be plotted serially — crossing centiles in either direction is neurological.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.125'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-49', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'Which index is used to assess underweight and obesity?',
  options:['Body Mass Index (BMI)','Mid-arm circumference','Waist/hip ratio','Growth velocity'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> BMI accounts for height and weight and is widely used to classify underweight, overweight, and obesity.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — BMI, and the two things that make the paediatric version different.** `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` states the definition and the method:\n\n- “Obesity is defined as an excessive accumulation of body fat.”\n- “Body mass index (BMI) is the most reliable and most commonly used clinical indicator of obesity.”\n- “BMI is calculated by dividing the weight in Kg by the height in meters squared (kg/m2).”\n- “In children BMI should be plotted on appropriate charts.”\n- “BMI in children is **age- and sex-specific**.”\n\nThat last pair of lines is the examinable difference. An adult uses fixed cut-offs; a child cannot, because body composition changes continuously with age and differs between the sexes. So a child’s BMI is converted to a **centile for age and sex**:\n\n| BMI centile | Category (`15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt`) |\n|---|---|\n| **> 95th** | **OBESE** |\n| **85th – 95th** | **Over weight** |\n| **5th – 85th** | **Healthy Wt** |\n\n**Why it matters that BMI is plotted rather than read.** The same deck gives the management framework once a child is classified — “Eat Healthy, More active, ↓sedentary activity”, with “Successful management requires sustained changes in lifestyle”; drug treatment (orlistat, “a lipase inhibitor which reduces the absorption of dietary fat and thus produces steatorrhoea”) only “in children over the age of 12 years who have physical co-morbidities or severe psychological problems”; and bariatric surgery only in children who have “almost achieved maturity”, with a BMI above 40, or 35–40 with complications such as type 2 diabetes or hypertension, after all else has failed. It also names the causes: idiopathic obesity, and endogenous obesity from endocrine causes (hypothyroidism, hypercortisolism, acquired hypothalamic syndrome), syndromic obesity such as Prader–Willi, and gene defects such as leptin deficiency.\n\n**The one-line triage that goes with BMI.** `13) Short stature.txt`: “A. Nutritional Obesity: Obese + Tall. B. Endocrinal causes: Obese + Short/poor height velocity.” A fat, tall child is over-eating; a fat, short child with a poor height velocity has an endocrine disorder. Always plot the **height** alongside the BMI.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Mid-arm circumference** — a rapid screen for **acute severe malnutrition**, and `16) P.E.M.txt` gives it under anthropometric data with the 11.5 cm cut-off. It detects wasting; it says nothing about obesity.\n- **Waist/hip ratio** — an adult measure of **central fat distribution** and cardiovascular risk. It is not used to classify underweight, and paediatric norms are not part of the cached material.\n- **Growth velocity** — cm per year, a measure of the **rate of linear growth**. `13) Short stature.txt` uses it to define short stature (“Height velocity <1.5 SD for age and pubertal staging”) and to trigger GH investigation (“Poor growth velocity (< 4cm per year)”). It is about height over time, not about body composition.\n\n**Filing note.** Secondary chapter **nutrition**.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'BMI (kg/m²) classifies underweight and obesity, and in children must be plotted as an age- and sex-specific centile: >95th obese, 85th–95th overweight.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.127'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-50', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'What is constitutional growth delay considered as?',
  options:['A variation of normal growth','Growth hormone deficiency','Malnutrition','Endocrine disorder'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> It is a benign pattern with delayed growth and puberty but normal adult height.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — the whole chapter is summarised in this one answer.** `13) Short stature.txt` closes with a summary slide whose first statement is: **“Most short children are normal variants.”** There are two such variants, and constitutional delay of growth and puberty is one of them:\n\n| | **Familial short stature** | **Constitutional delay (CDGP)** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Nature | Normal variant | **Normal variant** |\n| Parents | Short | Normal height; often a late-developing parent |\n| Bone age | Normal | **Delayed** |\n| Puberty | Normal timing | **Delayed** |\n| Growth velocity | Normal | Normal, then a late catch-up |\n| **Final adult height** | Short, as predicted | **NORMAL** |\n| Sex | Either | Boys |\n| Investigation needed | None | Bone age, and enough screening to exclude disease |\n\n**Why it is a variant and not a disease.** Nothing is missing and nothing is failing. The child’s biological clock simply runs behind the calendar. Because the epiphyses stay open longer, the growing period is extended, and the height that looked lost is recovered — which is why the defining feature is a **normal final adult height**. `13) Short stature.txt` bears this out in Case 2: a 13-year-old boy with no puberty yet, a father who was a “late bloomer”, Tanner stage 1, a bone age delayed 2 years and **normal labs**, answered as constitutional delay of growth and puberty and managed with “Reassurance ± puberty induction, 3-6 months testosterone injections to stimulate puberty at 13-14 years.” Treatment, where given, is for the psychological burden — not to buy height.\n\n**The one caution, and it is important.** “Normal variant” is a conclusion, not a starting assumption. A delayed bone age is shared by CDGP **and** by endocrine disease: “A. Delayed → Constitutional delay of growth and puberty/ **Endocrine**.” So a child is only labelled constitutionally delayed once the screening that `13) Short stature.txt` sets out has been passed — Step One (CBC, ESR, renal functions, electrolytes, LFT), Step Two (TSH and fT4, coeliac screen, IGF-1 and IGFBP-3, bone age, karyotyping in all girls) — and, in a girl, only once Turner syndrome has been excluded. The lecture’s Case 3 exists to make that point: a 7-year-old girl with a bone age delayed 5 years turned out to have **mosaic Turner syndrome**, which may show “No, or minimal manifestations”.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Growth hormone deficiency** — a genuine disease with low IGF-1 and IGFBP-3, a growth velocity under 4 cm a year, severe short stature, confirmation by two GH provocation tests, and treatment with daily subcutaneous recombinant human GH. Untreated, final height is **lost**; in CDGP it is not.\n- **Malnutrition** — PEM causes growth failure with weight affected more than height (`16) P.E.M.txt`), with clinical signs — loss of subcutaneous fat, muscle wasting, oedema in kwashiorkor — and normal labs would not be expected.\n- **Endocrine disorder** — the closest wrong answer, since endocrine disease shares the delayed bone age. But in CDGP the endocrine axes are **normal**; the timing of their activation is simply late.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Constitutional delay is a diagnosis of normality that must nevertheless be earned by excluding disease — and once earned, its management is explanation and reassurance.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Constitutional delay of growth and puberty is a normal variant with a delayed bone age, late puberty and a normal final adult height.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.129'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-51', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'Which condition presents with delayed puberty and short stature in teenage years?',
  options:['Constitutional growth delay','Familial short stature','Turner syndrome','Hypothyroidism'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Teens with delayed puberty and proportionate short stature usually have constitutional delay, often with family history.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — three words in the stem select the answer: *delayed puberty*, *short stature*, *teenage*.** Only one condition needs all three to be present at once, and it is constitutional delay of growth and puberty.\n\n| Option | Delayed puberty? | Short in the teens? | Presents in the teens? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Constitutional growth delay** | **Yes — defining** | **Yes** | **Yes — that is when it declares itself** |\n| Familial short stature | **No** — puberty on time | Yes | Usually noticed much earlier |\n| Turner syndrome | Yes (girls only) | Yes | Often much earlier, and with stigmata |\n| Hypothyroidism | Yes | Yes | Any age, with systemic symptoms |\n\n**Why the timing is diagnostic.** Constitutionally delayed children grow along a low-normal line without attracting attention. They become conspicuous at 12–14, when their classmates enter the pubertal growth spurt and pull away — `14) Puberty.txt` records that “Girls have peak height velocity average 2 years before boys”, so a boy who is himself two years behind his own cohort can appear four years behind the girls in his class. The definition to apply first is in the same deck: delayed puberty is “Failure of development of any pubertal feature by 13 yrs of age in females or by 14 yrs of age in males.”\n\n**The confirmatory test and the management.** A bone age of the left wrist and hand read against Greulich and Pyle: “A. Delayed → Constitutional delay of growth and puberty/ Endocrine. B. Normal → Familial” (`13) Short stature.txt`). The lecture’s Case 2 gives the management for exactly this patient: “Reassurance ± puberty induction, 3-6 months testosterone injections to stimulate puberty at 13-14 years.”\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Familial short stature** — the short child of short parents, with a **normal** bone age and **normally timed** puberty. It fails on the delayed-puberty requirement, which is the single most discriminating item in the stem.\n- **Turner syndrome** — a real cause of short stature with delayed puberty, but only in **girls**, and it is **hypergonadotropic** (raised LH and FSH from gonadal dysgenesis) rather than a delay in activation. `6) Genetically determined disease_.txt` describes it as “a chromosomal monosomy syndrome of short female with sexual infantilism”, with neck webbing, widely spaced nipples, a wide carrying angle, spoon-shaped nails and coarctation of the aorta. It also usually causes short stature from early childhood, not from the teens. Because mosaic Turner can be almost silent, `13) Short stature.txt` still requires “Karyotyping (All girls)”.\n- **Hypothyroidism** — causes short stature and can delay puberty (`14) Puberty.txt` lists “Acquired hypothyroidism” under hypogonadotropic hypogonadism), but it brings the systemic picture with it: lethargy, constipation, cold intolerance, dry skin, declining school performance, and the characteristic growth pattern of **height falling while weight is preserved or rises** (`52) hypothyrodism.txt`; `13) Short stature.txt`: “Height affected, Weight normal/↑ → Endocrine”). A plain delayed teenager without those features is not hypothyroid — but TSH and fT4 are still checked, in Step Two, before the label of constitutional delay is applied.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** In the teenage clinic, delayed puberty plus short stature plus a normal, well child plus a family history of late development equals constitutional delay — confirmed by a bone age, and treated mostly with an explanation.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Short stature with delayed puberty declaring itself in the teenage years is constitutional delay of growth and puberty.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.131'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-52', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'Growth during childhood is usually:',
  options:['Slower than in infancy','Faster than infancy','Equal to puberty','Independent of nutrition'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Childhood growth is steadier and slower compared to rapid infant growth.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — the shape of the growth-velocity curve is the single most useful picture in this chapter.** Velocity is maximal before birth and falls steeply through infancy, reaches its lowest sustained value in mid-childhood, then rises to a second, smaller peak at puberty before falling to zero at epiphyseal fusion.\n\n| Phase | Velocity | Share of final height | Driver |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Fetal | **Highest of life** | **30%** | Placenta, maternal nutrition |\n| **Infancy** (0–2 y) | Very fast, decelerating (~25 cm in year one) | **15%** | **Nutrition** |\n| **Childhood** (2 y – puberty) | **Slowest and steadiest** (~5–7 cm/year) | ~40% | **GH / IGF-1** |\n| Puberty | Second peak (~8–10 cm/year at its maximum) | **15%** | Sex steroids |\n\nThe 30% and 15% figures are from the endpoint file’s own summary slide (`Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf` p.7) and the pubertal 15% from `14) Puberty.txt` (“Add 15% to final adult height”); *the centimetre-per-year velocities are filled from general medical knowledge — not taken from the course material*.\n\n**Note that the childhood phase contributes the largest share of final height despite the slowest velocity** — simply because it lasts the longest, about eight to ten years. Slow does not mean unimportant, and a sustained small deficit over that period costs a great deal of height. This is why `13) Short stature.txt` makes growth **velocity** rather than height a criterion in its own right: short stature is defined partly as “Height velocity <1.5 SD for age and pubertal staging”, and GH investigation is triggered by a “Poor growth velocity (< 4cm per year)”.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Faster than infancy** — the reverse. An infant grows roughly four to five times faster than a school-age child.\n- **Equal to puberty** — no: puberty is a distinct acceleration. `14) Puberty.txt` describes the pubertal growth spurt as a separate event, “Mediated by GONADAL STEROIDS (testosterone and estradiol)”, with oestrogen contributing “more than androgens”, adding 15% to adult height, and terminated by the same steroids causing “fusion of epiphyseal growth plates and cessation of growth”. If childhood velocity equalled pubertal velocity there would be no spurt to see, and Tanner staging against height velocity would be meaningless.\n- **Independent of nutrition** — dangerously wrong. Growth in childhood is *less immediately* nutrition-limited than in infancy, because GH dominates and a well child has reserves — but chronic undernutrition stunts, and `16) P.E.M.txt` lists growth failure as the first clinical feature of protein–energy malnutrition, with weight typically affected more than height (`13) Short stature.txt`: “Weight affected > Height → Systemic disease”). Adequate nutrition is a **precondition** for GH to act, not an irrelevance.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Because childhood velocity is low and steady, deviation from it is easy to see and always meaningful. A school-age child crossing centiles downward is abnormal, and this is the phase in which endocrine disease and chronic illness are most often unmasked.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Childhood growth is the slowest, steadiest phase — but it lasts longest and supplies the largest share of final height, so deviation always matters.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.133'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-53', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'In constitutional growth delay, the final adult height is usually:',
  options:['Normal','Less than expected','Below 150 cm','Above average'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Although delayed during adolescence, final adult height is typically within the normal range.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — this is the defining fact of the condition, and the reason it is not a disease.** In constitutional delay of growth and puberty the biological clock is set back but nothing is broken. Because sex steroids are what close the growth plates — `14) Puberty.txt`: “Sex steroids cause fusion of epiphyseal growth plates and cessation of growth” — a child whose puberty is late keeps his epiphyses open for longer and therefore keeps growing after his peers have stopped. The height that appeared to be lost during adolescence is recovered afterwards.\n\n**Two consequences follow, and both are examinable.**\n\n1. **Reassurance is the treatment.** `13) Short stature.txt`, Case 2: “Reassurance ± puberty induction, 3-6 months testosterone injections to stimulate puberty at 13-14 years.” The testosterone is offered for the psychological burden of being smallest and least developed in the year group; it does not increase, and must not be allowed to reduce, final height.\n2. **The mirror image is precocious puberty**, in which early sex-steroid exposure closes the plates early and produces a child who is **tall for age but short as an adult**. That is precisely the loss GnRH agonist therapy is given to prevent.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Less than expected** — this is the outcome of **pathological** short stature: untreated growth hormone deficiency, chronic disease, Turner syndrome, untreated hypothyroidism, severe malnutrition. `13) Short stature.txt` describes GH deficiency treated with “recombinant human GH... Daily subcutaneous injection”, and monitored by “Increase in height and change in height velocity” — treatment exists precisely because final height would otherwise be less than expected. If a child labelled “constitutional delay” ends up shorter than his mid-parental height, the label was wrong.\n- **Below 150 cm** — a specific pathological figure; untreated Turner syndrome is the classic example, which is why `6) Genetically determined disease_.txt` notes growth hormone therapy against “Short stature” in its Turner list. It has nothing to do with a normal variant.\n- **Above average** — no mechanism. The extended growing period recovers the child’s **own** genetic potential; it does not exceed it. Final height in CDGP is expected to fall within the mid-parental range, which is exactly the retrospective test the bank applies elsewhere in this section.\n\n**The one caveat worth stating to a family.** “Normal” means normal *for that family* — a constitutionally delayed boy whose parents are both short will still end up a short adult, because the mid-parental height is the target. What CDGP guarantees is that nothing has been **lost**, not that the outcome will be tall.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** If the bone age is delayed, puberty is late, the labs are normal and the family history fits, tell the family plainly: he will be later than his friends and he will get there. The prognosis is the diagnosis.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Constitutional delay postpones growth without losing it — final adult height is normal and within the mid-parental range.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.135'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-54', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'The infantile growth phase is largely dependent on:',
  options:['Nutrition','Genetics','Hormones','Puberty'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Early postnatal growth is primarily nutrition-dependent.\n\n---\n\n**⚠️ A third option-set variant of one fact.** The endpoint file asks the driver of infantile growth three times in this section over three **different** option lists — a five-option version separating *infant* from *maternal* nutrition, a four-option version testing nutrition against genetic, hormonal and activity factors, and this one. Because the option sets are replaced rather than trimmed, each is held as a separate question by the standard folding test. The fact is one fact; the discriminations differ.\n\n**Expansion — the four phases and their drivers.**\n\n| Phase | Age | Driver | Share of final height |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Fetal | to birth | Placenta, maternal nutrition, uterine environment | **30%** |\n| **Infantile** | **birth to ~2 y** | **NUTRITION** | **15%** |\n| Childhood | ~2 y to puberty | **GH / IGF-1**, thyroid permissive | ~40% |\n| Pubertal | puberty to fusion | **Sex steroids** | **15%** |\n\n(Fetal and infantile percentages from the endpoint file’s own summary slide, `Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf` p.7; the pubertal figure from `14) Puberty.txt`.)\n\n**Why nutrition and not hormones, when hormones are obviously present.** Hormones are present but are not **rate-limiting** in infancy. A newborn with congenital growth hormone deficiency has a normal birth length, and GH-dependent growth only becomes established through the first year or two — which is why `13) Short stature.txt` lists the neonatal clues of GH deficiency as “Hypoglycemia, Prolonged jaundice, microphallus” rather than short length. Thyroid hormone is genuinely essential in this period, both for skeletal maturation and for the brain, which is why newborn screening exists (`52) hypothyrodism.txt`: “Analyzed in central lab. For TSH”) — but it is **permissive**: its absence causes disaster, its presence does not set the rate. What sets the rate is calorie and protein supply.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Genetics** — sets the destination, not the early speed. This is exactly why infants normally **cross centiles** in the first months, migrating from the size the uterus produced toward the size their genes specify. It becomes the visible determinant during the childhood phase.\n- **Hormones** — the childhood and pubertal answer. Offering it for infancy is a phase error, and the bank tests the correct pairing directly elsewhere by keying growth hormone for the childhood phase.\n- **Puberty** — not a determinant of infant growth at all; it is a later phase. (Note the neonatal “mini-puberty” illustrated in `14) Puberty.txt`, a transient postnatal surge of gonadotrophins and sex steroids — real, but it does not drive somatic growth.)\n\n**The clinical translation.** Because infancy is nutrition-driven, growth faltering clusters there, and the first response is a feeding history. `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` calls faltering growth a “Description, not a diagnosis”, present when weight falls “across two centile spaces”, managed by “Dietary and behavioural modification designed to increase energy intake and monitoring growth”, with investigations “guided by history and examination”. The red flag that redirects you away from simple intake is telling: “Failure to gain weight **despite adequate caloric intake**.”\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'The infantile growth phase is nutrition-dependent; hormones are permissive and genes set the destination rather than the rate.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.137'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-55', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'The infantile growth phase accounts for approximately how much of eventual height?',
  options:['40%','30%','20%','15%'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The key is D, 15%, and the endpoint file states it on its own summary slide:** “Infantile phase (**15% of final height**)” (`Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf` p.7). The same slide gives the neighbouring figure that generates every distractor on this list: “Fetal phase (**30% of final height**, Fastest period of Growth)”.\n\n**The whole ledger of final height, phase by phase.**\n\n| Phase | Timing | Share of final height | Driver | Velocity |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Fetal** | conception → birth | **30%** | Placenta, maternal nutrition, uterine environment | **Fastest of life** |\n| **Infantile** | birth → ~2 years | **15%** | **Nutrition** | Fastest postnatal, steeply decelerating |\n| **Childhood** | ~2 years → puberty | ~40% | **GH / IGF-1**, thyroid permissive | Slowest and steadiest |\n| **Pubertal** | puberty → epiphyseal fusion | **15%** | **Sex steroids** | Second peak |\n\nThe pubertal 15% is stated in `14) Puberty.txt`: the growth spurt is “Mediated by GONADAL STEROIDS (testosterone and estradiol)”, with oestrogen contributing “more than androgens”, and it “Add[s] 15% to final adult height”, after which “Sex steroids cause fusion of epiphyseal growth plates and cessation of growth”. The childhood share is the remainder — the largest single block, because although its velocity is the lowest, it lasts eight to ten years *(the arithmetic of the remainder is inference, not a printed figure — not taken from the course material)*.\n\n**Why 15% is a smaller number than it feels like it should be.** In absolute terms infancy is spectacular: a baby grows roughly 25 cm in the first year and about 12 cm in the second, so around half of birth length is added in two years. But *final adult height* is the denominator, and 37 cm of a 170 cm adult is close to a fifth — much of which is offset because the fetal phase has already banked 50 cm, or 30%. The apparent mismatch between the drama of infant growth and its modest share of adult height is exactly why the examiner asks it *(the centimetre figures are filled from general medical knowledge — not taken from the course material)*.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **40%** — closest to the **childhood** share, which is the largest of the four. Choosing it for infancy confuses the phase that grows fastest with the phase that grows longest.\n- **30%** — the **fetal** share, printed on the same slide. This is the trap answer, and it catches anyone who remembers a number without remembering which phase it belongs to.\n- **20%** — no phase carries this value; it is a plausible-looking filler placed between the two real figures.\n\n**Why the shares matter clinically.** They tell you what can still be recovered. An insult in fetal life has already cost part of a fixed 30%, and `13) Short stature.txt` notes that “10% intrauterine growth restriction do not catch-up growth”. An insult in infancy threatens 15% but is the most reversible, because the driver — nutrition — is the one thing a clinician can directly supply. An insult in puberty is the least recoverable, because sex steroids simultaneously drive the spurt and close the plates, so time lost there cannot be regained.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Learn the four percentages as a set — 30 / 15 / ~40 / 15 — because the bank asks for each of them in turn and the distractors are always the other three.\n\nWritten for this bank — Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'The infantile phase contributes about 15% of final height, against 30% fetal and a further 15% at puberty.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.139'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-56', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'What is the most common cause of faltering growth in infants?',
  options:['Inadequate weight gain','Genetic short stature','Growth hormone deficiency','Endocrine disorder'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Insufficient nutrition is the leading cause, rather than endocrine or genetic disorders.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — the file states this on its own summary slide.** “Most common cause of faltering growth: **Inadequate calorie intake**” (`Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf` p.27). The keyed option, “inadequate weight gain”, is that idea expressed as the observation rather than the mechanism, and the point is the same: faltering growth is overwhelmingly a **nutritional** problem, and overwhelmingly a problem of what goes in.\n\n**Why intake dominates.** Infancy is the nutrition-driven phase — the endpoint file’s summary slide gives it 15% of final height, and the driver is what reaches the gut rather than any hormone. So anything that reduces intake shows up on the weight line almost immediately: poor feeding technique or attachment, insufficient breast milk, over-dilute formula, an aversive or frustrating feeding relationship, illness-related anorexia, neglect, or poverty. `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` accordingly gives management as “Dietary and behavioural modification designed to increase energy intake and monitoring growth”, notes that “Simple behaviral advice can improve quantity and quality of nutritional intake”, and insists that “Investigations should be guided by history and examination” — not fired off as a panel.\n\n**The framing that prevents over-investigation.** The same deck opens by calling faltering growth a **“Description, not a diagnosis”**, “present if an infant’s weight falls across two centile spaces”, and closes by repeating that it “is not a disease, but rather a description of a relatively common growth pattern”. Before anything else, “Ensure accurate serial measuring and plotting of height and weight on appropriate growth chart” — and the first question is always whether the plot is even correct (right chart, right age, corrected age in a preterm infant, same scale).\n\n**What moves you off the nutritional answer.** The red-flag list from `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt`:\n\n- Dysmorphic features\n- **Failure to gain weight despite adequate caloric intake**\n- Recurrent vomiting, diarrhoea, or dehydration\n- Organomegaly or lymphadenopathy\n- Signs suggestive of chronic respiratory disease\n- Developmental delay\n\nThe second of these is the pivot: if the calories are genuinely going in and the weight is still not rising, the problem is malabsorption, loss, or increased expenditure, and the differential opens up — coeliac disease, cow’s milk protein enteropathy, cystic fibrosis, chronic infection, a significant cardiac shunt.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Genetic short stature** — familial short stature is a **normal variant** that tracks a low centile without crossing it. It affects **height** in line with the parents, not weight, and it does not produce faltering.\n- **Growth hormone deficiency** — rare, and it is a childhood-phase disorder. `13) Short stature.txt` requires severe short stature more than 3 SD below the mean, height affected **more** than weight, and a growth velocity under 4 cm a year before it is even investigated, and warns “Do not jump to GH tests early !!”. Faltering growth in infancy is weight-led, which is the opposite pattern.\n- **Endocrine disorder** — the general form of the same error. `13) Short stature.txt` gives the discriminator in two lines: “Weight affected > Height → **Systemic disease**; Height affected, Weight normal/↑ → **Endocrine**.” Faltering growth means weight is leading, so it points away from the endocrine column.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Weigh, plot, and take a feeding history. Most faltering infants need a feeding plan and a follow-up appointment, not a blood test.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Faltering growth in infancy is usually inadequate calorie intake — investigate only when a red flag or failure to gain despite adequate intake appears.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.141'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-57', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'A 7-year-old girl presents to your office with her mother noting pubic hair growth over the last month. The girl also has started to develop breast buds. Growth and development are normal. Maternal history is remarkable for pubarche at age 11 years. Past medical history and family history are otherwise unremarkable. Your physical examination reveals genitalia and breast Tanner stage 3. Which of the following laboratory findings is most likely to be found?',
  options:['Decreased estradiol, luteinizing hormone (LH) detectable, normal bone age for age','Decreased estradiol, LH undetectable, normal bone age for age','Elevated estradiol, LH detectable, advanced bone age for age','Elevated estradiol, LH detectable, normal bone age for age','Elevated estradiol, LH undetectable, advanced bone age for age'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Central precocious puberty shows activation of the HPG axis with elevated LH and estradiol, and bone age is typically advanced relative to chronological age.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — the option list is a truth table, and each column must be decided separately.** Every option combines three variables. Work out each one from the clinical picture and only one row survives.\n\n**Column 1 — oestradiol: ELEVATED.** She has breast Tanner stage 3. Breast tissue is an oestrogen end-organ; there is no other way to grow it. Options A and B, which offer *decreased* oestradiol, are refuted by the physical examination alone.\n\n**Column 2 — LH: DETECTABLE.** This is the column that separates central from peripheral precocity, and it is the heart of the question.\n\n| | **Central (gonadotrophin-dependent)** | **Peripheral (gonadotrophin-independent)** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Mechanism | “Early activation of the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis” (`14) Puberty.txt`) | Sex steroid from an adrenal or gonadal source, or exogenous |\n| **LH / FSH** | **Detectable / pubertal** | **Suppressed — undetectable** (negative feedback) |\n| Sequence | Follows the normal pubertal sequence | Out of sequence |\n| Ovaries on US | **Multicystic**, uterus enlarging and pear-shaped | Ovaries quiet, or a unilateral mass |\n| GnRH agonist | **Effective** | Ineffective |\n\nHer development has followed the **normal sequence** — breast buds with pubic hair, developing together, both reaching Tanner 3 — and `14) Puberty.txt` states that central precocious puberty “follows the sequence observed in normal puberty”. That, plus the sex and age, makes central disease overwhelmingly likely: the same deck records that central precocity “occurs 5-10 folds more frequently in girls than in boys because ovaries are very sensitive to gonadotrophin secretion”, with “A high prevalence of idiopathic central precocious puberty... in girls (90%)”. Option E, with **undetectable** LH, describes peripheral disease and is eliminated.\n\n**Column 3 — bone age: ADVANCED.** Sex steroids accelerate skeletal maturation as well as growth. Any child with sustained, genuine sex-steroid exposure — Tanner 3 in both breast and pubic hair — will have a bone age ahead of her chronological age. This is not incidental: it is the mechanism by which untreated precocious puberty **costs adult height**, because `14) Puberty.txt` records that “Sex steroids cause fusion of epiphyseal growth plates and cessation of growth”. Option D, offering elevated oestradiol and detectable LH with a **normal** bone age, is internally inconsistent and is eliminated.\n\n**Only option C survives: elevated oestradiol, detectable LH, advanced bone age.**\n\n**The distractor detail in the stem.** “Maternal history is remarkable for pubarche at age 11 years” invites you to reach for a familial or benign-variant answer. It does not help: 11 is a normal age for pubarche, and it does not make 7 normal. Compare the true benign variant, premature adrenarche, which `14) Puberty.txt` defines as “the appearance of sexual hair before the age of 8 yr in girls or 9 yr in boys **without other evidence of maturation or growth acceleration**” — this girl has breast Tanner 3, so she fails that definition outright.\n\n**What happens next.** `14) Puberty.txt` lists the investigations: pelvic ultrasound (“Infantile: tubular; Pubertal: pear-shaped; Endometrial lining identification”) and “MRI brain with contrast for pituitary gland in gonadotropin-dependent PP” — because although 90% of girls are idiopathic, the 10% that are not include hypothalamic and pituitary lesions. Treatment where progression is rapid is a GnRH agonist, to protect final height.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Central precocious puberty gives elevated oestradiol, detectable LH and an advanced bone age; suppressed LH would mean a peripheral source.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.143'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-58', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'Parents bring their 6-month-old son to see you. He is symmetrically less than the fifth percentile for height, weight, and head circumference on routine growth curves. He was born at 30 weeks of gestation and weighed 1000 g. He was a planned pregnancy, and his mother’s prenatal course was uneventful until an automobile accident initiated the labor. He was ventilated for 3 days in the intensive care unit (ICU), but otherwise did well without ongoing problems. He was discharged at 8 weeks of life. Which of the following is the mostly likely explanation for his small size?',
  options:['Chromosomal abnormality','Protein-calorie malnutrition','Normal ex-premie infant growth','Intestinal malabsorption','Congenital hypothyroidism'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Preterm infants often track below standard growth curves but follow their own percentile pattern; this symmetric low growth is typical for a healthy ex-premature infant without additional pathology.\n\n---\n\n**⚠️ Transcription note.** The stem prints “the mostly likely explanation”; this has been tidied to “the most likely explanation” and nothing else altered.\n\n**Expansion — do the correction before you do the diagnosis.** He was born at 30 weeks, so he is **10 weeks preterm**. At a chronological age of 6 months (about 26 weeks) his **corrected age is roughly 4 months**. Plotted at corrected age on a standard chart he is far less small than he appears, and the vignette explicitly says his growth is **symmetrical** — height, weight and head circumference all together — which is the signature of a small, well-scaled child rather than a sick one.\n\n`13) Short stature.txt` gives the rule: correction for gestational age is “Continued for the first year of life. In extremely preterm infants, until 2 years of age.” `Lec 1 Normal development_.txt` gives the developmental counterpart with a worked example: “The anticipated developmental skills of a 9-month-old baby (chronological age) born 3 months early at 28 weeks’ gestation are more like those of a 6-month-old baby (corrected age)”, and “Correction is not required after age of 2 years.”\n\n**Everything else in the history is reassuring, and each detail was placed deliberately.** The cause of the prematurity was **mechanical** — a road traffic accident initiating labour — not a maternal illness, an infection or a placental problem, so there is no chronic intrauterine insult. The pregnancy was otherwise uneventful. He needed only **3 days** of ventilation and “otherwise did well without ongoing problems”, so he escaped the complications that would predict poor growth: `7) Intro to neonatology.txt` lists chronic lung disease (“Need for oxygen >36 weeks postmenstrual age”), necrotising enterocolitis, intraventricular haemorrhage and periventricular leukomalacia among the frequent hazards of prematurity. He was discharged at 8 weeks, on schedule for his gestation.\n\n**Note also that 1000 g at 30 weeks is appropriate**, not growth-restricted — it is **extremely low birth weight** by the classification in `7) Intro to neonatology.txt` (“LBW - < 2500 grams; VLBW - < 1500 grams; ELBW - < 1000 grams”), which is a statement about *prematurity*, not about being small for dates.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Chromosomal abnormality** — would usually bring dysmorphic features, congenital anomalies or developmental concerns; `13) Short stature.txt` lists “Dysmorphic features” as a specific thing to examine for, and names Turner, Russell–Silver, Down and Noonan among syndromic causes. None is described, and a chromosomal cause does not appear from nowhere in a baby whose prematurity has a mechanical explanation.\n- **Protein-calorie malnutrition** — would give **asymmetrical** failure: weight falling first and furthest, with head circumference spared longest. `16) P.E.M.txt` describes marasmus as weight below 60% of the 50th centile with loss of subcutaneous fat and muscle wasting. Symmetrical smallness across all three parameters is not this.\n- **Intestinal malabsorption** — again weight-led, and would come with stool changes, vomiting or abdominal signs. The history is explicitly free of ongoing problems.\n- **Congenital hypothyroidism** — detected by newborn screening, and by 6 months an untreated case would show coarse facies, a large tongue, an umbilical hernia, constipation, lethargy, a hoarse cry and a wide anterior fontanelle (`52) hypothyrodism.txt`). It also produces the endocrine growth pattern — height affected with weight preserved — not symmetrical smallness with a small head.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** In any small ex-preterm infant, correct the age and re-plot **before** reaching for a diagnosis. Then keep plotting: it is the trend at corrected age, not one point, that tells you whether catch-up is happening.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Plot ex-preterm infants at corrected age; symmetrical smallness in a well ex-premature baby with an uncomplicated course is normal.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.145'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-59', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'A 2-year-old boy had been slightly less than the 50th percentile for weight, height, and head circumference, but in the last 6 months he has fallen to slightly less than the 25th percentile for weight. The pregnancy was normal, his development is as expected, and the family reports no psychosocial problems. The mother says that he is now a finicky eater (wants only macaroni and cheese at all meals), but she insists that he eat a variety of foods. The meals are marked by much frustration for everyone. His examination is normal. Which of the following is the best next step in his care?',
  options:['Sweat chloride testing','Ophthalmologic examination for retinal hemorrhages','Reassurance and counseling for family about normal childhood development','Testing of stool for parasites','Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) of the brain'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Mild faltering due to picky eating in an otherwise healthy child is common; supportive counseling and monitoring are preferred over unnecessary tests.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — measure the fall before you react to it.** He has moved from “slightly less than the 50th” to “slightly less than the 25th” centile for weight. That is a fall of **less than one centile space**, and `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` sets the threshold for faltering growth at a weight falling “**across two centile spaces**”. By the syllabus’s own definition this child does not yet meet the criterion — and his height and head circumference have not moved at all.\n\n**Then check the red flags, and note that all of them are absent.** `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` lists: dysmorphic features; failure to gain weight despite adequate caloric intake; recurrent vomiting, diarrhoea or dehydration; organomegaly or lymphadenopathy; signs suggestive of chronic respiratory disease; developmental delay. He has none. His development is “as expected”, his examination is normal, and the pregnancy was normal.\n\n**What he does have is a classic, age-appropriate feeding conflict.** Food neophobia and fixed food preferences peak in the second and third years, and the frustration described — a mother who “insists that he eat a variety of foods”, with meals “marked by much frustration for everyone” — is the mechanism by which a normal phase becomes a self-sustaining problem. Pressure at mealtimes reduces intake. `15.2) Infant Feeding.txt` addresses precisely this under **active responsive feeding**: it “Allows the child to guide feeding”, “Balances helping with encouraging self-feeding”, “Minimizes distractions during meals and feedings”, “Offers different food combinations, tastes, and textures”, and adds “Children are slow eaters; Do not hurry the infant while eating..... Be patient” and “Do not be frustrated from mealtime mess. children learn out of mess”. `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` makes the same point in one line: “Simple behaviral advice can improve quantity and quality of nutritional intake.”\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Sweat chloride testing** — the test for cystic fibrosis, which would present with weight-led failure **plus** a chest or gut story: recurrent respiratory infections, chronic cough, bulky offensive stools, and usually a much steeper fall. “Signs suggestive of chronic respiratory disease” is a red flag he does not have.\n- **Ophthalmologic examination for retinal hemorrhages** — a test for **abusive head trauma**. The vignette explicitly states “no psychosocial problems” and a normal examination and development. This option exists to see whether an unsupported suspicion of maltreatment can be planted; neglect and abuse are genuine causes of faltering growth, but nothing here points to them.\n- **Testing of stool for parasites** — would be reasonable with diarrhoea, abdominal pain, anaemia or an exposure history. `16) P.E.M.txt` includes “Stools analysis for ova & parasites” in the work-up of established malnutrition — but that is malnutrition, not a well toddler who has dipped one centile band.\n- **MRI of the brain** — reserved for neurological signs, a falling head circumference, developmental regression, or severe isolated growth failure suggesting hypothalamic–pituitary disease. He has a normal head circumference and normal development.\n\n**What “reassurance and counselling” actually means here.** Not dismissal. It means: explain that toddler food refusal is developmental and self-limiting; stop the mealtime battle, because pressure lowers intake; offer a variety without insisting; keep milk within limits so it does not displace solids; **and re-weigh in a defined interval**. `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` requires that you “Ensure accurate serial measuring and plotting of height and weight on appropriate growth chart” — follow-up is part of the plan, not an alternative to it. If the weight then crosses a second centile space, or a red flag appears, the work-up begins.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'A well toddler who dips less than two centile spaces with normal height, head circumference and development needs feeding advice and follow-up, not tests.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.147, box on p.148'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-60', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'An 8-year-old boy has short stature. He has no significant past medical history and his physical examination is normal. The growth curve shows growth parallel to a growth line at the fifth percentile. His father is 5 ft 4 in (163 cm), and his mother is 5 ft 1 in (155 cm). Radiograph of the left wrist shows a bone age equal to the chronological age. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?',
  options:['Reassure the parents that the child has normal prepubertal development.','Order thyroid function tests.','Order chromosomal analysis.','Refer to pediatric endocrinology for evaluation.','Obtain further radiographic imaging.'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Proportionate short stature with growth tracking along a percentile line and normal bone age usually indicates familial short stature. No further testing is required unless growth pattern changes.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — every element needed for the diagnosis has already been supplied, which is why the answer is to stop.**\n\n1. **The growth curve runs *parallel* to the 5th centile.** Parallel means a normal growth velocity. `13) Short stature.txt` defines pathological short stature partly by the opposite — “Serial measurements cross growth centile lines” and “Height velocity <1.5 SD for age and pubertal staging”. Neither applies.\n2. **The bone age equals the chronological age.** This is the decisive investigation and it has already been done. The lecture’s rule is unambiguous: “A. Delayed → Constitutional delay of growth and puberty/ Endocrine. **B. Normal → Familial.**”\n3. **Both parents are short** — 163 cm and 155 cm. His height is concordant with his mid-parental height, which is what familial short stature means.\n4. **History and examination are normal**, so there is no systemic disease, no dysmorphism and no skeletal abnormality among the things the lecture requires you to look for.\n\nTogether these give familial short stature — and `13) Short stature.txt` closes with exactly this message: “**Most short children are normal variants.**”\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Order thyroid function tests** — TSH and fT4 sit in **Step Two** of the investigation ladder and would be entirely correct in a child whose **height was falling while weight was preserved or rising**, with lethargy or constipation (`52) hypothyrodism.txt`; `13) Short stature.txt`: “Height affected, Weight normal/↑ → Endocrine”). Hypothyroidism also **delays** bone age — and this boy’s is normal, which is evidence against it.\n- **Order chromosomal analysis** — the lecture does say “Karyotyping (**All girls**)”, and Turner syndrome is the reason. This patient is a **boy**, and there is no dysmorphism or other feature to suggest a chromosomal disorder.\n- **Refer to pediatric endocrinology for evaluation** — the tempting “safe” answer, and it is wrong for a specific reason: the diagnosis is already made, and referral converts a normal variant into a medical problem in the family’s mind while consuming a clinic slot. `13) Short stature.txt` explicitly discourages escalation, warning “Do not jump to GH tests early !!”, and sets a high bar before growth hormone deficiency is even suspected: “Severe short stature (height >3 SD below the mean); Height affected > Weight; Height >1.5 SD below MPH; Poor growth velocity (< 4cm per year).” The 5th centile is about −1.6 SD, his height is not discordant with his mid-parental height, and his velocity is normal — he meets none of the four.\n- **Obtain further radiographic imaging** — a skeletal survey is for **disproportionate** short stature, where a dysplasia such as achondroplasia is suspected; `13) Short stature.txt` requires that “Proportions (upper/lower segment, arm span)” be checked, and his examination is normal. Repeating or extending the imaging adds radiation and no information.\n\n**What “reassure” must include, so that it is safe.** Explain the diagnosis and the expected adult height from the mid-parental calculation; state plainly that he is healthy; **and arrange to re-measure**, because the one thing that would overturn the diagnosis is a change in the slope. A normal variant is defined by continuing to track, so the follow-up measurement is what makes the reassurance evidence rather than opinion.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Growth parallel to a low centile, short parents and a bone age equal to chronological age is familial short stature — reassure and re-measure, do not refer.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.150, box on p.151'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-61', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'A 16-year-old boy complains that he is the shortest boy in his class. He has a normal past medical history, and although always a bit small for age, he has really noticed that he has fallen behind his peers in the last 2 years. He is Tanner stage 3 and is at the 5th percentile for height. His father began puberty at the age of 16 and completed his growth at the age of 19; he is now 6 ft 2 in (188 cm) tall. His mother began her pubertal development at the age of 10 and had her first menstrual period at the age of 13; her height is 5 ft 4 in (163 cm). Which of the following is the single most appropriate first intervention?',
  options:['Chromosomal analysis','Liver function studies','Measurement of bone age','Measurement of somatomedin C','Pediatric endocrinology referral'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Bone age assessment helps differentiate constitutional delay of growth and puberty from pathological causes and is the first step in evaluating delayed puberty.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — the father’s history is the loudest clue in the vignette.** “His father began puberty at the age of 16 and completed his growth at the age of 19; he is now 6 ft 2 in (188 cm).” That is a textbook description of constitutional delay in the previous generation, followed by an entirely normal — indeed tall — adult height. `13) Short stature.txt` builds its Case 2 on the same feature, a father who was a “late bloomer”, and answers it as constitutional delay of growth and puberty.\n\nThe boy’s own story fits: always a bit small, but only **falling behind in the last 2 years** — which is when his classmates began their pubertal spurts and he did not. He is Tanner stage 3, so he *is* in puberty, simply later than his peers. With a father of 188 cm and a mother of 163 cm his mid-parental height is well above average, so the 5th centile is markedly discordant — the discordance that would be worrying if it were not explained by delay.\n\n**Why bone age is the first test.** It is cheap, quick, single-view, and it answers the only question that matters at this stage. `13) Short stature.txt`: “Most use: Greulich and Pyle; X-ray left wrist and hand. A. Delayed → Constitutional delay of growth and puberty/ Endocrine. B. Normal → Familial.” A delayed bone age in this boy confirms that he has growing time in hand and that his adult height will approach his mid-parental target. A bone age **equal** to his chronological age would be far more concerning, because it would mean the height is not going to be recovered.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Chromosomal analysis** — the lecture instructs “Karyotyping (All girls)”, for Turner syndrome. In a boy the analogous condition would be Klinefelter syndrome (47,XXY), which characteristically produces a **tall** boy with small firm testes, not a short one. Nothing here calls for it.\n- **Liver function studies** — part of the **Step One** systemic screen (“CBC, ESR, Renal functions, Electrolytes, LFT”), appropriate where chronic disease is suspected. His past medical history is normal and he has no systemic features, and a screen ordered without a question in mind mostly generates incidental findings.\n- **Measurement of somatomedin C** — “somatomedin C” is the older name for **IGF-1**. It is a legitimate and useful test, sitting in **Step Two** alongside IGFBP-3, and it is the right test when growth hormone deficiency is genuinely suspected. But GH deficiency requires a poor growth velocity and severe short stature — `13) Short stature.txt` gives the threshold as height more than 3 SD below the mean, height affected more than weight, and velocity under 4 cm a year — and this boy is at the 5th centile and progressing through puberty. The lecture also warns “Do not jump to GH tests early !!”\n- **Pediatric endocrinology referral** — reasonable eventually, but not the “single most appropriate **first** intervention”. The bone age can be obtained in primary care, and in most cases it converts the consultation into reassurance without a referral at all.\n\n**What follows a delayed bone age.** `13) Short stature.txt`, Case 2: “Reassurance ± puberty induction, 3-6 months testosterone injections to stimulate puberty at 13-14 years.” At 16 and already Tanner 3, this boy is progressing on his own; the main intervention is an explanation and a predicted adult height, which for an adolescent worried about being smallest in the class is the treatment he actually needs.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'A bone age of the left wrist and hand is the first test in delayed puberty — a delayed bone age with a late-developing parent means constitutional delay.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.153'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-62', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'You are seeing 10-year-old girl for evaluation of short stature. Which of the following are important to the workup?',
  options:['History and physical including examination of growth chart','Parental height, growth pattern, and timing of puberty','Birth weight and length','Assessment for underlying systemic disease','All of the above'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> A complete evaluation includes history, growth chart review, parental heights, birth history, and assessment for systemic disease to identify potential causes.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — this is `13) Short stature.txt`’s own history-and-examination slide, broken into four options.** The lecture lists under **History**: “Birth size: SGA”, “Growth pattern”, “Nutrition”, “Chronic illness, Drugs”, “Family history of growth and puberty”, and “Mid-parental height calculation”. Under **Examination**: “Accurate height measurement”, “Proportions (upper/lower segment, arm span)”, “Dysmorphic features”, “Skeletal abnormalities”, “Puberty staging”, and “Thyroid signs”. Every option on this list is one of those items, so “All of the above” is not a lazy answer here — it is the syllabus.\n\n**What each element actually decides:**\n\n| Element | The question it answers |\n|---|---|\n| **Growth chart** | Is she **tracking** or **crossing**? Crossing is pathological; tracking is usually a variant. “A single measurement is less important than growth trend.” |\n| **Parental height** | Is she short **for her family**? Mid-parental height turns “short” into “short *relative to potential*”, and short stature is defined partly as “Height >2 SD below mid-parental height” |\n| **Timing of parental puberty** | A late-developing parent supports **constitutional delay**, the second normal variant |\n| **Birth weight and length** | Was she **SGA**? “10% intrauterine growth restriction do not catch-up growth”, so an SGA birth explains lifelong short stature without any postnatal disease |\n| **Systemic disease assessment** | Coeliac disease, chronic renal failure, chronic anaemia, inflammatory bowel disease, cystic fibrosis — all cause the pattern “Weight affected > Height → Systemic disease” |\n\n**And the item the option list does not name but the vignette makes mandatory: she is a girl.** `13) Short stature.txt` places “Karyotyping (**All girls**)” in its investigation ladder, because Turner syndrome is the commonest missed cause of short stature in a girl and mosaic Turner may show “No, or minimal manifestations” — the lecture’s Case 3 is a 7-year-old girl with a bone age delayed 5 years and no major complaints who proved to have mosaic Turner syndrome.\n\n**Why an “All of the above” option is still worth reasoning through rather than guessing.** The examiner is testing whether you know that short stature is diagnosed by **history and examination**, with investigation used only to confirm. The investigation ladder is deliberately staged for that reason: Step One (CBC, ESR, renal functions, electrolytes, LFT), Step Two (TSH and fT4, coeliac screen, IGF-1 and IGFBP-3, bone age, karyotyping in all girls), Step Three (GH provocation testing) — with the instruction “Do not jump to GH tests early !!” written across it. Each of the four options in this question is a step that must be completed **before** the ladder is climbed at all; skipping them is how a normal variant ends up in an endocrine clinic and how a coeliac child ends up with a GH test.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Plot her, measure her parents, ask about their puberty, ask about her birth, and examine her for chronic disease and dysmorphism. In most children that sequence gives the diagnosis; in a girl, add a karyotype regardless.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Short stature is worked up by history and examination first — growth chart, parental heights and puberty timing, birth size, and a systemic disease screen.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.155'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-63', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'A 10-year-old girl is 4 ft 10 in (147 cm) tall. Her father is 5 ft 10 in (178 cm) tall, and her mother is 5 ft 5 in (165 cm) tall. Over the past 3 years she has dropped from the 25th percentile for height to the 5th percentile. Her weight has remained stable. Radiographs show bone age is delayed. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in her management?',
  options:['Send patient to the emergency department for CT evaluation of the brain.','Obtain growth hormone level.','Obtain IGF-1 level.','Start growth hormone injections.','Provide reassurance to parents'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> This child has evidence of possible growth hormone deficiency. Obtaining an IGF-1 level would be the most appropriate initial choice. Growth hormone deficiency is not an emergent condition but the earlier the diagnosis is made the sooner appropriate treatment can be initiated. Obtaining a growth hormone level would not be diagnostically helpful because its secretion is pulsatile. Referral to a pediatric endocrinologist would be warranted if initial screening tests confirm the suspected growth hormone deficiency.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — the box is unusually complete for this bank, and the lecture backs every sentence of it.**\n\n**Why this girl is suspected of GH deficiency.** She has dropped from the 25th to the 5th centile over 3 years while her **weight has remained stable** — the classic endocrine pattern of `13) Short stature.txt`: “Weight affected > Height → Systemic disease; **Height affected, Weight normal/↑ → Endocrine**.” Her parents are of average and above-average height, so she is discordant from her mid-parental height. Her bone age is delayed, consistent with the lecture’s rule that a delayed bone age points to “Constitutional delay of growth and puberty/ **Endocrine**”.\n\n**Why a random GH level is useless — the single most testable fact here.** `13) Short stature.txt` prints it as a heading: “**GH testing: No use of basal GH level !**”, with the reason underneath: “**GH secreted in pulses**”. A random sample is as likely to catch a trough as a peak, so a low value proves nothing and a normal value excludes nothing. Confirmation requires provocation: “GH provocation test confirms diagnosis... GH levels fail to rise after stimulation... **2 GH provocation tests (sequential or on separate days) are required**”, using glucagon, insulin, arginine or clonidine.\n\n**Why IGF-1 is the right screening test.** IGF-1 is the mediator of GH action and, unlike GH, circulates at a **stable** concentration bound to its binding proteins, so a single sample is interpretable. `13) Short stature.txt` places “IGF-1, IGFBP-3” in **Step Two** of the ladder — before Step Three GH testing — and states that in GH deficiency you find “Low levels of IGF-1 and IGFBP-3” together with a “Delayed bone age”. (Note the caveat that makes clinical sense of it: IGF-1 is also low in undernutrition and chronic illness, which is why Step One systemic bloods and the coeliac screen come first.)\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Emergency CT of the brain** — imaging is part of the pathway, but *after* biochemical confirmation and as an MRI, not an emergency CT. `13) Short stature.txt` places it under “Considering potential causes of GHD”: “MRI brain (pituitary view), Fundus: papilledema, Visual field assessment: Visual field loss – bitemporal hemianopia (optic chiasma)”, hunting a craniopharyngioma (“4% – a developmental tumor arising from squamous remnant of Rathke’s pouch... Not truly malignant, but locally invasive”). Slow growth over 3 years is not an emergency; headache with papilloedema would be.\n- **Obtain growth hormone level** — refuted above, and refuted by the lecture in bold.\n- **Start growth hormone injections** — treating before diagnosing. Treatment is “recombinant human GH... Daily subcutaneous injection”, monitored by “Increase in height and change in height velocity”, and it carries real risks: `13) Short stature.txt` lists pseudotumor cerebri, slipped capital femoral epiphysis, worsening of scoliosis, insulin resistance and pancreatitis.\n- **Provide reassurance to parents** — the correct answer for a child **tracking** a low centile with a normal bone age and short parents. This girl is **crossing** centiles with tall parents. Reassurance here would be a missed diagnosis, and the lecture’s point that “Most short children are normal variants” explicitly does not extend to a child whose growth line is falling.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Crossing centiles + stable weight + delayed bone age = screen the GH axis with IGF-1 and IGFBP-3, not with a GH level, and not with a prescription.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Screen the GH axis with IGF-1 and IGFBP-3 — a basal GH level is worthless because GH is secreted in pulses.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.157'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-64', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'A 5-year-old girl has bilateral breast development that was first noticed 6 months ago. She takes no medications, and no source of exogenous estrogen is present in the home. Family history is unremarkable. Physical examination reveals a girl who is at the 50th percentile for height and weight, with normal blood pressure, normal skin without oiliness, Tanner stage II breasts, soft abdomen without palpable masses, no body odor, no pubic/axillary hair, and mild estrogenization of the vagina. Which of the following is the most likely explanation for the child’s breast development?',
  options:['Adrenal tumor','Central precocious puberty','Congenital adrenal hyperplasia','Premature adrenarche','Premature thelarche'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Isolated, mild breast development in young girls without pubic/axillary hair or accelerated growth is benign premature thelarche.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — the examination in this stem is written as a list of *negatives*, and each one closes a door.**\n\n| Finding | What it excludes |\n|---|---|\n| **50th centile for height and weight** — no growth acceleration | Central precocious puberty; any significant sex-steroid excess |\n| **No pubic or axillary hair, no body odour** | Every **androgen**-driven cause: adrenal tumour, congenital adrenal hyperplasia, premature adrenarche |\n| **Normal skin without oiliness** | Androgen excess again (acne, seborrhoea) |\n| **Soft abdomen, no palpable mass** | An ovarian or adrenal tumour |\n| **Normal blood pressure** | Congenital adrenal hyperplasia of the hypertensive variants; adrenal pathology |\n| **No exogenous oestrogen in the home** | Peripheral precocity from an exogenous source |\n| **Tanner II only, mild vaginal oestrogenisation** | Progressive, complete precocious puberty |\n\nWhat remains is **isolated, mild, non-progressive breast tissue in a young girl** — premature thelarche, which `14) Puberty.txt` classifies among the **“Benign incomplete variants”** of premature sexual development.\n\n**The defining feature of an incomplete variant** is that one pubertal sign appears **alone**, without growth acceleration or bone-age advance, because the hormone exposure is minimal or transient. The same deck records the neonatal analogue: “High maternal level of prolactin can cause neoborn babies to be born with breast buds and even to lactate (self-limited)” — breast tissue can be produced by small amounts of oestrogen without any pubertal process.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Adrenal tumor** — an androgen (or occasionally oestrogen) secreting mass. Androgen excess would give pubic and axillary hair, body odour, oily skin and rapid growth, none of which is present; an abdominal mass is explicitly absent.\n- **Central precocious puberty** — would drive the whole axis: progression through the pubertal sequence, a **growth spurt**, an **advanced bone age**, and on ultrasound the changes `14) Puberty.txt` describes — multicystic ovaries and a uterus moving from “Infantile: tubular” to “Pubertal: pear-shaped” with an identifiable endometrial lining. She is on the 50th centile for height with no acceleration, which is strong evidence against it.\n- **Congenital adrenal hyperplasia** — adrenal androgen excess, virilising: pubic hair, clitoromegaly, accelerated growth, markedly advanced bone age, and in the salt-wasting form a neonatal crisis. `14) Puberty.txt` reserves the “ACTH STIMULATION TEST to exclude CAH” for **atypical premature adrenarche**, defined by “marked growth acceleration, clitoral (girls) or phallic (boys) enlargement, cystic acne, advanced bone age (>2 SD above the mean for age)”. She has none of these.\n- **Premature adrenarche** — the *other* benign incomplete variant, and the closest wrong answer. But it is the **hair** variant, not the breast one: `14) Puberty.txt` defines it as “the appearance of sexual hair before the age of 8 yr in girls or 9 yr in boys without other evidence of maturation or growth acceleration”, notes it is “more frequent in girls than in boys”, attributes it to “sensitivity to androgen production by adrenal gland (6-8 yrs)”, allows “a slight increase of growth rate and bone age”, calls it “Self-limited”, and adds the long-term caveat that “Girls have higher risk for polycystic ovarian syndrome later on”. This girl has **no** pubic or axillary hair, so adrenarche is excluded by definition.\n\n**Management.** Observation with re-measurement of height and re-examination in a few months, and a bone age if there is any doubt. What would change the diagnosis is **progression** — growth acceleration, an advancing bone age, or the appearance of pubic hair — at which point she is investigated as central precocious puberty.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Isolated breast development with normal growth, no androgen signs and no mass is premature thelarche — a benign incomplete variant to observe.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.159'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-66', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'What is the first sign of normal puberty in girls?',
  options:['Height acceleration','Pubic hair changes','Menarche','Palpable breast bud','Clitoris growth'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Thelarche is the first sign of puberty in girls, typically around age 9–11. Menarche and pubic hair changes occur later.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — the sequence of female puberty, in order, and why the order is examined.**\n\n| Order | Event | Name | Hormonal driver |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **1** | **Breast bud** (Tanner B2) | **Thelarche** | Ovarian **oestrogen** |\n| 2 | Pubic and then axillary hair | Pubarche / adrenarche | **Adrenal androgens** |\n| 3 | **Peak height velocity** — the growth spurt | — | Gonadal steroids, “Estrogen more than androgens” |\n| 4 | **First menstrual period** | **Menarche** | Established oestrogen cycling |\n| 5 | Regular ovulatory cycles, completion of Tanner V | — | — |\n\nThe hormonal detail is from `14) Puberty.txt`, which states that the pubertal growth spurt is “Mediated by GONADAL STEROIDS (testosterone and estradiol)”, with “Estrogen more than androgens”, adding “15% to final adult height”.\n\n**Two facts make this sequence worth learning rather than memorising as a list.**\n\n1. **Thelarche and pubarche have different sources.** Breast development is **ovarian oestrogen**; pubic hair is **adrenal androgen**. They usually run together, but they are separate axes — which is exactly why the two benign incomplete variants exist as separate entities. `14) Puberty.txt` groups them under “Benign incomplete variants”: premature **thelarche** is isolated breast development, while premature **adrenarche/pubarche** is “the appearance of sexual hair before the age of 8 yr in girls or 9 yr in boys without other evidence of maturation or growth acceleration”.\n2. **The growth spurt comes *before* menarche in girls, and this differs from boys.** A girl who has started her periods has already passed her peak height velocity and has limited growth remaining, because oestrogen has begun closing the epiphyses — `14) Puberty.txt`: “Sex steroids cause fusion of epiphyseal growth plates and cessation of growth.” In boys the spurt comes comparatively later in the sequence, which is part of why they end taller; the same deck notes that “Girls have peak height velocity average 2 years before boys”.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Height acceleration** — real, but it is event number three, after breast budding and pubic hair, and it is much harder to detect clinically than a breast bud unless serial heights are being plotted.\n- **Pubic hair changes** — event number two, and driven by a different axis. Pubic hair *before* breast development, especially before age 8, suggests an androgen problem rather than normal puberty.\n- **Menarche** — the **last** major event, not the first. The bank pairs this question with another in the same section asking for the *last* sign, keyed menarche, so read the stem carefully.\n- **Clitoris growth** — not part of normal female puberty at all. Clitoromegaly is a **virilising** sign and should raise congenital adrenal hyperplasia, an androgen-secreting tumour, or exogenous androgen. `14) Puberty.txt` lists “clitoral (girls) or phallic (boys) enlargement” among the features of **atypical** premature adrenarche that mandate an “ACTH STIMULATION TEST to exclude CAH”.\n\n**The clinical threshold.** Breast budding before **8 years** in a girl is precocious and needs assessment; absence of any pubertal feature by **13 years** is delayed puberty (`14) Puberty.txt`). Between those two ages, watch and reassure.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Female puberty begins with the breast bud (thelarche), then pubic hair, then the growth spurt, with menarche last.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.163'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-67', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'During which period of human growth does faltering growth typically occur?',
  options:['Fetal phase','Infant phase','Childhood phase','Prepubertal phase','Pubertal phase'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Faltering growth is most commonly seen in infancy when nutrition and feeding problems are most likely.\n\n---\n\n**⚠️ A four-option version of this same question also appears in this section** (Infantile / Childhood / Pubertal / Fetal), with the same key. Because this printing **replaces** the option set — adding a prepubertal phase and rewording the others — the two are held as separate questions by the standard folding test.\n\n**Expansion — “faltering growth” is a term with a specific meaning, and that meaning is confined to infancy.** `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` defines it in two lines: it is a “**Description, not a diagnosis**”, and it is “present if an **infant’s weight** falls across two centile spaces.” The definition names the infant, and it names weight.\n\n**Three reasons infancy is the vulnerable phase:**\n\n1. **Growth is fastest postnatally and the nutritional demand is greatest** — the endpoint file’s own summary slide assigns the infantile phase 15% of final height (`Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf` p.7), packed into two years.\n2. **The driver of growth in this phase is nutrition itself.** Any shortfall in intake or absorption translates straight onto the weight line, with no hormonal buffer.\n3. **The infant depends entirely on a carer**, and the feeding method changes twice in the first year — `15.2) Infant Feeding.txt` gives complementary feeding “after 6 months”, then “6m Pureed, mashed and semisolid; 8m Finger foods; 12m Family foods”. Each transition is a chance for intake to fall, and weaning coincides with rising exposure to enteric infection as maternal antibody wanes.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Fetal phase** — growth restriction certainly occurs in utero, but it is called **IUGR / SGA**, not faltering growth. `7) Intro to neonatology.txt` classifies it as asymmetrical (“Head spare”, onset after the 18th week, chronic hypertension, severe malnutrition) or symmetrical (“Proportionate to the rest of the body”, TORCH, chromosomal anomalies, anaemia, substance abuse), and `13) Short stature.txt` adds the outcome: “10% intrauterine growth restriction do not catch-up growth”.\n- **Childhood phase** — growth is at its slowest and steadiest here and is GH-driven, and the child feeds herself. Growth failure in this phase is less common but **more significant**: it is where endocrine disease and chronic illness declare themselves, and a falling centile after school entry always warrants investigation.\n- **Prepubertal phase** — the tail of childhood; the same reasoning applies, and there is no particular vulnerability.\n- **Pubertal phase** — demand is high during the spurt and severe undernutrition can delay puberty and blunt it, but the adolescent controls her own intake and has reserves that an infant does not.\n\n**What to do with a faltering infant.** `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt`: “Ensure accurate serial measuring and plotting of height and weight on appropriate growth chart”; “Investigations should be guided by history and examination”; “Dietary and behavioural modification designed to increase energy intake and monitoring growth”; and “Simple behaviral advice can improve quantity and quality of nutritional intake”. Its red flags — dysmorphic features, failure to gain weight despite adequate caloric intake, recurrent vomiting, diarrhoea or dehydration, organomegaly or lymphadenopathy, signs of chronic respiratory disease, developmental delay — are what shift the diagnosis away from simple intake.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Faltering growth is by definition an infant phenomenon — a weight falling across two centile spaces, usually from inadequate intake.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.165'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-70', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'perinatal',
  stem:'Which problem is commonly associated with large for gestational age infants?',
  options:['Hyperglycemia','Hypercalcemia','Anemia','Birth asphyxia','Heart failure'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> LGA infants, often of diabetic mothers, are prone to shoulder dystocia, birth trauma, and birth asphyxia.\n\n---\n\n**⚠️ Filing note.** This is a **perinatal / neonatal** question printed inside the Growth and Puberty section. It is filed under `perinatal` because it concerns delivery and the transition at birth; the secondary chapter is **neonatal**.\n\n**⚠️ Note the deliberate inversion in option A.** The infant of a diabetic mother is **HYPOglycaemic**, not hyperglycaemic — and it is the single most tested fact about these babies. The mechanism is in `7) Intro to neonatology.txt`: the aetiology of the IDM picture is “Maternal Hyperglycemia, Fetal Hyperglycemia & Hyperinsulinemia.” Maternal glucose crosses the placenta; maternal insulin does not. The fetus therefore makes its **own** insulin in large amounts, and insulin is a growth factor — hence macrosomia. At delivery the cord is cut, the maternal glucose supply stops, but the baby’s hyperinsulinaemia persists for hours to days, and the blood glucose falls. Option A states the maternal problem and attributes it to the baby.\n\n**Why birth asphyxia is the answer.** A macrosomic fetus is mechanically difficult to deliver. Shoulder dystocia, a prolonged second stage, instrumental delivery and birth trauma all follow from size, and each can interrupt gas exchange during delivery. The syllabus treats birth asphyxia as a major topic in its own right (`12) HIE.txt`) and lists it among the causes in `9) Respiratory distress in newborn.txt` and `58) cerebral palsy.txt`.\n\n**The full IDM problem list, verbatim from `7) Intro to neonatology.txt`:**\n\n1. Fetal demise\n2. **Macrosomia**\n3. Congenital malformations\n4. RDS\n5. Hyperbilirubinaemia\n6. **Hypoglycaemia**\n7. Hypocalcaemia\n8. Polycythaemia\n9. Renal vein thrombosis\n10. Cardiomyopathy\n11. Congenital malformation: sacral hypoplasia and small left colon syndrome\n\nThe deck adds a slide on “HYPERTROPHIC CARDIOMYOPATHY IN IDM”, which is the one mechanism by which an LGA infant of a diabetic mother genuinely can develop cardiac compromise — septal hypertrophy from fetal hyperinsulinaemia, causing outflow obstruction, and usually resolving over weeks to months as the insulin drive falls away *(the natural history is filled from general medical knowledge — not taken from the course material)*.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Hyperglycemia** — inverted, as above. Expect **hypo**glycaemia, and screen for it in the first hours.\n- **Hypercalcemia** — again inverted: the IDM list gives **hypocalcaemia**.\n- **Anemia** — the opposite. These infants are prone to **polycythaemia**, driven by chronic relative fetal hypoxia and raised erythropoietin, which in turn feeds the hyperbilirubinaemia on the same list.\n- **Heart failure** — hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is real, but it is usually asymptomatic or causes an outflow murmur; frank heart failure is uncommon, and it is not the problem “commonly associated” with LGA babies as a group.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** For an LGA baby: anticipate a difficult delivery with a skilled resuscitator present, then check the **blood glucose** early and repeatedly, and watch for jaundice, polycythaemia and a murmur.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'LGA infants risk shoulder dystocia and birth asphyxia at delivery, then hypoglycaemia — never hyperglycaemia — hypocalcaemia and polycythaemia afterwards.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.171'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-75', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'Which is a character of "constitutional growth delay"?',
  options:['It is a pathological condition.','It occurs only in girls.','There is a lower final height.','There is a delayed onset of puberty.','It presents early in infancy.'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Constitutional delay is benign, with normal final height but delayed puberty.\n\n---\n\n**⚠️ Not a reprint, despite the near-identical stem.** The endpoint file asks a very similar question twice elsewhere in this section, but there the option set is different (“Adequate bone age / More common in girls / Final height is affected / A delayed onset of puberty / Presentation is in infancy”). Here the options have been **replaced** — only the key text survives, and the distractors have been rewritten as absolute statements. By the standard folding test a replaced option set makes a new question, so both are held. The discriminations differ too: this version tests *pathological vs variant* and *only in girls* rather than the bone age.\n\n**Expansion — take each option as a statement to be judged true or false.**\n\n| Option | Verdict | Reason |\n|---|---|---|\n| “It is a pathological condition.” | **False** | `13) Short stature.txt` summary: “**Most short children are normal variants.**” CDGP is one of the two variants, alongside familial short stature |\n| “It occurs only in girls.” | **False** | It is commoner in **boys** — and “only” makes an already wrong statement absolute |\n| “There is a lower final height.” | **False** | Growth is **postponed, not lost**. Final height falls within the mid-parental range |\n| **“There is a delayed onset of puberty.”** | **TRUE** | The condition is *constitutional delay of growth **and puberty*** |\n| “It presents early in infancy.” | **False** | It declares itself in **late childhood and adolescence**, when peers begin their spurt |\n\n**Note the examiner’s technique.** Three of the four wrong options are **absolutes** — “only”, “pathological”, “lower”. Absolute statements about a benign variant are almost always false, and spotting that pattern is a reliable route through this style of question even before the content is recalled.\n\n**Why the final height is preserved — the mechanism, in one line.** Sex steroids fuse the epiphyses (`14) Puberty.txt`: “Sex steroids cause fusion of epiphyseal growth plates and cessation of growth”), so a child whose sex-steroid exposure begins late keeps his growth plates open longer and grows for longer. The lecture’s Case 2 shows the whole picture: a 13-year-old boy with no puberty, a father who was a “late bloomer”, Tanner stage 1, a bone age delayed 2 years and **normal labs**, diagnosed as constitutional delay of growth and puberty and managed with “Reassurance ± puberty induction, 3-6 months testosterone injections to stimulate puberty at 13-14 years.”\n\n**The one safeguard against calling it benign too soon.** A delayed bone age is shared with endocrine disease: “A. Delayed → Constitutional delay of growth and puberty/ **Endocrine**.” So screening comes first — Step One systemic bloods, Step Two TSH and fT4, coeliac screen, IGF-1 and IGFBP-3, bone age, and **karyotyping in all girls**, because mosaic Turner syndrome may show “No, or minimal manifestations”, as the lecture’s Case 3 demonstrates.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Constitutional delay is a variant, commoner in boys, presenting in adolescence, with a delayed bone age, delayed puberty, and a normal adult height — but the label is earned by excluding disease, not assumed.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Constitutional delay is a benign variant, commoner in boys, defined by delayed puberty, and it does not reduce final adult height.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.181'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-79', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gi-abdopain',
  stem:'An 8-year-old girl presents with a 6-month history of pain most days around the umbilicus which lasts for an hour and responds to paracetamol syrup. It does not occur at weekends usually. She opens her bowels daily and passes a soft stool. She is thriving with her weight and height on the 75th centile. Which of the following options is the appropriate next step?',
  options:['Abdominal radiograph','Referral to a pediatric gastroenterologist','Liver function tests','Reassurance and no further investigation','Prescription for laxatives'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> The girl’s pain is functional abdominal pain since growth and examination are normal, and symptoms stop on weekends. No further tests are needed.\n\n---\n\n**⚠️ Filing note.** This is a **gastroenterology** question printed inside the Growth and Puberty section of the endpoint file and twice more inside its Gastroenterology section. It is filed under `gi-abdopain` with the rest of the abdominal pain questions; the growth data in the stem are there as evidence of normality rather than as the subject.\n\n**Expansion — `24)Abdominal pain.txt` gives the exact table this question is built on.**\n\n| Clinical feature | **Organic** | **Functional** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Site of pain** | **Localized** | **Vague midline** |\n| **Timing of pain** | May be **nocturnal** | **Never nocturnal** |\n| **Alarm signals** | **Present** | **Absent** |\n| Family history | IBD, acid peptic disease | FGIDs, anxiety, headache |\n\nEvery row favours a functional diagnosis in this girl. The pain is **periumbilical** — the definition of vague and midline; nothing suggests it wakes her; and no alarm signal is present.\n\n**The alarm signals, from the same deck, so you can confirm their absence deliberately rather than by impression.** From the history: age under 5 years; nocturnal symptoms; persistent right upper or right lower quadrant pain; referred pain; **weight loss or growth retardation**; chronic, bile- or blood-stained vomiting; dysphagia; prolonged, mucoid or bloody diarrhoea; dysuria, haematuria or flank pain; constitutional or extraintestinal symptoms; a family history of organic GI disease. On examination: an abnormal physical examination; **growth deceleration, delayed puberty**; jaundice; significant anaemia; rebound, guarding or organomegaly; perianal disease (tags, fissures, fistulas). She has **none** — and the two that are growth-related are precisely why the stem tells you she is “thriving with her weight and height on the 75th centile”.\n\n**The “not at weekends” clue.** Symptoms that remit when school does are the classic signature of a functional disorder with a psychosocial trigger. `24)Abdominal pain.txt` sets functional gastrointestinal disorders in the ROME IV framework and notes the associated pattern of “Excess healthcare visits” and “Pain behavior”, with a family history of FGIDs, anxiety and headache. The pain is entirely real; what is absent is organic disease.\n\n**The epidemiology that makes reassurance the high-probability answer.** Chronic abdominal pain is “defined as chronic or recurrent if at least one episode of pain occurs per month for two consecutive months and is severe enough to interfere with routine functioning”, and it “occurs in **10-15 %** of children”.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Abdominal radiograph** — no diagnostic role here; it exposes a child to radiation and typically produces an unhelpful comment on faecal loading that starts an unnecessary treatment.\n- **Referral to a paediatric gastroenterologist** — the diagnosis is already made in the clinic. Referral without alarm features reinforces the family’s belief that something is being missed, which perpetuates the pain behaviour the deck warns about.\n- **Liver function tests** — no jaundice, no hepatomegaly, no relevant symptom. `24)Abdominal pain.txt` cautions that investigation “should be done only when indicated according to the suspected organic disease”, that “Relevant abnormalities were found in less than 1 %”, and that ultrasound in particular “will also identify **incidental findings** such as mesenteric lymphadenitis, which may not need any intervention” — the harm of testing without a question.\n- **Prescription for laxatives** — she opens her bowels daily and passes a **soft** stool. There is no constipation to treat.\n\n**What good reassurance contains.** Name the diagnosis (functional abdominal pain), affirm the pain is real, explain the gut–brain mechanism, identify the school-related trigger, agree a plan for normal school attendance, and arrange review — with a clear statement of what would change the plan (weight loss, nocturnal pain, vomiting, blood, or a change in the pattern). Note that functional pain is formally a “Diagnosis of Exclusion”, but exclusion here is achieved by a careful history and examination, not by a battery of tests.\n\n**⚠️ Within-bank reprint — pp.457 and 534.** The endpoint file prints this vignette three times: here on p.189 inside its Growth and Puberty section, and twice more inside Gastroenterology. It is held once in the app. p.534 reprints p.189 word for word. p.457 rewords the stem, keeps the same key, and prints its own shorter box:\n\n> Chronic periumbilical pain in a thriving child with normal bowel habits and no red flags suggests functional abdominal pain. It is common and does not require further investigation if there are no alarm features.\n\n**The three printings disagree in exactly one place.** p.189 and p.534 print the third option as “Liver function tests”; p.457 prints it as “Inflammatory markers and liver function tests”. The option list above is p.189’s, the page this entry cites.\n\n**Which is worth a moment, because of where the wording comes from.** The House bank prints this same vignette (`pedhd-gastro-22`) with p.189’s stem word for word but with p.457’s options — “paediatric” and the inflammatory-markers wording alike. The endpoint file is therefore assembling this question from two upstream printings rather than simply reprinting itself, which is the same seam visible elsewhere in its Gastroenterology section.\n\n**The key is untouched by any of it.** “Reassurance and no further investigation” is the answer in all three endpoint printings and in House, and the argument against the third option holds whichever wording it carries: inflammatory markers and liver enzymes are both organic-disease screens, and it is the absence of alarm signals — not the choice of test — that makes ordering one unjustified.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Vague periumbilical pain, never nocturnal, sparing weekends, with normal growth and no alarm signals is functional abdominal pain — reassure without investigating.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.189 (the same vignette is printed again on p.534 word for word, and on p.457 reworded and with one option worded differently; all three carry the same key)'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-85', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'Which of the following is the last sign of puberty in girls?',
  options:['Breast development','Pubic hair growth','Menarche','Axillary hair growth'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Menarche is typically the last major event of puberty after breast and pubic hair development.\n\n---\n\n**Expansion — the same sequence as the “first sign” question, read from the other end.** The endpoint file asks both in this section, so learn the order once and answer either:\n\n| Order | Event | Name | Driver |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **1** | Breast bud | **Thelarche** | Ovarian **oestrogen** |\n| 2 | Pubic hair | Pubarche | **Adrenal androgens** |\n| 3 | Axillary hair | — | Adrenal androgens |\n| 4 | Peak height velocity — the growth spurt | — | Gonadal steroids, “Estrogen more than androgens” |\n| **5** | **First menstrual period** | **Menarche** | Established oestrogen cycling |\n\n**Why menarche comes last, mechanistically.** Menstruation requires a uterus that has been oestrogenised for long enough to build an endometrium, and an axis mature enough to cycle. `14) Puberty.txt` describes the ultrasound correlate — the uterus moves from “Infantile: tubular” to “Pubertal: pear-shaped”, with “Endometrial lining identification” — a structural change that takes a sustained period of oestrogen exposure. Breast tissue, by contrast, responds to much smaller and briefer amounts, which is why premature thelarche exists as a benign variant while premature menarche does not have a comparably common benign counterpart.\n\n**The single most useful clinical corollary.** **Menarche marks the end of most of a girl’s growing.** The growth spurt precedes it, and by the time periods begin the epiphyses are already closing under the influence of oestrogen — `14) Puberty.txt`: “Sex steroids cause fusion of epiphyseal growth plates and cessation of growth.” Typically only a few more centimetres remain *(the residual growth figure is filled from general medical knowledge — not taken from the course material)*. So:\n\n- A short girl who has **not** yet menstruated still has growth to work with, and intervention may help.\n- A short girl who is already **two years post-menarche** has little height left to influence, which changes both the urgency and the realistic goals of treatment.\n- **Precocious puberty** brings menarche forward and therefore closes the plates early — which is why an untreated child is tall for age and short as an adult, and why GnRH agonist therapy is aimed at preserving final height.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.** *Breast development* is the **first** sign. *Pubic hair growth* is second and comes from a different axis (adrenal androgen rather than ovarian oestrogen), which is why premature adrenarche and premature thelarche are separate benign variants in `14) Puberty.txt`. *Axillary hair growth* follows pubic hair but still precedes menarche.\n\n**The thresholds to carry with the sequence.** Any pubertal sign before **8 years** in a girl is precocious and needs assessment; no pubertal feature by **13 years** is delayed puberty (`14) Puberty.txt`: “Failure of development of any pubertal feature by 13 yrs of age in females or by 14 yrs of age in males”).\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Menarche is the last major pubertal event in girls and follows the growth spurt — so little growth remains after periods begin.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.201'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-87', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'Which of the following conditions may cause macrocephaly?',
  options:['Chronic subdural hematoma','Cerebral palsy','Congenital infection','Hypoglycemia','Perinatal asphyxia'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The key is A, chronic subdural haematoma, and the endpoint file grounds it on its own summary slide:** “Macrocephaly ... Causes: ... **Raised ICP (hydrocephalus, subdural hematoma, brain tumor)**” (`Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf` p.20).\n\n**The discrimination being tested is macrocephaly against microcephaly, and four of the five options belong to the other column.**\n\n| | **Macrocephaly** | **Microcephaly** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Mechanism | Something inside the skull has **increased** — brain, CSF, or blood | The brain has **failed to grow** or has been **injured** |\n| Causes | **Hydrocephalus**, **chronic subdural haematoma**, **brain tumour** (all raising ICP); benign familial macrocephaly; some metabolic and storage disorders | **Congenital infection (TORCH)**, **perinatal asphyxia / HIE**, severe prenatal insults, genetic and metabolic syndromes, and any severe early brain injury — the same injuries that produce **cerebral palsy** |\n\nThe macrocephaly column is from the endpoint file’s summary slide; the microcephaly column is grounded in the syllabus by `1) Normal development.txt`, which lists **MICROCEPHALY** and **SEVERE HYDROCEPHALUS** among the conditions recognisable from the face in a child with intellectual disability, and in `12) HIE.txt` and `58) cerebral palsy.txt`, which cover perinatal asphyxia and its consequences.\n\n**Why a chronic subdural haematoma enlarges the head.** In infancy the sutures are open and the fontanelles unfused, so the skull is a **compliant** container. A slowly accumulating extra-axial collection is accommodated by the head expanding rather than by immediate herniation — which is why chronic subdurals in infants present as **a head circumference crossing centiles upward**, with a full fontanelle, prominent scalp veins, splayed sutures, vomiting, irritability, developmental regression, and in advanced cases the sun-setting sign *(the sign list is filled from general medical knowledge — not taken from the course material; the endpoint file names the cause, not the presentation)*.\n\n**And the safeguarding point that must always accompany it.** A chronic subdural haematoma in an infant with no adequate explanation is a **classic presentation of abusive head trauma**, and the finding mandates a full evaluation — skeletal survey, retinal examination for haemorrhages, and a child-protection assessment. This is the single most consequential fact attached to this question *(not taken from the course material)*.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Cerebral palsy** — a disorder of movement and posture from a **non-progressive** insult to the developing brain. Where the insult was destructive, head growth is **impaired**: acquired microcephaly is common in severe spastic quadriplegic CP. It does not enlarge the head.\n- **Congenital infection** — the TORCH group. Cytomegalovirus and toxoplasmosis characteristically cause **microcephaly** with intracranial calcification. (The exception proves the rule: congenital toxoplasmosis can cause obstructive **hydrocephalus** and therefore a large head — but the option as written, “congenital infection” in the abstract, is a microcephaly answer, and the bank’s own list keys the subdural.)\n- **Hypoglycemia** — a metabolic emergency, and severe or recurrent neonatal hypoglycaemia injures the brain (`10) new Seizures . Birth injuries , Hpoglycemia 2.txt`; `7) Intro to neonatology.txt` lists it among the metabolic problems of prematurity, from decreased glycogen stores). Injury reduces subsequent brain growth; it does not increase head size.\n- **Perinatal asphyxia** — the cause of hypoxic-ischaemic encephalopathy (`12) HIE.txt`) and a leading cause of cerebral palsy. Its late consequence is **microcephaly**, not macrocephaly. Note that cerebral **oedema** in the acute phase can transiently increase intracranial volume, but that is measured in days, not as a growing head circumference.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Plot the head circumference at every infant visit alongside weight and length. It reflects brain growth (`Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf` p.8: “Head circumference (OFC) ... Reflects brain growth”), and a measurement **crossing centiles in either direction** is a neurological finding requiring explanation — upward, think raised intracranial pressure and image the head; downward, think brain injury or failure of brain growth.\n\nWritten for this bank — Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Macrocephaly means increased intracranial contents — hydrocephalus, subdural haematoma or tumour; asphyxia, congenital infection and cerebral palsy cause microcephaly.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.205'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-88', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'growth-puberty',
  stem:'A 5-year-old girl is brought to the clinic because her parents are concerned about her short stature. Her height is below the 3rd percentile, and her weight is in the 25th percentile. Her growth velocity is normal. A bone age assessment reveals that her bone age is consistent with her chronological age. What is the most appropriate next step in management?',
  options:['Initiate growth hormone therapy','Order a karyotype analysis','Monitor growth every 6 months','Refer to a pediatric endocrinologist','Test for celiac disease'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> The child has proportionate short stature with normal bone age and velocity, suggesting a benign variant. Close monitoring is appropriate.\n\n---\n\n**⚠️ Note a tension with the lecture, which is worth knowing before an examination.** `13) Short stature.txt` lists **“Karyotyping (All girls)”** in its investigation ladder for short stature — an unqualified instruction, and Turner syndrome is the reason: `6) Genetically determined disease_.txt` describes it as “a chromosomal monosomy syndrome of short female with sexual infantilism”, and the lecture’s own Case 3 is a 7-year-old girl with “No major complaints” who proved to have **mosaic Turner syndrome**, in which there may be “No, or minimal manifestations” because “Only a small percentage of cells have a chromosomal abnormality 45,XO”. By that instruction, option B would be defensible in this girl. The bank keys monitoring because **both** her growth velocity **and** her bone age are normal, which is the pattern of a benign variant. The key is left as printed; the discrepancy is recorded here so that a learner who answered “karyotype” knows the lecture supports them, and so that in clinical practice the karyotype is not forgotten in a short girl.\n\n**Why monitoring is nonetheless a sound answer on the stem as written.**\n\n1. **Normal growth velocity.** She is short but not *becoming* shorter. `13) Short stature.txt` defines pathological short stature partly by the opposite — “Serial measurements cross growth centile lines” and “Height velocity <1.5 SD for age and pubertal staging”.\n2. **Bone age equal to chronological age.** The lecture’s rule: “A. Delayed → Constitutional delay of growth and puberty/ Endocrine. **B. Normal → Familial.**” A normal bone age argues against both constitutional delay and endocrine disease, including hypothyroidism and GH deficiency, both of which delay skeletal maturation.\n3. **Weight above height (25th vs <3rd), but growth is not decelerating.** Height affected more than weight is nominally the endocrine pattern — “Height affected, Weight normal/↑ → Endocrine” — which is precisely why this stem is not trivially benign, and why the normal velocity and normal bone age have to be doing the work.\n\n**Why each wrong option is wrong.**\n\n- **Initiate growth hormone therapy** — treating without a diagnosis. `13) Short stature.txt` sets the bar for even suspecting GH deficiency (“Severe short stature (height >3 SD below the mean); Height affected > Weight; Height >1.5 SD below MPH; Poor growth velocity (< 4cm per year)”), requires two provocation tests to confirm it, and lists real harms of treatment: pseudotumor cerebri, slipped capital femoral epiphysis, worsening of scoliosis, insulin resistance, pancreatitis.\n- **Order a karyotype analysis** — see the note above. It is the option with the strongest claim to be also correct, and in practice it should be sent in a short girl.\n- **Refer to a pediatric endocrinologist** — premature. The screening that would justify referral has not shown anything abnormal, and the lecture warns “Do not jump to GH tests early !!”\n- **Test for celiac disease** — the coeliac screen sits in Step Two and is the right test when the **weight** line falls faster than the height line, or with gut symptoms. Her weight is comfortably above her height centile, which is the opposite pattern.\n\n**What monitoring must actually mean.** Not “do nothing”. Re-measure height accurately at defined intervals, plot on the correct chart, calculate the growth **velocity** between visits, calculate and record the **mid-parental height** so that her centile can be judged against her family, and re-examine for pubertal timing. `13) Short stature.txt`: “A single measurement is less important than growth trend.” If the velocity falls, or she does not enter puberty on time, the work-up restarts.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Short stature with a normal growth velocity and a bone age equal to chronological age is a benign variant to monitor — though the lecture still asks for a karyotype in every short girl.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.207'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gp-89', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition',
  stem:'Which of the following should be avoided in an infant’s diet during the first year of life?',
  options:['Pureed vegetables','Honey','Mashed fruits','Iron-fortified cereals'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Honey can cause infant botulism and must be avoided before 12 months.\n\n---\n\n**⚠️ Filing note.** This is a **nutrition** question, printed as the last item of the Growth and Puberty section. It is filed under `nutrition`.\n\n**Expansion — infant botulism, since the box states the conclusion without the mechanism.** Honey can contain **spores** of *Clostridium botulinum*. In an older child or adult the spores pass harmlessly through a mature gut whose established flora and acidity prevent them germinating. In an infant under 12 months the colonic flora is not yet established, so the spores **germinate in the gut, colonise it, and produce botulinum toxin in situ** — this is a *toxico-infection*, not food poisoning by pre-formed toxin. The toxin blocks acetylcholine release at the neuromuscular junction, giving a **descending, symmetrical flaccid paralysis**: constipation is characteristically the first sign, then poor feeding, a weak cry, a lost head control, ptosis, sluggish pupils, and hypotonia — the classic “floppy baby” — with respiratory failure in severe cases. Treatment is supportive care with ventilation as needed plus human-derived botulism immune globulin; antibiotics are avoided because bacterial lysis releases more toxin. Prevention is simply the rule in the key: **no honey before 12 months**, in any form, including on a dummy or in cooking, since spores survive normal cooking temperatures. *(This entire paragraph is filled from general medical knowledge — none of the cached Pediatrics lecture files covers infant botulism; `56) Floppy infant .txt` addresses the floppy infant without naming it.)*\n\n**Why each of the other three options is not merely harmless but actively recommended.** `15.2) Infant Feeding.txt` sets out complementary feeding from **6 months**, with texture graded by developmental stage:\n\n| Age | Texture (from the lecture) |\n|---|---|\n| **6 months** | “Pureed, mashed and semisolid” |\n| **8 months** | “Finger foods” |\n| **12 months** | “Family foods” |\n\n- **Pureed vegetables** — exactly the 6-month texture. The same deck adds a practical warning: “Do not use mixer/ blender in preparation of vegetable foods, it transforms it into a liquid form unsuitable for training the baby to swallow semisolid foods. Better use strainer.”\n- **Mashed fruits** — likewise the 6-month texture.\n- **Iron-fortified cereals** — appropriate and important. The lecture stresses that “Nutritional gaps starts to appear from 6 months”, which is why complementary feeding begins then; iron is the classic gap, because fetal iron stores are exhausted around that age and breast milk is low in iron *(the iron-store rationale is not taken from the course material)*. The deck also requires **variety** — “Eating a wide selection of foods within and among the major food groups” — and that foods “must be prepared, served, and stored safely”, with the instruction to “**Avoid Foods that may cause choking or allergy**”.\n\n**Two other first-year rules worth carrying alongside honey** *(not taken from the course material)*: **whole cow’s milk should not be the main drink before 12 months**, because of its low iron content and the risk of occult gastrointestinal blood loss; and **choking hazards** — whole nuts, whole grapes, popcorn, hard raw vegetable pieces — should be avoided or modified in shape, which is the specific content of the deck’s own instruction to avoid foods that may cause choking.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** From 6 months, offer a wide variety of appropriately textured foods including iron-rich ones, and give **no honey until the first birthday**.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Honey must be avoided in the first year because C. botulinum spores germinate in the immature gut and cause infant botulism.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.209'
},

{
  id:'pedep-nut-1', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-vit',
  image:'q-pd-ep-238', imgAlt:'Clinical photograph of a child\'s forearm and wrist beside a plain radiograph of the same wrist, labelled (a) and (b).',
  stem:'Sunit, a 13-month-old boy, presents with faltering growth. He is still entirely breastfed. On examination, he is miserable and his wrist appears abnormal, as shown in the figure. An X-ray of his wrist is also provided. What is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Vitamin A deficiency','Vitamin B1 deficiency','Vitamin D deficiency','Vitamin E deficiency','Vitamin K deficiency'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Clinical signs such as widened wrists, irritability, and poor growth in a breastfed infant are classic for rickets due to vitamin D deficiency.\n\n---\n\n**Reading the two panels.** `17) Vitamin D Defficiency Rickets(1).txt` names the wrist X-ray as the single best early investigation for rickets, showing broadening, cupping and fraying of the lower radius and ulna as the uncalcified osteoid piles up at the metaphysis. On this crop the clinical photo (a) shows the wrist visibly widened, and the radiograph (b) is soft enough that only the coarse pattern reads reliably: an irregular, widened metaphyseal contour where a sharp, crisp growth-plate line should be — not fine cupping or fraying detail, which the resolution here cannot support.\n\n**Why the story points to vitamin D, not the other three deficiencies on the ladder.** An exclusively breastfed 13-month-old is the textbook risk profile: breast milk carries only around 30 IU of vitamin D per litre (`Vitamin deficiency  Rickets.txt`), so an infant who has never had sun exposure or a supplement runs down his stores through the first year. Faltering growth and irritability are the lecture\'s own early features of rickets, and epiphyseal widening at the wrist (Marfan sign) is the classic bony sign once it becomes established.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Vitamin A deficiency* causes night blindness, xerophthalmia and skin changes, not a wrist deformity. *Vitamin B1 (thiamine)* deficiency is beriberi — polyneuropathy, oedema and heart failure. *Vitamin E* deficiency causes haemolysis and neurological signs (ataxia, areflexia), mainly in preterm infants. *Vitamin K* deficiency causes bleeding, not bone disease — that is the diagnosis tested a few pages later in this same bank (`pedep-nut-8`), where the clinical picture is haemorrhage, not a widened wrist.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'An exclusively breastfed infant with wrist widening and faltering growth has vitamin D deficiency rickets until proved otherwise.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.238'
},

{
  id:'pedep-nut-2', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition',
  stem:'Sarah, a 9-year-old girl, is referred by the school nurse to the paediatric clinic because of her weight. She weighs 43 kg (98th centile) and is 141 cm tall (91st centile). She has followed her height centiles over the last 9 months, but her weight centile has increased. Her body mass index is at the 97th centile. Her mother reports that she hardly eats and, when she does, has a very healthy diet. Which of the following statements is most likely to be correct?',
  options:['A calorie-restricted diet is the treatment of choice','Sarah\'s adrenocortical axis should be checked to rule out Cushing syndrome','Sarah has a higher risk of an abnormal lipid profile and raised blood pressure in adult life','Sarah is obese'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Sarah is overweight [Body mass index (BMI) > 91st centile] rather than obese (BMI > 98th centile). It places her at increased risk of an abnormal lipid profile and raised blood pressure in adult life unless action is taken\n\n---\n\n**A cut-off worth flagging.** `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` defines the bands differently from the box above — obese as BMI above the 95th centile and overweight as 85th–95th. This bank uses the UK convention (overweight 91st–98th, obese above 98th) rather than the cached deck\'s figures *(the 91st/98th cut-offs themselves are not taken from the course material)*. The two schemes disagree on where the line falls, but Sarah\'s own BMI (97th) sits below either "obese" threshold, so she is overweight on both readings — the key does not move.\n\n**Why C is right.** Overweight and obese children carry a well-established later-life risk of dyslipidaemia and hypertension, which is exactly what the box states; this is the point the question is testing, not the label itself.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Calorie restriction* is not first-line management in a growing 9-year-old — restrictive dieting in childhood risks impairing linear growth and can precipitate disordered eating; the approach is a balanced diet and activity, not calorie counting. *Checking for Cushing syndrome* is not indicated: `13) Short stature.txt`\'s discriminator is that systemic and nutritional causes of obesity raise weight while height keeps climbing normally — "Obese + Tall" — whereas endocrine causes blunt height ("Obese + Short/poor height velocity"). Sarah\'s height has tracked its own centile for 9 months, so there is no height-faltering to explain. *"Sarah is obese"* fails by the bank\'s own printed cut-off, since her BMI (97th) sits below the 98th-centile line it draws for obesity.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Overweight (BMI 91st–98th centile) already raises the adult risk of dyslipidaemia and hypertension; a normal height trajectory argues against an endocrine cause.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.240'
},

{
  id:'pedep-nut-3', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition',
  stem:'Which of the following term newborns has the lowest risk of cardiovascular disease in later life?',
  options:['1.8 kg','2.1 kg','2.4 kg','3.9 kg','4.6 kg'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Both low birth weight and high birth weight are associated with higher cardiovascular risk. Birth weight around 3.9 kg is optimal.\n\n---\n\n**The relationship between birthweight and adult cardiovascular disease is U-shaped, not linear** — *not taken from the course material; none of the cached nutrition decks teaches a birthweight–cardiovascular-risk relationship at all.* Both tails of the birthweight distribution carry excess risk: low birthweight is linked to the "thrifty phenotype" (fetal undernutrition programming permanent metabolic adaptations that predispose to insulin resistance and hypertension), and high birthweight (macrosomia, often from maternal diabetes or obesity) is linked to childhood and adult obesity and its own cardiometabolic consequences. A weight in the mid-to-upper normal range for a term infant carries the lowest risk of either extreme.\n\n**Why each option ranks as it does.** 1.8 kg and 2.1 kg are frankly low birthweight for a term infant and sit at the undernourished end of the curve. 2.4 kg is borderline low. 4.6 kg is macrosomic and sits at the other extreme. 3.9 kg is a normal, comfortably mid-range term weight — neither growth-restricted nor macrosomic — which is why the box calls it optimal.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Both low and high birthweight carry excess adult cardiovascular risk; a normal mid-range term weight is lowest-risk.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.242'
},

{
  id:'pedep-nut-4', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-feed',
  stem:'A mother asks you whether there are any disadvantages to breastfeeding. Although you would prefer to inform her about the many advantages of breastfeeding, you wish to answer her question honestly. Which of the following is most likely to be a true potential disadvantage?',
  options:['Breastfeeding will reduce her chance of having more children','The absence of cow\'s milk protein in breast milk increases the risk that the child will develop a milk allergy when weaning','The higher interferon level in breast milk increases the risk of severe bronchiolitis in children who develop respiratory syncytial virus infection','The lower vitamin K concentration in breast milk can result in life-threatening bleeding'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Breast milk has low vitamin K, putting exclusively breastfed infants at risk of VKDB (vitamin K deficiency bleeding) without supplementation.\n\n---\n\n**Why D is the genuine disadvantage.** Vitamin D and vitamin K are the two nutrients breast milk is consistently short on. Without the routine intramuscular vitamin K given at birth, an exclusively breastfed infant can develop haemorrhagic disease of the newborn (vitamin K deficiency bleeding) — a real, occasionally fatal risk this bank tests again at `pedep-nut-8`.\n\n**Why each other option is not a genuine disadvantage.** *Reduced fertility* — lactational amenorrhoea is a real physiological effect, but it is a variably reliable side-effect, not a health disadvantage being asked about here, and it is not the "true potential disadvantage" the question is steering toward. *Milk allergy risk from the absence of cow\'s milk protein* reverses the evidence: `15.2) Infant Feeding.txt` lists breastfeeding as reducing, not raising, allergic and infective disease; early exposure to allergens is what is now thought to lower allergy risk, not the reverse, and breast milk itself is protective rather than sensitising. *Higher interferon increasing RSV bronchiolitis risk* also reverses the evidence: the same deck lists breastfeeding as reducing the risk of respiratory infection, including bronchiolitis, through its immunological components (IgA, active lymphocytes), not raising it.\n\nThis question also straddles `nutrition-vit`, since the key turns on a specific vitamin deficiency rather than feeding practice in general.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Breast milk is low in vitamin K, so an exclusively breastfed infant without vitamin K prophylaxis is at risk of vitamin K deficiency bleeding.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.244'
},

{
  id:'pedep-nut-5', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-pem',
  stem:'Anil is a 2.5-year-old boy who lives in India and attends the local health clinic near their village for a routine check-up. Both his parents are subsistence farmers. He is asymptomatic. On examination, he is very thin, but his hair and skin appear normal, and there is no oedema or other clinical abnormalities. His height is on the 5th centile, but his weight is well below the 0.4th centile (z-score between -2 and -3 below the median).What is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Kwashiorkor','Marasmus','Normal child','Rickets','Severe gastro-oesophageal reflux'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Marasmus is characterized by severe wasting without edema. It results from chronic calorie deficiency.\n\n---\n\n**Why marasmus fits.** `16) P.E.M.txt` defines marasmus as a form of protein-energy malnutrition, usually in the first three years of life, with growth failure, loss of subcutaneous fat, muscle wasting, no oedema, no dermatosis and normal or only mildly changed hair — every one of those negatives is present here (normal hair and skin, no oedema). A weight z-score of -2 to -3 with a much less affected height centile is the anthropometric signature of a predominantly wasting, calorie-deficient process rather than a chronic stunting one — subsistence farming without a reported acute illness is a plausible chronic under-nutrition context.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Kwashiorkor* requires oedema, and usually hair and skin changes and hepatomegaly — all explicitly absent here. *Normal child* is excluded by a weight two to three standard deviations below the median, which is frank wasting, not a constitutional variant (contrast with `pedep-nut-11`, where the corresponding child has no such deficit). *Rickets* is a bone disease from vitamin D deficiency, not a wasting syndrome, and no bony signs are described. *Severe gastro-oesophageal reflux* could cause poor weight gain but would usually come with a history of vomiting or feeding difficulty, which this vignette does not give.\n\n**A near-identical vignette reappears later in this bank** as `pedep-nut-23` — same rural farming family, same thin child with normal hair and skin and no oedema, but the name and country are dropped and the weight centile is given as "below the 3rd" rather than "below the 0.4th". The key is the same (marasmus) in both.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Severe wasting with normal hair, skin and no oedema in a chronically under-fed toddler is marasmus, not kwashiorkor.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.246'
},

{
  id:'pedep-nut-6', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition',
  stem:'Harry is a 13-year-old boy who attends the paediatric clinic because of obesity. His height is on the 98th centile and his weight is above the 99.6th centile. Which of the following is least likely to be associated with obesity?',
  options:['Asthma','Hypertension','Low self-esteem','Slipped upper femoral epiphysis','Type 1 diabetes'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Obesity is more associated with type 2 diabetes. Type 1 diabetes is an autoimmune disease and not typically linked to obesity.\n\n---\n\n**Why type 1 diabetes is the odd one out.** Type 1 diabetes is caused by autoimmune destruction of pancreatic beta cells and its incidence is not driven by adiposity, unlike type 2 diabetes, where insulin resistance from excess fat mass is central to pathogenesis. Harry\'s pattern — tall as well as heavy (height 98th centile, weight >99.6th) — is also the box\'s own signature of simple nutritional obesity rather than an endocrine cause, matching `13) Short stature.txt`\'s rule that nutritional obesity presents as "Obese + Tall".\n\n**Why each other option is a real association.** *Asthma* — obesity is a recognised risk factor for asthma and for harder-to-control disease, through mechanical and inflammatory mechanisms. *Hypertension* — excess weight raises blood pressure directly, the same risk named in `pedep-nut-2`\'s box. *Low self-esteem* — psychosocial morbidity, including poor self-esteem and bullying, is a well-documented consequence of childhood obesity. *Slipped upper femoral epiphysis (SUFE)* is mechanically driven by excess load across a still-open growth plate in an adolescent, and obesity is its best-established risk factor *(the SUFE association specifically is not taken from the course material, which does not name it among the obesity comorbidities)*.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Obesity is associated with type 2, not type 1, diabetes — type 1 is autoimmune and its incidence is not driven by adiposity.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.248'
},

{
  id:'pedep-nut-7', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-vit',
  stem:'Ahmed is an 18-month-old Pakistani boy who was born in the UK with a weight of 3.2 kg. He is on a mixed diet. His height is on the 10th centile and his weight is on the 0.4th centile. He is noted to be miserable. On examination, his wrists feel wider than normal. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Vitamin D deficiency','Vitamin K deficiency','Kwashiorkor','Marasmus','Cow\'s milk protein allergy'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Features such as wide wrists, poor growth, and irritability in a young child suggest rickets due to vitamin D deficiency.\n\n---\n\n**This five-way ladder repeats across four vignettes in this bank** (`pedep-nut-7`, `pedep-nut-8`, `pedep-nut-9`, `pedep-nut-10`); each keys on one clinical picture out of the five. The comparison, written once here:\n\n| Diagnosis | The clue that picks it |\n|---|---|\n| **Vitamin D deficiency** (this entry) | Wide wrists (metaphyseal widening) + irritability + poor growth |\n| **Vitamin K deficiency** (`pedep-nut-8`) | Severe bleeding/shock in a newborn born at home, no vitamin K prophylaxis |\n| **Kwashiorkor** (`pedep-nut-9`) | Oedema + distended abdomen + hair changes on a protein-poor (maize) diet |\n| **Marasmus** (`pedep-nut-10`\'s neighbours, e.g. `pedep-nut-5`) | Severe wasting, no oedema, normal hair and skin |\n| **Cow\'s milk protein allergy** (`pedep-nut-10`) | Diarrhoea + eczema + faltering growth after a switch from breast to formula |\n\n**Why this vignette is vitamin D deficiency specifically.** An 18-month-old on a mixed diet, dark-skinned (higher melanin reduces cutaneous vitamin D synthesis for a given sun exposure — general physiology, consistent with `17) Vitamin D Defficiency Rickets(1).txt`\'s point that dark skin raises risk), with wide wrists (epiphyseal widening, Marfan sign) and irritability, is the textbook rachitic picture; low weight-for-age reflects the faltering growth that accompanies established rickets.\n\n**Why the other four fail here.** There is no bleeding (against vitamin K deficiency), no oedema or hair/skin change (against kwashiorkor), no described severe wasting with normal skeleton (against marasmus, whose sign is soft tissue loss, not bone deformity), and no diarrhoea, eczema or history of a formula switch (against cow\'s milk protein allergy).\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Wide wrists with irritability and faltering growth in a young child on a mixed diet is vitamin D deficiency rickets.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.250'
},

{
  id:'pedep-nut-8', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-vit',
  stem:'Harry is a 3-week-old infant who has been exclusively breastfed by his mother. He was born at home as his mother wanted “everything to be natural” and declined all interventions. His birthweight was 3.4 kg. He presents to the hospital with severe rectal bleeding and shock. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Vitamin D deficiency','Vitamin K deficiency','Kwashiorkor','Marasmus','Cow\'s milk protein allergy'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Newborns are at risk of Vitamin K deficiency bleeding (VKDB), especially if they have not received intramuscular Vitamin K at birth. This infant\'s exclusive breastfeeding and home delivery without prophylaxis increase the risk. VKDB can present with severe bleeding, including GI hemorrhage and shock.\n\n---\n\nThis question shares its five-option ladder with `pedep-nut-7`, `pedep-nut-9` and `pedep-nut-10` — the full comparison is written there. Harry\'s own discriminating token is the combination of a **home birth that declined the routine vitamin K injection** with **severe GI bleeding and shock at 3 weeks**: classic late-onset vitamin K deficiency bleeding, which is disproportionately severe (it can be intracranial as well as GI) precisely because no prophylaxis was ever given. Nothing here suggests bone disease, oedema, wasting or an allergic/GI intolerance picture, which is why the other four options on the shared ladder do not fit.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'GI bleeding and shock in a breastfed neonate who never received vitamin K prophylaxis is vitamin K deficiency bleeding.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.252'
},

{
  id:'pedep-nut-9', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-pem',
  stem:'Jonas is an 18-month-old Black African boy in KwaZulu-Natal, South Africa. He was born weighing 3.2 kg. He was breastfed until 9 months of age when his sibling was born. He now mainly eats traditional maize-based porridge, which is grown on the family farm. His weight is just below the 0.4th centile. He looks thin but has a distended abdomen. There is oedema around his eyes and feet. His hair has a red tinge. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Vitamin D deficiency','Vitamin K deficiency','Kwashiorkor','Marasmus','Cow\'s milk protein allergy'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Kwashiorkor is a form of protein-energy malnutrition associated with a protein-deficient diet (like maize). Classic features include edema, hepatomegaly, growth failure, and hypopigmented hair.\n\n---\n\nThis question shares its five-option ladder with `pedep-nut-7`, `pedep-nut-8` and `pedep-nut-10` — the full comparison is written there. Jonas\'s own discriminating token is the triad `16) P.E.M.txt` lists as "always present" in kwashiorkor: growth failure, oedema, and disturbed muscle/fat ratio, plus a "usually present" feature, hair colour change — here a red tinge, the depigmentation the lecture describes as "dry, uncurled, loss of luster... discolored". Weaning onto a maize porridge that is calorie-adequate but protein-poor after his sibling displaced him from the breast is exactly the classic kwashiorkor trigger the lecture names: a diet "persistently deficient in essential proteins" in the weaning and post-weaning period. The abdominal distension reflects hepatomegaly and hypotonic gut wall, not the flat wasting of marasmus.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Oedema, a distended (hepatomegalic) abdomen and discoloured hair on a protein-poor weaning diet is kwashiorkor.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.254'
},

{
  id:'pedep-nut-10', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-feed',
  stem:'Jamie is a 5-month-old male infant who was born with a weight of 3.5 kg (25th centile). He was initially breastfed and was growing well. His mother developed mastitis, so he was changed to formula milk. He now weighs 5.0 kg (<0.4th centile). He has frequent loose stools and eczema. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Vitamin D deficiency','Vitamin K deficiency','Kwashiorkor','Marasmus','Cow\'s milk protein allergy'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Cow\'s milk protein allergy can present with GI symptoms (diarrhea, poor weight gain), skin manifestations (eczema), and failure to thrive after starting formula.\n\n---\n\nThis question shares its five-option ladder with `pedep-nut-7`, `pedep-nut-8` and `pedep-nut-9` — the full comparison is written there. Jamie\'s own discriminating token is the timing: he was **growing well on breast milk** and only develops loose stools, eczema and a fall from the 25th to below the 0.4th centile **after the switch to formula**, which is the standard cow\'s milk protein allergy story — a temporal link between introducing cow\'s milk protein and the onset of GI and skin symptoms. There is no oedema (against kwashiorkor), no bony sign (against vitamin D deficiency), no bleeding (against vitamin K deficiency), and the weight loss is too acute and too tightly linked to a dietary change to be the chronic wasting of marasmus.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Diarrhoea, eczema and faltering growth appearing after a switch from breast milk to formula is cow\'s milk protein allergy.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.256'
},

{
  id:'pedep-nut-11', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition',
  stem:'Tanya, an 11-month-old Caucasian girl, is being monitored by her health visitor. Her birthweight was 2.4 kg (0.4th centile), and she has remained on the 2nd centile, now weighing 7.0 kg. Her mother is on the 5th centile and her father is on the 40th centile for height. She is well, has a good appetite, and has never needed to visit her doctor. She has no abnormal signs on examination, and her development is normal.Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Cystic fibrosis','Obesity','Vitamin C deficiency','Marasmus','Normal'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> This is a case of constitutional (familial) small stature. The child is growing steadily along her centile, is healthy and developing normally. No signs of disease are present.\n\n---\n\n**Why "Normal" is right.** `13) Short stature.txt`\'s familial short stature discriminator is a small, low-centile parent and a child who tracks her own centile steadily with a normal bone age — exactly Tanya\'s picture: a low-centile mother, a birthweight already on the 0.4th centile, and a girl who has held her own centile (a mild upward drift from 0.4th toward the 2nd, which is still within a stable band) with good appetite, normal development and no abnormal findings. A well child growing steadily along a low-but-consistent centile with no red flags is not disease.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Cystic fibrosis* would be expected to bring recurrent chest or GI symptoms (steatorrhoea, poor appetite), none of which are present. *Obesity* is the opposite problem — Tanya is small, not overweight. *Vitamin C deficiency* (scurvy) causes irritability, bleeding gums and purpura, none described here. *Marasmus* is severe wasting from calorie deficiency with a falling or badly depressed weight-for-age; Tanya\'s weight is holding its own low centile, not crossing down, and she has a good appetite and no illness — the pattern is constitutional smallness, not malnutrition (contrast `pedep-nut-5`, where the weight is 2–3 SD below the median and the appetite/history is not reassuring).\n\nThis question also straddles `growth-puberty`, since the discriminator is a growth-monitoring pattern rather than a nutritional intake problem.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'A well child tracking a low, stable centile with a small parent and no red flags is constitutional small stature, not disease.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.258'
},

{
  id:'pedep-nut-12', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-vit',
  stem:'A 3-year-old boy of Asian origin presents to his GP accompanied by his father. He has a 6-month history of bilateral leg pains and occasionally finds it difficult to walk and has to rest. On examination, he has bowing of his legs but otherwise appears developmentally normal. His father mentions that his son is also due to see the dentist for “bad teeth.” What is the most appropriate initial blood test to aid in diagnosis?',
  options:['Bone profile','Liver function','Full blood count','C-reactive protein','Renal function'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> The presentation suggests rickets, likely due to vitamin D deficiency. A bone profile (calcium, phosphate, alkaline phosphatase) is the initial investigation to assess mineral metabolism.\n\n---\n\n**Why bone profile is right.** Bowed legs, leg pain, and delayed/defective teeth in an Asian child (higher risk due to reduced cutaneous vitamin D synthesis and dietary pattern) is a classic rickets presentation. `17) Vitamin D Defficiency Rickets(1).txt` lists the biochemical picture as usually normal or low serum calcium, low phosphate, and raised alkaline phosphatase — the three tests a bone profile bundles together — making it the single most efficient first blood test.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Liver function* is relevant only if defective 25-hydroxylation from liver disease were suspected, which is a second-line cause, not the initial test. *Full blood count* would help if anaemia or infection were suspected but does not address the bone question being asked. *C-reactive protein* screens for inflammation/infection, irrelevant to a 6-month history of leg pain and bowing with no fever mentioned. *Renal function* matters for chronic renal disease as a cause of rickets (renal osteodystrophy), but again that is a cause to consider only after the bone profile confirms a rachitic biochemical picture, not the first test to order.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Bowed legs, leg pain and dental defects in a child point to rickets; a bone profile (Ca, phosphate, ALP) is the first-line blood test.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.260'
},

{
  id:'pedep-nut-13', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-feed',
  stem:'Breastfeeding is best for babies, and there are very few contraindications. Which of the following is an absolute contraindication to breastfeeding in the UK?',
  options:['Galactosaemia','Phenylketonuria','Neonatal jaundice','Cleft palate','ABO incompatibility'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Classic galactosaemia is the only absolute contraindication to breastfeeding because affected infants cannot metabolize galactose, which can lead to severe complications.\n\n---\n\n**Why galactosaemia is absolute** *(the breastfeeding-specific reasoning below is not taken from the course material — `6) Genetically determined disease_.txt` names galactosaemia only in a list of autosomal recessive disorders and does not cover its management)*: classic galactosaemia is a deficiency of galactose-1-phosphate uridyltransferase, so any exposure to galactose — present in both breast milk and standard cow\'s-milk formula lactose — causes toxic accumulation, liver failure, cataracts and sepsis risk. The infant must be fed a galactose-free (soy or specialised) formula from birth, which is why it is the one truly absolute breastfeeding contraindication on this list.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Phenylketonuria* needs careful monitoring and usually a mix of breast milk with a phenylalanine-free formula under dietitian supervision — restricted, not absolutely excluded. *Neonatal jaundice*, including breastfeeding-associated and breast-milk jaundice, is usually managed by continuing and optimising breastfeeding, not stopping it (see `pedep-nut-30`). *Cleft palate* makes latching mechanically harder and may need feeding aids or expressed milk, but it does not forbid breast milk itself. *ABO incompatibility* causes mild neonatal jaundice from haemolysis but is not a reason to withhold breastfeeding.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Classic galactosaemia is the one absolute contraindication to breastfeeding; the other listed conditions only modify or complicate it.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.262'
},

{
  id:'pedep-nut-14', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition',
  stem:'A 6-week-old baby is referred by the health visitor as she has only just regained her birth weight. She is being fed with formula milk, and her stools are normal. On examination, she appears thin but otherwise normal. She is admitted to the ward and fed by the nursing staff for a week as her mother is readmitted with an infection. She demonstrates excellent weight gain during this period. What is the most likely cause for her initial poor weight gain?',
  options:['Cow\'s milk protein intolerance','Gastro-oesophageal reflux','Cystic fibrosis','Inadequate intake/neglect','Urinary tract infection'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> The excellent weight gain when fed by staff indicates that poor weight gain was due to insufficient intake rather than medical conditions like reflux, cow\'s milk intolerance, or CF.\n\n---\n\n**Why the ward trial is the whole answer.** `15.2) Infant Feeding.txt` gives good weight gain as 20–30 g/day and lists inadequate weight gain among the criteria of insufficient feeding; here the baby has barely regained her birth weight by 6 weeks (organic disease would not simply resolve with a change of feeder), and then gains excellently the moment someone else takes over feeding. That is a controlled experiment: same milk, same baby, different feeder, different outcome — which isolates the problem to how she was being fed at home (technique, volume, or a wider psychosocial/neglect context, especially with the mother\'s own concurrent illness) rather than to anything intrinsic to the baby or the formula.\n\n**Why each organic distractor fails the same test.** *Cow\'s milk protein intolerance* and *gastro-oesophageal reflux* would still limit intake and weight gain regardless of who is feeding her — normal stools also argue against CMPI. *Cystic fibrosis* causes malabsorption and steatorrhoea that would not resolve simply because staff took over feeding, and her stools are described as normal. *Urinary tract infection* causes poor feeding through acute illness, not a chronic pattern that reverses instantly with a feeder change.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'When weight gain corrects the moment feeding technique/supervision changes, the cause was inadequate intake, not organic disease.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.264'
},

{
  id:'pedep-nut-15', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-pem',
  stem:'Which of the following not a finding in Kwashiorkor?',
  options:['hyperglycemia','muscle wasting','distributed muscle: fat ratio','dermatosis','edema'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Kwashiorkor is characterized by edema, dermatosis, and muscle wasting with disproportionate fat retention, but hyperglycemia is not typical.\n\n---\n\n**Why hyperglycaemia is the odd one out.** `16) P.E.M.txt` lists growth failure, oedema, disturbed muscle/fat ratio and psychic changes as always present, with hair changes, dermatosis and hepatomegaly usually present — hyperglycaemia is not among them. Chronic protein-energy malnutrition drives hypoglycaemia if anything (it is listed among the life-threatening complications requiring emergency correction in the same lecture), because calorie intake is inadequate and glycogen reserves are depleted, not excessive.\n\n**Why each other option is a real feature of kwashiorkor.** *Muscle wasting* and a *disturbed (redistributed) muscle-to-fat ratio* are named directly in the lecture as always-present findings — fat is relatively preserved while muscle is lost, unlike the global wasting of marasmus. *Dermatosis* — the flaky, hyperpigmented/depigmented skin changes — is a usually-present feature. *Oedema* is the defining always-present sign that separates kwashiorkor from marasmus on the Wellcome classification.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Kwashiorkor is marked by oedema, dermatosis and a disturbed muscle:fat ratio, not by hyperglycaemia — malnutrition drives hypoglycaemia instead.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.266'
},

{
  id:'pedep-nut-16', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-pem',
  stem:'Edema in kwashiorkor is mainly due to:',
  options:['Proteinuria','Hepatomegaly','Elevated serum sodium','low protein intake','Protein loss in stool'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Protein deficiency reduces plasma oncotic pressure, leading to fluid leakage into interstitial spaces and resulting in edema.\n\n---\n\n**Why low protein intake is the mechanism.** Plasma proteins, chiefly albumin, hold water inside blood vessels by generating oncotic (colloid osmotic) pressure. When dietary protein intake is chronically inadequate — the defining feature of kwashiorkor — hepatic albumin synthesis falls, oncotic pressure drops, and fluid shifts out of the vasculature into the interstitial space, producing the characteristic pitting oedema. `16) P.E.M.txt` names serum albumin as a specific investigation "in Kwashiorkor" for exactly this reason.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Proteinuria* and *protein loss in stool* would also lower plasma protein, but neither is the actual mechanism in kwashiorkor — the deficit is inadequate protein intake, not abnormal protein loss through the kidney or gut. *Hepatomegaly* is a consequence of kwashiorkor (fatty infiltration of the liver from impaired lipoprotein synthesis to export fat), not a cause of the oedema. *Elevated serum sodium* is not a feature of kwashiorkor at all; if anything, dilutional hyponatraemia can occur as fluid shifts.\n\n**⚠️ Within-bank reprint — p.366.** The endpoint file asks this question twice, once as a sentence completion and once as a direct question. The five options are the same, in the same order, with the same key; the only differences are that p.366 capitalises the key option and prints the last distractor as “stools” rather than “stool”. It is held once in the app, under the p.268 printing.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Kwashiorkor oedema results from low protein intake lowering plasma oncotic pressure, not from renal or GI protein loss.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.268 (the same question is printed again on p.366 as “What is the main cause of edema in kwashiorkor?”, with the same five options in the same order and the same key; two options differ only in casing and in stool/stools)'
},

{
  id:'pedep-nut-17', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-vit',
  stem:'You are counseling a mother of nutrition to her children, she is very interested in the roles that vitamins play in health. Which is appropriate advice?',
  options:['Vit C deficiency can lead to petechiae, and mucosal bleeding','Vit k is only important in synthesis of RBCs','Vit E deficiency leads to decrease clot degeneration','Vit B12 deficiency is common in infants','Folic acid has no role in blood production'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Vitamin C is essential for collagen synthesis; deficiency leads to scurvy, causing bleeding gums and petechiae.\n\n---\n\n**Why A is correct.** `Vitamin deficiency  Rickets.txt`\'s vitamin table lists scurvy\'s features as "Irritability, Purpura, Bleeding gums, Periosteal hemorrhages, Bone aches" — vitamin C is needed for collagen synthesis in vessel walls, and its deficiency causes fragile capillaries that bleed into skin (petechiae/purpura) and mucosa (gums).\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *"Vitamin K is only important in synthesis of RBCs"* is wrong on the mechanism — vitamin K carboxylates clotting factors (II, VII, IX, X), not red cells; its deficiency causes bleeding through failed coagulation, not anaemia. *"Vitamin E deficiency leads to decreased clot degeneration"* inverts the real picture: vitamin E is an antioxidant, and its deficiency causes haemolysis (especially in preterm infants), ataxia and areflexia — nothing to do with clot breakdown. *"Vitamin B12 deficiency is common in infants"* is false in general — B12 deficiency is uncommon in infancy except when the mother herself is deficient (e.g. strict vegan diet) and the infant is exclusively breastfed; it is not a routine risk the way vitamin D or K deficiency are. *"Folic acid has no role in blood production"* is the reverse of the truth — the same table lists folic acid\'s role as DNA synthesis and its deficiency as megaloblastic anaemia.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Vitamin C deficiency (scurvy) causes petechiae and mucosal bleeding through impaired collagen synthesis in vessel walls.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.270'
},

{
  id:'pedep-nut-18', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-vit',
  stem:'An 11th presented to the emergency room with convulsions. He had a history of diarrhea 3 days and was afebrile. His mother described that he was and had no past history of convulsions. Examination revealed large head wide anterior fontanelle, prominent costochondral junctions and broad wrists. His eyes and skin pinch were normal and rectal temperature was 37.8 c. What is the emergency room initial treatment?',
  options:['ORS','Antipyretic drug and cold fomentations','low sodium I.V fluids','IV Ceftriaxone','I.V calcium gluconate'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> The child\'s convulsions are due to low calcium from rickets, indicated by skeletal signs. Emergency treatment is IV calcium to rapidly correct hypocalcemia.\n\n---\n\n**The printed vignette is defective in the source itself.** It was re-rendered at 200 dpi to rule out a scan misread, and the printed text really does read "An 11th presented to the emergency room" and "His mother described that he was and had no past history of convulsions." Read clinically, it must mean something close to: an 11-month-old infant presented with convulsions, a 3-day history of diarrhoea, no fever, and no past history of convulsions — the physical findings (large head, wide anterior fontanelle, prominent costochondral junctions/rickety rosary, broad wrists) then make sense as a child with established rickets.\n\n**Why IV calcium gluconate is right.** `17) Vitamin D Defficiency Rickets(1).txt` lists hypocalcaemic tetany as a neurological complication of rickets, and `Vitamin deficiency  Rickets.txt`\'s management for manifest tetany/convulsions is emergency intravenous calcium gluconate (5–10 mL of 10% solution slowly over 10 minutes) — the skeletal signs (wide fontanelle, rachitic rosary, broad wrists) point straight to rickets-driven hypocalcaemia as the cause of the seizure, so correcting the calcium is the emergency treatment, not just symptomatic seizure control.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *ORS* treats dehydration from the diarrhoea but does nothing for an active seizure from hypocalcaemia, and the exam finds a normal skin pinch (not dehydrated). *Antipyretics and cold fomentations* address fever, but the child is explicitly afebrile (37.8°C is not a fever) and this is not a febrile convulsion. *Low-sodium IV fluids* has no role here and risks worsening cerebral oedema in a seizing child without an indication. *IV Ceftriaxone* would be appropriate if sepsis or meningitis were suspected, but there is no fever and the skeletal findings point to a metabolic, not infective, cause.\n\nThis question also straddles `neurological`, since the presenting complaint and the treatment decision are both about the seizure rather than the rachitic bone changes themselves.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'A convulsing child with rachitic skeletal signs (wide fontanelle, rosary, broad wrists) needs emergency IV calcium gluconate for hypocalcaemic tetany.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.272'
},

{
  id:'pedep-nut-19', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-vit',
  stem:'When advising a mother about the importance of vitamins, what should you tell her?',
  options:['Vitamin A decreases susceptibility to infection','Vitamin D improves vision in dim light','Vitamin K enhances immune system function','Vitamin C prevents rickets'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Vitamin A is critical for immune function, especially in reducing the severity and incidence of infections in children.\n\n---\n\n**Why A is right.** `Vitamin deficiency  Rickets.txt` states vitamin A deficiency is "usually associated with increased susceptibility to infection" and "increased mortality", because it maintains epithelial integrity and supports immune cell differentiation — so adequate vitamin A does the opposite, lowering susceptibility to infection. This is also why WHO gives prophylactic high-dose vitamin A specifically in severe malnutrition, persistent diarrhoea, respiratory infection and measles, per the same source.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** Vision in dim light (night vision) is a **vitamin A** function, not vitamin D — the same lecture lists "formation of the visual pigment... essential for vision in dim light" under vitamin A, so this option swaps the two fat-soluble vitamins. Vitamin K\'s role is carboxylating clotting factors, not immune enhancement — confusing it with immune function is the same error tested in `pedep-nut-17`. Rickets is caused by vitamin D deficiency, not prevented by vitamin C — vitamin C deficiency causes scurvy, a separate disease with its own bone and vessel findings.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Vitamin A supports immune function and epithelial integrity, lowering susceptibility to infection.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.274'
},

{
  id:'pedep-nut-20', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-pem',
  stem:'Which of the following is NOT a feature of marasmus?',
  options:['Looking apathetic','No edema','Gross muscle wasting','Loss of subcutaneous fat','Weight for height is >3SD below the median in growth chart'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> This question is of lesser importance. Apathy occurs in both marasmus and kwashiorkor, but it is more common with kwashiorkor. Therefore, we go with option A, as it is the agreed-upon answer. However, the wording of the question is not very good, but it has appeared before in the old system, so we included it as a backup.\n\n---\n\n**The box above is unusually informal — an editorial aside from the bank\'s own authors rather than a clinical explanation. Here is the clinical reasoning it skips.** `16) P.E.M.txt` describes marasmus\'s "psychic changes" as "anxious, irritable, excessively crying, little sleeping" — an alert, distressed child, not a flat, withdrawn one. Kwashiorkor\'s psychic change is listed simply as "apathy, etc." among its always-present features. So while some apathy can be seen in both severe forms, it is the more classically taught and more consistently present sign in kwashiorkor, which is why the bank marks "looking apathetic" as the one that does **not** belong on a marasmus list.\n\n**Why the other four genuinely are marasmus features.** *No oedema*, *gross muscle wasting* and *loss of subcutaneous fat* are the lecture\'s core clinical picture of marasmus (points 1–3 under clinical features, plus "no oedema" under the following slide). *Weight-for-height >3SD below the median* is the anthropometric definition of severe wasting used by WHO to define severe acute malnutrition, and marasmus is by definition a severe wasting disorder — this is the same discriminator anchored at `pedep-nut-21`.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Marasmus is wasting without oedema, with irritable (not classically apathetic) affect; profound apathy is more typically taught as a kwashiorkor sign.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.276'
},

{
  id:'pedep-nut-21', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-pem',
  stem:'A 2-year-old girl presented to the clinic for a routine check-up. She does not like to eat but she loves potato chips. The best method to check her nutritional status is to assess:',
  options:['Head circumference','Height','Weight','Weight for height','Skin fold thickness'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Weight-for-height assesses acute malnutrition and is the most sensitive indicator of current nutritional status in children.\n\n---\n\n**Why weight-for-height wins.** `16) P.E.M.txt` lists "Weight for age & weight for height" together under anthropometric assessment, but weight-for-height is the one that isolates *current* nutritional status: it compares a child\'s weight against the weight expected for her own height, so it is not confounded by chronic stunting the way weight-for-age can be, and it responds quickly (over weeks) to a poor diet — which is exactly the scenario here, a picky eater living on low-nutrient snack food.\n\n**Why each distractor is a weaker choice.** *Head circumference* tracks brain growth, not general nutritional status, and is far less sensitive to short-term dietary change. *Height* reflects chronic, long-term growth and changes far too slowly to flag a nutritional problem that has only just begun. *Weight* alone cannot distinguish a short, proportionate child from a genuinely underweight one — it needs to be referenced against either age or height to mean anything. *Skin fold thickness* estimates fat stores but is technique-dependent and less standardised as a routine screening tool than weight-for-height.\n\n**This question anchors two others in this bank** — `pedep-nut-33` and `pedep-nut-35` — which ask the same underlying question ("what identifies acute/severe malnutrition") in different words and with different option ladders; all three share the same answer, weight-for-height.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Weight-for-height is the most sensitive anthropometric marker of current (acute) nutritional status, unconfounded by chronic stunting.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.278'
},

{
  id:'pedep-nut-22', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-pem',
  stem:'The following is NOT a cause of anemia in severe protein-energy malnutrition:',
  options:['Iron deficiency','Carbohydrate deficiency','Protein deficiency','Folic acid deficiency','Infections'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Anemia in PEM is due to deficiencies in iron, folic acid, protein, or secondary infections; carbohydrate deficiency does not directly cause anemia.\n\n---\n\n**Why carbohydrate deficiency is the exception.** `16) P.E.M.txt` names anaemia among the features of the underweight child, attributed there to "iron, folic and protein deficiency" specifically — carbohydrate is an energy source, not a haematinic or a building block of red cells or haemoglobin, so a shortfall in carbohydrate intake alone does not impair erythropoiesis the way the other four causes do.\n\n**Why each other option genuinely causes anaemia in severe PEM.** *Iron deficiency* limits hameoglobin synthesis directly. *Protein deficiency* limits globin chain synthesis and overall marrow protein turnover. *Folic acid deficiency* impairs DNA synthesis in erythroid precursors, causing a megaloblastic picture. *Infections*, which are near-universal in severe PEM because of impaired immunity, drive anaemia of chronic disease through inflammatory suppression of erythropoiesis and marrow iron use.\n\n**The bank prints this exact question twice**, on p.280 and again on p.294 — same stem, same options, same key, with only the explanation box reworded. It is held here once, and both pages are cited.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Anaemia in severe PEM comes from iron, folate and protein deficiency plus infection — not from carbohydrate deficiency, which is not a haematinic pathway.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.280 (the same question is printed again on p.294)'
},

{
  id:'pedep-nut-23', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-pem',
  stem:'A 2-year-old boy lives in a village and attends the local health clinic near their village for a routine check. Both his parents are farmers. He is asymptomatic. On examination, he is very thin, but his hair and skin appear normal and there is no edema or other clinical abnormalities. His height is on the 5th centile, but his weight is well below the 3rd centile (z-score between -2 and -3 below the median). What is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Kwashiorkor','Marasmus','Normal child','Rickets','Severe gastro-esophageal reflux'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Severe wasting without edema and normal hair/skin indicates marasmus, reflecting chronic protein-energy deficiency.\n\n---\n\nThis is the same vignette as `pedep-nut-5` — a rural farming family, a very thin child with normal hair and skin, no oedema, height on the 5th centile and a weight z-score of −2 to −3 — with the name and country dropped and the weight band reworded from "below the 0.4th centile" to "below the 3rd centile". The full reasoning (why marasmus, and why kwashiorkor, a normal child, rickets and reflux are excluded) is written at `pedep-nut-5`; nothing about the diagnosis changes with the reworded centile, since both figures describe a z-score of −2 to −3, the range the box itself quotes.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Same marasmus picture as `pedep-nut-5`, reworded — normal hair/skin, no oedema, severe wasting on a chronically low-calorie diet.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.282'
},

{
  id:'pedep-nut-24', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-feed',
  stem:'Which of the following is true when comparing breast milk to cow\'s milk?',
  options:['Breast milk has higher lactose content','Breast milk has higher protein content','Breast milk has higher renal solute load','Breast milk has lower essential fatty acids'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Breast milk contains more lactose, which provides energy and supports gut microbiota, whereas protein content is lower than cow\'s milk.\n\n---\n\n**Why A is right.** `15.2) Infant Feeding.txt`\'s own bar chart of breast versus cow\'s milk gives lactose as roughly 7 g/dL for breast milk against about 3.3–3.8 g/dL for cow\'s milk — breast milk carries close to double the lactose, described elsewhere in the same deck as "Breast Milk: DOUBLE lactose (7:4)". That higher lactose helps calcium absorption, is essential for brain cerebroside synthesis, and feeds beneficial gut lactobacilli.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Higher protein content* is the reverse of the truth — the same chart shows cow\'s milk protein roughly four times breast milk\'s, and it is this excess protein and mineral load, not a breast-milk excess, that makes unmodified cow\'s milk unsuitable for young infants. *Higher renal solute load* also belongs to cow\'s milk: its higher protein, sodium and mineral content produces a heavier solute burden on the immature infant kidney, which is one reason whole cow\'s milk is deferred until after 12 months (`pedep-nut-38`). *Lower essential fatty acids* is not supported by the material; breast milk fat composition is tailored to infant needs (more unsaturated fat than cow\'s milk, per `15.2) Infant Feeding.txt`\'s "60% Unsaturated" versus cow\'s milk\'s "40% Unsaturated"), not deficient in essential fatty acids.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Breast milk carries roughly double cow\'s milk\'s lactose content, while cow\'s milk carries more protein and a higher renal solute load.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.284'
},

{
  id:'pedep-nut-25', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-feed',
  stem:'Breast milk is richer than cow\'s milk in the following:',
  options:['Proteins','Saturated fatty acids','Calcium and phosphorus','Iron','Sodium and potassium'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Breast milk has highly bioavailable iron, despite lower total iron content, compared to cow\'s milk.\n\n---\n\n**Why "richer" here means bioavailability, not raw content.** `Iron deficiency anemia pdf.txt` states breast milk is "low iron content but 50% of iron is absorbed", giving adequate iron if an infant is totally breastfed for the first 6 months, while cow\'s milk is "low iron content **and** poor bioavailability, so poor source of iron" — cow\'s milk\'s iron is both scarcer and harder to absorb, so functionally breast milk delivers more usable iron to the infant despite a similar or lower total content. That absorption gap is also why the same source flags early cow\'s milk feeding as a cause of iron deficiency.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Proteins*, *calcium and phosphorus*, and *sodium and potassium* are all higher in cow\'s milk, not breast milk — cow\'s milk is formulated for a calf\'s much faster mineral and protein growth needs, which is precisely why its high renal solute load makes it unsuitable for infants under 12 months (`pedep-nut-24`). *Saturated fatty acids* are also proportionally higher in cow\'s milk (40% unsaturated) than in breast milk (60% unsaturated), per `15.2) Infant Feeding.txt`.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Breast milk delivers more bioavailable iron than cow\'s milk despite similar or lower total iron content, because absorption is much higher.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.286'
},

{
  id:'pedep-nut-26', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-feed',
  stem:'Cow\'s milk differs from breast milk in which of the following?',
  options:['Increased iron','Less protein','Decreased carbohydrate','Higher lactose'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Cow\'s milk differs from breast milk because it contains less lactose (carbohydrates) and more protein\n\n---\n\n**Why decreased carbohydrate is right.** `15.2) Infant Feeding.txt`\'s chart shows lactose at roughly double in breast milk compared with cow\'s milk (about 7 g/dL versus 3.3–3.8 g/dL), so cow\'s milk\'s carbohydrate (lactose) content is the one that is genuinely decreased relative to breast milk.\n\n**Why each other option is the reverse of the truth.** *Increased iron* is wrong — `Iron deficiency anemia pdf.txt` describes cow\'s milk as having "low iron content and poor bioavailability", not more iron than breast milk. *Less protein* is wrong — cow\'s milk carries substantially more protein than breast milk on the same chart, which is why unmodified cow\'s milk is not used for young infants. *Higher lactose* is wrong for the same reason decreased carbohydrate is right — cow\'s milk has less lactose, not more.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Cow\'s milk has less lactose (carbohydrate) and more protein than breast milk, not more iron or more lactose.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.288'
},

{
  id:'pedep-nut-27', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-feed',
  stem:'Which of the following is NOT true about breast feeding?',
  options:['Recommended food for infants both term and preterm','50% of energy from proteins','Contains immunological benefits (i.e. IgA, active lymphocytes)','Promotes growth of lactobacillus in GI','Decreases incidence of allergic disorders'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Breast milk provides 5–7% of energy from proteins, with the majority from fats and carbohydrates. The other options reflect true benefits of breastfeeding.\n\n---\n\n**Why "50% of energy from proteins" is false.** `15.2) Infant Feeding.txt`\'s own composition chart shows breast milk protein at roughly 0.8–1 g/dL against fat around 3.8 g/dL and lactose around 7 g/dL — protein is the smallest of the three macronutrients by a wide margin, and the box\'s 5–7% figure is consistent with that: most of breast milk\'s energy comes from fat and lactose, not protein.\n\n**Why each other statement is genuinely true.** *Recommended for both term and preterm infants* — the same lecture lists breast milk as providing "the ideal nutrition for infants during the first 4 to 6 months of life" without restricting this to term babies (specialised fortification for very preterm infants is a refinement, not a contradiction). *Immunological benefits (IgA, active lymphocytes)* are listed explicitly among breastfeeding\'s advantages, reducing gastrointestinal and respiratory infection and otitis media. *Promotes lactobacillus growth* follows directly from breast milk\'s high lactose and prebiotic content (FOS and GOS), which the deck states help the growth of lactobacilli. *Decreases allergic disorders* is listed among the maternal/infant advantages of breastfeeding in the same source.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Breast milk derives only 5–7% of its energy from protein, not 50% — most comes from fat and lactose.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.290'
},

{
  id:'pedep-nut-28', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-vit',
  stem:'A 12-month-old patient is seen in the clinic. His mother reports that she exclusively breast-fed the child until he was 6 months of age. He still breast feeds approximately four to five times daily but the mother reports he also drinks 2 to 3 oz of water daily, 8 to 10 oz of juice daily, and has been doing well eating table foods. What dosing of vitamin D would be the most appropriate for this child?',
  options:['200 IU daily','400 IU daily','600 IU daily','800 IU daily','1000 IU daily'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> All infants younger than 12 months should receive 400 IU daily beginning within the first few days after birth. Healthy children 1 to 18 years of age should receive 600 IU daily. Children with liver disease, kidney disease, or malabsorption (ie, secondary to cystic fibrosis, celiac disease, inflammatory bowel disease [IBD], etc.) as well as premature infants need even higher doses to account for the limitations in vitamin D metabolism or absorption brought on by these health issues.\n\n---\n\n**Why 600 IU is right.** The box draws its line at 12 months: *younger than* 12 months gets 400 IU, and healthy children from **1 to 18 years** get 600 IU. This child is exactly 12 months old — the age at which he crosses from the infant band into the 1–18-year band — so 600 IU/day is the dose the box\'s own cut-off assigns him, not the 400 IU figure that applies only while he is still under a year. `17) Vitamin D Defficiency Rickets(1).txt` sets the shared under-12-months figure for prevention as "400 IU (or 10 μg) of vitamin D/day, starting from birth," which matches the box for infancy but does not itself state the older-child 600 IU step *(not taken from the course material beyond the shared infant figure)*.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *200 IU* is below the standard dose at any paediatric age in either source. *400 IU* is the infant maintenance dose, but it applies only while the child is younger than 12 months — this child has just reached that boundary, so it is one month too late, not too early. *800 IU* and *1000 IU* are the higher doses reserved for at-risk groups (prematures, twins, malabsorption, liver or kidney disease), not a well 12-month-old on a normal mixed diet.\n\n*The 1–18-year 600 IU figure and the malabsorption/organ-disease doses above are the endpoint box\'s own addition beyond what the cached lecture states; the 400 IU infant figure they share is unchanged.*\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'The box\'s dosing cut-off is age 12 months: younger than 12 months gets 400 IU/day, 1–18 years gets 600 IU/day — a child who has just turned 12 months old falls into the 600 IU band.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.292'
},

{
  id:'pedep-nut-30', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-feed',
  stem:'A 3-week-old infant has been referred to you because of persistent jaundice. Her current weight is 4050 g. Her neonatal screening test was normal, which one of the following is the most appropriate management?',
  options:['STOP breastfeeding','Commence phototherapy','Reassure the mother and continue breastfeeding','Perform liver function tests','Arrange an urgent liver ultrasound'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> In a healthy infant with normal weight gain and normal neonatal screen, breastfeeding jaundice is usually benign. No intervention is needed other than reassurance and continued feeding.\n\n---\n\n**Why reassurance is right.** `8) COMMON PROBLEMS IN THE TERM NEWBORN.txt` describes "breast-milk jaundice" as unconjugated hyperbilirubinaemia "lasting until 2–3 months of age" that is "common in breastfed infants" — a benign, self-limiting pattern distinct from early breastfeeding-associated jaundice caused by poor intake. At 3 weeks, with a normal neonatal screen (excluding hypothyroidism and other metabolic causes screened at birth) and a weight of 4050 g that shows the infant is feeding and growing adequately, this is the textbook benign breast-milk jaundice picture, and the correct management is reassurance and continued breastfeeding, not intervention.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Stopping breastfeeding* is unnecessary and would remove a healthy infant\'s primary nutrition for a self-limiting, benign condition. *Phototherapy* is reserved for levels approaching a defined treatment threshold, which nothing in this vignette suggests — jaundice alone at 3 weeks in a well, well-grown baby does not itself trigger phototherapy. *Liver function tests* would be the right move only if there were red flags for pathological (conjugated) jaundice — pale stools, dark urine, hepatosplenomegaly, poor weight gain — none of which are described. *Urgent liver ultrasound* is reserved for suspected biliary obstruction (e.g. biliary atresia), again flagged by acholic stools or a conjugated bilirubin, not by isolated prolonged unconjugated jaundice in a thriving breastfed infant.\n\nThis question also straddles `neonatal`, since the discriminator (benign breast-milk jaundice versus a pathological cause) is a neonatal-jaundice work-up rather than a feeding-practice question.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Prolonged unconjugated jaundice in a well, adequately-weight-gaining breastfed infant with a normal neonatal screen is benign breast-milk jaundice — reassure and continue feeding.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.296'
},

{
  id:'pedep-nut-31', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-pem',
  stem:'A farmer\'s child presents with weight below the 2nd centile and no edema. What is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Marasmus','Normal','Kwashiorkor','Obesity'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Marasmus presents with severe wasting without edema, often in children with inadequate caloric intake. Kwashiorkor, in contrast, has edema despite some fat stores.\n\n---\n\n**Why marasmus fits.** `16) P.E.M.txt` defines marasmus by growth failure, wasting and the explicit absence of oedema — the same combination given here (weight well below the 2nd centile, no oedema). A subsistence-farming background is the same low-calorie, chronic-undernutrition context used across this bank\'s marasmus vignettes (`pedep-nut-5`, `pedep-nut-23`).\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Normal* is excluded by a weight this far below the 2nd centile without any supporting detail (steady growth, good appetite) that would justify calling it constitutional, unlike the reassuring picture in `pedep-nut-11`. *Kwashiorkor* is specifically excluded by the stem\'s "no oedema" — oedema is kwashiorkor\'s defining always-present sign. *Obesity* is the opposite pattern of excess, not deficient, weight.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Low weight-for-centile with no oedema in a chronically under-fed child is marasmus; oedema is what would instead point to kwashiorkor.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.298'
},

{
  id:'pedep-nut-32', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-feed',
  stem:'At what age can semi-skimmed cow\'s milk be introduced as the main drink for a child?',
  options:['2 years','6 months','12 months','18 months'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Semi-skimmed cow\'s milk is not recommended before 2 years because toddlers require higher fat content for brain development. Whole milk is preferred until then.\n\n---\n\n**Why 2 years is right.** Fat is a concentrated energy source and is essential for the rapid brain growth of infancy and toddlerhood; reduced-fat (semi-skimmed) milk removes much of that energy and fat-soluble vitamin content at a stage when a toddler\'s stomach capacity cannot make up the shortfall from other foods, so full-fat (whole) cow\'s milk is used as the main drink from 12 months until at least 2 years, after which semi-skimmed can safely replace it *(the specific 2-year cutoff for reduced-fat milk is not detailed in the cached feeding lecture, which covers formula types and weaning timing but not this later milk-fat transition)*.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *6 months* and *12 months* are both far too early — even whole cow\'s milk is not recommended as the main drink before 12 months (`pedep-nut-38`), let alone a reduced-fat version. *18 months* is closer but still inside the window where full fat content is preferred for brain development, per the box.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Whole (full-fat) cow\'s milk is the main drink from 12 months to 2 years; semi-skimmed milk waits until 2 years, once fat needs for brain growth ease.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.300'
},

{
  id:'pedep-nut-33', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-pem',
  stem:'Nutritional status in severe malnutrition is best expressed by which measurement?',
  options:['Weight for age','Weight for height','Height for age','Mid-arm circumference'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Weight-for-height is the most sensitive measure for acute malnutrition, reflecting current nutritional status rather than long-term stunting.\n\n---\n\nThis is the same underlying question as `pedep-nut-21` (worded differently, with a different option ladder) and shares its answer: weight-for-height. The full reasoning — why weight-for-height isolates current nutritional status while weight-for-age, height-for-age and mid-arm circumference each answer a related but different question — is written there.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Same "best measurement of acute malnutrition" question as `pedep-nut-21` — weight-for-height.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.302'
},

{
  id:'pedep-nut-34', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-pem',
  stem:'Which tool allows identification of moderate and severe malnutrition?',
  options:['BMI','Weight for age','Mid-arm circumference','Skin fold thickness'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Mid-arm circumference is a quick, age-independent screening tool for moderate and severe malnutrition in children 6 months–5 years.\n\n---\n\n**Why mid-arm circumference (MUAC) is right.** `16) P.E.M.txt` lists mid-arm circumference under anthropometric data with a reference cut-off of 11.5 cm, and it is the tool designed for exactly this purpose: a single tape measurement around the mid-upper arm that needs no scale, no height board and almost no calculation, which is why community and field screening programmes use it to flag moderate and severe acute malnutrition on the spot.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *BMI* needs both an accurate height and weight and age/sex-specific centile charts, and is used mainly from about 2 years upward to classify overweight and obesity rather than to screen for wasting in young children. *Weight for age* needs a known birth date and a growth chart, and cannot distinguish a short, proportionate child from a genuinely wasted one, unlike a direct field measure. *Skin fold thickness* estimates fat stores but is technique-dependent (calliper placement and reader variability) and is not the standardised community screening tool that MUAC is.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Mid-arm circumference is the quick, equipment-light field tool for identifying moderate and severe malnutrition, unlike BMI, weight-for-age or skin fold thickness.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.304'
},

{
  id:'pedep-nut-35', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-pem',
  stem:'Which measurement reflects acute malnutrition?',
  options:['Height for age','Weight for height','Mid-arm circumference','Head circumference'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Acute malnutrition causes weight loss faster than height changes, so weight-for-height is the key measure.\n\n---\n\nThis is a third printing of the same underlying question as `pedep-nut-21`, again with a different option ladder. The full reasoning for why weight-for-height — not weight-for-age, height-for-age or mid-arm circumference — is the marker of acute malnutrition is written there; it applies unchanged here.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Third printing of the "measurement for acute malnutrition" question — see `pedep-nut-21` for the full comparison. Weight-for-height falls fast in acute wasting; height-for-age falls slowly, reflecting chronic stunting instead.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.306'
},

{
  id:'pedep-nut-36', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-pem',
  stem:'Severe malnutrition can be defined by the presence of which clinical sign?',
  options:['Severe wasting','Stunting','Bilateral edema','Apathetic look'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'The WHO clinical definition of severe acute malnutrition rests on either a very low weight-for-height/MUAC (severe wasting) **or** bilateral pitting oedema of nutritional origin — either one alone is sufficient to make the diagnosis, and the presence of oedema is what specifically separates kwashiorkor-type severe malnutrition from pure marasmic wasting. Bilateral oedema is listed among the always-present clinical features of kwashiorkor: fluid retention from the combination of hypoalbuminaemia (low oncotic pressure) and increased capillary permeability lets fluid leak into the interstitial space, producing the classic puffy face and pitting oedema of the feet and legs.\n\n*Severe wasting* is also a recognised criterion for severe acute malnutrition (marasmic type) but is not the single sign this stem is pointing to when only one option can define the presence of severe disease independent of the wasting pathway — bilateral oedema is the sign that, by itself, confirms severe malnutrition even in a child whose weight-for-height is not yet critically low. *Stunting* reflects chronic, long-standing undernutrition (low height-for-age) rather than an acute or severe state, and a stunted child can otherwise be clinically well. *Apathetic look* is a real but non-specific feature of advanced malnutrition — it accompanies severe disease but is not, by itself, a defining anthropometric or clinical criterion the way oedema is.\n\nWritten for this bank — Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Bilateral pitting oedema is, by itself, a WHO-recognised defining sign of severe acute malnutrition (the kwashiorkor pathway), distinct from the wasting-based (marasmic) pathway.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.308'
},

{
  id:'pedep-nut-37', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-feed',
  stem:'At what age should weaning from exclusive breastfeeding ideally be started?',
  options:['2 months','4–6 months','9 months','12 months'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Complementary foods should be introduced around 6 months while continuing breastfeeding to ensure adequate nutrition.\n\n---\n\n`15.2) Infant Feeding.txt` states the WHO recommendation as exclusive breastfeeding **to 6 months** (180 days), with complementary feeding introduced from that point while breastfeeding continues — a stricter single figure than this stem\'s "4–6 months" bracket, though it is the same underlying key (starting complementary feeding through the mid-late first year, not at 2, 9 or 12 months). The answer is left as printed since 6 months falls inside the 4–6 month range offered.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *2 months* is far too early — the gut and kidneys are not mature enough for solids, and exclusive breastfeeding alone should still be meeting all nutritional needs. *9 months* and *12 months* are both too late by either the box\'s or the lecture\'s figure, and delaying complementary foods this long risks the nutritional gaps in energy, iron and protein that the lecture specifically flags for infants kept on breast milk alone past 6 months.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'WHO recommends exclusive breastfeeding to 6 months, then complementary feeding while breastfeeding continues; this bank\'s printed "4–6 months" bracket is looser than the lecture\'s single 6-month figure but shares the same key.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.310'
},

{
  id:'pedep-nut-38', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-feed',
  stem:'Whole pasteurized cow\'s milk can be given as the main drink:',
  options:['With iron supplements before 1 year','Safely after 1 year','At 6 months','From birth'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Before 1 year, cow\'s milk can lead to iron deficiency and renal solute overload. Whole milk is safe after 12 months.\n\n---\n\n`Iron deficiency anemia pdf.txt` grounds the box directly: breast milk has around 50% iron absorption while cow\'s milk offers much poorer bioavailability, and early introduction of cow\'s milk before 12 months is listed as a specific cause of iron deficiency in infancy — the same lecture caps cow\'s milk intake below 500 mL/day even once it is introduced, to protect iron intake from other foods. The high protein, sodium and mineral (solute) load of cow\'s milk also outstrips a young infant\'s renal concentrating ability, the "renal solute overload" the box names, which is a separate reason from iron deficiency for waiting until 12 months.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *With iron supplements before 1 year* does not neutralise the risk — the concern is not iron content alone but the combined iron-poor, high-solute profile of cow\'s milk that a supplement does not fully correct, which is why the guidance is a hard age cut-off, not a workaround. *At 6 months* and *From birth* are both well within the window where cow\'s milk carries the highest risk of iron deficiency and renal overload, long before the gut and kidneys mature enough to handle it as the main drink.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Whole cow\'s milk should not replace breast milk/formula as the main drink before 12 months — earlier introduction risks iron deficiency and renal solute overload, and iron supplementation alone does not remove the risk.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.312'
},

{ id:'pedep-nut-39', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-feed',
  stem:'Delaying the introduction of allergenic foods (e.g., peanuts, eggs) will:',
  options:['Prevent food allergy','Delay food allergy','Increase risk of allergy','Have no effect'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Early introduction of allergenic foods like peanuts and eggs reduces the risk of developing allergies, delaying them can increase risk.\n\n---\n\nThe old advice to keep peanut and egg out of the diet until later infancy has been reversed. `26)allergy_.txt` describes the mechanism as **oral tolerance induction** — "the induction of ‘immune tolerance’ by feeding common food allergens such as eggs, cow’s milk and peanut to young infants to reduce the risk of allergy," now "suggested in infant feeding guidelines for countries where food allergy is common." The same slide gives the two competing exposure routes: tolerance depends on early exposure through the gut, while sensitisation instead develops through an **impaired skin barrier** — so avoiding the food does not remove exposure, it only shifts it to the route that provokes allergy rather than tolerance.\n\nWhy the wrong options fail: *Prevent* overstates the effect — early introduction lowers risk, it is not a guarantee. *Delay* and *Have no effect* both describe delayed introduction as neutral or protective, which is the outdated view the box is correcting.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Delaying allergenic foods like peanut and egg increases allergy risk; early introduction (oral tolerance induction) reduces it.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.314' },

{ id:'pedep-nut-40', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition',
  stem:'Developmental delay occurs in weight faltering if:',
  options:['Any degree of faltering is present','Only prolonged and severe faltering is present','Only mild faltering is present','Never occurs'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Mild or short-term weight faltering usually does not affect development, whereas prolonged severe malnutrition can impair growth and cognition.\n\n---\n\n`15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` frames weight faltering as "a description, not a diagnosis," present whenever "an infant’s weight falls across two centile spaces" — a definition that says nothing about severity or duration. Development is a separate, later casualty: `16) P.E.M.txt` lists "Permanent delay in intellectual developement" among the systemic **Consequences of PEM**, alongside impaired immunity and delayed wound healing — consequences that only appear once malnutrition is established and severe, not after a single crossed centile.\n\nWhy the wrong options fail: *Any degree* and *Only mild faltering* both wrongly attach cognitive harm to trivial or short-lived dips, which the vast majority of faltering infants show without developmental consequence. *Never occurs* ignores the real, well-described harm of chronic severe PEM.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Only prolonged, severe weight faltering — not mild or short-term dips — carries a risk of developmental delay.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.316' },

{ id:'pedep-nut-41', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition',
  stem:'Weight faltering is usually identified when:',
  options:['Weight fluctuates within one centile space','Weight crosses down two or more centile spaces','Height falters','BMI increases'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Crossing downward by ≥2 centile spaces on a growth chart is considered clinically significant weight faltering.\n\n---\n\nThis is the definition printed almost word for word in `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt`: weight faltering "is present if an infant’s weight falls across two centile spaces," and the same slide insists it is "a description, not a diagnosis" of an underlying cause still to be found.\n\nWhy the wrong options fail: *fluctuates within one centile space* is normal, expected variation and does not meet the threshold. *Height falters* describes stunting, the chronic consequence of long-standing disease, not the acute weight sign that triggers the initial workup. *BMI increases* points the opposite direction, toward obesity rather than faltering growth.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Weight faltering is defined by a weight that crosses down two or more centile spaces on the growth chart.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.318' },

{ id:'pedep-nut-42', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition',
  stem:'Severe prolonged weight faltering usually affects:',
  options:['Height','Only weight','Only head circumference','Only mid-arm circumference'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Chronic malnutrition eventually affects linear growth, leading to stunting, while acute malnutrition initially affects weight.\n\n---\n\nThe cached nutrition decks do not spell out this acute-versus-chronic timeline in words — grepped "stunt" across `content\\peds\\lectures\\` and the only hit is an unrelated mention under congenital heart disease complications — so the mechanism below is *not taken from the course material*. Weight is the fast-moving variable: an infant who stops gaining enough calories loses weight, or fails to gain it, within days to weeks. Height is the slow variable, built by growth-plate activity that needs sustained substrate over months; only once malnutrition has run long enough does the skeleton itself fall behind, producing stunting.\n\nWhy the wrong options fail: *Only weight* describes the early, acute picture, not a prolonged one. *Only head circumference* and *only mid-arm circumference* isolate measures that are either preserved until malnutrition is extreme (head circumference tracks brain growth) or that simply track fat and muscle bulk rather than the skeletal consequence being asked about.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Prolonged severe malnutrition eventually impairs linear growth (height), unlike brief or mild faltering, which affects weight alone — not taken from the course material.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.320' },

{ id:'pedep-nut-43', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition',
  stem:'Weight faltering in children was formerly called:',
  options:['Protein energy malnutrition','Wasting','Failure to thrive','Growth failure'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> "Failure to thrive" was the old term for weight faltering or poor growth, reflecting inadequate nutrition or chronic illness.\n\n---\n\nThe cached decks never use "failure to thrive" as a synonym for weight faltering — the phrase turns up only as a symptom inside unrelated topics (renal, cardiac, cholestasis, immunodeficiency lectures) — so the terminology history itself is *not taken from the course material*. Clinical usage moved away from "failure to thrive" partly because it can sound blaming toward the family, whereas "faltering growth" is deliberately descriptive: `15.1) Faltering ﻿Growth.txt` states plainly it is "a description, not a diagnosis." The underlying growth pattern the two terms refer to is the same.\n\nWhy the wrong options fail: *Protein energy malnutrition* and *Wasting* are specific nutritional diagnoses with their own criteria (Wellcome classification, weight-for-height), not renamed synonyms for the general descriptive term. *Growth failure* is close in meaning but is not the name that was actually retired in favour of "faltering growth."\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'"Faltering growth" is the modern, purely descriptive term that replaced the older "failure to thrive" — not taken from the course material.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.322' },

{ id:'pedep-nut-44', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-pem',
  stem:'Nutritional status of a child can be reliably assessed by:',
  options:['A single weight measurement','A single height measurement','Multiple anthropometric indices','Only head circumference'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> A single measurement is insufficient; weight, height, BMI, and mid-arm circumference together provide a comprehensive assessment.\n\n---\n\n`16) P.E.M.txt` sets out the anthropometric panel used at the bedside under "CLINICAL ASSESSMENT — 3. ANTHROPOMETRIC DATA": "Length / Height (use percentile charts), Weight for age & weight for height, Head, chest & abdominal circumf., Mid-arm circumference(11.5 cm), Others: skin fold thickness, etc." No single item on that list stands alone — each captures a different axis (linear growth, current mass, brain growth, fat and muscle bulk), and only combining them separates a small-but-healthy child from a genuinely malnourished one.\n\nWhy the wrong options fail: *a single weight*, *a single height*, and *only head circumference* each isolate one axis, exactly the trap the box is written against — a single number cannot distinguish a proportionately small child from one who is wasted or stunted.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Nutritional assessment needs multiple anthropometric indices together — no single measurement is reliable on its own.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.324' },

{ id:'pedep-nut-45', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-pem',
  stem:'Mid-arm circumference is independent of age in children between:',
  options:['Birth–6 months','6 months–5 years','5–10 years','Adolescence'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> In this age range, mid-arm circumference can be used without age adjustment to screen for malnutrition.\n\n---\n\n`16) P.E.M.txt` places mid-arm circumference in the anthropometric panel with a single cut-off, "Mid-arm circumference(11.5 cm)," but does not state the age window over which that one number applies — so the 6-months-to-5-years range itself is *not taken from the course material*. Upper-arm circumference grows only slowly and steadily through this window, unlike weight or length, which is exactly what makes a single cut-off usable as a fast field screen (the Shakir tape) where scales and stadiometers are unavailable.\n\nWhy the wrong options fail: *Birth–6 months* is too early — arm circumference is still changing rapidly in early infancy. *5–10 years* and *adolescence* are both too late — the age-independent window closes well before school age, and by adolescence body composition is again shifting with the pubertal growth spurt.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Mid-arm circumference is a fast, age-independent malnutrition screen between 6 months and 5 years — not taken from the course material.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.326' },

{ id:'pedep-nut-46', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-pem',
  stem:'Which of the following is a poor tool for nutritional assessment?',
  options:['Skin fold thickness','Weight for height','Mid-arm circumference','BMI'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Skin fold measurements are operator-dependent and less reliable than weight-for-height or mid-arm circumference for clinical assessment.\n\n---\n\n`16) P.E.M.txt` lists skin fold thickness only as an afterthought under anthropometric data — "Others: skin fold thickness, etc." — set apart from the panel of routinely used measures (length/height, weight for age and for height, circumferences). That placement matches the box: skinfold calipers need a trained observer and consistent technique, and small differences in where and how hard the fold is pinched change the reading, which is why it stays a research tool rather than a bedside one.\n\nWhy the wrong options fail: *weight for height*, *mid-arm circumference*, and *BMI* are all reproducible, chart-based or single-cut-off measures used routinely at the bedside precisely because they do not depend on an examiner’s calliper technique.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Skinfold thickness is the least reliable routine nutritional index — it is operator-dependent, unlike weight-for-height, MUAC or BMI.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.328' },

{ id:'pedep-nut-47', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-pem',
  stem:'Immunodeficiency in malnutrition is mainly indicated by:',
  options:['Low serum immunoglobulins','Impaired cell-mediated immunity','Reduced neutrophil count','Low complement levels'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Protein-energy malnutrition primarily causes T-cell defects, while immunoglobulin levels are often preserved.\n\n---\n\n`16) P.E.M.txt` lists "Impaired immunity" among the systemic **Consequences of PEM** but does not break the defect down by immune compartment — grepped "cell-mediated" and "T-cell" together with "malnutrition" across `content\\peds\\lectures\\` and found no match, so the compartment-specific claim in the box is *not taken from the course material*. The thymus and peripheral T-cell pools are unusually sensitive to protein-calorie deprivation, so cell-mediated immunity (delayed-type hypersensitivity, T-cell counts and function) falls first and hardest; B cells keep making antibody, so total immunoglobulin levels are often normal or even raised — which is why malnourished children develop severe viral and intracellular infections (measles, TB) out of proportion to their bacterial risk.\n\nWhy the wrong options fail: *low serum immunoglobulins*, *reduced neutrophil count*, and *low complement levels* each describe a different, less characteristic defect — humoral, phagocytic and complement function are relatively preserved compared with the T-cell defect.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'PEM mainly impairs cell-mediated (T-cell) immunity while antibody levels are usually preserved — not taken from the course material.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.330' },

{ id:'pedep-nut-48', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-feed',
  stem:'Unmodified cow’s milk in infancy is:',
  options:['Suitable from birth','Unsuitable','Safe at 3 months','Recommended after 6 months'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Cow’s milk is not recommended in the first year due to risk of iron deficiency, high renal solute load, and protein excess.\n\n---\n\n`17) Vitamin D Defficiency Rickets(1).txt` independently confirms the same milk as a *cause* of disease in infancy, not just a poor iron source: unmodified cow’s milk is listed among the "rachitogenic diets," because "its high P content decreases the absorption of Ca." So unmodified cow’s milk works against a young infant on two fronts at once — a poor iron source, and a high renal-solute, high-phosphate load a young gut and kidney handle poorly.\n\nWhy the wrong options fail: *suitable from birth* and *safe at 3 months* both put an actively harmful food far too early. *Recommended after 6 months* is still too early — whole pasteurised cow’s milk only becomes safe from 1 year, consistent with the "not recommended in the first year" rule given here.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Unmodified cow’s milk is unsuitable in the first year of life — it is iron-poor and carries a high renal solute and phosphate load.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.332' },

{ id:'pedep-nut-49', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-feed',
  stem:'Compared to human milk, cow’s milk contains:',
  options:['Less protein and minerals','More protein and minerals','Equal protein and minerals','No protein'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Cow’s milk contains higher protein and mineral content, which can stress an infant’s kidneys compared to breast milk.\n\n---\n\n`15.2) Infant Feeding.txt` gives the actual numbers behind the box, in its "Cow’s Versus Breast Milk" chart: protein sits near 1 g in breast milk against roughly 3.3–3.8 g in cow’s milk, while breast milk carries the higher lactose (about 7 versus 4). The same deck’s "Quality of Protein" chart adds why the protein difference matters clinically, not just numerically: breast milk protein is dominated by immunoglobulins, lactalbumin and lactoferrin, with casein only about 40%, while cow’s milk protein is about 80% casein — a denser, harder-to-digest curd that also raises the renal solute load.\n\nWhy the wrong options fail: *less protein and minerals* and *equal protein and minerals* both reverse or flatten a real and clinically important gap. *No protein* is simply false — cow’s milk is a protein-rich food, just poorly suited to a young infant’s kidneys and gut.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Cow’s milk carries more protein and minerals than human milk, raising renal solute load in a young infant.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.334' },

{ id:'pedep-nut-50', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-feed',
  stem:'A modified cow’s milk–based formula should be continued until at least:',
  options:['6 months','9 months','12 months','18 months'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Formula is usually continued until 12 months, after which toddlers can transition to cow’s milk or varied diet.\n\n---\n\n`15.2) Infant Feeding.txt` sets both ends of the timeline that makes this answer necessary: exclusive breastfeeding (or formula) to 6 months, complementary feeding from 6–24 months, and continued breastfeeding "until 2 years or beyond" for a breastfed infant. The formula-fed infant needs an equivalent bridge — modified formula supplies the iron, vitamin D and controlled protein/mineral load that unmodified cow’s milk does not, as set out at `pedep-nut-48`, which shows exactly why cow’s milk cannot substitute before 12 months.\n\nWhy the wrong options fail: *6 months* and *9 months* both stop the modified formula while the infant still needs the protection it provides against iron deficiency and renal overload. *18 months* is unnecessarily late — once a varied diet plus cow’s milk is established from 12 months, the modified formula’s job is done.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Modified cow’s milk-based formula bridges infancy until at least 12 months, when plain cow’s milk becomes safe.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.336' },

{ id:'pedep-nut-51', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-pem',
  stem:'Wasting is best defined as:',
  options:['Low height for age','Low weight for height','Low BMI','Low mid-arm circumference'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Wasting reflects acute malnutrition, distinguishing it from stunting (chronic malnutrition) or underweight (mixed).\n\n---\n\nThe cached decks never define "wasting" against a weight-for-height ratio by name — grepped "wasting" and the only hits describe muscle wasting in neuromuscular topics, none defining the anthropometric term — so the strict definition below is *not taken from the course material*. `16) P.E.M.txt` describes the same clinical picture under a different label: **marasmus** is "Growth failure (Wt. <60%)... Loss of subcutaneous fat... Muscle wasting," the severe end of the same acute-deprivation axis that weight-for-height measures.\n\nWhy the wrong options fail: *low height for age* is stunting, the chronic axis. *Low BMI* overlaps in practice but is not the index preferred for wasting in young children (weight-for-length/height is standard under 5). *Low mid-arm circumference* is a rapid screening proxy for wasting, not its definition.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Wasting is defined as low weight-for-height, the acute-malnutrition axis, distinct from stunting and underweight — not taken from the course material.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.338' },

{ id:'pedep-nut-52', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-pem',
  stem:'A child with marasmus typically has:',
  options:['Severe edema','No edema','Puffy face','Ascites'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Marasmus is severe wasting without edema, unlike kwashiorkor which features edema and fatty liver.\n\n---\n\n`16) P.E.M.txt` prints marasmus and kwashiorkor as a deliberate contrast pair. Marasmus clinical features: "Growth failure (Wt. <60%), Loss of subcutaneous fat, Muscle wasting" and, under a second slide, explicitly "No Edema, No Dermatosis, No Hair changes (Or mild ones)." Kwashiorkor, by contrast, is "ALWAYS PRESENT: Growth Failure, Edema with NO Ascites, Muscle Wasting & Disturbed muscle/fat ratio."\n\nWhy the wrong options fail: *severe edema* and *puffy face* both belong to kwashiorkor, not marasmus. *Ascites* is excluded from kwashiorkor itself by the lecture’s own wording ("Edema with NO Ascites") and is not a feature of marasmus either.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Marasmus is severe wasting with no edema, no dermatosis and no hair changes — the opposite pattern from kwashiorkor.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.340' },

{ id:'pedep-nut-53', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-feed',
  stem:'Criteria for underfeeding in breast-fed babies do NOT include:',
  options:['Slow irregular weight gain','Sleeping much after nursing','Decreased amount of urine and ↓ frequency of urination','Sucking fists between feeds','Stay on breast for a long period'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Long feeding sessions alone do not indicate underfeeding; the focus is on growth, urine output, and feeding efficiency.\n\n---\n\n`15.2) Infant Feeding.txt` prints both lists side by side. Criteria of **adequate** breastfeeding include "Baby sleeps well 2 - 4 hours after nursing" and "Good amount of urine (6 or more full diapers per day)." Criteria of **insufficient** breastfeeding include "Inadequate weight gain or weight loss," "Inadequate urine and stools," "Fretfulness, crying and sucking of fists between the feeds," and "Unsatisfied staying on each breast for a long period (more than 30 minutes)." Sleeping well after a feed is explicitly the sign of adequacy, not insufficiency, so "Sleeping much after nursing" is the reversal of the true criterion and the one item that does not belong on the underfeeding list.\n\nWhy the other options ARE genuine underfeeding signs: slow irregular weight gain sits on the same axis as "inadequate weight gain"; decreased urine and frequency matches "inadequate urine"; sucking fists between feeds and staying on the breast a long time are both printed verbatim in the insufficient-feeding list.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Sleeping well after a feed is a sign of adequate breastfeeding, not underfeeding — the other four options are all printed criteria of insufficient feeding.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.342' },

{ id:'pedep-nut-54', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-feed',
  stem:'Which of the following statements about preterm and term formulae and breast milk is most factually correct?',
  options:['Babies fed on fortified breast milk have a decreased incidence of late-onset sepsis and necrotizing enterocolitis when compared with babies fed on preterm formulae','Preterm babies fed with fortified breast milk show similar weight gain profiles to preterm babies fed on preterm formula milks','Preterm formula contains more fat, protein, and electrolytes but the same amount of carbohydrate as term formula or unfortified breast milk','Preterm formula has a lower sodium content than term formula','Preterm formula has the same protein content as term formula'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Fortified breast milk combines the nutritional adequacy needed for preterm growth with the immunologic and protective benefits of breast milk. Preterm formula supports growth but does not provide the same protective immune factors, so infants fed formula are at higher risk for serious complications like NEC and late-onset sepsis.\n\n---\n\nThe cached decks do not cover preterm-versus-term formula composition or the fortified-milk-versus-formula infection comparison named in the box — grepped "preterm formula," "fortified breast milk," "NEC" and "late-onset sepsis" together and found only a general NEC entry under the preterm GI-problem list in `7) Intro to neonatology.txt` ("NEC... Incidence inversely related to GA"), with no comparison by feed type — so the reasoning below is *not taken from the course material*.\n\nA preterm infant needs more protein, fat, calcium, phosphate and sodium per kilogram than a term infant, to make up outside the uterus what the third trimester would otherwise have supplied. **Fortifying** the mother’s own breast milk adds that extra load while keeping every protective component formula lacks (secretory IgA, lactoferrin, oligosaccharides, live cells) — and it is exactly those components that lower NEC and late-onset sepsis risk, both driven by an immature, unprotected gut mucosa. **Preterm formula** supplies the same extra nutrients but is nutritionally inert, with none of that protection.\n\nWhy the other options fail: *similar weight gain profiles* is a half-truth — growth on either feed can look comparable, which is precisely why the real difference is in infection risk, not growth. *Same carbohydrate content* singles out the one nutrient that is comparatively similar across preterm and term feeds, while the true divergence (protein, fat, electrolytes) is left out. *Lower sodium content than term formula* reverses the truth — preterm formula runs **higher** sodium to cover a preterm kidney’s high urinary losses. *Same protein content as term formula* is also reversed — preterm formula is fortified specifically because preterm protein needs are higher.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Fortified breast milk gives a preterm infant both adequate nutrition and protective factors that lower NEC and late-onset sepsis risk versus preterm formula — not taken from the course material.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.344 (the explanation box is printed alone on p.345)' },

{ id:'pedep-nut-55', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-vit',
  stem:'An 18-month-old infant is seen in the pediatrician’s office as a new patient for a well-child visit having moved from Minnesota to Texas. A comparison of new to previous growth parameters suggest he has dropped to less than fifth percentile for height, weight, and head circumference. Physical examination findings are significant for lower-extremity bowing, wrist enlargement, frontal bossing, and some hypotonia. The mother is concerned because he does not pull to stand nor walking. She reports that he was exclusively breast-fed until he was 9 to 10 months old, currently drinks about 24 oz of whole milk daily, and has never taken medications or dietary supplements. The mother also has a question about whether he needs allergy testing given his history of frequent upper respiratory infections. Which of the following laboratory findings would be expected?',
  options:['Low calcium, normal phosphorus, elevated alkaline phosphatase, elevated parathyroid hormone(PTH)','Low calcium, low phosphorus, low alkaline phosphatase, elevated PTH','Low calcium, low phosphorus, elevated alkaline phosphatase, elevated PTH','Low calcium, high phosphorus, elevated alkaline phosphatase, elevated PTH','Low calcium, low phosphorus, low alkaline phosphatase, low PTH'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Nutritional rickets from vitamin D deficiency commonly presents with hypocalcemia and hypophosphatemia, which leads to secondary hyperparathyroidism and elevated alkaline phosphatase. Clinical signs include bowing of legs, wrist enlargement, frontal bossing, and hypotonia.\n\n---\n\nThe vignette adds an allergy-testing question about frequent upper respiratory infections; it is not what the stem asks and does not change the answer. `Vitamin deficiency  Rickets.txt` walks the same biochemical chain the box compresses: low vitamin D drops calcium absorption, which triggers secondary hyperparathyroidism; PTH raises calcium back up (often into the low-normal range) at the cost of driving phosphate excretion up and serum phosphate down, while active osteoblasts trying to mineralise unmineralised osteoid drive alkaline phosphatase high. That gives the classic infantile-rickets pattern the box states: low(-ish) calcium, low phosphate, high ALP, high PTH.\n\nWhy the other option sets fail: *normal phosphorus* (A) understates the phosphate wasting PTH causes. *Low alkaline phosphatase* (B, E) is a direct reversal — ALP is high, not low, from active bone remodelling; the lecture separately gives the reference range as roughly 5–15 Bodansky U/dl or 145–200 IU/L, well below what active rickets produces. *High phosphorus* (D) reverses the direction of the phosphate change entirely. *Low PTH* (E) is also reversed — the whole compensatory response depends on PTH rising, not falling.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Nutritional (vitamin D-deficiency) rickets gives low-normal calcium, low phosphate, high alkaline phosphatase and high PTH — the biochemical signature of secondary hyperparathyroidism.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.347 (the explanation box is printed alone on p.348)' },

{ id:'pedep-nut-56', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-vit',
  stem:'Which of the following would be the most likely radiographic finding in a child who has been diagnosed with calcium-deficient rickets?',
  options:['Widening of the ribs','Shortening of the radius and ulna','Vertebral fissures and fractures','Prominence of the costochondral junctions','Increased densities in the long bone shafts'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Calcium-deficient rickets causes expansion of the growth plates and metaphyseal changes, often seen as prominent costochondral junctions (“rachitic rosary”). Other classic radiographic features may include cupping and fraying of metaphyses.\n\n---\n\n`Vitamin deficiency  Rickets.txt` names the same sign directly: "Rickety (or rachitic) rosary: prominent enlargement of the costochondral junctions seen and felt as a row of beads about the size of cherries" along the costochondral line. The same source’s active-rickets radiograph list is the wider version of the box’s "cupping and fraying": "Broadening + cupping (concave) + fraying (irregularity) of the lower ends of radius and ulna," an indistinct epiphyseal line, and reduced shaft bone density (not increased).\n\nWhy the wrong options fail: *widening of the ribs* is not a printed rickets sign — the chest sign is the rosary at the costochondral junction, not rib widening itself. *Shortening of the radius and ulna* reverses the finding — the wrist ends are broadened and cupped, not shortened. *Vertebral fissures and fractures* is not among the printed radiographic features. *Increased densities in the long bone shafts* directly reverses the lecture’s own point — active rickets gives reduced shaft density, not increased.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'The rachitic rosary — prominent costochondral junctions — is the classic thoracic radiographic sign of rickets, alongside metaphyseal cupping and fraying at the wrist.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.350' },

{ id:'pedep-nut-57', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-feed',
  stem:'Which of the following types of food is contraindicated and has to be avoided in a child under one year of age?',
  options:['Eggs','Honey','Peanut','All of the above'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> ● Foods high in salt and sugar should be avoided and honey must not be given until 1 year of age because of risk of infantile botulism.\n> ● Early introduction of common allergens such as egg and peanut at, or around 4 months of age in infants at high risk of developing IgE-mediated allergy may reduce the risk of developing allergy to these foods.\n\n---\n\nNone of the cached lecture material covers honey or infantile botulism at all — grepped "honey" and "botulism" across every lecture cache and the only hits are unrelated (impetigo’s "honey-coloured crusting"), so the mechanism is *not taken from the course material*. **Honey can carry *Clostridium botulinum* spores**, which an infant’s immature gut flora cannot yet suppress; germinating spores produce toxin in situ (infant botulism), unlike adult botulism, which needs pre-formed toxin already in the food. An infant’s gut matures enough to resist this by around 12 months, which is why the cutoff is exactly the first birthday.\n\nWhy the other options fail: *eggs* and *peanut* are the two foods the box itself says should be introduced **early** (around 4 months in higher-risk infants) rather than avoided, consistent with the oral-tolerance-induction reasoning at `pedep-nut-39`. *All of the above* would require eggs and peanut to also be contraindicated, which the box explicitly contradicts.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Honey is avoided under 1 year for infantile botulism risk; egg and peanut are instead introduced early to reduce allergy risk — the botulism mechanism is not taken from the course material.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.352' },

{ id:'pedep-nut-58', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-vit',
  stem:'What is appropriate regarding the role of vitamins in children’s nutrition?',
  options:['Vitamin C deficiency can lead to petechiae and mucosal bleeding','Vitamin K’s main function is in the synthesis of RBCs','Vitamin E deficiency leads to decreased clot generation','Vitamin B12 deficiency is common in infants','Folic acid has no role in blood production'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Vitamin C deficiency (scurvy) leads to defective collagen synthesis, bruising, bleeding gums, and petechiae. Vitamin K is for coagulation, not RBC synthesis.\n\n---\n\n`Vitamin deficiency  Rickets.txt` carries a full vitamin function/deficiency/source table that grounds every option in this question and its reordered sibling `pedep-nut-70`:\n\n| Vitamin | Function | Deficiency |\n|---|---|---|\n| C | Reducing agent; collagen metabolism (cartilage, bone matrix, blood-vessel connective tissue) | Scurvy: irritability, purpura, bleeding gums, periosteal haemorrhages, bone aches |\n| K | Carboxylation of clotting factors | Hemorrhagic disease of the newborn, prolonged prothrombin time |\n| E | Antioxidant | Haemolysis in preterm infants, ataxia, areflexia |\n| B12 | DNA synthesis; co-enzyme in folate metabolism | Megaloblastic anaemia, vitiligo, peripheral neuropathy |\n| Folate (B10) | DNA synthesis | Megaloblastic anaemia |\n\nVitamin C’s collagen role is exactly why its deficiency (scurvy) causes petechiae and mucosal/gum bleeding — collagen supports capillary walls, and defective collagen makes them fragile.\n\nWhy the other options fail: vitamin **K**’s printed function is clotting-factor carboxylation, not RBC synthesis. Vitamin **E** deficiency causes *haemolysis*, not decreased clot generation — that confuses it with vitamin K. Vitamin **B12** deficiency is not described as common in infants in the table; it is a co-factor deficiency more typical of malabsorption or strict maternal veganism in breastfed infants. **Folic acid** is explicitly listed with a DNA-synthesis/blood-production role (megaloblastic anaemia on deficiency), the reverse of "no role."\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Vitamin C deficiency (scurvy) causes petechiae and mucosal bleeding through defective collagen synthesis; the other four statements each reverse a printed vitamin function.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.354' },

{ id:'pedep-nut-59', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-feed',
  stem:'What is the main benefit of human milk oligosaccharides?',
  options:['Kill microorganisms','Work as prebiotics','Help protein absorption','Help lactose digestion','Prevent infant colic'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> HMOs promote growth of beneficial gut microbiota, supporting immunity and gut health.\n\n---\n\n`15.2) Infant Feeding.txt` names the carbohydrate class behind this answer without using the term "human milk oligosaccharides" verbatim — its carbohydrate-differences slide states plainly that "Breast milk contains prebiotics (FOS & GOS)" and that breast milk’s higher lactose content "helps growth of lactobacilli (probiotics)." Fructo- and galacto-oligosaccharides are the same broad family of non-digestible sugars that human milk oligosaccharides belong to: they pass undigested into the colon and selectively feed beneficial bacterial genera, which is the definition of a prebiotic.\n\nWhy the other options fail: *kill microorganisms* describes an antimicrobial action, which belongs to other breast-milk components (secretory IgA, lactoferrin), not the prebiotic sugars. *Help protein or lactose digestion* both misassign a digestive-enzyme role to a carbohydrate that is, by definition, not digested by the infant at all. *Prevent infant colic* is not a printed or established function of HMOs.\n\n**⚠️ Within-bank reprint — p.387.** The endpoint file asks this twice, as “What is the main benefit” here and as “How does human milk oligosaccharides work?” on p.387. The five options run in the same order with the key in the same position; p.387 respells it from “Work as prebiotics” to “Function as prebiotics”, which changes nothing about what is being asked. It is held once in the app, under the p.356 printing, whose wording the option list above reproduces.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Human milk oligosaccharides are indigestible sugars that act as prebiotics, feeding beneficial gut flora rather than being digested or killing microbes directly.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.356 (the same question is printed again on p.387 as “How does human milk oligosaccharides work?”, with the same five options in the same order and the key in the same position, respelled there from “Work as prebiotics” to “Function as prebiotics”)' },

{ id:'pedep-nut-60', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-vit',
  stem:'An 18-month-old exclusively breastfed infant has not started standing or walking yet. On examination, she has swelling of her wrists, an open fontanelle, and has not yet erupted any teeth. Which of the following is the most likely cause?',
  options:['Vitamin A deficiency','Vitamin B deficiency','Vitamin C deficiency','Vitamin D deficiency','Vitamin E deficiency'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> These are classic features of rickets due to vitamin D deficiency, common in exclusively breastfed infants without supplementation.\n\n---\n\n`17) Vitamin D Defficiency Rickets(1).txt` prints each of these signs individually: wrist swelling from metaphyseal widening, delayed fontanelle closure, and delayed motor milestones ("delayed sitting, standing and walking, but other milestones as language and social are normal") from the hypotonia and skeletal load of active rickets; delayed dentition is a separately listed rickets feature. Human milk is a poor vitamin D source, and an exclusively breastfed infant without supplementation is the textbook at-risk profile the box names.\n\nWhy the other options fail: vitamin A deficiency produces eye and epithelial signs (night blindness, xerophthalmia), not wrist swelling or fontanelle changes. Vitamin B and E deficiencies produce neurological or dermatological signs (peripheral neuropathy, dermatitis, ataxia, haemolysis) with no skeletal-mineralisation picture. Vitamin C deficiency produces bleeding and gum signs, not bone widening or delayed fontanelle closure.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Wrist swelling, open fontanelle and delayed dentition/milestones in an exclusively breastfed, unsupplemented infant are the classic presentation of vitamin D-deficiency rickets.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.358' },

{ id:'pedep-nut-61', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-vit',
  stem:'A three-day term infant born at home, exclusively breastfed, presents with lethargy and hematemesis. On examination, he is markedly pale but not jaundiced and has bulging anterior fontanel. His hemoglobin is 7.2 g/dL in venous blood gas (VBG) report. What is the most likely etiology of his illness?',
  options:['Vitamin D deficiency','Vitamin K deficiency','Folate deficiency','Iron deficiency','Hypocalcemia'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Newborns who are exclusively breastfed and did not receive vitamin K prophylaxis can develop hemorrhagic disease of the newborn.\n\n---\n\nA home birth is the operative detail — it means the infant almost certainly missed the standard intramuscular vitamin K prophylaxis given at hospital delivery, and breast milk itself is a poor source of vitamin K. `Vitamin deficiency  Rickets.txt`’s vitamin table lists vitamin K’s function as "carboxylation of clotting factors" and its deficiency state as "hemorrhagic disease of the newborn, prothrombin time [prolonged]" — exactly the haematemesis, pallor, low haemoglobin and bulging fontanelle (from intracranial bleeding) this three-day-old shows. That the infant is *not* jaundiced argues against a haemolytic process.\n\nWhy the other options fail: vitamin D deficiency and hypocalcemia present with tetany, seizures or skeletal signs over weeks to months, not acute haemorrhage at 3 days. Folate and iron deficiency both take weeks to months of inadequate intake to produce clinically significant anaemia — neither can develop by day 3 of life, and neither causes bleeding.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Acute haemorrhage in an unsupplemented, home-born, breastfed neonate is vitamin K deficiency bleeding (hemorrhagic disease of the newborn), from a missed prophylaxis dose.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.360' },

{ id:'pedep-nut-62', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-vit',
  stem:'An eleven-month-old boy presented to the emergency room with convulsions. He had a history of diarrhea for 3 days and was afebrile. His mother described that he was not thirsty and had no history of convulsions. Examination revealed a large head with wide anterior fontanelle, prominent costochondral junctions, and broad wrists. His eyes and skin pinch were normal, and rectal temperature was 37.8 °C. What is the emergency room initial treatment?',
  options:['ORS','Antipyretic drug and cold fomentations','Low sodium IV fluids','IV Ceftriaxone','IV calcium gluconate'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> The child has hypocalcemic seizures from vitamin D deficiency rickets. Emergency management is IV calcium gluconate.\n\n---\n\nThe stem screens out the obvious diarrhoea-related distractors on its own findings: skin pinch is normal and he is not thirsty, so this is not a dehydration seizure needing ORS or IV fluids, and a temperature of 37.8°C is not a fever needing antipyretics. What is left is the rickets triad — wide fontanelle, rachitic rosary at the costochondral junctions, broad wrists — pointing to hypocalcaemic (tetanic) convulsions. `Vitamin deficiency  Rickets.txt` gives the exact emergency protocol the box compresses: **"Intravenous Calcium gluconate (5–10 ml of 10% solution) slowly over 10 minutes,"** with oxygen for laryngeal stridor and IV diazepam reserved for the seizure itself if calcium alone does not settle it — but the calcium correction is the first, causal step, not a symptomatic one.\n\nWhy the other options fail: ORS and low-sodium IV fluids treat dehydration, which the exam findings rule out. Antipyretics and cold fomentations treat fever, not present here. IV ceftriaxone treats a bacterial infection, for which there is no evidence — the child is afebrile with normal eyes and skin turgor.\n\nThis vignette shares its clinical picture with `pedep-nut-71`, which asks what investigations are expected rather than what treatment to give — a different question, not a repeat.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Hypocalcaemic convulsions from vitamin D-deficiency rickets are treated emergently with slow IV calcium gluconate (5–10 mL of 10% solution over 10 minutes) — the dehydration and fever distractors are excluded by the exam findings.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.362' },

{ id:'pedep-nut-63', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-feed',
  stem:'A 5-month-old infant is exclusively breastfed until yesterday when her mother started her on formula as she is planning to go back to work. The mother noticed that her baby developed an urticarial rash all over her body very soon after the formula feed. What is the most likely mechanism of this baby’s urticaria?',
  options:['IgE-mediated cow milk allergy','Non-IgE mediated cow milk allergy','Non-allergic hypersensitivity','Lactose intolerance','Immune complex-mediated hypersensitivity reaction'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Immediate urticaria after exposure is due to IgE-mediated allergy. Non-IgE allergy causes delayed GI symptoms.\n\n---\n\n`26)allergy_.txt` draws exactly this line: cow’s milk protein allergy splits into an IgE-mediated form with immediate, often cutaneous reactions (urticaria, angio-oedema, anaphylaxis) and a non-IgE-mediated form that presents with delayed gastrointestinal signs — proctitis with bloody stools, vomiting, or poor feeding over hours to days, as seen at `pedep-nut-72`. A body-wide urticarial rash appearing "very soon after" the first formula feed is the immediate, IgE-driven pattern.\n\nWhy the other options fail: *non-IgE mediated allergy* produces delayed GI signs, not immediate urticaria. *Non-allergic hypersensitivity* and *lactose intolerance* are non-immune mechanisms and do not produce urticaria at all — lactose intolerance causes bloating, gas and diarrhoea from unabsorbed sugar, not a skin reaction. *Immune complex-mediated hypersensitivity* (type III) is a delayed, days-to-weeks reaction (e.g. serum sickness), not an immediate rash.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Immediate urticaria minutes after a first cow’s-milk-formula feed is IgE-mediated cow’s milk protein allergy; non-IgE cow’s milk allergy instead causes delayed gastrointestinal signs.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.364' },

{ id:'pedep-nut-65', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-pem',
  stem:'Which of the following is a feature of marasmus?',
  options:['Dermatosis','Gross muscle wasting','Edema','Hair changes'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Marasmus = severe wasting without edema. Kwashiorkor shows edema and dermatosis.\n\n---\n\n`16) P.E.M.txt` prints the two syndromes as a direct contrast: marasmus is "No Edema, No Dermatosis, No Hair changes (Or mild ones)" alongside "Growth failure (Wt. <60%)... Loss of subcutaneous fat, Muscle wasting." Kwashiorkor is the reverse pattern — oedema, dermatosis and hair changes are its hallmark, on a background of comparatively preserved fat stores. Gross muscle wasting from severe overall calorie deficit is therefore the one option that belongs to marasmus rather than kwashiorkor.\n\nWhy the wrong options fail: dermatosis, edema and hair changes are each explicitly listed as absent or only mild in marasmus, and are instead the features that define kwashiorkor — the mirror-image question at `pedep-nut-52` and `pedep-nut-16` covers the kwashiorkor side of the same contrast.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Gross muscle wasting without edema, dermatosis or hair changes is the marasmus pattern — the opposite of kwashiorkor’s edema-dermatosis-hair-change triad.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.368' },

{ id:'pedep-nut-66', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-vit',
  stem:'Which of the following laboratory findings is diagnostic of infantile rickets?',
  options:['Normal calcium level and low phosphorus level','Low calcium level and normal phosphorus level','High calcium level and low phosphorus level','High calcium level and normal phosphorus level','High calcium level and high phosphorus level'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Rickets typically shows low phosphate, normal/low calcium, and high alkaline phosphatase.\n\n---\n\n`Vitamin deficiency  Rickets.txt` gives the reference-range biochemistry behind this: "Serum calcium: usually normal due to compensatory hyperparathyroidism," while "Serum phosphate: Low (<3 mg/dl) due to compensatory hyperparathyroidism (normal = 4.5–6.5 mg/dl)." PTH raises calcium reabsorption enough to keep serum calcium near-normal while it simultaneously wastes phosphate at the kidney, so the diagnostic pattern is a **normal** calcium alongside a **low** phosphorus, not a low calcium.\n\nWhy the wrong options fail: options with a **low** calcium (B) understate the PTH compensation that keeps calcium normal in typical infantile rickets. Every option with a **high** calcium (C, D, E) reverses the direction of the calcium change entirely — active rickets does not raise serum calcium. A **normal** phosphorus (D) misses the phosphate wasting that is the more reliable early biochemical marker.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Infantile rickets classically shows a compensated normal serum calcium with a low serum phosphate, from PTH-driven renal phosphate wasting.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.370' },

{ id:'pedep-nut-67', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-pem',
  stem:'Which of the following is a key sign of protein-energy malnutrition in infants?',
  options:['Edema','Hyperactivity','Hypoactivity','Diarrhea','Polyuria'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Edema is a hallmark of protein-energy malnutrition, especially kwashiorkor. It results from low albumin and protein deficiency.\n\n---\n\n`16) P.E.M.txt` lists oedema as "ALWAYS PRESENT" in kwashiorkor and among the consequences of PEM more broadly, arising through the same low-albumin, low-oncotic-pressure mechanism detailed at `pedep-nut-16`. A malnourished infant is also typically apathetic and lethargic rather than active, which is worth noting against the two activity-level distractors here.\n\nWhy the wrong options fail: *hyperactivity* is the opposite of the lethargy/apathy that malnourished children actually show. *Hypoactivity* is closer to the true clinical picture but is not the option the box or the lecture material singles out as the *key* diagnostic sign — oedema is the specific, visible hallmark used to flag PEM at the bedside. *Diarrhea* can accompany or precipitate PEM but is not itself a defining sign of the malnourished state. *Polyuria* is not a feature of PEM.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Edema is the bedside hallmark sign of protein-energy malnutrition (kwashiorkor), from hypoalbuminaemia lowering plasma oncotic pressure.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.372' },

{ id:'pedep-nut-68', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-vit',
  stem:'Which vitamin is often deficient in breastfed infants?',
  options:['Vitamin A','Vitamin B12','Vitamin C','Vitamin D','Vitamin E'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Exclusively breastfed infants often develop vitamin D deficiency, since breast milk is low in vitamin D. This can lead to rickets if not supplemented.\n\n---\n\nThis is the general recall version of the risk-factor pattern worked through in full at `pedep-nut-60` and `pedep-nut-69`: human milk carries too little vitamin D to meet an infant’s needs without either maternal supplementation, adequate sun exposure, or direct infant supplementation, so exclusively breastfed infants are the population `17) Vitamin D Defficiency Rickets(1).txt` and `Vitamin deficiency  Rickets.txt` both name as most at risk of rickets.\n\nWhy the wrong options fail: vitamin A, C and E are not flagged in the cached material as characteristically low in breast milk. Vitamin B12 deficiency in a breastfed infant is a real but much narrower scenario — it depends on the mother herself being B12-deficient (e.g. strict veganism), not a general property of breast milk the way vitamin D’s low content is.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Breast milk is characteristically low in vitamin D, making exclusively breastfed, unsupplemented infants the population most at risk of vitamin D deficiency.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.374' },

{ id:'pedep-nut-69', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-vit',
  stem:'A 6-month-old exclusively breastfed infant presents with wide anterior fontanelle and craniotabes. The mother follows a vegan diet and does not take any supplements. What is the most appropriate next step?',
  options:['Begin Vitamin D supplementation','Start calcium supplementation','Introduce solid foods','Reassure and monitor','Perform skull X-rays'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> The findings suggest rickets due to vitamin D deficiency, especially in a vegan mother not supplementing. Calcium deficiency is less likely.\n\n---\n\n`17) Vitamin D Defficiency Rickets(1).txt` names craniotabes — thinning of the outer table of the skull, detected by firm, gentle finger pressure — as "the earliest bony change" in rickets, alongside a wide anterior fontanelle from delayed ossification. `Vitamin deficiency  Rickets.txt` separately lists low maternal vitamin D nutrition during pregnancy as a printed risk factor for infantile rickets. **The specific detail that an unsupplemented vegan mother compounds this risk is *not taken from the course material*** — grepped "vegan" and "vegetarian" together with "vitamin D" and found only a vegetarian-diet line under vitamin A deficiency, not vitamin D — but it follows the same logic the lecture material does give: a mother with little or no dietary/sunlight vitamin D has little to transfer via breast milk, which is already a poor vitamin D source in its own right, so an exclusively breastfed 6-month-old in this situation is doubly deprived. The correct next step is to start vitamin D treatment, not merely to investigate further, since the clinical picture is already diagnostic.\n\nWhy the other options fail: *calcium supplementation* treats the wrong deficiency — rickets here is a vitamin D problem, and calcium alone will not correct the underlying defect. *Introduce solid foods* does not address a vitamin D shortfall and is not the specific next step. *Reassure and monitor* under-treats an infant with active bony changes. *Skull X-rays* would confirm rather than treat, and the clinical findings are already diagnostic enough to start treatment without waiting on imaging.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Craniotabes and a wide fontanelle in an exclusively breastfed infant of an unsupplemented vegan mother is vitamin D-deficiency rickets — start vitamin D rather than investigate further.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.376' },

{ id:'pedep-nut-70', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-vit',
  stem:'What is appropriate about the role of vitamins in children’s nutrition?',
  options:['Folic acid has no role in blood production','Vitamin K main function is in the synthesis of RBCs','Vitamin E deficiency leads to decreased clot generation','Vitamin B12 deficiency is common in infants','Vitamin C deficiency can lead to petechiae and mucosal bleeding'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Vitamin C is required for collagen synthesis; deficiency causes scurvy with bleeding signs.\n\n---\n\nThis is the same five vitamin statements as `pedep-nut-58`, reordered so the Vitamin C statement moves from position A to position E — the key moves with it, from 0 to 4, and each `answer` here is checked against this page’s own printed key, not carried over from the sibling. The full vitamin function/deficiency table anchored at `pedep-nut-58` covers all five statements; only the wording differs slightly (this page prints "Vitamin K main function," without the possessive "K’s," transcribed as printed).\n\nWhy the wrong options fail, in the table at `pedep-nut-58`: vitamin K’s function is clotting-factor carboxylation, not RBC synthesis; vitamin E deficiency causes haemolysis, not decreased clot generation; vitamin B12 deficiency is not characterised as common in infants generally; and folic acid has a direct, printed role in blood production (megaloblastic anaemia on deficiency) — the reverse of "no role."\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Vitamin C deficiency (scurvy) causes petechiae and mucosal bleeding via defective collagen synthesis — the same five statements as `pedep-nut-58`, reordered so the correct statement sits at position E.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.378' },

{ id:'pedep-nut-71', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-vit',
  stem:'A 9-month-old boy presented to the emergency room with convulsions. He had a history of mild diarrhea for 3 days and was afebrile. His mother described that he was not thirsty and had no past history of convulsions. The examination revealed a large head, prominent fontanelles, and broad wrists. His eyes and skin were normal, and rectal temperature was 37.8°C. Which of the following is expected in his investigations?',
  options:['Normal calcium level, low phosphate level, high alkaline phosphatase level','Low calcium level, low phosphate level, high alkaline phosphatase level','Low calcium level, normal phosphate level, normal alkaline phosphatase level','Normal calcium level, normal phosphate level, high alkaline phosphatase level','Low calcium level, normal phosphate level, high alkaline phosphatase level'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> This 9-month-old with convulsions, large head, open fontanelles, and broad wrists has vitamin D deficiency rickets.\n> • Vitamin D deficiency → ↓ calcium absorption → hypocalcemia.\n> • Secondary hyperparathyroidism → ↑ phosphate excretion → hypophosphatemia.\n> • Alkaline phosphatase ↑ due to active osteoblasts trying to mineralize bone.\n> Classic labs in nutritional rickets = low calcium + low phosphate + high ALP.\n\n---\n\nThis vignette shares its clinical picture — infant with convulsions, mild diarrhoea, afebrile, large head, wide fontanelle, broad wrists — with `pedep-nut-62`, but the two are not the same question: `pedep-nut-62` asks for the emergency **treatment** and keys IV calcium gluconate; this page asks what the **investigations** are expected to show, and keys the biochemical pattern above. Read together they cover the same case end to end — diagnosis-to-labs here, labs-to-treatment there.\n\nOn the biochemistry itself: this page’s box keys a **low** calcium alongside low phosphate and high ALP, which is worth flagging against `pedep-nut-66`’s printed teaching that calcium is "usually normal due to compensatory hyperparathyroidism" — the two pages of the same source do not agree with each other on whether calcium stays normal or drops in classic infantile rickets. **The disagreement is recorded here, and the key is left exactly as this page prints it** — option B, low calcium/low phosphate/high ALP — rather than resolved in favour of either page.\n\nWhy the other options fail on this page’s own key: a **normal** calcium (A, D) does not match this box’s explicit "hypocalcemia." A **normal** phosphate (C, E) does not match "hypophosphatemia." A **normal** alkaline phosphatase (C) contradicts the active-osteoblast mechanism the box itself gives.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Investigations in infantile vitamin D-deficiency rickets classically show low calcium, low phosphate and high alkaline phosphatase — this page’s key disagrees with `pedep-nut-66`’s printed "usually normal calcium," and both are recorded as printed rather than reconciled.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.380 (the explanation box is printed alone on p.381)' },

{ id:'pedep-nut-72', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-feed',
  stem:'A 4-month-old baby girl is assessed by her general practitioner because of constant crying and poor feeding. She is fed by bottle on infant formula. Her mother tearfully complains that she is finding it very difficult to cope. Her development, growth, and physical examination are normal, but her stools frequently contain streaks of blood. What is the likely cause of the girl’s problem?',
  options:['An inherited genetic condition','Cow’s milk protein allergy','Down syndrome','Gastroesophageal reflux','Cystic fibrosis'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Blood-streaked stools in an otherwise healthy infant suggest cow’s milk protein allergy. Reflux does not cause blood.\n\n---\n\n`26)allergy_.txt` describes exactly this presentation: non-IgE-mediated cow’s milk protein allergy "sometimes presents in an infant with blood in the stools in the first few weeks of life from proctitis" — a delayed, gut-localised reaction, distinct from the immediate IgE-mediated urticaria covered at `pedep-nut-63`. Normal growth and development argue against a chronic systemic or genetic disease process, and the temporal link to formula feeding (rather than breastfeeding) fits a reaction to cow’s milk protein specifically.\n\nWhy the wrong options fail: *an inherited genetic condition* and *Down syndrome* would be expected to show dysmorphic features or growth/developmental abnormality, both explicitly normal here. *Gastroesophageal reflux* causes regurgitation and feeding difficulty but, as the box states plainly, does not cause blood in the stool. *Cystic fibrosis* typically presents with respiratory signs, failure to thrive, or meconium ileus, not isolated bloody stools with otherwise normal growth.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Blood-streaked stools with poor feeding in an otherwise well, normally growing formula-fed infant points to non-IgE cow’s milk protein allergy (proctitis), not reflux or a genetic condition.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.383' },

{ id:'pedep-nut-73', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-feed',
  stem:'Which of the following is considered a drawback of breastfeeding?',
  options:['Reduces the risk of diabetes mellitus','Promotes maternal-child bonding','Provides passive immunity to the infant','May be harmful in some genetic disorders'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Generally breastfeeding is beneficial, but in rare metabolic disorders like galactosemia, it can be harmful.\n\n---\n\n`43)Infantile cholestasis_.txt` prints galactosaemia among its own vignette questions and directly names it as "an absolute contraindication to breast feeding," alongside cataracts, jaundice and a positive septic-screen picture as classic galactosaemia clues. The mechanism is that breast milk’s lactose is broken down to glucose and **galactose**, which a galactosaemic infant cannot metabolise past the first step, so ordinary breastfeeding (or any lactose-containing feed) becomes actively harmful rather than merely suboptimal.\n\nWhy the wrong options fail: *reduces the risk of diabetes mellitus*, *promotes maternal-child bonding*, and *provides passive immunity* are all well-established **benefits** of breastfeeding — the opposite of a drawback — leaving the genetic-disorder exception as the one genuine downside among the four.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Breastfeeding is an absolute contraindication in galactosaemia, since breast milk’s lactose is a direct source of the galactose these infants cannot metabolise — the only listed drawback among otherwise well-established benefits.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.385' },

{ id:'pedep-nut-75', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-feed',
  stem:'A 10-day-old infant is brought in by her parents, who are concerned that she is not breastfeeding well. The infant was born at term and has been exclusively breastfed. On examination, the baby is alert but has lost 10% of her birth weight. Which of the following is the next best step in management?',
  options:['Advise immediate weaning and start formula','Encourage more frequent breastfeeding sessions','Introduce solid foods','Supplement with water between feeds'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> A 10% weight loss is common in the first days but should improve. The best management is encouraging effective, frequent breastfeeding, not formula or water.\n\n---\n\n`8) COMMON PROBLEMS IN THE TERM NEWBORN.txt` names ineffective or insufficient breastfeeding as a driver of excess early neonatal weight loss and jaundice risk, consistent with the box’s framing here. **The specific 10% figure as the accepted upper limit of normal early weight loss is not spelled out as a numeric cut-off in the cached material** — grepped "10%" together with "weight loss" and found no explicit percentage threshold stated — so the exact number is *not taken from the course material*, though the underlying principle (early weight loss is expected and usually resolves with better feeding, not by stopping breastfeeding) is. At 10 days and still alert with no other red-flag findings given, the priority is to fix the feeding technique and frequency, per the underfeeding criteria worked through at `pedep-nut-53`, rather than to intervene more drastically.\n\nWhy the wrong options fail: *immediate weaning and formula* abandons breastfeeding prematurely, when the more conservative fix (more frequent, more effective feeds) has not been tried. *Introduce solid foods* is inappropriate and unsafe at 10 days old. *Supplement with water between feeds* dilutes calorie intake further and carries its own risk (water intoxication, displaced feeds) without addressing the underlying feeding problem.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Ten-percent early weight loss in an otherwise well breastfed neonate is managed by improving breastfeeding frequency and effectiveness, not by weaning to formula or supplementing with water — the numeric threshold itself is not taken from the course material.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.389' },

{ id:'pedep-nut-76', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'nutrition-feed',
  stem:'Which specialized milk formula best suits infants with cow’s milk protein allergy?',
  options:['Lactose free formula','Amino acid-based formula','Soy protein formula','Formula high in omega 3 fatty acids','Medium-chain fatty acids formula'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> In cow’s milk protein allergy (CMPA), the immune system reacts to the proteins in cow’s milk (casein and whey). Therefore, formulas that still contain these proteins (even in modified form) can trigger allergic reactions.\n\n---\n\n`15.2) Infant Feeding.txt`’s "Types of infant formula" slide lists amino acid-based (elemental) formula among the special formulas, alongside hydrolysed formula, lactose-free formula and low-birth-weight formula. Because CMPA is an immune reaction to intact cow’s milk protein, only a formula broken all the way down to free amino acids removes every trace of the allergenic protein structure the immune system recognises — a partially or even extensively hydrolysed formula can still carry enough intact peptide to provoke a reaction in a sensitised infant.\n\nWhy the wrong options fail: *lactose-free formula* addresses lactose intolerance, a sugar-digestion problem, not a protein-allergy one — it still contains cow’s milk protein. *Soy protein formula* substitutes one intact foreign protein for another, and cross-reactivity between soy and cow’s milk protein allergy is common enough that it is not the safest choice. *Omega-3-enriched* and *medium-chain fatty acid* formulas address fat composition, not the protein antigen driving CMPA, and do nothing to remove the allergenic protein.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Amino acid-based (elemental) formula removes the intact cow’s milk protein structure entirely, making it the formula of choice for cow’s milk protein allergy — lactose-free and soy formulas both still carry an allergenic protein risk.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.391' },

{
  id:'pedep-gi-1', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gi-diarrhoea',
  stem:'Benjamin is a 6-year-old boy who is seen in the Paediatric Emergency Department. He has been vomiting and has had diarrhea for 3 days. His stool is watery and foul-smelling but contains no blood. He has never left the UK. Examination reveals mild dehydration but is otherwise normal. What is the most likely organism that has caused his symptoms?',
  options:['Campylobacter','Escherichia coli','Giardia lamblia','Rotavirus','Shigella'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Rotavirus is a common cause of acute viral gastroenteritis in young children, especially under 5. It presents with vomiting, foul-smelling watery diarrhea, and often occurs without blood or recent travel.\n\n---\n\n**Sorting the five organisms on this list.** `23).1) Acute diarrhea.txt` splits the causes of acute gastroenteritis by relative frequency: viruses (~70%) — Rotavirus, Norovirus, adenovirus and others — bacteria (10–20%) — Campylobacter jejuni, non-typhoid Salmonella, enteropathogenic E. coli, Shigella, Yersinia, Shiga-toxin-producing E. coli, Salmonella typhi/paratyphi, Vibrio cholerae — and protozoa (<10%) — Cryptosporidium, Giardia lamblia, Entamoeba histolytica. Of these, the deck names Rotavirus, enterotoxigenic E. coli, Shigella, Campylobacter jejuni and Cryptosporidium as the most important causes of acute infective diarrhoea.\n\n**Why the pattern here points to a virus, and specifically Rotavirus.** The deck describes two mechanisms behind acute watery diarrhoea — secretory (toxin-driven) and osmotic (villous damage) — both of which produce loose, watery stool rather than the bloody, mucoid stool of invasive bacterial or amoebic dysentery. Benjamin\'s stool is watery and foul-smelling with **no blood**, he has **no travel history**, and he is under 5 — exactly the age band and stool character the deck attaches to viral gastroenteritis, of which Rotavirus is the single largest contributor.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Campylobacter* and *Shigella* are bacterial causes that classically produce dysentery — blood and mucus in the stool, sometimes with tenesmus — not this watery, non-bloody picture. *Escherichia coli*, in its more invasive or Shiga-toxin-producing forms, is likewise associated with bloody stool and carries a risk of haemolytic uraemic syndrome, again not matching this presentation. *Giardia lamblia* is a protozoal cause typically linked to contaminated water exposure and produces a more prolonged, often greasy, malabsorptive-type diarrhoea rather than a 3-day acute illness with no travel history.\n\n**⚠️ Within-bank reprint — p.538, and the two printings are not identical.** The endpoint file prints this question twice: here on p.414 in a 6-year-old who has never left the UK, and again on p.538 in a 2-year-old with the travel-history line dropped. The five options, their order and the key are the same on both pages. It is held once in the app, under the p.414 printing, whose stem is the fuller of the two.\n\n**The age difference is worth naming rather than smoothing over.** Rotavirus incidence peaks below two years, so the p.538 printing is the more typical presentation and the 6-year-old kept here is the harder one — the reasoning holds for both, but it is the stool character, the short course and the absence of blood that carry it at either age, not the age itself.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Watery, foul-smelling, non-bloody diarrhoea with vomiting and no travel history in a young child is most likely viral gastroenteritis — Rotavirus is the single most common cause.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.414 (the same question is printed again on p.538 with the child\'s age changed from 6 years to 2 years and the travel-history line dropped; same five options, same order, same key)'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-2', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gi-diarrhoea',
  stem:'Rodney, a boisterous 2-year-old, has had diarrhea for the last 3 months. He produces up to four stools a day, which are loose, brown in color, and usually contain undigested food. The rest of the family is well. He has never been abroad. Examination is normal, and his personal child health record shows that he is growing along the 50th centile.\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Chronic non-specific diarrhea','Coeliac disease',"Cow's milk protein allergy",'Inflammatory bowel disease','Lactose intolerance'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> This condition, also called toddler\'s diarrhea, presents with chronic loose stools containing undigested food but normal growth and development. It is common in well children aged 1-5 years.\n\n---\n\n**The name, and the one fact that carries the whole question.** *(Toddler\'s diarrhoea is not named in the cached lectures — not taken from the course material.)* The single finding that does the diagnostic work here is on the growth chart, not the stool chart: Rodney is **growing steadily along the 50th centile** despite three months of loose stools. `23).1) Acute diarrhea.txt` defines diarrhoea itself simply as an increase in the fluidity, volume or number of stools relative to a child\'s usual habit, and lists loose stools containing undigested food as a benign, self-limited pattern once a malabsorptive or inflammatory cause is excluded — which normal growth does here.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Coeliac disease* causes malabsorption severe enough to blunt growth, and typically comes with additional features such as anaemia or abdominal distension — contrast `pedep-gi-21`, where the same-shaped stem is paired with 4 kg of weight loss and microcytic anaemia and the key becomes coeliac disease instead. *Cow\'s milk protein allergy* usually presents with additional features such as eczema, blood-streaked stool, or faltering growth, and is most often triggered by a change in feed. *Inflammatory bowel disease* is a chronic autoimmune process that would be expected to impair growth and often brings blood in the stool, abdominal pain or systemic symptoms — none of which are present. *Lactose intolerance* would be expected to track with dairy intake and often causes bloating and flatulence from fermentation, which is not described here.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Chronic loose stools with undigested food in a well toddler who is growing normally is chronic non-specific (toddler\'s) diarrhoea — a diagnosis that rests on normal growth, not on the stool itself.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.416'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-3', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gi-diarrhoea',
  stem:'Ellie is a 4-year-old girl who has been complaining of tummy pain for a month. It is worse when she goes to the toilet; her stools are firm, and she opens her bowels only every 2-3 days. She has not had any vomiting. For the last 2 weeks, her stools have become loose. On examination, she has a mass in the left iliac fossa. What is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Appendix mass','Constipation','Gastroenteritis','Intussusception',"Hirschsprung's disease"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Chronic constipation can lead to overflow diarrhea and a palpable fecal mass in the left iliac fossa. Pain with defecation and hard stools suggest functional constipation.\n\n---\n\n**Why constipation is right.** `23).2)Constipation.txt` defines constipation as delay or difficulty in defecation present for **2 or more weeks** and sufficient to cause distress — Ellie\'s month-long history of pain, firm stools and infrequent defecation every 2–3 days fits directly. The deck also names a palpable **fecal mass on the left lower quadrant (loaded sigmoid colon)** as an examination finding, matching Ellie\'s left iliac fossa mass exactly. Her recent switch to loose stool is the classic overflow pattern the box describes: liquid stool leaking around an impacted, hard faecal mass.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Appendix mass* would be expected to sit in the right, not left, iliac fossa, and typically follows an acute febrile illness rather than a month of painful, infrequent defecation. *Gastroenteritis* is an acute illness lasting days, not the chronic month-long course described here. *Intussusception* presents acutely with episodic colicky pain, vomiting and a right-sided mass, often with red-currant-jelly stool, not a gradually evolving month-long constipation history. *Hirschsprung\'s disease* causes constipation from birth with delayed passage of meconium; Ellie\'s history began only a month ago in what reads as a previously normal child, which argues against a congenital aganglionic cause.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'A left iliac fossa fecal mass with a history of painful, infrequent hard stools and new overflow-type looseness is constipation, not an acute surgical abdomen.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.418'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-4', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gastroenterology',
  stem:'Alysha is a 2-month-old baby who is seen in the paediatric outpatient department. She was born at term, weighing 3.5 kg, and is breastfed. Her mother is concerned as she has vomited some of the milk after most feeds since birth. She cries when she vomits. She is continuing to grow along the 50th centile. What is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Gastro-oesophageal reflux','Helicobacter pylori infection','Infant colic','Overfeeding','Pyloric stenosis'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Physiological reflux is common in infants due to immature lower esophageal sphincter tone. Vomiting without weight loss or systemic signs supports this diagnosis.\n\n---\n\n**Why reflux is right.** `22)Vomiting.txt` names gastro-oesophageal reflux as the **common cause of vomiting in infants**, driven by functional immaturity of the lower oesophageal sphincter, the predominantly fluid diet, a horizontal posture and a short intra-abdominal length of oesophagus. It adds that most infants with reflux are **putting on weight normally**, exactly as Alysha is doing along the 50th centile, and that reflux resolves spontaneously by around 12 months as the sphincter matures and the diet becomes more solid.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Helicobacter pylori infection* is an uncommon cause of vomiting in this age group and would be expected to bring features of peptic disease rather than a simple, feed-related posset since birth. *Infant colic* is defined by excessive, otherwise-unexplained crying rather than vomiting as the primary complaint. *Overfeeding* is a genuine differential for regurgitation, but nothing in the stem quantifies excess feed volumes, and the lecture flags reflux — not overfeeding — as the default first diagnosis for this presentation. *Pyloric stenosis* presents at 2–8 weeks with **non-bilious vomiting that increases in frequency and forcefulness over time**, eventually becoming projectile, and typically brings weight loss if presentation is delayed; Alysha\'s vomiting has been stable "since birth" rather than progressively worsening, and her growth is normal, arguing against it.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Vomiting after most feeds since birth in a thriving, normally growing infant is physiological gastro-oesophageal reflux, not pyloric stenosis or overfeeding.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.420'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-5', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gi-abdopain',
  stem:'Claire, a 7-year-old girl, has had abdominal pain for the last 6 months. On several occasions, it has been sufficiently severe for her to be sent home early from school. The pain happens once or twice a week in the afternoon or early evening. It is periumbilical in nature. It does not wake her at night. She has not had vomiting or diarrhoea. She is growing well. Her examination is normal. Her urine is clear on dipstick testing. What is the most likely cause for her pain?',
  options:['Functional abdominal pain','Gastritis','Hepatitis A','Irritable bowel syndrome','Meckel diverticulum'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Functional abdominal pain is common in school-aged children and is not associated with red flag features like weight loss or night waking. It often affects otherwise healthy, growing children.\n\n---\n\n**Why functional abdominal pain is right.** `24)Abdominal pain.txt` sets out an organic-versus-functional table: organic pain is **localised** and **may be nocturnal**, while functional pain is **vague and midline** and **never nocturnal**. Claire\'s pain is periumbilical (vague midline) and specifically does not wake her at night. The deck\'s alarm-signal list includes nocturnal symptoms, weight loss or growth retardation, and an abnormal urinalysis — Claire has none of these: she is growing well, her exam is normal and her urine dipstick is clear. Functional pain is formally a "diagnosis of exclusion", and here the exclusion is achieved by a careful history rather than by investigation.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Gastritis* would be expected to bring epigastric rather than periumbilical pain, often with a relation to meals, and sometimes haematemesis — none described. *Hepatitis A* would be expected to bring jaundice, malaise or hepatomegaly, which a normal examination excludes. *Irritable bowel syndrome* is a functional disorder too, but by the deck\'s own definition it is characterised by crampy pain **linked to altered bowel habit** (diarrhoea or constipation around the pain) — Claire has had no diarrhoea, whereas the sibling vignette at `pedep-gi-26` describes exactly that bowel-habit link and keys IBS instead. *Meckel diverticulum* classically presents with painless rectal bleeding from ectopic gastric mucosa, not recurrent afternoon abdominal pain.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Chronic periumbilical pain, never nocturnal, in a well, normally growing child with a normal exam and urinalysis is functional abdominal pain — a diagnosis made by excluding alarm features on history, not by testing.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.422'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-6', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gi-diarrhoea',
  stem:"Ben, aged 9 months, has had a 3-day history of diarrhoea and vomiting. On examination, he is found to be quiet but alert, is tachypnoeic, has a tachycardia but normal pulses, a dry mouth, no mottling of the skin but reduced skin turgor and a sunken fontanelle. The capillary refill time is 2 seconds. His blood pressure is normal for his age. He continues to vomit even with an oral rehydration solution given via a nasogastric tube. Ben's plasma sodium is found to be 156 mmol/L (normal range: 135-145 mmol/L). He needs fluid as he has clinical dehydration. How would this fluid best be replaced?",
  options:['Immediate bolus of 20 ml/kg of 0.9% sodium chloride followed by reassessment and replacement of the remaining deficit over 24 hours with 0.9% sodium chloride solution','Rehydration over 6 hours followed by repeat urea and electrolyte measurement and maintenance fluid only for a further 18 hours','Rehydration over 24 hours with 0.18% sodium chloride/5% glucose solution','Rehydration over 24 hours with 0.9% sodium chloride/5% glucose solution','Rehydration over 48 hours with 0.9% or 0.45% saline'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Ben has hypernatremic dehydration, which requires slow and cautious rehydration over 48 hours to avoid the risk of cerebral oedema. Rapid correction with hypotonic fluids or quick boluses is dangerous due to the osmotic shifts involved in hypernatremia.\n\n---\n\n**Classifying Ben\'s dehydration.** `23).1) Acute diarrhea.txt` names hypertonic (hypernatraemic) dehydration as one of the three types alongside isotonic and hypotonic, and notes it is the **least common** pattern (10–15% of cases) but carries the most dangerous fluid physiology. Ben\'s plasma sodium of 156 mmol/L against a normal range of 135–145 confirms this is the hypernatraemic type, and clinically his signs are consistent with the deck\'s general assessment findings for dehydration — a dry mouth, sunken fontanelle and reduced skin turgor — on top of a 3-day diarrhoeal illness he cannot rehydrate orally.\n\n**Why slow, 48-hour correction is the safe answer.** *(The specific 48-hour timeframe and the cerebral-oedema mechanism are general paediatric fluid-therapy knowledge — not taken from the course material, which covers dehydration typing and standard rehydration volumes but not the hypernatraemic-specific protocol.)* In hypernatraemic dehydration, water has been lost disproportionately to sodium, so brain cells have adapted by generating intracellular osmoles to protect their volume against the high extracellular osmolality. If serum sodium is corrected too quickly, water shifts rapidly into these osmotically primed brain cells, causing cerebral oedema, seizures and can be fatal. Rehydrating slowly, over roughly 48 hours, with a fluid that does not itself lower serum osmolality too fast (isotonic 0.9% saline, or half-strength 0.45% saline used cautiously) avoids that swing.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Immediate 20 ml/kg bolus then 24-hour replacement* — Ben is not in shock (normal blood pressure, capillary refill 2 seconds, normal pulses), so a resuscitation bolus is not indicated by the deck\'s own severity-based fluid plan, and the follow-on 24-hour correction is still too fast for hypernatraemia. *Rehydration over 6 hours* is drastically too rapid and would risk a dangerous, rapid fall in osmolality. *Rehydration over 24 hours with 0.18% saline/5% glucose* pairs too fast a timeframe with a markedly hypotonic fluid relative to Ben\'s hypernatraemic state — the combination most likely to precipitate cerebral oedema. *Rehydration over 24 hours with 0.9% saline/5% glucose* uses an appropriately isotonic fluid but still corrects twice as fast as the recommended 48 hours.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Hypernatraemic (hypertonic) dehydration must be corrected slowly, over about 48 hours, with an isotonic or near-isotonic fluid — rapid correction risks a fatal osmotic shift into brain cells (cerebral oedema).',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.424, explanation box p.425'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-7', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gastroenterology',
  stem:'Matthew is a 3-day-old term infant who has not passed meconium since birth. On examination, his abdomen is distended, but the remainder of the examination is normal. An x-ray of the abdomen shows distended loops throughout the bowel, including the rectum. What is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Congenital hypothyroidism','Cystic fibrosis','Duodenal atresia',"Hirschsprung's disease",'Rectal atresia'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Failure to pass meconium in the first 48 hours with abdominal distension suggests Hirschsprung\'s disease. The rectum may still appear full on imaging due to functional obstruction.\n\n---\n\n**Why Hirschsprung\'s disease fits.** *(Hirschsprung\'s disease itself is not covered in the cached lecture set beyond a passing mention as a feature of Down syndrome — the pathophysiology and diagnostic reasoning below are general paediatric surgical knowledge, not taken from the course material.)* Hirschsprung\'s disease is caused by the congenital absence of ganglion cells in a segment of distal bowel, usually beginning at the rectum and extending proximally for a variable distance. The aganglionic segment cannot relax, so it acts as a functional obstruction: stool backs up proximal to it, meconium is passed late or not at all, and the abdomen distends. Failure to pass meconium within the first 24–48 hours of life is the classic trigger for suspecting it, and Matthew is 3 days old with no meconium and a distended abdomen. The x-ray finding of distension **throughout the bowel, including the rectum**, matches the box\'s point that the rectum can still look "full" on imaging even though it is functionally obstructed, because the bowel above the narrowed segment dilates with retained stool and gas.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Congenital hypothyroidism* can cause constipation as one feature among many (macroglossia, hypotonia, prolonged jaundice, lethargy), but it does not produce an acute picture of distended bowel loops on imaging in the first days of life, and Matthew\'s exam is otherwise normal. *Cystic fibrosis* can present as meconium ileus, but that classically obstructs more proximally in the small bowel and often shows a "soap-bubble" appearance on x-ray rather than distension extending all the way through to the rectum. *Duodenal atresia* obstructs high in the gut and classically produces bilious vomiting from day one with a "double bubble" on x-ray — gas in the stomach and duodenum only, not distension of the whole bowel including the rectum. *Rectal atresia* would be suspected on finding an abnormal or absent anal opening on examination, which is not described here.\n\n**A near-identical vignette reprints later in this section**, at `pedep-gi-19` (a 2-month-old with the same delayed-meconium/distension picture) and `pedep-gi-23` (the same story, now asked as which test confirms the diagnosis) — the reasoning above carries across all three.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Delayed passage of meconium beyond 24–48 hours with a distended abdomen and distended bowel loops (including the rectum) on x-ray is Hirschsprung\'s disease until proved otherwise.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.427'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-8', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gastroenterology',
  stem:'James, an 8-month-old infant, is brought to the Emergency Department by his parents. He is having episodes of abdominal pain and is just recovering from an upper respiratory tract infection. He seems well in between, but then suddenly seems to be in pain and looks pale. He has vomited several times. On questioning, he has had no blood in his stool but has not opened his bowels for 24 hours. Which of the following is the most likely cause of vomiting in this patient?',
  options:['Intussusception','Diabetic ketoacidosis','Appendicitis',"Coeliac disease",'Pyloric stenosis'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Sudden episodic abdominal pain with pallor and vomiting in infants suggests intussusception, often post-viral. Early features may occur before the passage of red currant jelly stool.\n\n---\n\n**This option list cycles through three vignettes in this bank** (`pedep-gi-8`, `pedep-gi-9`, `pedep-gi-10`), each picking a different one of the five causes of vomiting. Written once here, so the siblings only need to argue their own case:\n\n| Diagnosis | Signature | Grounded in |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Intussusception** (this entry) | Well infant with **sudden episodic pain, pallor, vomiting**, often post-viral, peak 3 months–2 years | `24)Abdominal pain.txt` |\n| **Diabetic ketoacidosis** (`pedep-gi-9`) | Vomiting + **deep/rapid (Kussmaul) breathing** + dehydration + weight loss, no diarrhoea | `51) DKA.txt` |\n| **Appendicitis** | Fever + progressive, localising pain + tenderness/rigidity (older child); irritability and feed refusal (infant) | `24)Abdominal pain.txt` |\n| **Coeliac disease** | Chronic, not acute — diarrhoea, weight loss/growth faltering over months *(general medical knowledge; not the acute-vomiting pattern this ladder is testing)* | — |\n| **Pyloric stenosis** (`pedep-gi-10`) | **2–8 weeks old**, forceful/projectile **non-bilious** vomiting, hungry straight after | `22)Vomiting.txt` |\n\n**Why James is intussusception specifically.** `24)Abdominal pain.txt` describes intussusception as the invagination of proximal bowel into a distal segment — most commonly ileum into caecum through the ileocaecal valve — and names it the **most common cause of intestinal obstruction in infants after the neonatal period**, with a peak age of **3 months to 2 years**. James is 8 months old, just recovering from a viral URTI (a recognised trigger, as enlarged Peyer\'s patches from the infection can act as a lead point), and has the classic pattern of being **well in between episodes** and then suddenly painful and pale — the box\'s "episodic" pattern. Not opening his bowels for 24 hours fits early bowel obstruction, appearing here before any red-currant-jelly stool, which the box notes may not yet have appeared.\n\n**Why the other four fail in James\'s case.** No Kussmaul breathing, dehydration or weight loss is described (against DKA). There is no fever and no localising right iliac fossa tenderness or rigidity described (against appendicitis, and James is also younger than the typical age). Coeliac disease is a chronic malabsorptive process, not an acute episodic pain-and-vomiting illness. Pyloric stenosis presents at 2–8 weeks with forceful, non-bilious vomiting worsening over time, not episodic pain in an 8-month-old.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'A previously well infant aged 3 months to 2 years with sudden episodic abdominal pain, pallor and vomiting, often after a viral illness, has intussusception until proved otherwise.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.429'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-9', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gastroenterology',
  stem:'Bridgitta, an 8-year-old girl, presents to her family doctor with vomiting and abdominal pain. Her vomiting only started today and she has no diarrhoea or fever. She looks unwell and has clinical dehydration on examination and has deep, rapid breathing. She is thirsty and pale. She has lost weight over the last few weeks. Which of the following is the most likely cause of the vomiting in this patient?',
  options:['Intussusception','Diabetic ketoacidosis','Appendicitis','Coeliac disease','Pyloric stenosis'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Vomiting with Kussmaul respiration, dehydration, and weight loss in a child points to new-onset type 1 diabetes with DKA. The absence of diarrhea supports a metabolic cause.\n\n---\n\nThis question shares its five-option list with `pedep-gi-8` and `pedep-gi-10` — the full comparison table is written there. Bridgitta\'s own discriminating token is the combination `51) DKA.txt` calls the **clinical signs of DKA**: nausea and vomiting, abdominal pain, and **tachypnoea** — here specifically "deep, rapid breathing", the Kussmaul pattern that compensates for metabolic acidosis. Add to that clinical dehydration, thirst and pallor (osmotic diuresis from hyperglycaemia), and weeks of weight loss (the classic build-up to a first presentation of type 1 diabetes), and the picture fits the deck\'s classical triad of DKA — hyperglycaemia, metabolic acidosis and ketonaemia/ketonuria — even though the actual blood results are not given in the stem. The **absence of diarrhoea or fever** is what the box leans on to exclude the infective, surgical and post-viral causes on the same list, since a metabolic cause does not need either.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Vomiting with Kussmaul (deep, rapid) breathing, dehydration and weeks of preceding weight loss in a child is new-onset diabetic ketoacidosis, even before any blood result is known.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.431'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-10', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gastroenterology',
  stem:'Noah is 5 weeks old and has been breastfeeding well and putting on weight. However, over the last 36 hours, he has been vomiting after almost every feed. The vomit goes everywhere, and he then wants to feed again. All the vomits are milky. He was born at term (birth weight 3.8 kg). Which of the following is the most likely cause of the vomiting in this patient?',
  options:['Intussusception','Diabetic ketoacidosis','Appendicitis','Coeliac disease','Pyloric stenosis'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Pyloric stenosis presents in young infants with non-bilious, forceful vomiting and hunger after vomiting. It typically appears between 2-8 weeks of age.\n\n---\n\nThis question shares its five-option list with `pedep-gi-8` and `pedep-gi-9` — the full comparison table is written there. Noah\'s own discriminating token is his age and the character of the vomit: `22)Vomiting.txt` places pyloric stenosis at **2–8 weeks of age** — Noah is 5 weeks — with **non-bilious vomiting that increases in frequency and forcefulness**, becoming forceful enough to "go everywhere" (the lay description of projectile vomiting), and with the infant **feeding normally, even hungrily, straight after vomiting**, exactly as Noah "wants to feed again". The vomit being "all milky" confirms it is non-bilious, ruling out the bile-stained emergencies (malrotation, obstruction) on the wider vomiting differential. Nothing here suggests episodic pain and pallor (against intussusception), Kussmaul breathing or weight loss (against DKA), fever or localising tenderness (against appendicitis), or a chronic malabsorptive course (against coeliac disease).\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Non-bilious, increasingly forceful vomiting in a 2–8-week-old who remains hungry and feeds eagerly straight after vomiting is pyloric stenosis.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.433'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-11', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gastroenterology',
  stem:'Amir was born by elective caesarean section for maternal pre-eclampsia. His birthweight was 3.3 kg. He is 36 hours old. He has started to establish breastfeeding but has been vomiting after every feed. The vomit is noted by the midwife to be green. On examination, his temperature is 37.2°C. His abdomen is slightly distended. The rest of his examination is normal. Which of the following is the most likely cause of the vomiting in this patient?',
  options:['Intussusception','Malrotation','Meningitis','Inflammatory bowel disease','Migraine'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Bilious vomiting in a neonate is a surgical emergency and strongly suggests malrotation with volvulus. The green vomit reflects obstruction distal to the ampulla of Vater.\n\n---\n\n**This option list is also shared, with `pedep-gi-12`** — the discriminator there is an older girl with a classic migraine, not a neonate. The table across both:\n\n| Diagnosis | Signature |\n|---|---|\n| **Malrotation** (this entry) | Neonate, **green (bile-stained) vomiting**, mild abdominal distension — surgical emergency |\n| Intussusception | Peak age 3 months–2 years, not a 36-hour-old neonate |\n| Meningitis | Fever, neck stiffness, lethargy — none described in either vignette |\n| Inflammatory bowel disease | Chronic course, not an acute neonatal or single-episode presentation |\n| **Migraine** (`pedep-gi-12`) | Older child/adolescent, unilateral headache with photophobia, normal exam |\n\n**Why malrotation is right for Amir.** `22)Vomiting.txt` is explicit that **"green bile in vomit is an emergency, as it suggests obstructed bowel"**, and that the more proximal the obstruction, the sooner it becomes bile-stained, **unless the obstruction is proximal to the ampulla of Vater** — the green colour tells you the block sits distal to where bile enters the gut. The same summary slide names malrotation, alongside intussusception and strangulated inguinal hernia, as the emergency causes of bile-stained vomiting to consider. Amir is a term neonate, only 36 hours old, vomiting bile after every feed with a distended abdomen — the classic early presentation of malrotation with volvulus, where the midgut has twisted around an abnormally narrow mesenteric attachment and needs urgent surgical correction to prevent bowel ischaemia.\n\n**Why each other option fails.** *Intussusception* has a peak age of 3 months to 2 years and would be very unusual in a 36-hour-old. *Meningitis* would be expected to bring fever, lethargy or neck stiffness — Amir\'s temperature is essentially normal and the rest of his exam is unremarkable. *Inflammatory bowel disease* is a chronic condition and does not present acutely in the first days of life. *Migraine* is not a neonatal diagnosis at all; it is the answer to the paired sibling question instead, in an adolescent with a headache.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Bile-stained (green) vomiting in a neonate is a surgical emergency until proved otherwise, and malrotation with volvulus is the diagnosis to exclude first.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.435'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-12', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gastroenterology',
  stem:'Jennifer, a 14-year-old girl, presents to the Emergency Department with a severe headache for the last 6 hours, mainly affecting the left side of her head. She just wants to lie still in the dark and dislikes being disturbed, but her mother is concerned as she has never had such an episode before and is normally a very lively girl who is doing well at school. She has been vomiting for the last 2 hours and cannot keep anything down and is also complaining of tummy pain. On examination, she is distressed by her headache and dislikes having the examination light shone on her. Her temperature is 37.2°C. She does not have neck stiffness or papilloedema. The rest of her examination is normal. Which of the following is the most likely cause of the vomiting in this patient?',
  options:['Intussusception','Malrotation','Meningitis','Inflammatory bowel disease','Migraine'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> This is a classic migraine presentation in an adolescent: unilateral headache, photophobia, and vomiting, with normal neurological exam and no meningeal signs. First episodes often present in adolescence.\n\n---\n\nThis question shares its five-option list with `pedep-gi-11`, where the full comparison table is written. Jennifer\'s own discriminating token is that the vomiting is **secondary to a headache**, not a primary gut problem: unilateral (left-sided) headache, photophobia (dislikes the exam light), a desire to lie still in the dark, and abdominal pain accompanying it are a recognisable migraine constellation, particularly as migraine can present with prominent nausea, vomiting and abdominal pain in children and adolescents. Her **normal temperature, absent neck stiffness and absent papilloedema** argue against meningitis or raised intracranial pressure, and a normal neurological exam with no prior episodes in an otherwise well, high-functioning teenager fits a first migraine attack rather than a chronic or structural cause. None of the other four options on the shared list — intussusception, malrotation, meningitis, inflammatory bowel disease — involve a headache at all, which is the feature driving this presentation.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Vomiting that is secondary to a unilateral, photophobic headache in an adolescent, with a normal neurological exam and no meningeal signs, is migraine.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.437'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-13', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gi-abdopain',
  stem:'Max, aged 9 years, has been brought to the Emergency Department because he is crying and saying his tummy hurts. He has had a 2-day history of fever and coryza. He has been drinking orange juice but has only eaten some jelly and yogurt. He has not opened his bowels. His temperature is 38.2°C. His throat is red and he has tender cervical lymph nodes. He is not dehydrated. He has mild generalized tenderness of the abdomen, with no guarding. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis in this patient?',
  options:['Constipation','Pneumonia','Appendicitis','Mesenteric adenitis','Functional abdominal pain'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Mesenteric adenitis commonly follows a viral illness and mimics appendicitis. The presence of fever, coryza, red throat, and cervical lymphadenopathy suggests a viral etiology causing inflamed mesenteric lymph nodes.\n\n---\n\n**This option list cycles through four vignettes in this bank** (`pedep-gi-13`, `pedep-gi-14`, `pedep-gi-15`, `pedep-gi-16`), each picking a different one of five causes of childhood abdominal pain. Written once here:\n\n| Diagnosis | Signature | Grounded in |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Mesenteric adenitis** (this entry) | Recent/current URTI + cervical lymphadenopathy, **mild, generalised** tenderness, **no guarding**, pain resolves in 24–48h | `24)Abdominal pain.txt` |\n| **Pneumonia** (`pedep-gi-14`) | Cough, tachypnoea, fever + **referred** RUQ/abdominal pain | `24)Abdominal pain.txt` |\n| **Appendicitis** (`pedep-gi-15`) | Progressive, **localising** RIF pain, fever, unable to stand straight, worsening course | `24)Abdominal pain.txt` |\n| **Constipation** (`pedep-gi-16`) | No bowel movement for days, palpable indentable fecal mass, minimal tenderness | `23).2)Constipation.txt` |\n| **Functional abdominal pain** | Chronic, vague, never nocturnal, no alarm signs *(not the acute-febrile pattern this ladder tests)* | `24)Abdominal pain.txt` |\n\n**Why Max is mesenteric adenitis specifically.** `24)Abdominal pain.txt` describes non-specific abdominal pain and mesenteric adenitis as pain that **resolves in 24–48 hours**, is **less severe than appendicitis**, with **variable** right iliac fossa tenderness, and **often accompanied by an upper respiratory tract infection with cervical lymphadenopathy**. Max has a 2-day history of fever and coryza, a red throat, tender cervical nodes, and only **mild, generalised** abdominal tenderness **with no guarding** — the deck\'s own contrast against appendicitis, where tenderness is expected to be more localised and accompanied by guarding as the disease progresses.\n\n**Why each other option fails for Max.** *Pneumonia* is not supported — there is no cough, tachypnoea or respiratory sign described. *Appendicitis* is undercut precisely by the mild, generalised (not localising) tenderness and complete absence of guarding — the deck\'s discriminator against mesenteric adenitis. *Constipation* does not fit a 2-day febrile coryzal illness with a red throat and lymphadenopathy, and no fecal mass is described. *Functional abdominal pain* is a chronic diagnosis of exclusion, not an acute febrile presentation with a clear preceding viral illness.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Mild, generalised, non-guarding abdominal pain in a child with a current or recent viral URTI and cervical lymphadenopathy is mesenteric adenitis, which mimics but is milder than appendicitis.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.439'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-14', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gi-abdopain',
  stem:"Pete, aged 4 years, is brought by his mother to the Emergency Department because he is crying and saying his tummy hurts. He has had a 2-day history of fever, coryza, and cough. He is sitting quietly on his mother's lap and is reluctant to play. He has a temperature of 38.2°C and a respiratory rate of 50 breaths/min. On examination, his throat is red and he has tender cervical lymph nodes. He complains of tenderness on palpation of the right upper quadrant of the abdomen. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis in this patient?",
  options:['Constipation','Pneumonia','Appendicitis','Mesenteric adenitis','Functional abdominal pain'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Abdominal pain in children can be a presenting feature of lower lobe pneumonia due to diaphragmatic irritation. The cough, coryza, fever, tachypnea, and RUQ tenderness point toward pneumonia rather than a primary abdominal pathology.\n\n---\n\nThis question shares its five-option list with `pedep-gi-13`, `pedep-gi-15` and `pedep-gi-16` — the full comparison table is written there. Pete\'s own discriminating token is his **respiratory rate of 50 breaths/min and a cough**, which the other three siblings do not have: `24)Abdominal pain.txt` lists **lower lobe pneumonia** as a cause of abdominal pain through **referred pain**, since the diaphragm shares its nerve supply with the lower chest and upper abdomen. Right upper quadrant tenderness with tachypnoea, fever and cough in a young child is the classic way pneumonia masquerades as an abdominal complaint — a diagnostic trap the same deck flags explicitly (pneumonia is listed among the acute infections that present with the fever–vomiting–abdominal-pain triad, and again among the "other causes" of referred abdominal pain). Nothing here is a surgical abdominal sign (no guarding, no progressive localisation), which is why the diagnosis lies in the chest rather than the gut.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'A raised respiratory rate and cough alongside upper-quadrant abdominal tenderness in a febrile child should raise lower lobe pneumonia as a cause of referred abdominal pain.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.441'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-15', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gi-abdopain',
  stem:'Molly, aged 10 years, is brought to the Paediatric Assessment Unit as she has been vomiting and had central abdominal pain for 2 days. She has also had some diarrhoea. She has only had apple juice and no food for the last day. Her pain is getting worse. On examination, she has a temperature of 38.2°C and a heart rate of 110 beats/min. She has mild dehydration. There is tenderness in the lower right abdomen, but no guarding. When asked to walk, she is unable to stand up straight because of pain. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis in this patient?',
  options:['Constipation','Pneumonia','Appendicitis','Mesenteric adenitis','Functional abdominal pain'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Appendicitis presents with worsening abdominal pain, usually starting centrally and localizing to the RIF. Inability to stand upright and focal tenderness despite mild dehydration is highly suggestive of appendicitis.\n\n---\n\nThis question shares its five-option list with `pedep-gi-13`, `pedep-gi-14` and `pedep-gi-16` — the full comparison table is written there. Molly\'s own discriminating token is the **trajectory and the gait sign**: `24)Abdominal pain.txt` describes appendicitis in older children as fever, nausea, vomiting and **right lower quadrant pain that localises and worsens**, and Molly\'s pain began centrally (periumbilical, the classic first phase as the appendix inflames) and is now **getting worse** and localising to the lower right abdomen. Being **unable to stand up straight** because of pain is a recognised sign of peritoneal irritation, and her tachycardia and mild dehydration reflect an evolving acute surgical illness rather than the milder, self-limiting course of mesenteric adenitis (Max, `pedep-gi-13`), whose pain is explicitly **less severe** and resolves within 24–48 hours. The absence of guarding here does not exclude appendicitis — guarding is a later sign, and the deck\'s own point is that the trajectory (worsening, localising) is what separates it from adenitis, not tenderness alone.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Central abdominal pain that worsens and localises to the right iliac fossa, with an inability to stand up straight, is appendicitis even before guarding appears.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.443'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-16', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gi-abdopain',
  stem:'Ted, aged 2 years, has had a 2-day history of low-grade fever and coryza. His mother has brought him to the Emergency Department because he is crying inconsolably. She thinks his tummy is hurting him. He has not opened his bowels for 2 days. He appears reasonably well, has minimal abdominal tenderness, but has an indentable mass on the left side of the abdomen. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Constipation','Pneumonia','Appendicitis','Mesenteric adenitis','Functional abdominal pain'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Chronic constipation can present with a palpable fecal mass and abdominal discomfort. The minimal tenderness and history of not opening bowels support this diagnosis over acute surgical causes\n\n---\n\nThis question shares its five-option list with `pedep-gi-13`, `pedep-gi-14` and `pedep-gi-15` — the full comparison table is written there. Ted\'s own discriminating token is the **left-sided, indentable mass** together with **minimal tenderness** and the child looking **"reasonably well"**: `23).2)Constipation.txt` names a fecal mass on the **left lower quadrant (loaded sigmoid colon)** as an examination finding of constipation, and an indentable quality is what a soft faecal mass, rather than an inflamed appendix or lymph node mass, feels like on palpation. Ted has not opened his bowels for 2 days, and his mild coryzal illness is incidental rather than the driver of his pain — unlike Max (`pedep-gi-13`), whose fever and lymphadenopathy are the point, or Molly (`pedep-gi-15`), whose pain is worsening and localising with systemic upset. The combination of minimal tenderness, no guarding, and a well-looking child argues against appendicitis or pneumonia, and there is no sign this is chronic enough to be labelled functional pain — this is a discrete, explicable episode.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'A left-sided, indentable abdominal mass with minimal tenderness in a well-looking child who has not opened his bowels for days is constipation, not a surgical or infective abdomen.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.445'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-17', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gastroenterology',
  stem:'A 5-week-old baby boy is brought to the Emergency Department with a 48-hour history of projectile vomiting. The infant is hungry after vomiting and has not opened his bowels in 3 days. Clinical examination reveals a mass in the left upper quadrant. A blood gas is performed: pH 7.50, pCO2 5.5 kPa, and bicarbonate 30 mmol/L. What does this blood gas represent?',
  options:['Normal findings','Metabolic acidosis','Metabolic alkalosis','Respiratory acidosis','Respiratory alkalosis'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Projectile vomiting leads to loss of gastric hydrochloric acid, resulting in metabolic alkalosis. The high pH, elevated bicarbonate, and clinical signs support this acid-base imbalance.\n\n---\n\nThe vignette is pyloric stenosis: onset around 5 weeks of age (typically 2–8 weeks), non-bilious **projectile** vomiting, a baby who remains **hungry after vomiting**, and infrequent stooling from reduced gut throughput. Repeated vomiting of gastric contents strips out hydrochloric acid and, with it, hydrogen and chloride ions; the kidney responds by conserving hydrogen and chloride at the expense of retaining bicarbonate, producing the classic **hypochloraemic, hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis** of pyloric stenosis (`22)Vomiting.txt`). The gas here — **raised pH, raised bicarbonate, pCO2 not low** — is exactly that picture: a primary metabolic process (bicarbonate up) with the pH moving in the same direction, not a respiratory one.\n\nEach distractor is a different pattern the same gas does not fit. *Normal findings* — a pH of 7.50 and bicarbonate of 30 mmol/L are both clearly outside the normal range. *Metabolic acidosis* would show a **low** pH and **low** bicarbonate, the opposite of what is printed. *Respiratory acidosis* needs a **low** pH with a **raised** pCO2 driving it; here pCO2 is unremarkable and pH is high, not low. *Respiratory alkalosis* needs a **low** pCO2 (hyperventilation blowing off CO2) with a compensatory fall in bicarbonate — here pCO2 is normal-to-mildly-raised (a degree of respiratory compensation for the metabolic alkalosis) and bicarbonate is high, not low — the reverse pattern.\n\nOne point in the stem is worth naming plainly rather than passing over: the mass of hypertrophic pyloric stenosis is classically described as an **olive-shaped mass in the right upper quadrant or epigastrium**, palpable during or after a test feed — not the left upper quadrant this stem prints. The rest of the vignette (age, projectile vomiting, hunger after vomiting, constipation, and the acid-base picture) is unambiguously pyloric stenosis regardless of that detail, and the side printed here is carried exactly as the page prints it.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Pyloric stenosis produces a hypochloraemic, hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis from repeated loss of gastric hydrochloric acid — raised pH and raised bicarbonate with pCO2 not driving the change.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.447'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-18', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gi-abdopain',
  stem:'A 12-year-old boy presents to the Emergency Room with a 4-hour history of groin pain. He is complaining of nausea and has vomited twice. On examination, there is tenderness and swelling of the scrotum and right testicle, with absence of the cremasteric reflex on that side. There is no fever or erythema. Routine blood tests are normal. He describes several previous episodes of short-lived pain. What is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Testicular torsion','Torsion of the hydatid of Morgagni','Inguinal hernia','Renal stone','Epididymo-orchitis'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Testicular torsion presents with sudden onset of unilateral testicular pain, absent cremasteric reflex, and sometimes nausea or vomiting. This is a surgical emergency and must be recognized promptly.\n\n---\n\n*(This vignette is not covered in the peds GI lectures — the acute scrotum is a surgical topic, so the reasoning below is drawn from general medical knowledge, not taken from the course material.)*\n\nTesticular torsion is a time-critical surgical emergency: the testis twists on its spermatic cord, cutting off its own blood supply. It classically presents with **sudden, severe unilateral testicular or groin pain**, often with nausea and vomiting from the visceral pain response, and examination finds a **swollen, tender testis with an absent cremasteric reflex** on the affected side — exactly this boy\'s picture. The history of **several previous short episodes of pain** fits intermittent torsion-detorsion, a recognised pattern before a full torsion that does not spontaneously resolve. **No fever, no erythema, and normal blood tests** argue against an infective process, which is the key discriminator against the two infective distractors.\n\nEach distractor fails a specific feature. *Torsion of the hydatid of Morgagni* (a vestigial embryological remnant on the testis) causes testicular pain but the cremasteric reflex is typically **preserved**, and a tender nodule ("blue dot sign") rather than diffuse scrotal swelling is more typical — the absent reflex here points away from it. *Inguinal hernia* would usually present with a reducible or irreducible groin swelling tracking toward the inguinal canal rather than isolated testicular/scrotal tenderness, and normal bloods and the acute onset with an absent reflex do not fit an incarcerated hernia\'s more gradual, bowel-obstruction-type picture. *Renal stone* causes colicky loin-to-groin pain that may radiate to the testis, but it does not cause scrotal swelling or an absent cremasteric reflex, and urinalysis (not given here as abnormal) would typically show haematuria. *Epididymo-orchitis* is excluded by the **absence of fever and erythema** and **normal inflammatory markers on routine bloods**, plus it does not typically abolish the cremasteric reflex — the reflex loss is a torsion-specific sign.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Sudden unilateral testicular pain with an absent cremasteric reflex, especially with a history of prior short-lived episodes, is testicular torsion until surgically excluded — it does not wait for confirmatory tests.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.449'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-19', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gastroenterology',
  stem:'A 2-month-old infant is brought to the GP by his mother. She is concerned that he vomits after every feeding. She has noticed that his abdomen has become distended. On further questioning, he opens his bowels infrequently every 3-4 days and did not pass meconium until 72 hours of age. Abdominal X-ray shows distended loops of bowel with no air in the rectum. What is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Hirschsprung\'s disease','Constipation due to dehydration','Pyloric stenosis','Cow\'s milk protein allergy','Intussusception'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Delayed passage of meconium, chronic constipation, abdominal distension, and absence of rectal air on X-ray are classic features of Hirschsprung\'s disease. This is due to aganglionic segments of bowel leading to functional obstruction.\n\n---\n\nHirschsprung\'s disease is discussed earlier in this section at `pedep-gi-7`, where it presented as neonatal delayed meconium and constipation; this vignette adds the two other classic pillars. *(The following mechanism detail is general medical knowledge rather than something the peds lectures cover directly — not taken from the course material.)* The bowel wall\'s ganglion cells, which normally allow peristaltic relaxation, fail to develop in a segment starting at the anus and extending proximally for a variable distance; that segment stays permanently contracted and cannot relax to pass stool, so bowel proximal to it dilates and obstructs. **Delayed passage of meconium beyond 48 hours (here 72 hours), chronic infrequent stooling, progressive abdominal distension, and vomiting from functional obstruction** are the clinical picture, and an X-ray showing **distended bowel loops with no air reaching the rectum** is the classic radiological correlate of a distal aganglionic segment obstructing outflow. Confirmation is by rectal suction biopsy showing absent ganglion cells — the investigation tested separately at `pedep-gi-23`.\n\nEach distractor is excluded by a specific feature. *Constipation due to dehydration* is a functional, usually older-infant problem from inadequate fluid/feed volume; it does not explain a **72-hour delay in passing meconium at birth** or an X-ray with **no air in the rectum**, both of which point to a structural/functional obstruction present from birth. *Pyloric stenosis* causes non-bilious projectile vomiting from a hypertrophied pylorus, but it does not cause delayed meconium passage or infrequent stooling from birth, and it obstructs at the stomach outlet, not the distal bowel — the X-ray would not show distal bowel gas absence in this pattern. *Cow\'s milk protein allergy* causes vomiting, blood/mucus in stool, or eczema/atopy in a milk-fed infant, but does not explain a birth history of delayed meconium passage. *Intussusception* is an acute, usually older-infant (peak 6 months to 2 years) telescoping event with colicky pain and a palpable mass, not a chronic pattern present since the newborn period.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Delayed passage of meconium beyond 48 hours, chronic infrequent stooling, abdominal distension, and no rectal gas on X-ray together point to Hirschsprung\'s disease.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.451'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-20', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gi-abdopain',
  stem:'A 7-month-old infant presents with a 12-hour history of intermittent inconsolable crying. He is mottled, has cold peripheries, and has vomited several times. Examination reveals a mass in the right upper quadrant of the abdomen. What is the most likely cause of his symptoms?',
  options:['Colic','Incarcerated hernia','Gastroenteritis','Intussusception','Gastro-oesophageal reflux'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Intussusception typically presents with intermittent pain, vomiting, a palpable abdominal mass, and systemic signs like mottling and lethargy. It is a surgical emergency often seen in infants around this age.\n\n---\n\n`24)Abdominal pain.txt` describes intussusception as telescoping of one segment of bowel into an adjacent one, classically peaking around 6 months to 2 years of age, presenting with **intermittent, colicky, inconsolable crying** (the infant draws the legs up between episodes of apparent well-being), **vomiting**, and a **palpable sausage-shaped mass**, often in the right upper quadrant as the ileocaecal segment telescopes. **Mottling and cold peripheries** signal the child is becoming shocked — a surgical emergency requiring urgent reduction (air/contrast enema or surgery) rather than watchful waiting. Every element of this 7-month-old\'s presentation — the age, the intermittent pattern, the RUQ mass, and the systemic compromise — fits this diagnosis precisely.\n\nEach distractor lacks the mass or the systemic severity. *Colic* causes episodic infant crying but is a diagnosis of exclusion in a well, thriving infant with **no palpable mass and no systemic signs** — mottling and cold peripheries are never part of colic. *Incarcerated hernia* causes a tender, irreducible groin (not right-upper-quadrant abdominal) swelling; the mass location here does not fit. *Gastroenteritis* causes vomiting and often diarrhoea, but not an intermittent colicky crying pattern with a discrete abdominal mass — the mass is the finding that rules it out. *Gastro-oesophageal reflux* causes recurrent regurgitation/vomiting but does not cause intermittent severe colicky pain, a palpable mass, or shock signs; it is a far milder, chronic picture (`22)Vomiting.txt`).\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Intermittent inconsolable crying with vomiting, a palpable right-sided abdominal mass, and signs of shock (mottling, cold peripheries) in an infant is intussusception until reduced.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.453'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-21', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gi-diarrhoea',
  stem:'A 9-year-old presents with a 6-month history of non-bloody diarrhea associated with 4 kg of weight loss. His mother says he is less energetic than before and looks paler than normal. There is no history of foreign travel. Blood tests show microcytic anemia (Hb 8.9 g/dL, MCV 72) but normal inflammatory markers. What is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Crohn\'s disease','Salmonella infection','Coeliac disease','Cow\'s milk protein allergy','Ulcerative colitis'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Chronic diarrhea, weight loss, pallor, and microcytic anemia with normal inflammatory markers are classic for coeliac disease. This autoimmune condition is triggered by gluten intake and affects nutrient absorption.\n\n---\n\nThis question contrasts with `pedep-gi-2`, where a toddler\'s **normal-looking, thriving** chronic diarrhoea argued against coeliac disease; here the pattern is the opposite. *(Coeliac disease itself is not covered in detail in the peds GI lectures beyond being listed as a screening step in an unrelated context — the reasoning below draws on general medical knowledge, not taken from the course material.)* Coeliac disease is an autoimmune enteropathy triggered by dietary gluten, causing villous atrophy in the small bowel and consequent malabsorption. A **6-month history of chronic non-bloody diarrhoea with significant weight loss (4 kg), fatigue, pallor, and iron-deficiency-pattern microcytic anaemia (low Hb, low MCV)** is the classic malabsorption picture, and **normal inflammatory markers** are an important discriminator — coeliac disease is not primarily an inflammatory bowel process, so CRP/ESR are typically normal or only mildly raised, unlike true inflammatory bowel disease.\n\nEach distractor is excluded chiefly by that inflammatory-marker point or the exposure history. *Crohn\'s disease* can cause chronic diarrhoea, weight loss and anaemia, but it is a transmural inflammatory disease and would be expected to raise inflammatory markers (CRP/ESR), and often features abdominal pain, mouth ulcers or perianal disease not described here. *Salmonella infection* causes acute, often bloody diarrhoea with a preceding exposure (contaminated food or travel) and would raise inflammatory markers acutely — the 6-month chronicity and explicit absence of foreign travel argue against it. *Cow\'s milk protein allergy* presents in younger infants around the time of milk introduction, not a previously well 9-year-old with a half-year history. *Ulcerative colitis* is another inflammatory bowel disease that classically causes **bloody** diarrhoea and, like Crohn\'s, would be expected to raise inflammatory markers — both features this child lacks.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Chronic non-bloody diarrhoea with weight loss, fatigue and iron-deficiency microcytic anaemia but normal inflammatory markers should suggest coeliac disease over inflammatory bowel disease.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.455'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-23', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gi-diarrhoea',
  stem:'A 6-month-old baby presents with a history of constipation since birth. Her mother has been using glycerine suppositories every 3 days to help her defecate. When she inserts the suppository, a gush of liquid stool is passed. Her mother remembers she did not open her bowels until she was 3 days old. Which of the following investigations would confirm the diagnosis?',
  options:['Thyroid function tests','Barium enema','Bone profile','Abdominal film','Suction biopsy of the rectum'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> A suction rectal biopsy is the gold standard for diagnosing Hirschsprung\'s disease. The history of chronic constipation since birth and explosive stools after rectal stimulation are key clues.\n\n---\n\nThis case follows on from `pedep-gi-7` and `pedep-gi-19`, where Hirschsprung\'s disease was diagnosed clinically; here the question asks for the **confirmatory investigation**. The history is textbook: **constipation since birth**, **delayed passage of meconium (day 3)**, and — a specific clue — a **gush of liquid stool on rectal stimulation** (the "squirt sign"), which happens because stool has pooled proximal to the permanently contracted aganglionic segment and is released under pressure once the rectal exam or suppository disturbs it. *(The specifics of the confirmatory test are general medical knowledge, not detailed in the peds GI lectures — not taken from the course material.)* **Suction biopsy of the rectum**, examined histologically for the **absence of ganglion cells** (with hypertrophied nerve trunks and increased acetylcholinesterase activity), is the definitive diagnostic test, because Hirschsprung\'s disease is fundamentally a histological diagnosis — the bowel\'s nerve supply, not its shape, is the abnormality.\n\nEach distractor investigates the wrong system or only supports rather than confirms the diagnosis. *Thyroid function tests* would be relevant if congenital hypothyroidism were suspected as a cause of constipation, but the birth history (delayed meconium, gush sign) already points specifically to Hirschsprung\'s disease, not a metabolic cause. *Barium enema* can show a transition zone between the narrow aganglionic segment and the dilated normal bowel proximal to it, which is highly suggestive, but it is a **supportive** imaging test, not the confirmatory (histological) one. *Bone profile* investigates calcium/phosphate/vitamin D disorders, unrelated to bowel innervation. *Abdominal film* may show dilated bowel loops as a non-specific sign of obstruction, again supportive rather than diagnostic.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Suction rectal biopsy showing absent ganglion cells is the confirmatory (not merely supportive) investigation for Hirschsprung\'s disease.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.459'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-24', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gi-abdopain',
  stem:'A 9-year-old boy presents with a 24-hour history of abdominal pain, initially around the umbilicus and now localized to the right iliac fossa. His mother says he has not eaten today and has been listless. He finds it difficult to stand up straight. On examination, he is tender and guarding on palpation. What is the next step in management?',
  options:['Referral to surgical team','Abdominal radiograph','Intravenous antibiotics','Full septic screen','Admit to the ward for review on the ward round'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> The clinical picture is highly suggestive of appendicitis, with localized tenderness, guarding, and inability to stand upright. Urgent surgical evaluation is the appropriate next step.\n\n---\n\nThis vignette shares its clinical picture with `pedep-gi-15` (Molly), which asked for the **diagnosis** off the same pattern of pain migrating from periumbilical to the right iliac fossa, anorexia, an inability to stand up straight, and tenderness with guarding — this question asks for the **next step** once appendicitis is the working diagnosis. `24)Abdominal pain.txt` frames appendicitis as a surgical emergency: once the clinical picture is this convincing (guarding is a peritoneal sign, not a soft finding), the priority is **urgent surgical referral** for assessment and likely appendicectomy, not further delay for tests that a strong clinical diagnosis does not need.\n\nEach distractor either delays definitive care or targets the wrong problem. *Abdominal radiograph* rarely adds useful information in classic appendicitis and would only delay surgical review. *Intravenous antibiotics* may eventually form part of peri-operative management, but starting them is not the **next step** ahead of getting the surgical team involved, and antibiotics alone do not treat an inflamed, potentially perforating appendix. *Full septic screen* (blood cultures, urine, sometimes lumbar puncture) is the correct next step for a source-unclear sepsis picture, not a child with a clear localising surgical abdomen. *Admit to the ward for review on the ward round* dangerously delays a time-critical surgical assessment — appendicitis can progress to perforation while a child waits for a scheduled round.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Once a clinical picture is convincingly appendicitis (migrating pain, guarding, inability to stand straight), the next step is urgent surgical referral, not further delay for investigations.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.461'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-25', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gastroenterology',
  stem:'A 6-week-old male infant presents with a one-week history of worsening non-bilious vomiting. His mother reports that the vomiting is projectile and seems to be gradually worsening. He is eager to feed after vomiting but is having fewer wet nappies than usual. On examination, he is afebrile, appears hungry, and looks mildly dehydrated. What is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Cow\'s milk protein intolerance','Gastroenteritis','Meningitis','Pyloric stenosis','Malrotation and volvulus'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Progressive non-bilious projectile vomiting with persistent hunger and dehydration is typical of pyloric stenosis. Diagnosis is clinical and confirmed by imaging.\n\n---\n\nThis is the same diagnosis as `pedep-gi-10` and `pedep-gi-17`, this time asked as a straightforward recall from the vignette rather than against a differential list. `22)Vomiting.txt` describes pyloric stenosis presenting at **2–8 weeks of age** (this baby is 6 weeks) with **non-bilious, projectile, progressively worsening vomiting**, a baby who remains **hungry and eager to feed** immediately afterwards because the obstruction is at the gastric outlet and not further down the gut, and signs of **dehydration** (here, reduced wet nappies) from ongoing fluid loss. Being **afebrile** fits — this is a mechanical obstruction, not an infective illness.\n\nEach distractor is excluded by a specific mismatch. *Cow\'s milk protein intolerance* typically causes vomiting alongside diarrhoea, blood/mucus in stool, or eczema, not isolated progressively worsening projectile vomiting with hunger. *Gastroenteritis* usually comes with diarrhoea and fever, and does not typically produce true projectile vomiting with a hungry, otherwise well-looking infant between episodes. *Meningitis* would be expected to cause fever, lethargy or irritability, and possibly a bulging fontanelle — not an afebrile, hungry, alert infant with isolated progressive vomiting. *Malrotation and volvulus* classically causes **bilious (green)** vomiting, a surgical red flag distinct from this baby\'s **non-bilious** picture, and tends to present more acutely and unwell than a gradually worsening week-long course.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Progressive non-bilious projectile vomiting in a hungry 2–8-week-old infant with signs of dehydration is pyloric stenosis.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.463'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-26', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gi-abdopain',
  stem:'A 15-year-old girl attends a clinic concerned about episodic abdominal pain and loose stool over the past year. She dates the symptoms back to when she changed schools and says they occur more often on school days. She describes crampy abdominal pain followed by the need to pass a stool, occurring about twice a month. Her bowel habit is normal on other days. There has never been any blood, and she has not lost any weight. What is the most likely underlying cause of her symptoms?',
  options:['Cow\'s milk protein intolerance','Coeliac disease','Ulcerative colitis','Irritable bowel syndrome','Overflow diarrhea secondary to constipation'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Crampy abdominal pain associated with bowel habit changes, occurring during stress (school days), and without red flags suggests irritable bowel syndrome. This is a functional GI disorder common in adolescents.\n\n---\n\nThis question contrasts directly with `pedep-gi-5`, where the same "functional" family of diagnoses was tested via **functional abdominal pain** (pain without a bowel-habit trigger). Here, `24)Abdominal pain.txt`\'s functional GI disorders extend to **irritable bowel syndrome**: crampy pain **linked to defecation** (pain followed by the urge to pass stool, a core IBS feature), a clear **stress trigger** (worse on school days, since she changed schools a year ago), **episodic** rather than constant symptoms, and **normal bowel habit between episodes**. The **absence of blood and weight loss** over a full year is the reassuring negative that keeps this functional rather than organic.\n\nEach distractor is excluded by a mismatched feature. *Cow\'s milk protein intolerance* is a food-reactive process typically presenting in infancy, not a 15-year-old with a stress-linked, episodic pattern. *Coeliac disease* would be expected to cause weight loss or growth/nutritional impact over a year of active disease (as seen in `pedep-gi-21`), which this girl explicitly does not have. *Ulcerative colitis* causes **bloody** diarrhoea and is a chronic inflammatory process — both excluded here by "there has never been any blood" and the episodic, otherwise-normal bowel pattern. *Overflow diarrhoea secondary to constipation* would show a background of infrequent, hard stool with soiling episodes, not a normal bowel habit between discrete episodes of crampy pain and loose stool.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Episodic, stress-linked crampy abdominal pain relieved by defecation with a normal bowel habit between episodes and no red flags is irritable bowel syndrome.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.465'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-27', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gi-diarrhoea',
  stem:'A mother brings in her 3-month-old girl because of repeated diarrhea and vomiting that started the day before and is currently unable to retain anything in her stomach. You find that the infant is severely dehydrated and feverish with cold extremities. What is first thing you should do?',
  options:['cold foments and antipyretic','anti-emetic and trial of breast feeding','IV line and administer normal saline','continue breast feeding with and give ORS','IM antibiotics and oral rehydration solution'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> The infant is severely dehydrated and hemodynamically unstable; the priority is rapid fluid resuscitation with IV isotonic saline to restore perfusion. Symptomatic treatments or oral rehydration are insufficient at this stage.\n\n---\n\n`23).1) Acute diarrhea.txt` sets out dehydration severity and its management: mild-to-moderate dehydration can be managed with **oral rehydration solution (ORS)**, but **severe dehydration with shock features — cold extremities, being unable to retain any oral intake, and fever suggesting systemic compromise — needs immediate IV fluid resuscitation**, because the gut cannot be relied on to absorb fluid fast enough to correct a shocked circulation, and a vomiting infant cannot keep oral fluid down regardless. The **first** action in this severely unwell infant is therefore to secure **IV access and give a bolus of isotonic (0.9%) saline** to restore circulating volume, before any oral strategy is considered.\n\nEach distractor either treats the wrong problem or uses the wrong route. *Cold foments and antipyretic* only addresses the fever and does nothing for the underlying dehydration and shock. *Anti-emetic and trial of breastfeeding* still relies on the oral route, which this vomiting, severely dehydrated infant cannot tolerate. *Continue breastfeeding with ORS* is correct management for **mild-to-moderate** dehydration, not this severe, shocked presentation. *IM antibiotics and oral rehydration solution* both treats a presumed infection empirically (not established) and again relies on oral rehydration, which is inadequate once a child is in shock — fluid resuscitation always comes before antibiotics in an unstable child.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'A severely dehydrated, vomiting infant with shock signs (cold extremities, unable to retain intake) needs immediate IV isotonic fluid resuscitation before any oral rehydration strategy.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.467'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-28', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gi-abdopain',
  stem:'A previously healthy 6 months old child develops paroxysmal colicky abdominal pain. The infant has occasional vomiting. Over the next 12 hours the infant passes blood and mucous and becomes progressively lethargic. Following fluid resuscitation, the most appropriate next step in management is:',
  options:['IV antibiotics','stool culture','barium meal','abdominal US','empiric anti-dysentery therapy'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> The presentation is classic for intussusception. Ultrasound is the diagnostic modality of choice as it is non-invasive, sensitive, and can sometimes guide reduction.\n\n---\n\nThis is the same diagnosis as `pedep-gi-8` and `pedep-gi-20` — paroxysmal colicky pain, vomiting, and here the added classic sign of **redcurrant-jelly stool (blood and mucous)**, with progressive lethargy signalling the child is becoming unwell. Once initial fluid resuscitation has stabilised the child, `24)Abdominal pain.txt`\'s point about intussusception is that **abdominal ultrasound** is the investigation of choice: it is non-invasive, quick, highly sensitive for the "target" or "doughnut" sign of telescoped bowel, and — unlike some of the alternatives — carries no radiation and can be followed directly by an image-guided (air or contrast) reduction attempt in the same setting.\n\nEach distractor either targets the wrong diagnosis or is the wrong modality. *IV antibiotics* would be relevant if a bacterial dysenteric infection were confirmed, but the clinical picture (paroxysmal colic, a palpable course, redcurrant stool) points to a mechanical surgical problem, not an infection to be treated empirically before imaging. *Stool culture* looks for an infective cause of bloody stool, which is a reasonable differential but does not address the more urgent, more likely surgical diagnosis suggested by the colicky pattern and lethargy — and does not by itself guide immediate management. *Barium meal* images the upper GI tract and is not the tool used to diagnose or manage intussusception, which is typically distal small-bowel/ileocaecal. *Empiric anti-dysentery therapy* again assumes an infective cause without confirmation, and delays the imaging that would identify (and potentially treat) intussusception.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Once an infant with suspected intussusception is fluid-resuscitated, abdominal ultrasound is the next investigation of choice — non-invasive, sensitive, and able to guide reduction.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.469'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-29', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gi-diarrhoea',
  stem:'Which is not a consequence of watery diarrhea?',
  options:['dehydration','Hyponatremia','Hypokalemia','Hypernatremia','Metabolic alkalosis'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Watery diarrhea usually causes dehydration, hyponatremia, and hypokalemia; it typically leads to metabolic acidosis due to bicarbonate loss, not alkalosis.\n\n---\n\nThis is a straight recall question off `23).1) Acute diarrhea.txt`\'s list of the metabolic consequences of watery diarrhoea. Stool is rich in water, sodium, potassium and **bicarbonate**; losing large volumes of it produces **dehydration**, and depending on the balance of water versus electrolyte loss and on oral intake, either **hyponatraemia** (if replacement fluid is relatively electrolyte-poor, e.g. plain water) or **hypernatraemia** (if free water loss outpaces sodium loss, e.g. from fever and insensible losses) can result — both are recognised patterns of dehydration in diarrhoeal illness, which is why both appear as options that ARE consequences. **Hypokalaemia** follows directly from stool potassium loss. Because bicarbonate is lost in the stool, the acid-base consequence of watery diarrhoea is **metabolic acidosis**, not alkalosis — the option the question asks you to identify as the one that does **not** occur.\n\nThis question does not carry a shared option menu or a clinical vignette to work through per-distractor; each of the other four options (dehydration, hyponatraemia, hypokalaemia, hypernatraemia) is a genuine, recognised consequence of diarrhoeal fluid and electrolyte loss, which is exactly why *metabolic alkalosis* stands out as the one that is not — it is instead the acid-base pattern seen in **projectile vomiting/pyloric stenosis** (`pedep-gi-17`), where acid rather than bicarbonate is lost, the mirror image of this question.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Watery diarrhoea loses bicarbonate in stool and causes metabolic acidosis, not alkalosis — the mirror image of the metabolic alkalosis caused by vomiting in pyloric stenosis.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.471'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-30', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gi-diarrhoea',
  stem:'Which of the following is not consistent with Hirschsprung\'s disease?',
  options:['No passage of meconium after 24 hours of birth','Abdominal distension','Bile-stained vomiting','Refractory constipation','Treatment is by repeated finger dilatation'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Hirschsprung\'s disease is managed surgically (pull-through procedure), not by repeated digital dilatation. Other options reflect common clinical features.\n\n---\n\nThis vignette-free recall question appears to reprint elsewhere in this bank; the two printings are recorded separately here, not reconciled against each other. Taking this page as it stands: the first four options are all genuine features of Hirschsprung\'s disease already established across `pedep-gi-7`, `pedep-gi-19` and `pedep-gi-23` — **failure to pass meconium within 24–48 hours of birth**, **abdominal distension** from proximal bowel dilatation, **bile-stained vomiting** as the obstruction becomes more complete, and **refractory (treatment-resistant) constipation** from the permanently contracted aganglionic segment. Definitive treatment is **surgical**: a "pull-through" procedure that resects the aganglionic segment and anastomoses healthy, ganglion-containing bowel down to the anus, restoring normal peristalsis and relaxation. **Repeated finger (digital) dilatation** is not curative for an anatomically and histologically abnormal segment of bowel that lacks ganglion cells — dilating the anal canal does not restore nerve supply — which is why it is the option that is **not** consistent with the disease or its management.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Hirschsprung\'s disease is treated surgically (pull-through resection of the aganglionic segment), not by repeated digital dilatation, which cannot restore missing ganglion cells.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.473'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-31', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gi-abdopain',
  stem:'A 1-year-old presenting to the ER with abdominal colic and vomiting. X ray of the abdomen shows distended small bowel and absence of air in the distal colon and rectum, what is the diagnosis?',
  options:['intussusception','Hirschsprung disease','Incarcerated hernia','Peptic ulcer disease','Fecal impaction'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> The X-ray findings of distended small bowel with absence of air in the distal colon and rectum in a 1-year-old with acute abdominal colic and vomiting strongly suggest a mechanical small bowel obstruction, which is classic for intussusception in this age group\n\n---\n\nThis is the same diagnosis as `pedep-gi-8`, `pedep-gi-20` and `pedep-gi-28`, now identified purely from an **X-ray pattern** rather than the clinical vignette. A 1-year-old is squarely within the peak age range for intussusception (`24)Abdominal pain.txt`). **Colicky abdominal pain, vomiting, and an X-ray showing distended small bowel with no distal (colonic/rectal) gas** together describe a **mechanical small bowel obstruction with a distal cut-off**, which is exactly the picture produced when a segment of bowel telescopes into an adjacent segment and physically blocks the lumen — gas cannot pass the obstruction to reach the rectum, while bowel proximal to it dilates from trapped gas and fluid.\n\nEach distractor does not fit this specific radiological pattern at this age. *Hirschsprung disease* is a **chronic, since-birth** presentation, not an **acute** presentation at 1 year of age with colic; its X-ray pattern is a distal transition zone rather than a picture that develops acutely. *Incarcerated hernia* would show a visible groin swelling on examination alongside the obstructive picture — not mentioned here — and is a less common cause of this X-ray pattern at this age than intussusception. *Peptic ulcer disease* is rare in a 1-year-old and does not cause a small bowel obstruction pattern. *Fecal impaction* causes a colonic loading pattern with stool visible on X-ray, not distended small bowel with absent distal gas, and does not typically present with acute colic and vomiting in a previously well 1-year-old.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Distended small bowel with absent gas in the distal colon and rectum on X-ray, in a colicky, vomiting infant, is intussusception until proved otherwise.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.475'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-32', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gi-diarrhoea',
  stem:'A 6-year-old boy presents with polydipsia, polyuria and unexplained weight loss. On examination, he is lethargic and thin. His blood sugar is 34mmol/mol. His urine dipstick shows the presence of glucose and ketones, and his blood ketones are 4.0. You are about to take bloods, including a blood gas. Which ONE of these conditions should be screened for at presentation?',
  options:['Coeliac disease','Diabetic retinopathy','Diabetic neuropathy','Diabetic nephropathy','Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Children with type 1 diabetes have a higher risk of autoimmune conditions, especially coeliac disease, which should be screened at diagnosis.\n\n---\n\nThe vignette is new-onset diabetic ketoacidosis: polydipsia, polyuria, weight loss, lethargy, hyperglycaemia, and glucose plus ketones on dipstick with a markedly raised blood ketone level (`51) DKA.txt`). Type 1 diabetes is an autoimmune condition, and children with one autoimmune disease carry an increased risk of others — **coeliac disease is the classic co-screened autoimmune association at diagnosis of type 1 diabetes**, tested for the same reason it is raised as part of the short-stature/screening workup elsewhere in this course. Screening at presentation (rather than waiting for symptoms) matters because coeliac disease can be asymptomatic or subtle in children and, left undetected, adds a second cause of poor growth and malabsorption on top of the new diabetes diagnosis.\n\nEach distractor is a **complication of established, longstanding** diabetes, not something screened for **at presentation**. *Diabetic retinopathy*, *diabetic neuropathy* and *diabetic nephropathy* are all microvascular complications that develop after years of hyperglycaemic exposure and are screened for periodically once diabetes is established, not at the point of first diagnosis in a previously well child. *Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1* is a rare inherited tumour syndrome unrelated to type 1 diabetes\'s autoimmune associations and is not part of the standard diagnosis-time screen.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Coeliac disease should be screened for at the time of a new type 1 diabetes diagnosis, because both are autoimmune conditions that cluster together — unlike the microvascular complications, which are screened for only once diabetes is established.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.477'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-33', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gastroenterology',
  stem:'A 3-week-old breastfed baby has been referred by Emergency. His parents are very concerned because he has not opened his bowels for 3d. He has episodic screaming episodes which occur mostly in the evenings. He is the first baby of non-consanguineous parents. He was born at full term by emergency C-section dt prolonged 2nd stage of labor. There was meconium stained liquor. His APGARS were 9,10 and 10, and he was discharged home the next day. He is otherwise well and feeding well. What is the MOST likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Hirschsprung\'s disease','Infantile colic','Intussusception','Meckel\'s diverticulum','Pyloric stenosis'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Episodic evening crying in a well-fed, healthy infant with normal growth and no systemic symptoms is typical of colic; Hirschsprung\'s would show persistent constipation and abdominal distension.\n\n---\n\n*(Infantile colic itself is a clinical diagnosis of exclusion not covered in detail across the peds GI lectures — the reasoning below is drawn from general medical knowledge, not taken from the course material.)* This vignette is built to look concerning — not opening bowels for 3 days, and screaming episodes — but every detail that would normally raise alarm is deliberately reassuring: the baby is **otherwise well and feeding well**, had **excellent Apgars (9, 10, 10)** and an uncomplicated discharge, and the crying is specifically **episodic and evening-predominant**, the classic pattern of infantile colic (often described by the "rule of three": crying for more than 3 hours a day, more than 3 days a week, for more than 3 weeks, typically peaking in the evening, in an otherwise thriving infant). Three days without a stool in an exclusively breastfed infant is also not unusual — breastfed babies can have infrequent stools without this representing pathological constipation, since breast milk is very efficiently absorbed.\n\nEach distractor is undercut by a specific missing feature. *Hirschsprung\'s disease* would be expected to show **persistent, refractory constipation with abdominal distension** and typically a history from birth (delayed meconium), not a well, thriving baby with isolated evening crying — and here there is no mention of distension or a birth history of delayed meconium (the meconium-stained liquor mentioned is an intrapartum finding, unrelated to the baby\'s own bowel pattern). *Intussusception* causes acute paroxysmal colic with vomiting, lethargy, and eventually redcurrant-jelly stool — none of which is present in this well, feeding baby. *Meckel\'s diverticulum* classically presents with painless rectal bleeding, not with crying episodes and no stool. *Pyloric stenosis* causes progressive projectile vomiting, not isolated crying with normal feeding.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Episodic, evening-predominant crying in an otherwise well, thriving, well-feeding infant with no distension, vomiting or bleeding is infantile colic, a diagnosis of exclusion.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.479'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-34', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gastroenterology',
  stem:'A 5-week infant was brought by intractable vomiting and weight loss over the last 2 weeks. He was dehydrated, and his weight was 3 kg on feeding, visible gastric peristalsis was seen, and projectile non-bilious vomiting occurred. Abdominal examination reveals a palpable mass. The MOST suitable study for diagnosis is:',
  options:['Plain x ray','abdomen US','Barium enema','Serum electrolytes levels','Esophageal monitoring'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Classic presentation of pyloric stenosis; ultrasound is the diagnostic test of choice for measuring pyloric muscle thickness.\n\n---\n\nThis is the same diagnosis as `pedep-gi-10`, `pedep-gi-17` and `pedep-gi-25`, this time asked as the confirmatory investigation. `22)Vomiting.txt` describes the diagnostic triad the physical exam adds here: **visible gastric peristalsis** (waves moving left to right across the upper abdomen as the stomach tries to force contents past the obstructed pylorus), a **palpable "olive" mass** (the hypertrophied pyloric muscle), and **projectile non-bilious vomiting** with dehydration and weight loss over 2 weeks. **Abdominal ultrasound** is the diagnostic test of choice because it directly measures **pyloric muscle thickness and canal length**, is non-invasive, avoids radiation, and can be performed at the bedside without sedation — ideal in a young, dehydrated infant.\n\nEach distractor is either non-specific or the wrong tool. *Plain X-ray* may show a distended, gas-filled stomach as a non-specific sign but cannot measure the pyloric muscle itself or confirm the diagnosis. *Barium enema* images the colon and is used for lower GI pathology such as Hirschsprung\'s disease, not an upper GI outlet obstruction. *Serum electrolyte levels* are essential for assessing the metabolic consequence (hypochloraemic, hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis, `pedep-gi-17`) and guiding rehydration before surgery, but they do not establish the anatomical diagnosis. *Oesophageal monitoring* (pH studies) investigates gastro-oesophageal reflux, a different and much milder condition than this vignette\'s palpable mass and visible peristalsis.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Abdominal ultrasound, measuring pyloric muscle thickness directly, is the diagnostic investigation of choice for pyloric stenosis.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.481'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-35', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gastroenterology',
  stem:'A 6-week-old baby boy presents to the emergency department with a history of vomiting, the vomiting has been present since the third week of life but has suddenly increased in violence and frequency over the preceding 3 days. The baby is well but hungry and failing to thrive. The most useful next step is?',
  options:['Take an accurate feeding history and decrease feed volumes','Take a blood gas','Perform a septic screen','Start intensive gastro-oesophageal reflux therapy','Refer surgically immediately'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> The baby’s presentation is classic for infantile hypertrophic pyloric stenosis. The immediate priority is to check a blood gas to detect the typical hypochloremic, hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis and correct it before surgery. Definitive treatment is pyloromyotomy once the infant is stabilized.\n\n---\n\n**Why a blood gas comes before anything else.** `22)Vomiting.txt` places pyloric stenosis at 2-8 weeks of age, with non-bilious vomiting that increases in frequency and forcefulness over time and becomes projectile — exactly this baby\'s course, vomiting since week 3 now suddenly more violent and frequent, hungry but failing to thrive. The same lecture names the metabolic signature directly: repeated vomiting of acidic, chloride-rich stomach contents produces a hypochloraemic, hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis, and it states that pyloromyotomy follows correction of this acid-base and electrolyte imbalance, which "may take more than 24 hours of intravenous fluid rehydration." A blood gas is therefore the step that both confirms the expected derangement and starts the clock on safe surgical timing.\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong.** *Feeding history and reduced feed volumes* would be reasonable for simple overfeeding or uncomplicated reflux, but this baby\'s worsening violence and frequency of vomiting with failure to thrive is the pyloric-stenosis trajectory, not an overfeeding one; reducing volumes does not fix a mechanical gastric-outlet obstruction. *A septic screen* is the right instinct for any vomiting infant who looks unwell, but the vignette describes a well, hungry baby with a textbook pyloric-stenosis story rather than a lethargic, septic-looking one, so it is not the most useful next step here. *Intensive reflux therapy* targets the wrong diagnosis and would delay recognising the obstruction. *Immediate surgical referral*, without first checking and correcting the metabolic alkalosis, risks anaesthetising a baby who is not yet safe for theatre — the lecture is explicit that correction comes first.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'A vomiting infant with a pyloric-stenosis history needs a blood gas to detect and correct the hypochloraemic, hypokalaemic alkalosis before surgery, not immediate referral.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.483'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-36', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gi-abdopain',
  stem:'A 9-month-old infant presents with vomiting and crying. On examination she is afebrile and has a diffusely tender abdomen. No masses are palpable. Which one of the following diagnoses is the most important to exclude?',
  options:['Gastroenteritis','Intussusception','Mesenteric adenitis','Hirschsprung’s disease','Colic'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Intermittent abdominal pain with crying in an infant is a red flag for intussusception, which requires urgent diagnosis and management.\n\n---\n\n**Why intussusception is the one that cannot be missed.** `24)Abdominal pain.txt` names intussusception as invagination of proximal bowel into a distal segment, most often ileum passing into the caecum, and states it is "the most common cause of intestinal obstruction in infants after the neonatal period," with a peak age of 3 months to 2 years — this infant is squarely in that window. The classic triad of colicky pain, a palpable sausage-shaped mass and redcurrant-jelly stool is a late finding; a still-early presentation can look exactly like this — vomiting, crying and a diffusely tender abdomen with no mass yet felt — which is precisely why it must be actively excluded rather than assumed absent just because the textbook signs are not all present.\n\n**Why each distractor is a lower priority to exclude.** *Gastroenteritis* is a plausible cause of vomiting but does not typically cause the intermittent, crying-with-pain pattern described, and it is not the diagnosis that carries a risk of bowel ischaemia if missed. *Mesenteric adenitis* is explicitly benign in the same lecture, with pain that "resolves in 24-48 hours" and is milder than a surgical process — it does not need urgent exclusion. *Hirschsprung\'s disease* classically declares itself in the neonatal period with delayed passage of meconium and chronic constipation, not an acute vomiting-and-crying picture at 9 months. *Colic* is a diagnosis of infancy that typically resolves by 3-4 months and does not produce a diffusely tender abdomen on examination, so it does not fit either the age or the exam findings as well as intussusception does.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Vomiting with crying in an infant aged 3 months to 2 years must actively exclude intussusception, even before the classic mass or redcurrant-jelly stool appears.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.485'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-37', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gastroenterology',
  stem:'A 5-year-old girl has had a persistent nocturnal cough for over 2 years that has not responded to Rx including inhaled steroids & bronchodilators. The cough tends to be paroxysmal & is associated with vomiting, disturbing her sleep virtually every night. She is happy & normally active during the day & did not have any exercise intolerance. In other respects, she has been well with no significant past history. There is no family Hx of asthma/eczema/allergy. Clinically, her weight was on the 75th centile & her height on the 50th centile. Examinations were normal. CXR & CBC – normal range. CRP, PEF 99%, Mantoux negative. The next most useful test that will aid the diagnosis is?',
  options:['Bronchoscopy','CT scan of thorax','Ambulatory esophageal pH study','Barium meal','Spirometry'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Chronic nocturnal cough with vomiting and normal respiratory evaluation suggests gastroesophageal reflux; pH monitoring is diagnostic.\n\n---\n\n**Why pH monitoring is the next step.** `22)Vomiting.txt` lists 24-hour oesophageal pH monitoring as the investigation used "to quantify the degree of acid reflux," with a probe passed into the lower oesophagus, and names recurrent pulmonary aspiration — presenting as recurrent pneumonia, cough or wheeze — among the complications of gastro-oesophageal reflux disease. This girl has failed a full asthma work-up (no response to inhaled steroids and bronchodilators, normal CXR, normal PEF, no atopic family history), which argues against asthma and toward an alternative driver of a nocturnal, paroxysmal cough with vomiting: silent reflux irritating the airway. Her normal growth (weight even above height centile) argues against a systemic or malabsorptive illness and is consistent with a chronic but non-wasting process like reflux.\n\n**Why each distractor is not the next useful test.** *Bronchoscopy* and *CT of the thorax* look for structural or endobronchial airway disease, but her CXR is already normal and her presentation does not suggest a mass, foreign body or bronchiectasis. *Barium meal* is explicitly ruled out by the same lecture, which states a contrast study of the upper gastrointestinal tract "is not recommended to diagnose or assess the severity of gastro-oesophageal reflux disease." *Spirometry* re-tests airway function she has essentially already had assessed (PEF 99%, no exercise intolerance) and will not identify reflux as the cause of a cough that has already failed asthma therapy.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'A chronic nocturnal cough with vomiting that has failed asthma therapy and has a normal chest work-up points to reflux, confirmed by ambulatory oesophageal pH monitoring, not a barium study.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.487'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-38', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gi-diarrhoea',
  stem:'An 8-month-old infant has had watery diarrhoea for 4 days. There has been no recent travel. Her 2-year-old sibling has recently had a similar illness, though less severe. She is drinking her normal formula milk well and is not dehydrated. What is the diagnosis?',
  options:['Viral gastro-enteritis','Bacterial dysentery','Lactose intolerance','Giardiasis'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Self-limited watery diarrhea in a well-hydrated infant with a household contact points to a viral cause; supportive care is sufficient.\n\n---\n\n**Why viral gastro-enteritis fits.** `23).1) Acute diarrhea.txt` defines acute watery diarrhoea (acute gastroenteritis) as "passage of frequent loose or watery stools without visible blood," constituting about 80% of diarrhoea cases, and names viruses as the cause in roughly 70% of those — far ahead of bacteria (10-20%) or protozoa (under 10%). It also notes the illness "begins acutely" and "lasts less than 14 days (usually less than 7 days)," which matches this 4-day watery, non-bloody course. A sibling with a similar, milder illness is the classic person-to-person spread pattern of a viral pathogen (rotavirus and norovirus being the leading examples), and the infant remaining well hydrated and feeding normally fits an uncomplicated, self-limited process.\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong.** *Bacterial dysentery* is defined by the same lecture around blood in the stool, tenesmus and severe abdominal pain from large-bowel involvement — none of which is described, and the watery (not bloody) character argues against it. *Lactose intolerance* is a chronic or recurring problem tied to milk exposure, not an acute 4-day illness that coincides with a sick sibling. *Giardiasis*, a protozoal cause making up under 10% of cases, more typically produces a persistent or malabsorptive picture rather than this acute, short, household-clustered watery illness.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Acute, non-bloody, self-limited watery diarrhoea with a sick household contact is viral gastroenteritis, the cause of roughly 70% of acute infectious diarrhoea.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.489'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-39', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'neonatal-sepsis',
  stem:'A 2-week-old infant develops fever 38.9 °C, vomiting, and irritability. His heart rate is 170/min, and RR is 40/min. The infant\'s anterior fontanelle is full, but there is no nuchal rigidity. The rest of examination is unremarkable. What is the APPROPRIATE management?',
  options:['Oral fluid and follow up in 24 hr','Amoxicillin and follow up in 1 week','Admission to hospital for investigations and treatment','IM ceftriaxone for 5 days','IV Fluid for 6 hr and follow up in 24 hr'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Neonatal fever in a 2-week-old is a medical emergency; full septic workup and inpatient monitoring with empiric antibiotics are required.\n\n---\n\n**Why admission for full workup and treatment is correct.** `18) febrile child and life threatening infections.txt` sets the neonatal age band (≤28 days) apart from older infants: "All febrile children < 1 mo = admission," requiring "a WBC count with a manual differential, blood cultures, urinalysis and urine culture" and "CSF evaluation with culture and appropriate PCR testing," plus a chest X-ray if there are respiratory signs, together with empiric IV antibiotics for the common neonatal pathogens. This 2-week-old is exactly that age band, and a full anterior fontanelle is one of the lecture\'s own listed features of meningitis (`11) Neonatal sepsis.txt` lists "tense or bulging fontanelle" under meningitis signs) — even without nuchal rigidity, which is frequently absent in a neonate, the combination of fever, tachycardia, tachypnoea, irritability and a full fontanelle is enough to mandate the complete septic and CSF work-up that only an admission can provide.\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong.** *Oral fluid with follow-up in 24 hours* and *IV fluid for 6 hours with follow-up* both treat this as an outpatient problem and provide no antimicrobial cover or CSF evaluation, which the guideline explicitly forbids for this age. *Amoxicillin with a 1-week follow-up* gives unfocused oral antibiotic cover without the blood, urine and CSF cultures needed to identify the organism, and without inpatient monitoring for a neonate who can deteriorate rapidly. *IM ceftriaxone for 5 days* supplies an antibiotic but as an outpatient course, again skipping the cultures, CSF evaluation and observation that "admission" specifically buys — the lecture\'s message is that investigation and treatment happen together, in hospital, at this age.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Fever in an infant under 1 month old always mandates hospital admission for a full septic work-up — including CSF evaluation — and empiric antibiotics, never outpatient management.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.491'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-40', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gastroenterology',
  stem:'A 6-week-old male is brought to clinic with concern for frequent regurgitation of feeds. His mother reports that he vomits large volumes of curdled milk after feeding. He is interested in feeding and breast feeds without difficulty. His weight gain has averaged 32 grams per day since his last visit. On physical examination, he appears comfortable and well hydrated. A small amount of curdled milk is present in his mouth. The remainder of his examination is unremarkable. Which of the following should be used to confirm suspicion of gastroesophageal reflux in this infant?',
  options:['Endoscopy','Esophageal pH monitoring','History and physical alone','Upper gastrointestinal series'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Physiological reflux in infants is diagnosed clinically; invasive tests are not needed if the baby is thriving and asymptomatic otherwise.\n\n---\n\n**Why history and physical alone are sufficient.** `22)Vomiting.txt` states plainly that "gastro-oesophageal reflux is usually diagnosed clinically, and no investigations are required," with tests reserved for when "the history is atypical, complications are present, or there is failure to respond to treatment." This baby has none of those triggers: he feeds eagerly, is comfortable and well hydrated on examination, and is gaining weight briskly (32 g/day is a healthy rate for this age) — a picture of uncomplicated, physiological regurgitation, the lecture\'s own description of most infants with reflux as thriving normally.\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong.** *Endoscopy* is used to look for oesophagitis or to exclude other causes of vomiting when reflux disease (not simple reflux) is suspected — not indicated in a thriving, comfortable infant. *Oesophageal pH monitoring* quantifies acid reflux and is reserved for atypical or complicated presentations, again not this straightforward case. *Upper gastrointestinal (contrast) series* is explicitly named by the lecture as "not recommended to diagnose or assess the severity of gastro-oesophageal reflux disease in infants, children and young people" — it looks for anatomical problems, not physiological reflux, and carries unnecessary radiation for a baby who needs no test at all.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'A thriving, comfortable infant with regurgitation needs no investigation at all — gastro-oesophageal reflux is a clinical diagnosis unless the history is atypical or complicated.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.493'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-41', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'haem-bleeding',
  stem:'Which of the following statements is the most factually correct with regard to the administration of vitamin K to babies in the neonatal period?',
  options:['If an oral regimen is undertaken, a single dose of oral vitamin K is recommended in all formula-fed babies','Significant bleeding in the neonatal period normally occurs in children with significant risk factors for vitamin K deficiency bleeding','The vast majority of vitamin K deficiency-associated bleeding occurs within the first week of life','There is good evidence to support an increased risk of childhood leukemia in babies given vitamin K in the neonatal period','Vitamin K deficiency bleeding is a potentially fatal disorder that is virtually preventable with one dose of vitamin K given intramuscularly at birth'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> IM vitamin K at birth effectively prevents vitamin K deficiency bleeding, which can be severe. The risk of childhood leukemia is not supported by evidence, and most bleeding occurs after the first week in exclusively breastfed infants without prophylaxis.\n\n---\n\n**Why a single IM dose is the correct statement.** `47)Hemorrhagic disorders .txt`\'s comparison table separates vitamin K deficiency from DIC by a prolonged PT with an otherwise normal platelet count, TT and fibrinogen — a coagulation-factor problem, not a consumptive or platelet one, which is exactly why replacing the missing factor (vitamin K) at birth is enough to prevent it. The policy detail that one intramuscular dose at birth is "virtually preventable" and highly effective, that most vitamin K deficiency bleeding is actually **late-onset** (beyond the first week, in exclusively breastfed infants who received no prophylaxis) rather than concentrated in the first week, and that the childhood-leukaemia scare has not been supported by good evidence, are public-health and neonatal-care facts the lecture\'s lab table does not itself spell out *(not taken from the course material)*.\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong.** *A single oral dose "in all formula-fed babies"* misstates policy: oral vitamin K regimens require **multiple** doses over the following weeks, not one, because oral absorption and coverage are less reliable than an IM injection *(not taken from the course material)*. *"Significant bleeding normally occurs only with risk factors"* is false — vitamin K deficiency bleeding classically strikes previously well, low-risk exclusively breastfed infants who simply never received prophylaxis, which is precisely why universal, not risk-based, prophylaxis is given. *"The vast majority occurs in the first week"* reverses the epidemiology: **late-onset** disease (week 2 to 6 months) is the more dangerous, less recognised pattern, often presenting with intracranial haemorrhage. *"Good evidence for a leukaemia link"* was a hypothesis raised in the 1990s that large subsequent studies have not confirmed *(not taken from the course material)*.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'One intramuscular dose of vitamin K at birth is highly effective, near-universal prophylaxis against vitamin K deficiency bleeding, which is not confined to the first week of life and carries no confirmed leukaemia risk.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.495, explanation box p.496'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-42', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gastroenterology',
  stem:'In gastroesophageal reflux, which statement is TRUE?',
  options:['it is a rare phenomenon in infants','The parents can be reassured, it is a process the child will outgrow as they get older','Thickening the feedings does not work','Most infants have slow weight gain','Always deserves further evaluation'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> GER is common and physiological in infants, usually resolving spontaneously by 12–18 months. Most infants grow normally and do not require intervention unless complications arise.\n\n---\n\n**Why reassurance is the true statement.** `22)Vomiting.txt` states that "most cases resolve spontaneously by 12 months of age due to a combination of maturation of the lower oesophageal sphincter, an upright posture and more solids in the diet," and calls gastro-oesophageal reflux "a benign, self-limited condition" unless complications turn it into reflux disease. Parental reassurance is listed first among the lecture\'s own management steps, precisely because most infants simply outgrow it.\n\n**Why each distractor is false.** *"Rare in infants"* is the opposite of the lecture\'s framing — reflux is described as "very common in infancy," caused by the normal functional immaturity of the lower oesophageal sphincter, a fluid diet, horizontal posture and a short intra-abdominal oesophagus. *"Thickening the feedings does not work"* contradicts the lecture\'s management list, which offers "smaller, more frequent feeds or adding inert thickening agents to feeds" as a recognised first-line measure. *"Most infants have slow weight gain"* is also reversed: the lecture specifically notes "most infants with gastro-oesophageal reflux are putting on weight normally," with faltering growth listed only as a complication of the more severe reflux disease, not the typical course. *"Always deserves further evaluation"* contradicts the same lecture\'s investigations section, which states reflux is "usually diagnosed clinically, and no investigations are required," with tests reserved for atypical or complicated cases.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Gastro-oesophageal reflux is common, usually resolves spontaneously by 12 months, and needs no routine investigation — reassurance is the correct management for the typical, thriving infant.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.498'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-43', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gi-diarrhoea',
  stem:'Which of the following sets of signs and symptoms are most consistent with 5% dehydration?',
  options:['Alert and responsive, warm extremities, tears with crying, normal skin turgor, moist oral mucosa','Less active than usual, sticky oral mucosa, normal or slightly diminished skin turgor, normal capillary refill time','Oliguria, sunken eyes, pale mottled skin with reduced skin turgor, prolonged capillary refill time','Oliguria, sunken eyes, tachycardia, hypotension'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Mild dehydration (5%) causes subtle signs like slight lethargy and sticky mucous membranes; severe signs like hypotension or tachycardia indicate greater dehydration.\n\n---\n\n**Why option B matches 5% dehydration.** `23).1) Acute diarrhea.txt` grades dehydration in three bands: "no dehydration" (usually <5% loss), "some dehydration" ("clinical dehydration," usually 5% to 10% loss) and "severe dehydration" (usually >10% loss), with shock as a fourth, most severe category. The bedside assessment it teaches (GEMS: general condition, eyes, mouth, skin pinch) grades each sign as normal, or "slowly"/"very slowly" returning on skin pinch, and "lethargic, irritable" versus normal general condition. A child who is "less active than usual" with sticky mucous membranes and only mildly reduced turgor sits at the mild end of "some dehydration" — the lecture\'s own definition of roughly 5% loss — with capillary refill still normal because circulating volume is not yet critically reduced.\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong.** *Option A* (alert, warm extremities, moist mucosa, normal turgor) describes a child with **no dehydration**, not 5%. *Option C* (sunken eyes, pale mottled skin, reduced turgor, prolonged capillary refill) describes the lecture\'s "severe dehydration" band, over 10% loss — capillary refill only prolongs once perfusion is significantly compromised. *Option D* (oliguria, sunken eyes, tachycardia, hypotension) is frank **shock**, the lecture\'s most severe category, requiring an immediate 20 mL/kg bolus of 0.9% saline — far beyond a 5% deficit.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'5% ("some") dehydration presents as mild lethargy, sticky mucous membranes and only slightly reduced skin turgor, with capillary refill still normal — hypotension and prolonged refill mark shock, not this grade.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.500'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-44', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gi-abdopain',
  stem:'Most common abdominal pain in childhood is:',
  options:['Functional','Appendicitis','Peptic ulcer disease','Intussusception'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Functional abdominal pain (not due to organic disease) is the most common type in children and typically resolves without surgical or medical intervention.\n\n---\n\n**Why functional pain is the commonest.** `24)Abdominal pain.txt` states that chronic or recurrent abdominal pain "occurs in 10-15% of children," and frames functional pain as "a Diagnosis of Exclusion" reached only once organic disease has been screened out by history, examination and initial tests — the sheer scale of that 10-15% figure, set against how uncommon any single organic cause is, is what makes functional pain the numerically dominant category. The lecture\'s own comparison table also distinguishes functional pain by a vague, midline site, a pattern that is never nocturnal, and the absence of the "alarm signals" (weight loss, growth deceleration, nocturnal waking, blood in stool) that flag an organic cause.\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong.** *Appendicitis* is an important acute surgical diagnosis but is a single, comparatively uncommon cause of abdominal pain overall, not the dominant one across all presentations. *Peptic ulcer disease* is uncommon in children and is one of the organic causes functional pain is diagnosed by excluding, not a frequent diagnosis in its own right. *Intussusception* is likewise a specific, less common surgical cause, concentrated in the 3-month to 2-year age band, and does not approach the prevalence of functional pain across the whole paediatric population.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Functional abdominal pain, a diagnosis of exclusion once organic disease is screened out, is the most common cause of abdominal pain in children overall.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.502'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-45', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gi-diarrhoea',
  stem:'A 6-year-old boy presents to an urgent care clinic with a history of low-grade fevers, diffuse abdominal pain, and multiple episodes of non-bloody diarrhea for the last two days. He also had one to two episodes of non-bloody, non-bilious emesis each day, but this is slowing down. He is not eating as well as usual, but he is taking small bites and drinking plenty of fluids. On evaluation, he appears tired and is not playful, but his vital signs are normal and he appears to be well-hydrated. Which of the following is the most appropriate therapeutic approach for this patient?',
  options:['Antiemetic medication','Antimotility medication','Probiotic treatment','Zinc supplementation'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Acute gastroenteritis is a common infectious disease in children which manifests as frequent loose stools exceeding normal frequency with or without fever and vomiting, lasting from one day to two weeks.\n\n---\n\n**Why probiotic treatment is the printed key.** `23).1) Acute diarrhea.txt` lists probiotics as adjuvant therapy for acute gastroenteritis, and states that "two large systematic reviews have found probiotics (especially Lactobacillus GG) to be effective in reducing the duration of diarrhea in children presenting with acute gastroenteritis" — directly relevant to this well-hydrated, resolving, mildly ill boy who needs supportive, not aggressive, treatment. ⚠️ The same lecture also lists zinc supplementation as a routine recommendation "in all types of diarrhea, in all degree of dehydration," so a case can also be made for zinc from this material; the bank\'s printed key selects probiotic treatment, and that key is kept here rather than moved, with this note flagging the overlap for the record.\n\n**Why each other option is wrong regardless.** *Antiemetic medication* is explicitly discouraged: the lecture states "it is recommended that antiemetics not be routinely used in the management of children with AGE," reserving ondansetron for more severe vomiting with dehydration. *Antimotility medication* is stated to be "Not used for children," with drugs like loperamide contraindicated in acute gastroenteritis in children "because of their lack of benefit and increased risk of adverse effects, including ileus, drowsiness, and nausea" — actively dangerous, not merely unhelpful, in a young child.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Antiemetics and antimotility drugs are not used in paediatric acute gastroenteritis; supportive adjuvant measures such as probiotics or zinc are the recommended approach.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.504'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-46', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gi-diarrhoea',
  stem:'You are counselling a 3-day-old term infant who has not passed meconium since birth. On examination his abdomen is distended but the remainder of the examination is normal. An x-ray of the abdomen shows distended loops throughout the bowel, including the rectum. What is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Congenital hypothyroidism','Cystic fibrosis','Duodenal atresia','Hirschsprung disease','Malrotation'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Hirschsprung disease results from the absence of ganglion cells in the distal colon, causing functional obstruction. This leads to failure to pass meconium, abdominal distension, and on X-ray, dilated bowel loops including the rectum, distinguishing it from other causes like cystic fibrosis or duodenal atresia.\n\n---\n\n**Why Hirschsprung disease fits the picture.** *Hirschsprung disease is caused by failure of neural-crest-derived ganglion cells to migrate into the distal bowel wall during fetal development, leaving the affected segment permanently aganglionic and unable to relax — a functional, not mechanical, obstruction that classically presents as delayed passage of meconium beyond 24-48 hours in a term newborn, with abdominal distension and dilated proximal bowel visible on plain film (not taken from the course material; the cached constipation and vomiting decks do not cover Hirschsprung disease directly, though `6) Genetically determined disease_.txt` lists it once, as a recognised anomaly in Down syndrome).* A 3-day-old who has never passed meconium, with a distended abdomen and diffusely dilated loops on X-ray, is the textbook neonatal Hirschsprung presentation.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Congenital hypothyroidism* can cause constipation and a distended, doughy abdomen, but it is a chronic, gradually declining picture, not a same-week failure to pass meconium at all with diffuse obstructive dilation. *Cystic fibrosis* causes meconium ileus, which also delays meconium passage, but its classic X-ray finding is a "soap-bubble" pattern of mixed gas and inspissated meconium localised to the right lower quadrant with a narrow, unused microcolon distally — not the uniformly dilated bowel including the rectum described here. *Duodenal atresia* obstructs very proximally and presents with early bilious vomiting and a "double bubble" of a distended stomach and duodenal cap on X-ray, with a gasless distal abdomen, the opposite pattern to diffusely dilated loops throughout the bowel. *Malrotation* (with or without volvulus) likewise presents with bilious vomiting from a proximal obstruction, not primarily with failure to pass meconium and diffuse distal dilation.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Delayed passage of meconium beyond 24-48 hours with abdominal distension and diffusely dilated bowel loops including the rectum is the classic neonatal presentation of Hirschsprung disease.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.506'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-47', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gastroenterology',
  stem:'Features of gastro-esophageal reflux disease in infants do NOT include the following:',
  options:['Excessive crying','Poor weight gain','Vomiting','Anemia, malnutrition and apnea may occur','Hemoptysis'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> GERD in infants can cause vomiting, poor weight gain, apnea, or anemia, but coughing up blood is not a typical feature.\n\n---\n\n**Why hemoptysis is the odd one out.** `22)Vomiting.txt` lists the complications of gastro-oesophageal reflux disease as faltering growth from severe vomiting, oesophagitis (which can cause haematemesis — vomited blood — discomfort on feeding, heartburn and iron-deficiency anaemia), recurrent pulmonary aspiration (recurrent pneumonia, cough or wheeze, and apnoea in preterm infants), Sandifer syndrome and brief resolved unexplained events. Every one of the four other options in this question maps directly onto that list: crying (discomfort/heartburn), poor weight gain (faltering growth), vomiting (the core symptom) and anaemia/apnoea (named complications). *Hemoptysis — coughing up blood from the lower respiratory tract — is a distinct sign from the haematemesis the lecture actually names, and is not a recognised feature of reflux even when aspiration causes recurrent chest infection (not taken from the course material for this specific distinction).*\n\n**Why the confusion is easy to make, and why it still fails.** Recurrent aspiration from reflux can cause pneumonia and cough, and severe respiratory infection can occasionally produce blood-streaked sputum from airway inflammation, but this is not the same clinical entity as true hemoptysis, and it is not listed among the lecture\'s reflux complications the way hematemesis explicitly is. The question is testing the distinction between a genuine, lecture-sourced reflux feature and a plausible-sounding but unlisted one.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'GERD complications include faltering growth, haematemesis, anaemia, apnoea and recurrent aspiration pneumonia — hemoptysis is not among the recognised features.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.508'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-48', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gastroenterology',
  stem:'A 5-month-old girl presented with complaints of vomiting after feed, recurrent attacks of pneumonia, wheezy chest, and failure to thrive. The MOST likely diagnosis is:',
  options:['Congenital hypertrophic pyloric stenosis','Faulty feeding techniques','Hirschsprung\'s disease','Gastro-esophageal reflux disease','Physiological regurgitation'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Recurrent aspiration due to reflux can cause respiratory symptoms and failure to thrive. Pyloric stenosis usually presents earlier with projectile vomiting.\n\n---\n\n**Why GERD fits this 5-month-old.** `22)Vomiting.txt` names "recurrent pulmonary aspiration — recurrent pneumonia, cough or wheeze, apnea in preterm infants" and "faltering growth from severe vomiting" as the two complications that together define gastro-oesophageal reflux **disease** rather than simple, benign reflux. This girl has all three: vomiting after feeds, recurrent pneumonia with a wheezy chest (aspiration), and failure to thrive — the complete triad the lecture describes.\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong.** *Congenital hypertrophic pyloric stenosis* "presents at 2-8 weeks of age," with non-bilious vomiting that becomes projectile — this infant is 5 months old, well outside that window, and pyloric stenosis does not cause recurrent pneumonia or wheeze. *Faulty feeding technique* could cause simple overfeeding-related regurgitation but does not explain recurrent chest infections or growth failure. *Hirschsprung\'s disease* is a lower-bowel motility disorder presenting with constipation and abdominal distension, not vomiting with respiratory disease. *Physiological regurgitation* is by definition benign, with normal growth and no respiratory complications — the presence of failure to thrive and recurrent pneumonia here is exactly what separates disease from physiological reflux.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Vomiting with recurrent pneumonia, wheeze and failure to thrive in an infant beyond the pyloric-stenosis age window is gastro-oesophageal reflux disease from recurrent aspiration.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.510'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-49', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gi-diarrhoea',
  stem:'Which of the following is not consistent with Hirschsprung\'s disease?',
  options:['No passage of meconium after 24 hours of birth','Abdominal distension','Bile-stained vomiting','Refractory constipation','Resolves spontaneously'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Hirschsprung disease is a congenital absence of ganglion cells and does not resolve spontaneously; it requires surgical intervention.\n\n---\n\n**Why spontaneous resolution is the false statement.** *Hirschsprung disease is a fixed, structural absence of ganglion cells in the distal bowel wall — the aganglionic segment does not regain ganglion cells over time, so the functional obstruction it causes cannot resolve on its own (not taken from the course material; the cached constipation deck does not cover Hirschsprung disease directly). Definitive treatment is surgical, removing the aganglionic segment and pulling through healthy, innervated bowel — without that, the obstruction, and the risk of life-threatening Hirschsprung-associated enterocolitis, persists.*\n\n**Why each of the other four IS consistent with the disease.** *No meconium passed after 24 hours of birth* is the classic, most sensitive early sign, since the affected distal segment cannot relax to allow normal transit. *Abdominal distension* follows directly from proximal bowel dilating against the obstructed, aganglionic segment. *Bile-stained vomiting* can occur once the functional obstruction is severe enough to back up proximally to the point of causing vomiting. *Refractory constipation* — poorly responsive to the usual laxative and behavioural measures used for functional constipation — is exactly what should raise suspicion of an organic cause such as Hirschsprung disease rather than the far more common functional constipation.\n\n**This same near-identical stem and option set — a 5-way "not consistent with Hirschsprung\'s disease" list — is printed again elsewhere in this bank with a different final option**, offering "treatment is by repeated finger dilatation" in place of "resolves spontaneously"; each is keyed to its own distinct final option rather than being one repeated question.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Hirschsprung disease is a fixed anatomical absence of ganglion cells that never resolves spontaneously and always requires surgical correction.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.512'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-50', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gi-abdopain',
  stem:'A one-year-old boy presented to the emergency room with severe colicky abdominal pains associated with vomiting and bleeding per rectum. PR examination revealed a mass and blood on the finger on withdrawal. What is the MOST likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Intestinal polyp','Volvulus','Intussusception','Dysentery','Hirschsprung\'s disease'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Classic currant jelly stools, colicky pain, and palpable mass are pathognomonic for intussusception.\n\n---\n\n**Why intussusception is the diagnosis.** `24)Abdominal pain.txt` describes intussusception as the invagination of proximal bowel into a distal segment, most often ileum through the ileocaecal valve into the caecum, and states it is "the most common cause of intestinal obstruction in infants after the neonatal period," peaking between 3 months and 2 years — exactly this boy\'s age. The combination of severe colicky pain, vomiting, a palpable abdominal or rectal mass and blood found on withdrawal of the examining finger is *the classic bedside triad the lecture\'s intussusception content is built around (the specific detail that the blood is "redcurrant-jelly" in character — altered blood mixed with mucus from the intussuscepted, ischaemic bowel — is standard teaching but not spelled out in this lecture; not taken from the course material for that specific description).*\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Intestinal polyp* typically causes painless, intermittent rectal bleeding rather than acute colicky pain with a palpable mass. *Volvulus* causes sudden bilious vomiting and abdominal distension from a twisted loop of bowel, but a palpable mass with blood on PR examination is not its hallmark finding. *Dysentery* causes bloody, mucoid diarrhoea from mucosal inflammation, not a palpable mass or colicky, crampy pain pattern. *Hirschsprung\'s disease* is a chronic constipating condition from birth, not an acute presentation with colicky pain, vomiting and a mass.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Colicky abdominal pain, vomiting, and blood found on rectal examination with a palpable mass in a 3-month to 2-year-old is intussusception until proved otherwise.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.514'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-51', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'perinatal',
  stem:'A 2-week-old baby presents with irritability and difficult feeding. On examination, a white adherent coating of the tongue and buccal mucosa is observed. There is some associated erythema and the lesion bleeds when you try to remove the white coating. Which of the following is the MOST appropriate in caring for this baby?',
  options:['Admit to hospital and order a sepsis work-up','Admit to hospital and order a coagulation study as the lesion bleeds','Send the baby home and prescribe an oral antibiotic','Send the baby home and prescribe oral nystatin','Send the baby home and prescribe a parenteral antibiotic'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Oral thrush (candidiasis) presents with white patches that bleed when scraped. It is treated with topical antifungal; hospitalization is not usually needed in otherwise healthy neonates.\n\n---\n\n**Why outpatient oral nystatin is correct.** *A white, adherent coating on the tongue and buccal mucosa that bleeds when scraped off — rather than wiping away cleanly, which would suggest simple retained milk curds — is the classic description of oral candidiasis (thrush), a common, benign fungal overgrowth in young infants (not taken from the course material; no cached lecture in this set covers neonatal oral candidiasis specifically). The associated irritability and reluctance to feed reflect local discomfort rather than systemic illness. First-line treatment for uncomplicated oral thrush in an otherwise well neonate is a topical or oral antifungal such as nystatin suspension, applied to the affected mucosa, managed entirely as an outpatient.*\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong.** *Admission for a sepsis work-up* is not warranted, since nothing in the vignette — an isolated oral lesion with local irritability — suggests systemic infection, fever or a septic-looking baby. *Admission for a coagulation study* over-interprets the bleeding: the bleeding described is superficial, mechanical bleeding from disrupting an inflamed candidal plaque, not evidence of an underlying bleeding disorder, so a full coagulation admission is unnecessary. *Oral or parenteral antibiotics* both target bacteria, and thrush is a fungal infection — an antibacterial agent will not treat it and, by disrupting normal flora, can even worsen candidal overgrowth.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'A white, adherent oral coating that bleeds on scraping in a well neonate is oral candidiasis, treated as an outpatient with topical/oral antifungal, not antibiotics or admission.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.516'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-52', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gi-abdopain',
  stem:'A 6-year-old boy presents with joint pain, rash and severe abdominal pain. From mother\'s description, the rash has appeared in crops on his legs over the past week. What is the MOST likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Gastric bleed','Gastric ulcer','Henoch Schonlein purpura','Systemic lupus erythematosus','Varicella zoster (chicken pox)'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> HSP is a small-vessel vasculitis presenting with palpable purpura, abdominal pain, and arthralgia, often following upper respiratory infections.\n\n---\n\n**Why HSP fits exactly.** `47)Hemorrhagic disorders .txt` gives "the four main characteristics of Henoch-Schonlein purpura" as a purplish rash (reddish-purple spots resembling bruises, on the buttocks, legs and feet, "may be worse in areas of pressure"), swollen sore joints (mainly knees and ankles, sometimes preceding the rash), digestive-tract symptoms ("belly pain, nausea, vomiting and bloody stools," which can also precede the rash), and kidney involvement. This boy\'s rash appearing "in crops" over a week, together with joint pain and severe abdominal pain, is this exact triad — the rash presenting in successive crops reflects the episodic, small-vessel vasculitic process the lecture describes.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Gastric bleed* and *gastric ulcer* could each cause abdominal pain but neither explains a purpuric rash appearing in crops or joint pain — they are single-system findings being asked to cover a multisystem picture. *Systemic lupus erythematosus* can cause joint pain and abdominal symptoms, but its classic rash is a malar (facial) or photosensitive rash, not a crops-of-purpura pattern on the legs, and SLE is also considerably less common than HSP in this age group. *Varicella zoster* produces a vesicular, itchy rash in crops of blisters, not palpable purpura, and does not typically cause severe abdominal pain and arthralgia as a triad.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Purpuric rash in crops on the legs with joint pain and abdominal pain in a school-age child is Henoch-Schonlein purpura, an IgA-mediated small-vessel vasculitis.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.518'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-53', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gi-abdopain',
  stem:'A 3-month-old male infant presents with episodes of drawing up his legs, crying and redcurrant jelly-like stools. What investigation would confirm the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Abdominal computerized tomography (CT)','Abdominal magnetic resonance imaging (MRI)','Abdominal ultrasound','Abdominal X-ray','C-reactive protein (CRP) test'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Ultrasound is the diagnostic test of choice for intussusception, showing the characteristic "target" or "donut" sign.\n\n---\n\n**Why ultrasound is the confirmatory test.** `24)Abdominal pain.txt` names intussusception as the leading cause of intestinal obstruction in infants after the neonatal period, peaking at 3 months to 2 years — this boy is right at the start of that peak — and drawing up the legs with crying, plus redcurrant-jelly stool, is its classic bedside description. *Abdominal ultrasound is the first-line imaging test used to confirm the diagnosis, because the invaginated bowel loop produces a recognisable "target" or "donut" sign on a transverse scan — a radiation-free, rapid, highly sensitive test (not taken from the course material, which names the clinical picture but not the specific confirmatory imaging modality).*\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong.** *CT* can also show intussusception but carries ionising radiation and is not needed as a first-line test in an infant when ultrasound is available and equally diagnostic. *MRI* is not a practical first-line emergency investigation in an acutely unwell infant — it is slow and usually requires sedation. *Abdominal X-ray* may show nonspecific signs of obstruction or a soft-tissue mass but cannot reliably confirm intussusception the way a targeted ultrasound can. *CRP* is a nonspecific inflammatory marker that cannot make an anatomical diagnosis at all.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Abdominal ultrasound, showing the target/donut sign, is the first-line confirmatory investigation for suspected intussusception.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.520'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-54', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gi-abdopain',
  stem:'A common clinical condition that mimics appendicitis in children is:',
  options:['Mesenteric lymphadenitis','Pyloric stenosis','Intussusception','Peptic ulcer disease'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Mesenteric lymphadenitis can cause right lower quadrant pain and tenderness, mimicking appendicitis, especially after viral infections.\n\n---\n\n**Why mesenteric lymphadenitis is the classic mimic.** `24)Abdominal pain.txt` describes "non-specific abdominal pain and mesenteric adenitis" as abdominal pain that "resolves in 24-48 hours," is "less severe than in appendicitis," with variable tenderness in the right iliac fossa, and is "often accompanied by an upper respiratory tract infection with cervical lymphadenopathy." That right-iliac-fossa location is exactly why it is confused with appendicitis at the bedside — inflamed mesenteric nodes, usually reactive to a preceding viral illness, sit near enough to the appendix to produce a similar pain distribution, but the pain is milder and self-limiting rather than progressive and surgical.\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong.** *Pyloric stenosis* presents in early infancy (2-8 weeks) with projectile, non-bilious vomiting and a palpable "olive" mass in the upper abdomen — a completely different age group and clinical picture from appendicitis. *Intussusception* has its own distinct triad of colicky pain, a mass and redcurrant-jelly stool, and occurs in a younger age band (peak 3 months-2 years); while it is a genuine differential for acute abdominal pain, it is not the condition specifically known for closely mimicking the right-iliac-fossa presentation of appendicitis the way mesenteric lymphadenitis is. *Peptic ulcer disease* is uncommon in children and typically produces epigastric pain related to meals, not localised right lower quadrant tenderness.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Mesenteric lymphadenitis, often following a viral upper respiratory infection, is the classic self-limiting mimic of appendicitis in children.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.522'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-55', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gastroenterology',
  stem:'Surgical management of gastroesophageal reflux in infants is indicated:',
  options:['In all cases after 6 months of age','Only in severe cases not responding to medical therapy','As soon as reflux is diagnosed','In every child with vomiting'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Surgery is reserved for complications or failure of conservative management, not all infants with reflux.\n\n---\n\n**Why surgery is reserved for severe, refractory cases.** `22)Vomiting.txt` states surgical management (Nissen fundoplication) is "reserved for children with complications unresponsive to intensive medical treatment or oesophageal stricture." The lecture\'s own management ladder runs from parental reassurance and feeding changes, through thickening agents and alginate therapy, to acid-suppressing drugs (H2 antagonists or proton-pump inhibitors) for reflux disease — surgery sits only at the very end of that ladder, for the minority who fail every medical step or develop a stricture.\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong.** *"In all cases after 6 months of age"* has no basis in the lecture — most infants with simple reflux have already resolved or are resolving by then, since "most cases resolve spontaneously by 12 months of age," and age alone is never the trigger for surgery. *"As soon as reflux is diagnosed"* contradicts the entire stepped-management approach the lecture teaches, where reassurance and conservative measures come first for the large majority of infants who need nothing more. *"In every child with vomiting"* massively over-treats: most vomiting in infancy is simple reflux or feeding-related and needs no intervention at all, let alone surgery, which is reserved for the rare complicated or resistant case.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Surgical treatment of infant GORD (Nissen fundoplication) is reserved for complications or failure of maximal medical therapy, never a routine or early step.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.524'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-56', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gi-diarrhoea',
  stem:'In management of gastroenteritis, breastfeeding and milk feeds should be:',
  options:['Stopped immediately','Replaced with fruit juices','Continued as usual','Delayed until recovery'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Breastfeeding should continue during gastroenteritis as it provides fluids and nutrients, unless contraindicated.\n\n---\n\n**Why feeding continues as usual.** `23).1) Acute diarrhea.txt` is explicit under "Feeding during and after diarrhea": for breastfed infants, "continue breast feeding as usual during and after rehydration therapy," and for formula-fed infants, "continue same “normal” formula and same “normal” concentration AFTER rehydration," reserving lactose-free formula only for confirmed secondary lactose intolerance. For children on a mixed diet the lecture likewise advises continuing normal feeding with small, frequent meals, avoiding only over-sweetened, high-fibre, laxative or fatty foods — nowhere does it support stopping or delaying feeds.\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong.** *Stopping feeding immediately* deprives the child of fluid and calories during an illness that is already causing losses, worsening both dehydration and nutritional status — the opposite of the lecture\'s advice. *Replacing milk with fruit juice* removes protective, nutrient-dense breast milk or formula in favour of a high-sugar fluid that can itself worsen osmotic diarrhoea; the lecture\'s own recommended home-based fluids list plain, weak or unsweetened options, not fruit juice as a milk substitute. *Delaying feeding until recovery* is the same error as stopping feeds — the lecture\'s whole feeding-during-diarrhoea section exists specifically to counter this outdated practice, since prolonged starvation is even listed elsewhere in the lecture as a factor that worsens the course of diarrhoeal illness.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Breastfeeding and normal milk feeds should continue throughout an episode of gastroenteritis, not be stopped, delayed, or replaced with juice.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.526'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-57', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gi-abdopain',
  stem:'In acute appendicitis, the initial pain is typically:',
  options:['Right iliac fossa, constant','Central, colicky','Left hypochondrium','Epigastric burning'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Appendicitis often starts as periumbilical, colicky pain before localizing to the right lower quadrant as inflammation progresses.\n\n---\n\n**Why central, colicky pain is the initial pattern.** `24)Abdominal pain.txt` gives the "classic picture" in older children as "fever, nausea, vomiting, right lower quadrant pain, tenderness and rigidity," but this represents the *established* stage. *The migration from an initial vague, central (periumbilical) colicky pain — from early visceral distension of the appendix, referred to the midline via the embryological midgut innervation — to a later, sharper, localised right iliac fossa pain once the inflamed appendix irritates the adjacent parietal peritoneum, is standard surgical teaching (not spelled out step-by-step in this lecture; not taken from the course material for the visceral-to-parietal mechanism specifically).* This staged progression is exactly why appendicitis is notoriously easy to miss early: the pain does not start where it will eventually localise.\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong.** *Right iliac fossa, constant* describes the later, established stage of appendicitis, once localisation and peritoneal irritation have occurred — not the initial presentation the question asks about. *Left hypochondrium* pain is not a recognised feature of appendicitis at any stage; that region overlies the spleen and left upper abdominal organs, not the appendix or its referred pain pattern. *Epigastric burning* is a dyspeptic pattern typical of peptic or gastritis-type pain, not the periumbilical colic of early appendicitis.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Acute appendicitis classically begins as vague, central, colicky pain that migrates to constant right iliac fossa pain only as inflammation progresses to the parietal peritoneum.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.528'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-58', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gi-diarrhoea',
  stem:'Maternal polyhydramnios is frequently observed in all the following conditions except:',
  options:['Esophageal atresia','Duodenal atresia','Hirschsprung disease','Pyloric atresia'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Polyhydramnios is seen in conditions where swallowing is impaired (esophageal or duodenal atresia). Hirschsprung usually causes postnatal obstruction, not polyhydramnios.\n\n---\n\n**Why Hirschsprung disease is the exception.** *Polyhydramnios develops antenatally when the fetus cannot adequately swallow and absorb amniotic fluid through its gut — so it is a marker of a HIGH, proximal obstruction that blocks swallowing itself, not of a low, distal one (not taken from the course material; the cached genetics and constipation decks do not connect Hirschsprung disease to amniotic fluid volume). Hirschsprung disease is a distal, colonic aganglionosis: swallowing and upper gut transit are entirely normal in utero, amniotic fluid is swallowed and absorbed as usual, and the functional obstruction it causes only becomes clinically apparent after birth, as delayed passage of meconium and abdominal distension — it does not raise amniotic fluid volume antenatally.*\n\n**Why each of the other three DOES cause polyhydramnios.** *Oesophageal atresia* blocks the fetal swallowing pathway at its very start, so essentially no swallowed fluid reaches the gut to be absorbed, producing a marked, classic polyhydramnios. *Duodenal atresia* obstructs just distal to the stomach — swallowed fluid reaches the stomach and duodenum but cannot pass further to be absorbed by the small bowel — also producing polyhydramnios, with the characteristic postnatal "double bubble" sign this bank tests separately in `pedep-gi-46`. *Pyloric atresia* likewise obstructs proximally, at the gastric outlet, before any meaningful fluid absorption downstream, again producing polyhydramnios.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Polyhydramnios marks a HIGH fetal gut obstruction that blocks swallowing (oesophageal, duodenal or pyloric atresia); Hirschsprung disease is a distal, postnatal obstruction and does not raise amniotic fluid volume.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.530'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-59', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gastroenterology',
  stem:'A 2-month-old baby is seen in the pediatric outpatient department. She was born at term, weighing 3.5 kg, and is breastfed. Her mother is concerned as she has vomited some of the milk after most feeds since birth. She cries when she vomits. She is continuing to grow along the 50th centile. What is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Gastroesophageal reflux','Gastroenteritis','Infant colic','Overfeeding','Pyloric stenosis'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Reflux is common, especially if baby is thriving and growing normally. Red flags (projectile vomiting, poor growth) are absent.\n\n---\n\n**Why reflux fits.** `22)Vomiting.txt` describes gastro-oesophageal reflux as "very common in infancy," caused by functional immaturity of the lower oesophageal sphincter, a predominantly fluid diet and a horizontal posture — exactly the physiology of a 2-month-old who has vomited after most feeds "since birth." Her steady growth along the 50th centile matches the lecture\'s statement that "most infants with gastro-oesophageal reflux are putting on weight normally," and there is no red flag such as projectile vomiting, bile staining or faltering growth to suggest anything more serious.\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong.** *Gastroenteritis* is an acute, usually self-limited illness with diarrhoea as its defining feature, not a symptom present "since birth" with steady growth. *Infant colic* causes excessive crying, typically in bouts, but is not primarily a vomiting disorder. *Overfeeding* is a plausible mimic, but the lecture treats reflux, not overfeeding, as the default diagnosis once thriving and normal growth are established, and nothing in the vignette (such as very large feed volumes) specifically points to overfeeding instead. *Pyloric stenosis* is excluded by the pattern here: it is not present "since birth" in a mild, non-progressive form, and this baby is not projectile-vomiting or failing to thrive — the lecture places pyloric stenosis at 2-8 weeks with vomiting that "increases in frequency and forcefulness over time," not this stable, well-tolerated pattern.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'A thriving infant who vomits after most feeds since birth, with normal growth and no red flags, has physiological gastro-oesophageal reflux, not pyloric stenosis or overfeeding.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.532'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-61', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gi-diarrhoea',
  stem:'An 18-month-old girl is seen in the outpatient department. She presents with loose stools two to three times each day, with no blood in them. She is generally difficult, and it has become a battle to get her to feed. Examination of her abdomen, although difficult due to distress at being examined, is unremarkable apart from being distended. She is not on any medication. Her weight was at the 9th centile and is now below the 0.4th centile. What is the most likely cause for weight loss?',
  options:['Chronic non-specific diarrhea','Celiac disease','Hirschsprung disease','Lactose intolerance','Ulcerative colitis'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Failure to thrive, distended abdomen, and persistent diarrhea with weight dropping below centiles strongly suggest malabsorption due to celiac disease.\n\n---\n\n**Why celiac disease fits.** `23).1) Acute diarrhea.txt` lists "sensitivity to milk, gluten" among the non-infectious causes of chronic diarrhoea. *Coeliac disease produces gluten-driven villous atrophy and malabsorption, classically presenting around weaning onto gluten-containing cereals with loose stools, a distended abdomen from bloating and gas, irritability/feeding difficulty, and a crossing-down of weight centiles from malabsorbed nutrients (not spelled out in this level of clinical detail in the cached lecture, which names coeliac only as a category of chronic diarrhoea; not taken from the course material for the specific presentation described).* This girl\'s dramatic centile crossing (9th down to below 0.4th), distended abdomen and feeding difficulty is the classic malabsorptive weight-loss picture, distinct from a simple, well-child chronic diarrhoea.\n\n**Why each distractor fits the stool pattern less well.** *Chronic non-specific (toddler\'s) diarrhoea* is explicitly a benign diagnosis with normal growth — this bank\'s own `pedep-gi-63` shows the same label paired with a child still "growing along the 50th centile," the opposite of this girl\'s severe faltering growth. *Hirschsprung disease* causes constipation and obstruction, not loose stools with malabsorptive weight loss. *Lactose intolerance* causes loose, watery, often frothy stools after dairy but is not typically associated with such severe faltering growth if the rest of the diet is adequate. *Ulcerative colitis* classically causes bloody diarrhoea, which is explicitly absent here.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Loose non-bloody stools with a distended abdomen and severe centile-crossing weight loss around toddler age is the malabsorptive picture of coeliac disease, not benign toddler\'s diarrhoea.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.536'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-63', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gi-diarrhoea',
  stem:'A 2-year-old boy has had diarrhea for the last 3 months. He produces up to four stools a day, which are loose, brown in color, and usually contain undigested food. The rest of the family is well. Examination is normal, and his personal child health record shows that he is growing along the 50th centile. What is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Chronic non-specific diarrhea','Post-gastroenteritis syndrome','Cow\'s milk protein allergy','Inflammatory bowel disease','Lactose intolerance'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> This benign toddler\'s diarrhea shows loose stools with normal growth and no systemic illness.\n\n---\n\n**Why chronic non-specific (toddler\'s) diarrhoea fits.** `23).1) Acute diarrhea.txt` classifies "chronic diarrhea" among its main diarrhoea types and, separately, describes "persistent diarrhea" as usually post-infectious and associated with nutritional deterioration — the key distinguishing feature the lecture gives for that more worrying category is a link to malnutrition and weight loss. This boy has neither: he is "growing along the 50th centile" with an entirely normal examination, and his stool — loose, containing undigested food, several times a day, over months, in an otherwise thriving toddler — is the textbook description of chronic non-specific diarrhoea (toddler\'s diarrhoea), a benign motility variant rather than a disease process *(the specific label and stool description are standard toddler-diarrhoea teaching; not spelled out under that name in the cached lecture, so this detail is not taken from the course material)*.\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong.** *Post-gastroenteritis syndrome* follows a specific acute infective episode with ongoing malabsorption, not this insidious three-month history without a preceding acute illness described. *Cow\'s milk protein allergy* typically also produces other allergic features (eczema, blood/mucus in stool, or feeding-related distress) and is not this well-tolerated, growth-preserved picture. *Inflammatory bowel disease* would be expected to impair growth and often cause blood in the stool — both explicitly absent here, and the normal growth trajectory argues strongly against it. *Lactose intolerance* causes diarrhoea tied to dairy intake, not this constant three-month pattern independent of any noted dietary trigger.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Loose stools containing undigested food for months in a thriving, normally growing toddler with a normal examination is chronic non-specific (toddler\'s) diarrhoea, a benign diagnosis of normal growth.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.540'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-64', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gi-abdopain',
  stem:'What is the most common cause of recurrent abdominal pain in children?',
  options:['Appendicitis','Ascariasis','Functional','Hepatitis'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**Functional abdominal pain is the most common cause of recurrent abdominal pain in children.** `24)Abdominal pain.txt` defines recurrent (chronic) abdominal pain as at least one episode of pain a month for two consecutive months, severe enough to interfere with routine functioning, and states this "occurs in 10-15% of children" — a scale far larger than any single organic diagnosis. The lecture frames functional pain explicitly as "a Diagnosis of Exclusion," reached by history, examination and initial screening tests once the much rarer organic causes have been ruled out, and separates functional from organic pain by site (vague, midline rather than localised), timing (never nocturnal, unlike organic pain which may wake the child) and the absence of the alarm signals — weight loss, growth deceleration, blood in stool, family history of organic GI disease — that would point toward disease instead.\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong.** *Appendicitis* is an important acute surgical emergency, but it is a single episode of acute, not recurrent, pain, and it is far less common overall than the broad functional-pain group. *Ascariasis*, an intestinal worm infestation, can cause abdominal pain and even mechanical obstruction of the pancreatic duct in heavy infestations, but it is one specific, geographically variable organic cause, not the dominant one across the general paediatric population that this lecture\'s 10-15% figure describes. *Hepatitis* causes right-upper-quadrant pain as part of a broader illness with jaundice and malaise, not the isolated, recurring pain pattern the question is asking about, and is again a single organic cause rather than the leading overall category.\n\nWritten for this bank — Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Functional abdominal pain — a diagnosis of exclusion once organic causes and alarm signals are screened out — is the most common cause of recurrent abdominal pain in children, affecting 10-15% overall.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.542'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-65', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gi-diarrhoea',
  stem:'What is the most common cause of gastroenteritis in children?',
  options:['Shigella','Giardia','Cryptosporidium','Viruses','Campylobacter jejuni'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**Viruses are the leading cause of acute gastroenteritis in children.** `23).1) Acute diarrhea.txt` states that acute watery diarrhoea (acute gastroenteritis) makes up about 80% of all diarrhoea in children, and that within that group, viruses cause roughly 70% of cases — well ahead of bacteria (10-20%) and protozoa (under 10%). The lecture names rotavirus, norovirus, enteric adenoviruses, caliciviruses, astroviruses and enteroviruses as the leading viral agents, and separately identifies rotavirus specifically as one of the most important causes of acute infective diarrhoea in developing countries. This is also why rotavirus immunisation is highlighted in the lecture\'s prevention section — targeting the single largest contributor to the disease burden.\n\n**Why each named organism is wrong on its own.** *Shigella* and *Campylobacter jejuni* are both bacterial causes, together making up only part of the 10-20% bacterial share, and are specifically linked in the lecture to dysentery (bloody, tenesmus-associated diarrhoea) rather than the ordinary watery gastroenteritis that dominates overall numbers. *Giardia* and *Cryptosporidium* are both protozoal causes, the smallest of the three groups at under 10% of cases, and tend to produce more persistent or malabsorptive illness rather than accounting for most acute gastroenteritis presentations.\n\nWritten for this bank — Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Viruses cause roughly 70% of acute paediatric gastroenteritis, making them the leading cause overall, ahead of bacteria and protozoa combined.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.544'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-66', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gastroenterology',
  stem:'What is true about the management of gastroesophageal reflux in children?',
  options:['Parents can be reassured; it is a process the child will outgrow as they get older','Thickening the feedings does not work','Most infants have slow weight gain','It always deserves further evaluation','Surgery is indicated in preterm infants'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Most infants with GER are “happy spitters,” and reassurance is sufficient. It usually resolves by 12–18 months.\n\n---\n\n**Why parental reassurance is the true statement.** `22)Vomiting.txt` states that reflux "most cases resolve spontaneously by 12 months of age due to a combination of maturation of the lower oesophageal sphincter, an upright posture and more solids in the diet," and lists "parental reassurance" first among its management measures — this bank\'s own box adds the memorable clinical term "happy spitters" for these thriving, otherwise well infants, extending slightly beyond the lecture\'s wording but consistent with its message *(the specific phrase "happy spitters" itself is not printed in the cached lecture; not taken from the course material)*.\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong.** *"Thickening the feedings does not work"* contradicts the lecture\'s management list, which specifically offers "adding inert thickening agents to feeds" as a recognised measure. *"Most infants have slow weight gain"* reverses the lecture\'s own statement that "most infants with gastro-oesophageal reflux are putting on weight normally." *"It always deserves further evaluation"* contradicts the lecture\'s statement that reflux "is usually diagnosed clinically, and no investigations are required" unless the history is atypical or complicated. *"Surgery is indicated in preterm infants"* misstates the surgical criterion: the lecture reserves surgery for "complications unresponsive to intensive medical treatment or oesophageal stricture," a functional threshold, not prematurity itself, though preterm infants are separately listed as a group in whom reflux disease is more common overall.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Most infants with gastro-oesophageal reflux are thriving "happy spitters" who need only parental reassurance, since the condition usually resolves spontaneously by 12-18 months.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.546'
},

{
  id:'pedep-gi-67', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gi-abdopain',
  stem:'Which is a medical cause of acute abdominal pain?',
  options:['Diabetic ketoacidosis','Thalassemia major','Hyperglycemia','Hemosiderosis','Acute hemolytic anemia'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**Diabetic ketoacidosis is a recognised medical (non-surgical) cause of acute abdominal pain in children.** `24)Abdominal pain.txt` lists diabetic ketoacidosis explicitly among the "Important Extra-Abdominal Causes" of abdominal pain, alongside pharyngitis, abdominal migraine, familial Mediterranean fever, Henoch-Schonlein purpura, sickle cell disease, pneumonia, HUS, drugs and lead poisoning, and again under "Other causes" of acute abdominal pain alongside acute pancreatitis, lower-lobe pneumonia and HSP. DKA produces genuine, sometimes severe, abdominal pain through a combination of the metabolic acidosis itself, dehydration and ileus — a classic trap where the abdominal pain can mimic a surgical abdomen and delay recognition of the underlying metabolic emergency (the precise mechanism of DKA-related abdominal pain is standard endocrine teaching beyond what this lecture\'s single list-entry states; *not taken from the course material* for that mechanistic detail).\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong.** *Thalassaemia major*, *hyperglycaemia* alone (without ketoacidosis), *haemosiderosis* and *acute haemolytic anaemia* are haematological or metabolic states that are not named anywhere in this lecture\'s lists of causes of acute abdominal pain — hyperglycaemia without acidosis is typically asymptomatic abdominally, and the others are chronic or systemic haematological conditions without a recognised acute-abdomen presentation in this material.\n\nWritten for this bank — Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Diabetic ketoacidosis is a medical (extra-abdominal) cause of acute abdominal pain and a recognised mimic of a surgical abdomen in children.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.548'
},

{
  id:'pedep-acc-1', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'accidents',
  stem:'A 14-year-old girl self-presents to the emergency department with a history of deliberate self-poisoning.She agrees to blood tests which reveal high paracetamol levels necessitating treatment.\nWhich is the appropriate treatment for this teenager?',
  options:['Intravenous desferrioxamine','Intravenous N-acetylcysteine','Active cooling','Alkalinization of urine with bicarbonate','Activated charcoal'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> N-acetylcysteine (NAC) is the antidote for paracetamol overdose. It acts to replenish glutathione and prevent hepatic damage. Activated charcoal is only useful within 1 hour of ingestion and is not a substitute for NAC.\n\n---\n\n**Why IV N-acetylcysteine is right.** `5) Poisoning in children_.txt` covers paracetamol under "Some poisons and their treatment": early features are abdominal pain and vomiting, with liver failure emerging later, 12 to 24 hours after ingestion. Management is risk-assessed by measuring the plasma paracetamol concentration, and treatment is with intravenous acetylcysteine if that concentration is high or if liver function is already abnormal. This 14-year-old\'s deliberate ingestion with a high measured paracetamol level is exactly that scenario, so NAC is the indicated treatment.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Intravenous desferrioxamine* is the chelator the same lecture names for iron overdose, given in moderate-to-severe iron toxicity -- it has no role in paracetamol poisoning and does not touch glutathione depletion. *Active cooling* has no place in the paracetamol pathway; the lecture uses cooling nowhere on the paracetamol slide, and nothing here suggests hyperthermia. *Alkalinisation of urine with bicarbonate* is the lecture\'s salicylate intervention -- it increases renal excretion of salicylates, a different drug with a different toxicology, and does nothing for paracetamol-induced hepatotoxicity. *Activated charcoal* is genuinely useful in poisoning, but the lecture is explicit that it is only useful within 1 hour of ingestion and is not a substitute for NAC -- this girl has already reached the stage of confirmed high paracetamol levels needing treatment, well past the charcoal window, and charcoal does not reverse glutathione depletion the way NAC does.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'In paracetamol overdose, IV N-acetylcysteine -- not activated charcoal, urinary alkalinisation, active cooling or an iron chelator -- is the antidote, guided by the plasma paracetamol concentration.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.557'
},

{
  id:'pedep-acc-2', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'accidents',
  stem:'Louise, aged 4 years, was hit by a car in the local supermarket car park. She is brought to the Emergency Department by ambulance. An initial assessment shows:\nAirway: Talking to mother\nBreathing: Receiving oxygen via a rebreathing circuit; oxygen saturation 99%; air entry satisfactory and equal bilaterally; respiratory rate 30/min\nCirculation: Pulse 160/min, blood pressure 90/50 mmHg, capillary refill time 3 seconds\nDisability: Alert but frightened and agitated, moving all four limbs\nShe has abrasions to her left flank and pain in her left shoulder.\nWhat is the next intervention needed?',
  options:['Analgesia','Blood glucose measurement','Chest X-ray','Intravenous access','Intubation and ventilation'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Despite normal airway and breathing, Louise has signs of circulatory compromise (tachycardia and prolonged capillary refill). Establishing IV access is crucial for resuscitation and further investigations.\n\n---\n\n**Working through Louise on the ABCDE assessment.** `3) Pediatric resuscitation.txt` and `4) Shock in pediatrics_.txt` both structure the seriously unwell or injured child through Airway, Breathing, Circulation and Disability, with early recognition and intervention named as the key to preventing respiratory or circulatory failure before it becomes irreversible. Louise\'s airway is patent (talking to her mother) and her breathing is adequate (oxygen saturation 99%, equal bilateral air entry, and she is already receiving supplemental oxygen), so those two letters are covered. Circulation is where the compromise sits: a pulse of 160/min and a capillary refill time of 3 seconds are exactly the early, compensated features of shock the shock lecture lists -- tachycardia as the early sign, delayed capillary refill beyond 2 seconds as another -- while her blood pressure of 90/50 shows hypotension, the late sign, has not yet appeared. Catching and treating this compensated stage while it is still reversible is the lecture\'s explicit point, and the practical next step in circulatory support is establishing IV access, both to give fluid and to draw the bloods this trauma work-up will need.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Analgesia* matters for her shoulder pain and flank abrasions, but the ABCDE sequence works down from the most immediately threatening problem, and Louise\'s circulation has not yet been addressed. *Blood glucose measurement* sits under Disability in the same ABCDE structure, assessed alongside conscious level and pupils -- it comes after, not before, circulatory access is secured. *Chest X-ray* is an investigation for once resuscitation is under way, not an intervention that itself treats her circulatory compromise. *Intubation and ventilation* would answer an airway or breathing problem, and Louise has neither: she is talking, saturating at 99% and moving air equally on both sides, so nothing in her assessment indicates this step.\n\n**⚠️ Within-bank reprint — p.636.** This vignette is printed once in the Accidents section and again in Emergencies. The clinical content is identical down to every observation — the same pulse, blood pressure, capillary refill time, saturations and respiratory rate — and the option list, its order and the key are identical. The only differences are typographic: p.636 lays the findings out with arrows rather than colons and splits the Breathing findings across two rows, so “Breathing” is printed twice on that page. It is held once in the app, under the p.559 printing.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'In the ABCDE approach, a child in early (compensated) shock with a normal airway and breathing needs IV access next -- not analgesia, glucose testing, imaging or intubation.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.559 (the same vignette is reprinted on p.636 inside the Emergencies section, with the ABCDE findings laid out differently and the Breathing line split across two rows; same five options, same order, same key)'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-1', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'A female infant is born through emergency cesarean section to a 34-year-old mother whose pregnancy was complicated by hypertension and abnormal fetal heart monitoring. At delivery she is covered in thick, green meconium and is limp, apneic, and bradycardic. Which of the following is the best first step in her resuscitation?',
  options:['Administer IV bicarbonate.','Administer IV naloxone.','Initiate bag-and-mask ventilation.','Initiate chest compressions immediately.','Intubate with an endotracheal tube and suction meconium from the trachea.'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'This infant has every feature that turns thick meconium staining from a benign finding into a resuscitation problem: she is limp, apnoeic and bradycardic, not vigorous. `9) Respiratory distress in newborn.txt` explains why this matters -- chronic or acute fetal hypoxia is what stimulates fetal peristalsis and relaxes the anal sphincter, causing meconium to pass into the amniotic fluid in the first place, and the same hypoxic stress drives fetal gasping and breathing movements that aspirate the meconium-stained fluid into the airway. Once meconium reaches the lower airway it can cause complete obstruction with atelectasis, or a ball-valve partial obstruction with air trapping, over-inflation and the risk of pneumothorax, on top of chemical pneumonitis and surfactant deactivation -- the lecture lists these as the direct complications of meconium aspiration syndrome. Because a floppy, apnoeic, bradycardic infant covered in thick meconium cannot be assumed to have a clear airway, direct laryngoscopy with endotracheal intubation and suction of meconium from the trachea, before positive-pressure breaths are given, is the step that prevents whatever meconium sits at or above the cords from being driven further down into the lung *(the specific sequencing of "look before you ventilate" for a non-vigorous, meconium-stained infant is standard neonatal resuscitation practice -- not taken from the course material, which covers meconium aspiration syndrome and its consequences but does not spell out this delivery-room algorithm)*.\n\nEach distractor skips this step. *IV bicarbonate* has no role in the first minutes of a resuscitation and does nothing about an obstructed airway. *IV naloxone* reverses opioid-induced respiratory depression, not meconium plugging, and this stem gives no opioid exposure. *Bag-and-mask ventilation* is exactly what risks forcing meconium from the pharynx deeper into the trachea and bronchi in a non-vigorous infant, which is why airway inspection and suction come first. *Chest compressions* are reserved for a heart rate that stays low despite effective ventilation -- they are not the first action in an infant whose problem is an airway that has not yet been secured.\n\nWritten for this bank — Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'A non-vigorous, meconium-stained newborn is intubated and the trachea suctioned before positive-pressure ventilation is given, because bag-mask ventilation first can drive meconium further into the airway.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.577'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-2', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  image:'q-pd-ep-580', imgAlt:'Clinical photograph, close-up view of skin on the leg.',
  stem:'You are in the Acute Assessment Unit and assess David, a 15-month-old boy, who has a fever of 38.5°C. He has had a runny nose, cough, and fever for 3 days. Since this morning, he has been sleeping excessively and is difficult to wake. His heart rate is elevated. He has a rash scattered over his legs that does not blanch with pressure. What is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Acute lymphoblastic leukaemia','Henoch–Schönlein purpura','Immune thrombocytopenia','Non-accidental injury','Septicaemia'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> A non-blanching rash, fever, lethargy, and elevated heart rate are signs suggestive of meningococcal septicemia. This is a medical emergency requiring urgent antibiotics and supportive care.\n\n---\n\n`18) febrile child and life threatening infections.txt` lists the "red flags" that mark a febrile child as seriously unwell: lethargy or excessive irritability, respiratory distress, petechiae or purpura, and any change in consciousness. David carries three of these together -- he is difficult to wake (a change in consciousness), tachycardic, and has a rash that does not blanch on pressure, which is the clinical definition of petechiae/purpura. The lecture names meningococcaemia explicitly as the diagnosis a non-blanching rash raises, and stresses no delay in giving antibiotics and supportive therapy once it is suspected, with a third-generation cephalosporin such as ceftriaxone as the preferred agent -- this combination of a preceding viral-type prodrome (coryza, cough, fever) tipping over into rapid deterioration is the classic evolution of meningococcal disease the lecture describes as having very rapid progression and high morbidity and mortality.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Acute lymphoblastic leukaemia* can present with bruising and petechiae from marrow infiltration, but it evolves over days to weeks with pallor, bone pain or lymphadenopathy, not a 3-day febrile illness that suddenly produces reduced consciousness -- the acuity here does not fit. *Henoch-Schönlein purpura* classically gives a palpable purpuric rash over the buttocks and extensor surfaces of the legs, which can look similar on a photograph, but it is not typically accompanied by fever, tachycardia and a depressed conscious level; HSP does not explain why David is hard to wake. *Immune thrombocytopenia* causes petechiae and bruising from a low platelet count but, like HSP, does not produce fever with reduced consciousness -- the child is usually otherwise well. *Non-accidental injury* must always be considered with unexplained bruising in an infant, but nothing in the story (a viral prodrome, physiological tachycardia, and a rash that specifically does not blanch) points toward inflicted injury over an infective cause, and the red-flag combination here is textbook sepsis, not trauma.\n\nThe photograph shows a close crop of skin on the leg: several red macules are visible, one lesion is a darker, more purpuric colour than the rest, a strip of dressing tape crosses part of the frame, and the examiner is pressing a fingertip against the skin to test for blanching. The image is soft enough that fine skin texture and the exact margins of the lesions cannot be made out, but the colour contrast between the paler macules and the single darker lesion, and the blanching test itself, are legible -- consistent with the non-blanching purpuric component the stem describes.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'A febrile infant with reduced consciousness, tachycardia and a non-blanching rash is treated as meningococcal septicaemia and given antibiotics without delay, ahead of leukaemia, HSP, ITP or non-accidental injury.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.580'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-3', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'A 3-year-old boy who is unconscious arrives at the Emergency Department. Airway, breathing, and circulation have been managed. Blood glucose is normal. On examination, his pupils are bilaterally pinpoint. Temperature and other vitals are normal. What is the most likely cause?',
  options:['Third nerve lesion','Severe hypoxia','Hypothermia','Tentorial herniation','Opiate poisoning'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Pinpoint pupils in an unconscious child with stable vital signs and normal glucose strongly suggest opioid ingestion. Opiates cause central nervous system depression with characteristic miosis.\n\n---\n\nThis is a Disability-step assessment: `3) Pediatric resuscitation.txt` places pupil size and reactivity, alongside level of consciousness and posture, under the D of the ABCDE sequence once airway, breathing and circulation are already secured -- exactly the point this stem has reached. The two big reversible causes of reduced consciousness that Disability is designed to catch are hypoglycaemia and poisoning, and a normal blood glucose here has already excluded the first. Bilaterally symmetrical, pinpoint (constricted) pupils with a normal temperature and normal other vital signs is the classic pupillary sign of opioid toxicity *(the miosis-from-opioid-agonism mechanism itself is general toxicology knowledge, not spelled out on this Disability slide -- not taken from the course material)*. `5) Poisoning in children_.txt` covers several specific poisons and their toxidromes but does not detail opioids by name; the diagnosis here is reached by applying the same Disability-and-poisoning framework the lecture teaches, to a presentation the lecture does not itself walk through.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Third nerve lesion* produces a dilated, poorly reactive pupil on the affected side (with ptosis and a "down-and-out" eye), the opposite pupillary finding to bilateral miosis, and it would not be symmetrical. *Severe hypoxia* is a cause of altered consciousness in its own right, but airway and breathing have already been managed in this stem, and hypoxic/ischaemic insult more typically produces dilated or fixed pupils late, not pinpoint ones. *Hypothermia* is excluded directly -- the stem states temperature is normal -- and significant hypothermia tends toward sluggish, sometimes dilated pupils rather than bilateral miosis. *Tentorial (uncal) herniation* classically causes a unilateral fixed, dilated pupil from third-nerve compression as the uncus pushes against the nerve, not bilaterally symmetrical constriction, and it would usually come with other signs of raised intracranial pressure or a lateralising deficit that this stem does not describe.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'In an unconscious child with airway, breathing and circulation already managed and a normal glucose, bilaterally pinpoint pupils point to opioid poisoning, not a third nerve lesion, hypoxia, hypothermia or tentorial herniation.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.582'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-4', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'You are called to assess a 3-year-old boy with a high fever. The nurse reports that he is very sleepy. On entering the resuscitation room, the child makes no spontaneous response. When his name is called, there is no response. On stimulation, his eyes open, he cries, raises his hand, and pushes your hand away. What is this child' + String.fromCharCode(39) + 's Glasgow Coma Score (GCS)?',
  options:['8','9','10','11','12'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> •Eyes open to pain = 2\n> •Verbal response = cries = 2\n> •Motor response = (localizes pain)= 5\n> Total = 9 (moderate coma).\n\n---\n\n`3) Pediatric resuscitation.txt` places pupil size, posture and level of consciousness under the Disability step of ABCDE, but this bank\'s scoring itself -- the three-domain eye/verbal/motor breakdown with individual point values -- is not spelled out on that slide *(the GCS/Children' + String.fromCharCode(39) + 's Coma Scale scoring criteria are standard paediatric practice, not taken from the course material)*. Because this boy is 3 years old, the age-appropriate scale is the Children\'s Coma Scale (used under 4 years), which substitutes developmentally appropriate criteria for the verbal and motor domains rather than scoring speech and commands as an older child or adult would be scored. Working through the stem against the box\'s three components: he does not respond to voice but does open his eyes when stimulated (painfully) -- "eyes open to pain" scores 2 out of a maximum 4. He cries in response to stimulation rather than making any words or a spontaneous response -- "cries" is the Children\'s Coma Scale equivalent of a confused or inappropriate verbal response and scores 2 out of a maximum 5. He raises his hand and pushes the examiner\'s hand away, which is a purposeful attempt to remove a painful stimulus -- "localises pain" scores 5 out of a maximum 6, the second-highest motor grade below obeying commands. Summing 2 + 2 + 5 gives a total GCS of 9, which the box labels moderate coma (a GCS in the 9-12 range is conventionally graded moderate, below 8 severe, above 12 mild -- *not taken from the course material*).\n\n**Why the other totals are wrong.** *8* would follow from under-scoring one domain, for example scoring the motor response as flexion/withdrawal (4) instead of localising (5) -- but pushing the hand away purposefully is localising, not simple withdrawal. *10, 11 and 12* would each require crediting a higher verbal or motor score than the stem supports -- there is no spontaneous eye opening, no word-level or oriented speech, and no obeying of a command, so no combination reaching double figures above 9 is consistent with what is described.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Eyes-open-to-pain (2) + cries (2) + localises pain (5) on the Children' + String.fromCharCode(39) + 's Coma Scale sums to a GCS of 9, graded moderate coma.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.584'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-5', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'Ryan, aged 10 months, is rushed to the children' + String.fromCharCode(39) + 's emergency department after being found submerged in the bath. His mother runs in screaming, “Help my baby, please.” What is the next most appropriate step?',
  options:['Commence chest compressions in a ratio of 15:2','If the child is not breathing, commence bag and mask ventilation','Place the child on the examination couch and position his head neutrally','Remove wet clothing/towels and dry the baby vigorously','Stimulate the baby and shout for help'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> The initial steps include checking responsiveness by stimulating the child and calling for help immediately. Prompt assistance is critical in pediatric resuscitation due to the rapid deterioration children can undergo.\n\n---\n\nRyan has just been pulled from the bath -- nothing in the stem yet tells the responder whether he is breathing, whether his airway is clear, or whether he has a pulse. `3) Pediatric resuscitation.txt` frames the whole approach to a collapsed child around the sequence "when??? the diagnosis of cardiopulmonary arrest is based on the absence of a palpable pulse, unconsciousness, apnoea" -- none of which can be assessed before the responder has stimulated the baby and established that help is coming. The lecture\'s wider message, that "the key to management of the seriously ill child is early recognition and intervention," starts with exactly this: confirm responsiveness and summon assistance before moving to any specific airway, breathing or circulation manoeuvre, because a single rescuer cannot both assess a collapsed infant thoroughly and manage the airway or start compressions alone. Only once the response to stimulation and the call for help are under way does the rest of the ABCDE sequence -- airway positioning, breathing assessment, chest compressions if there is no pulse -- follow in order.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Chest compressions at 15:2* would only be started once Ryan is confirmed pulseless or has a heart rate too low to sustain the circulation -- that assessment has not yet been made, so compressions cannot be the next step. *Bag-and-mask ventilation "if the child is not breathing"* is conditional on an assessment of breathing that has not been done yet either, and it still comes after the initial stimulate-and-call-for-help step. *Placing the child on the couch and positioning the head neutrally* is part of opening the airway, which again presumes the responsiveness check has already happened. *Removing wet clothing and drying the baby* addresses the real risk of hypothermia after submersion, but it is a supportive measure that follows, rather than precedes, confirming the child\'s responsiveness and getting help on the way.\n\n**⚠️ Within-bank reprint — p.630.** The endpoint file prints this vignette twice inside its Emergencies section. The two printings differ only in wording that carries no clinical weight — “runs in screaming” against “runs screaming into the department”, “position his head neutrally” against “put his head into the neutral position”, and the compression ratio set as 15:2 on one page and 15 : 2 on the other. The five options, their order and the key are identical. It is held once in the app, under the p.586 printing.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'For a collapsed infant of unknown responsiveness, the first step is stimulating the child and calling for help -- before any airway, breathing or compression manoeuvre is chosen.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.586 (the same vignette is printed again on p.630 with the same five options in the same order and the same key, differing only in punctuation and a few words of phrasing)'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-6', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'Mohammed, aged 8 months, has been vomiting and refusing feeds for 2 days. Initially, he had episodes of intense crying, drawing his legs up as if in pain, and appeared irritable. Despite oral rehydration, his vomiting continued and he became lethargic. On hospital admission, he is in shock. What is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Gastroenteritis','Intussusception','Malrotation with volvulus','Meckel' + String.fromCharCode(39) + 's diverticulum','Strangulated hernia'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Classic features include episodic abdominal pain, vomiting, lethargy, and drawing up of legs. Prolonged symptoms and progression to shock suggest intestinal obstruction from intussusception.\n\n---\n\n`24)Abdominal pain.txt` defines intussusception as the invagination of proximal bowel into a distal segment, most commonly ileum passing into the caecum through the ileocaecal valve, and names it as the most common cause of intestinal obstruction in infants after the neonatal period, with a peak age of presentation between 3 months and 2 years -- Mohammed, at 8 months, sits squarely in that window. The episodic, colicky pain with drawing up of the legs is the classic intermittent-obstruction pattern intussusception produces as the telescoped bowel intermittently distends and squeezes; vomiting and progressive lethargy follow as the obstruction persists, and once oral rehydration fails to keep pace with ongoing losses and bowel-wall compromise, the child can deteriorate into hypovolaemic and eventually septic shock as the bowel becomes ischaemic. The same lecture lists intussusception among the causes of acute intestinal obstruction alongside incarcerated inguinal hernia, volvulus and impacted faecal mass, confirming it belongs in that same emergency surgical differential the stem is testing.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Gastroenteritis* causes vomiting and diarrhoea but not the episodic, colicky abdominal pain with leg-drawing that dominates this history, and it does not typically progress to shock this rapidly once oral rehydration has been attempted appropriately. *Malrotation with volvulus* is also a surgical emergency and the lecture flags bile-stained vomiting as the alarm feature pointing to it -- the stem gives no mention of bilious vomiting, so intussusception\'s episodic-colic-plus-lethargy pattern fits better. *Meckel\'s diverticulum* classically presents with painless rectal bleeding rather than this colicky, obstructive picture. *Strangulated hernia* would be expected to show a palpable, tender groin or scrotal swelling, which the stem does not describe, making it a less likely fit than the intermittent colic-and-lethargy pattern that points specifically to intussusception.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'An infant with episodic colicky pain, leg-drawing, vomiting and progression to lethargy/shock has intussusception until proven otherwise, distinguished from gastroenteritis, malrotation, Meckel' + String.fromCharCode(39) + 's diverticulum and strangulated hernia by its intermittent obstructive pattern.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.588'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-7', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'Mohammed (8 months old, 8 kg) is in shock and requires a normal saline 0.9% bolus. What is the initial volume you would administer?',
  options:['40 mL','160 mL','320 mL','680 mL','800 mL'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> The initial fluid bolus for a child in shock is 20 mL/kg of 0.9% saline. For an 8 kg infant, this equals 160 mL. This bolus may be repeated depending on the child’s clinical response. In specific conditions like trauma or diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA), smaller or more cautious volumes may be used.\n\n---\n\n`4) Shock in pediatrics_.txt` sets out fluid management for a shocked child as fluid resuscitation first, followed by deficit replacement, maintenance fluid and ongoing losses, and states directly that children who require more than 40 mL/kg of resuscitation fluid overall are likely to need mechanical ventilation -- implying resuscitation is given in discrete boluses well below that cumulative total, consistent with the box\'s 20 mL/kg-per-bolus figure *(the specific 20 mL/kg bolus rate itself sits on a slide the lecture cache did not capture as text; it is standard paediatric resuscitation practice and is printed unambiguously in this bank\'s own explanation box -- flagging the arithmetic rather than the rate as the sourced part)*. The calculation itself is straightforward: 20 mL/kg multiplied by Mohammed\'s 8 kg weight gives 160 mL, matching the box and the highlighted option.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *40 mL* corresponds to 5 mL/kg, far too small a bolus to make any meaningful impact on a shocked child\'s circulating volume. *320 mL* would follow from doubling the correct rate to 40 mL/kg -- the cumulative ceiling the lecture associates with needing ventilatory support, not a single bolus volume. *680 mL* does not correspond to any standard per-kg rate for this weight and looks like an arithmetic slip rather than a clinically meaningful dose. *800 mL* is exactly 100 mL/kg, the Holliday-Segar maintenance rate for the first 10 kg of body weight over 24 hours *(not taken from the course material)*, or equivalently a full day\'s worth of the deficit-plus-maintenance calculation the very next question in this sequence works through -- it is a real paediatric fluid number, but the wrong one for a single resuscitation bolus.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'The initial fluid bolus for a shocked child is 20 mL/kg of 0.9% saline -- 160 mL for an 8 kg infant -- not a maintenance or deficit-replacement volume.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.590'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-8', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'. Mohammed, aged 8 months, has presented with the clinical scenario described in the previous two questions. He has received the fluid bolus of normal saline 0.9%, which has improved his condition. From his presentation, you suspect he is 10% dehydrated. You receive his laboratory results, which reveal a plasma sodium of 138 mmol/L (within the normal range). His continuing fluid loss from vomiting is small and can be ignored. What is Mohammed' + String.fromCharCode(39) + 's total fluid requirement for the initial 24 hours?',
  options:['160 mL','320 mL','800 mL','880 mL','1600 mL'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Mohammed is 10% dehydrated, which equals an 800 mL deficit (10% of 8 kg). His maintenance requirement is 100 mL/kg/day = 800 mL. Therefore, his total fluid need over 24 hours is 800 mL (deficit) + 800 mL (maintenance) = 1600 mL.\n\n---\n\n`4) Shock in pediatrics_.txt` structures fluid management for a dehydrated or shocked child into four elements -- fluid resuscitation, fluid deficit replacement, maintenance fluid and ongoing losses -- and explicitly states that shock corresponds to a fluid deficit of 10% or more of body weight, with boluses given during resuscitation deducted from that deficit before the remainder is replaced. The stem has already given the resuscitation bolus in the previous question, so this question isolates the deficit-plus-maintenance arithmetic for the remaining 24 hours. Ten percent of Mohammed\'s 8 kg body weight is 0.8 kg, i.e. 800 mL of deficit fluid. Maintenance is calculated at 100 mL/kg/day for the first 10 kg of body weight (the Holliday-Segar formula) *(the specific 100 mL/kg/day figure is standard paediatric fluid-prescribing knowledge -- not spelled out as a numeric rate in the lecture cache, though it is printed unambiguously in this bank\'s explanation box)*, giving another 800 mL for an 8 kg infant. With ongoing losses from vomiting explicitly stated as negligible and plasma sodium normal (so no correction for hyper- or hyponatraemic dehydration is needed), the two components simply add: 800 + 800 = 1600 mL over 24 hours.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *160 mL* is the resuscitation bolus volume from the previous question, not the 24-hour total. *320 mL* does not correspond to either the deficit or the maintenance component alone, nor their sum. *800 mL* is only one of the two components -- either the deficit or the maintenance requirement on its own -- and omits the other. *880 mL* is close to but not equal to either 800 mL component and does not represent a correct combination of deficit and maintenance for this weight and dehydration percentage.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Total 24-hour fluid requirement = deficit (percentage dehydration x weight) + maintenance (100 mL/kg/day) -- 800 mL + 800 mL = 1600 mL for an 8 kg infant with 10% dehydration.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.592'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-9', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'A 6-year-old child is brought to the resuscitation room after a 15-minute generalized seizure. Buccal midazolam was given 5 minutes ago. Airway and oxygen are managed; capillary refill is <2 sec and heart rate is 120 bpm. What is the next most appropriate step?',
  options:['Administer another anticonvulsant','Check blood glucose','Gain IV access','Request senior review','Start bag-valve-mask ventilation'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Hypoglycemia must be ruled out in any child with ongoing seizure or reduced consciousness. It is a rapidly reversible cause of altered mental status.\n\n---\n\n`3) Pediatric resuscitation.txt` builds the approach to any seriously unwell child around ABCDE, with Disability -- level of consciousness, posture, pupils -- coming only after airway, breathing and circulation are addressed; this child\'s airway and oxygenation are already managed, and his capillary refill and heart rate show circulation is currently adequate, so the sequence has reached the point where the cause of ongoing impaired consciousness needs to be identified. `54) seizures and epilepsy (1).txt` defines status epilepticus as five or more minutes of continuous seizure activity, or recurrent seizures without regaining baseline consciousness, and names buccal midazolam as the standard rescue therapy given to children with prolonged seizures -- exactly what this child has already received. Because hypoglycaemia is a rapid, fully reversible cause of both seizures and reduced consciousness, and correcting it takes only a fingerprick test and, if needed, IV dextrose, checking blood glucose is the step that must not be skipped before escalating to further anticonvulsants or other interventions -- missing an easily correctable hypoglycaemic seizure while treating it as refractory epilepsy is the error this step exists to prevent.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Administering another anticonvulsant* jumps ahead of ruling out a reversible metabolic cause, and giving further sedating drugs on top of midazolam risks compounding respiratory depression if hypoglycaemia, not ongoing seizure activity, is actually driving the picture. *Gaining IV access* is useful and often done in parallel, but on its own it does not identify the cause of the seizure, and a glucose check can be done by fingerprick without waiting for a line. *Requesting senior review* is reasonable as the case escalates but does not substitute for the immediate, rapidly actionable step of checking glucose. *Starting bag-valve-mask ventilation* is not indicated here -- the stem states airway and oxygen are already managed, with no mention of inadequate breathing.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'In a child with a prolonged or ongoing seizure whose airway, breathing and circulation are already managed, blood glucose is checked next to catch a rapidly reversible hypoglycaemic cause before escalating anticonvulsant therapy.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.594'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-10', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'Seb, a 2-year-old boy, suddenly develops widespread urticaria and facial flushing at a party. Vitals are normal, and there is no respiratory compromise. What is the most appropriate medication?',
  options:['Intramuscular adrenaline','Intramuscular antihistamine','Intravenous hydrocortisone','Oral antihistamine','Oral corticosteroid'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> For urticaria without systemic involvement, oral antihistamines are sufficient. There is no need for adrenaline or steroids if no airway or circulatory compromise is present.\n\n---\n\n`27)Anaphylaxsis.txt` sets out the WAO clinical criteria for diagnosing anaphylaxis, and skin/mucous-membrane involvement on its own is not enough to meet them -- the criteria require skin involvement plus at least one of respiratory compromise, reduced blood pressure/end-organ dysfunction, or severe GI symptoms (or, with a known allergen, hypotension alone). Seb\'s presentation is exactly the skin-only picture: widespread urticaria and facial flushing, with normal vital signs and no respiratory compromise stated. This is isolated cutaneous allergy, not anaphylaxis, so it sits below the threshold the lecture sets for injectable adrenaline. `26)allergy_.txt` separately tabulates urticaria/angioedema as a rapid-onset, histamine-mediated reaction for which antihistamines help -- consistent with oral antihistamine being the appropriate treatment for skin-limited disease.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Intramuscular adrenaline* is the lecture\'s treatment for anaphylaxis itself -- reserved for reactions meeting the systemic criteria (respiratory, cardiovascular or severe GI involvement) that Seb does not have; giving it for isolated urticaria is treatment escalation the presentation does not warrant. *Intramuscular antihistamine* and *intravenous hydrocortisone* are both more invasive routes than needed when the oral route is adequate and there is no vomiting or shock preventing oral intake. *Oral corticosteroid* has a role as an additional treatment for anaphylaxis in the lecture\'s hospital-management list, alongside antihistamines and IV fluids, but it is not required for skin-only urticaria and does not act as quickly as an antihistamine for the itching and flushing themselves.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Widespread urticaria and flushing without respiratory, cardiovascular or severe GI involvement do not meet the criteria for anaphylaxis, so oral antihistamine -- not adrenaline or steroids -- is the appropriate treatment.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.596'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-11', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'Jenny, a 3-year-old girl, suddenly develops facial swelling, urticaria, and noisy breathing at a village fete. She is distressed and has widespread wheeze. What is the first-line medication?',
  options:['Intramuscular adrenaline','Oral corticosteroid','Intravenous hydrocortisone','Oral antihistamine','Intramuscular antihistamine'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Facial swelling, urticaria, respiratory compromise (wheeze) suggest anaphylaxis. Adrenaline IM is the first-line treatment.\n\n---\n\nUnlike `pedep-emg-10`, Jenny meets the WAO criteria for anaphylaxis that `27)Anaphylaxsis.txt` sets out: she has skin/mucous-membrane involvement (facial swelling, urticaria) plus respiratory compromise (noisy breathing, widespread wheeze), which is one of the "+1" respiratory criteria the lecture lists alongside hypotension/end-organ dysfunction and severe GI symptoms. The lecture\'s hospital-management sequence for anaphylaxis is explicit: rapidly assess airway, breathing, circulation, skin and weight; start CPR if indicated; lie the patient supine with legs elevated; and, as "the main therapy," inject adrenaline intramuscularly in the mid-outer thigh -- with oxygen, inhaled salbutamol, IV fluids, antihistamines and steroids listed only as additional treatments alongside it. Because anaphylaxis can progress rapidly to airway obstruction and cardiovascular collapse, IM adrenaline is given immediately once the diagnosis is met, not after other measures have been tried and failed.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Oral corticosteroid* and *intravenous hydrocortisone* both sit under the lecture\'s "additional treatments," given alongside or after adrenaline, not instead of it -- steroids act too slowly to reverse the acute bronchospasm and mucosal swelling threatening Jenny\'s airway. *Oral antihistamine* is the correct first-line choice for isolated urticaria without systemic features (as in `pedep-emg-10`), but Jenny has moved past that threshold into respiratory compromise, where antihistamines are too slow and too weak to be first-line. *Intramuscular antihistamine* is a faster route than oral but is still not the lecture\'s "main therapy" for anaphylaxis -- that designation belongs to adrenaline alone.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Facial swelling, urticaria and wheeze together meet the criteria for anaphylaxis, and IM adrenaline -- not antihistamines or steroids -- is the first-line, immediate treatment.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.598'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-12', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'The incidence of sudden infant death syndrome has decreased dramatically in the UK. What is the single most important factor responsible for this decline?',
  options:['Feet to foot of cot','Keeping baby in parent' + String.fromCharCode(39) + 's room for 6 months','Keeping room cool','Parents not smoking near the baby','Supine sleeping'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> The “Back to Sleep” campaign greatly reduced SIDS by recommending babies be placed on their backs to sleep. Supine sleeping is the most impactful intervention.\n\n---\n\n*Sudden infant death syndrome and its 1990s "Back to Sleep" campaign are not covered in the cached peds lecture material -- searched under "SIDS", "sudden infant death", "supine", "prone sleeping", "cot death" and "back to sleep" with no relevant hits, so this entry is answered from outside knowledge and tagged throughout (not taken from the course material).* Placing infants supine rather than prone for sleep is credited with the single largest reduction in SIDS incidence of any public-health intervention -- the UK "Back to Sleep" campaign, launched in 1991, was followed by a fall of roughly 70% in SIDS deaths over the following years, far outweighing the contribution of any other single risk-factor change *(not taken from the course material)*. The mechanism proposed is that prone sleeping increases the risk of rebreathing exhaled carbon dioxide, airway obstruction, and impaired arousal from sleep, all of which are reduced when an infant sleeps on the back *(not taken from the course material)*.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Feet-to-foot positioning of the cot* -- placing the baby with feet at the foot of the cot to prevent them from wriggling under the covers -- is a genuine, recommended safe-sleep measure, but it addresses a smaller, more specific risk (overheating and covering of the head) than the sleep-position change and is not credited with the scale of the population-level decline. *Keeping the baby in the parent' + String.fromCharCode(39) + 's room for the first 6 months* is another recommended measure, associated with a reduced SIDS risk, but again its individual contribution is smaller than that of the sleep-position change. *Keeping the room cool* addresses overheating, a recognised but secondary risk factor, not the dominant one. *Parents not smoking near the baby* reduces a genuine and important risk factor for SIDS, but smoking cessation campaigns did not produce the same magnitude or timing of decline as the supine-sleeping recommendation did when it was introduced (all not taken from the course material).\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Supine sleep positioning, from the "Back to Sleep" campaign, is credited as the single most important factor in the decline of SIDS incidence -- ahead of feet-to-foot positioning, room-sharing, room temperature or smoking avoidance individually.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.600'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-13', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'Nathaniel, a 4-year-old boy, presents with shortness of breath and oxygen saturation of 90%. He can speak and has a capillary refill time <2 seconds. What is the most appropriate next step?',
  options:['Check blood glucose','Check pupils','Airway opening manoeuvres','High-flow oxygen therapy','Start chest compressions using hands-encircling method'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> In a child with hypoxia and respiratory distress but stable circulation, high-flow oxygen is the next step. It improves oxygenation while the cause is investigated.\n\n---\n\nThis question is the first of five (`pedep-emg-13` here, plus `pedep-emg-14`, `pedep-emg-15`, `pedep-emg-16` and `pedep-emg-17`) that all print the identical five-option list -- Check blood glucose / Check pupils / Airway opening manoeuvres / High-flow oxygen therapy / Start chest compressions using hands-encircling method -- against five different vignettes. `3) Pediatric resuscitation.txt` structures the assessment of any seriously unwell child as Airway, then Breathing, then Circulation, then Disability, each addressed only once the step before it is confirmed adequate, so the same five options map onto five different points in that sequence depending on which letter each child\'s problem sits at:\n\n| Child | Key clinical detail | ABCDE problem | Correct option |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Nathaniel (here) | Can speak (patent airway), sat 90%, CRT <2s (circulation intact) | Breathing/oxygenation | High-flow oxygen therapy |\n| Kelsey (`pedep-emg-14`) | Found unconscious, gasping and moaning | Airway | Airway opening manoeuvres |\n| Ahmed (`pedep-emg-15`) | HR 40 despite effective bag-mask ventilation | Circulation | Chest compressions, hands-encircling |\n| Daniel (`pedep-emg-16`) | Known diabetic, collapsed after exertion | Reversible metabolic cause | Check blood glucose |\n| Aisha (`pedep-emg-17`) | Post-seizure, unresponsive to pain, glucose already being checked | Disability/neuro | Check pupils |\n\nNathaniel\'s airway is patent -- he can speak, which requires air to move past the vocal cords -- so airway opening manoeuvres are not the priority. His circulation is intact, with a capillary refill under 2 seconds, so chest compressions and glucose/pupil checks (which come later, under Disability) are premature. What remains unaddressed is Breathing: an oxygen saturation of 90% with shortness of breath is hypoxia, and the lecture\'s ABCDE approach treats the problem found at the current letter before moving on, so high-flow oxygen therapy is given next while the underlying cause of his breathlessness is investigated.\n\n**Why the other four options are wrong for Nathaniel specifically.** *Checking blood glucose* and *checking pupils* both belong to the Disability step, which is only reached once Breathing (his actual problem) has been addressed. *Airway opening manoeuvres* are unnecessary since he is speaking. *Chest compressions* would only be indicated if his circulation were failing, which the normal capillary refill excludes.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'On the ABCDE sequence, a breathless, hypoxic child with a patent airway and intact circulation is given high-flow oxygen next -- the Breathing step is treated before Disability (glucose, pupils) or Circulation (compressions) are reached.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.602'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-14', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'Kelsey, a 2-year-old girl, is found unconscious in the garden. In the resuscitation room, she is gasping and moaning. What is the next most appropriate step?',
  options:['Check blood glucose','Check pupils','Airway opening manoeuvres','High-flow oxygen therapy','Start chest compressions using hands-encircling method'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> An unconscious child requires immediate airway management. Gasping suggests inadequate breathing; airway positioning should be done first.\n\n---\n\nThis is one of the five questions sharing the identical option ladder anchored and tabulated at `pedep-emg-13` -- see that entry for the full comparison across all five vignettes. Kelsey\'s discriminating detail is that she is found unconscious and gasping and moaning rather than speaking or breathing normally: gasping/agonal-type breathing sounds are a sign of an inadequately protected or partially obstructed airway in an unconscious child, and `3) Pediatric resuscitation.txt` places airway assessment and opening manoeuvres (head positioning, jaw thrust) as the very first step of ABCDE, before breathing, circulation or disability are even assessed, because nothing downstream can be reliably evaluated or treated while the airway itself may be compromised. That places Kelsey\'s problem at the "A" letter, distinct from Nathaniel\'s Breathing problem, Ahmed\'s Circulation problem, or Daniel\'s and Aisha\'s Disability-level problems in the sibling questions.\n\n**Why the other four options are wrong for Kelsey specifically.** *Checking blood glucose* and *checking pupils* both sit under Disability, which is not reached until airway, breathing and circulation have each been secured -- Kelsey\'s airway has not yet been addressed. *High-flow oxygen therapy* is a Breathing-step intervention that will not help if the airway itself is not open enough to deliver that oxygen. *Chest compressions* would be considered if she were found to be pulseless once assessed, but airway opening comes first in the sequence and nothing in the stem yet establishes that circulation, rather than airway, is the acute problem.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'An unconscious, gasping child has an airway problem, and airway-opening manoeuvres are the first ABCDE step -- ahead of breathing, circulation or disability interventions.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.604'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-15', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'Ahmed, a 2-month-old baby, is pale and floppy in his cot. Paramedics are providing bag-valve-mask ventilation; chest is moving, but the heart rate is 40 bpm. What is the next step?',
  options:['Check blood glucose','Check pupils','Airway opening manoeuvres','High-flow oxygen therapy','Start chest compressions using hands-encircling method'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> A heart rate below 60 bpm with signs of poor perfusion in an infant indicates the need for chest compressions. The encircling hands technique is preferred in infants.\n\n---\n\nThis is one of the five questions sharing the identical option ladder anchored and tabulated at `pedep-emg-13` -- see that entry for the full comparison across all five vignettes. Ahmed\'s airway and breathing are already being managed effectively: the paramedics are ventilating him and his chest is confirmed to be moving, which rules out an airway or breathing problem as the next priority. What remains is Circulation, and a heart rate of 40 bpm despite effective ventilation is exactly the threshold `3) Pediatric resuscitation.txt` uses to define cardiopulmonary arrest requiring chest compressions -- a bradycardic infant heart rate this low cannot sustain adequate cardiac output. The lecture also specifies technique by age: for a two-rescuer scenario, one rescuer maintains the airway seal while the second compresses, and for infants the two-thumb, hands-encircling technique around the chest is preferred over the single/two-finger technique because it generates a more effective cardiac and thoracic pump mechanism for blood flow during compressions.\n\n**Why the other four options are wrong for Ahmed specifically.** *Checking blood glucose* and *checking pupils* are Disability-level assessments that come after Circulation has been addressed, and Ahmed\'s heart rate of 40 needs immediate action, not a diagnostic detour. *Airway opening manoeuvres* are unnecessary since ventilation is already effective with visible chest movement. *High-flow oxygen therapy* does not address the core problem here -- his oxygenation is already being supported by bag-valve-mask ventilation; what is failing is the heart rate itself.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'A heart rate under 60 bpm in an infant despite effective bag-mask ventilation is an indication for chest compressions, using the two-thumb hands-encircling technique.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.606'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-16', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'Daniel, aged 10 years, has diabetes and collapsed after playing football. What is the most appropriate next step?',
  options:['Check blood glucose','Check pupils','Airway opening manoeuvres','High-flow oxygen therapy','Start chest compressions using hands-encircling method'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> A diabetic child who collapses may be hypoglycemic. Blood glucose must be checked immediately as hypoglycemia is rapidly correctable.\n\n---\n\nThis is one of the five questions sharing the identical option ladder anchored and tabulated at `pedep-emg-13` -- see that entry for the full comparison across all five vignettes. Nothing in Daniel\'s stem describes an airway, breathing or circulation problem -- he is not said to be gasping, hypoxic, bradycardic or pulseless, only that he has collapsed. Combined with his known diabetes and the exertion of playing football (which increases insulin sensitivity and glucose consumption, raising hypoglycaemia risk), the clinical picture points straight to checking blood glucose, the same rapidly reversible-cause logic `pedep-emg-9` applies to a seizing child -- hypoglycaemia can cause collapse and reduced consciousness and is fully and quickly correctable once identified, so it is checked immediately rather than assumed away.\n\n**Why the other four options are wrong for Daniel specifically.** *Checking pupils*, *airway opening manoeuvres* and *chest compressions* would each require evidence that his airway, breathing or circulation is currently failing, none of which the stem provides. *High-flow oxygen therapy* likewise treats a breathing problem that has not been established -- Daniel\'s presentation is a diabetic collapse, and the single most useful, fastest test is a glucose check, not an empirical respiratory or circulatory intervention.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'A known diabetic child who collapses, with no described airway/breathing/circulation problem, has blood glucose checked immediately to catch reversible hypoglycaemia.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.608'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-17', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'Aisha, a 3-year-old girl, had a seizure. She is on high-flow oxygen and unresponsive to pain. Blood glucose is being checked. What is the next most appropriate step?',
  options:['Check blood glucose','Check pupils','Airway opening manoeuvres','High-flow oxygen therapy','Start chest compressions using hands-encircling method'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> After ABC and glucose check, assessing pupils helps identify potential neurological causes or drug toxicity following a seizure.\n\n---\n\nThis is the last of the five questions sharing the identical option ladder anchored and tabulated at `pedep-emg-13` -- see that entry for the full comparison across all five vignettes. Aisha\'s stem has already closed off the earlier steps in the sequence for the reader: she is already receiving high-flow oxygen (Breathing addressed), nothing suggests an airway obstruction, and blood glucose -- the reversible metabolic cause `pedep-emg-9` and `pedep-emg-16` also test -- is already being checked. What is left unassessed is the rest of Disability: `3) Pediatric resuscitation.txt` lists level of consciousness, posture and pupil size/reactivity together under this step, and Aisha remains unresponsive to pain despite the airway, breathing and glucose elements already in hand. Checking her pupils is the next Disability element to work through, since pupillary findings (as in `pedep-emg-3`\'s pinpoint-pupil opioid scenario) can point toward a structural neurological cause or a toxic/drug ingestion behind her ongoing unresponsiveness after the seizure.\n\n**Why the other four options are wrong for Aisha specifically.** *Checking blood glucose* is already stated to be under way, so repeating it is not the next step. *Airway opening manoeuvres* and *high-flow oxygen therapy* are already accounted for -- she is already on oxygen and nothing suggests airway compromise. *Chest compressions* are not indicated; there is no mention of pulselessness or bradycardia.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Once airway, breathing and a glucose check are already in hand for a post-seizure child who remains unresponsive, the next Disability-step assessment is the pupils, to look for a neurological or toxic cause.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.610'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-18', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'A 9-year-old boy presents with reduced consciousness, fever, and petechiae. He is cardiovascularly stable, with GCS 14/15. What is the most appropriate immediate management?',
  options:['Arrange urgent CT brain','Give 20 mL/kg normal saline bolus','Give 3 mL/kg 10% dextrose','IV access, blood cultures, and broad-spectrum antibiotics','Administer vitamin K'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Fever with petechiae and reduced consciousness suggests meningococcemia. Early antibiotics are critical; blood cultures must be taken before administration.\n\n---\n\n`18) febrile child and life threatening infections.txt` lists fever with petechiae/purpura and any change in consciousness among its red flags for a seriously unwell febrile child, the same combination `pedep-emg-2` works through for a younger infant. The lecture is explicit about management once meningococcaemia is suspected: no delay in the administration of antibiotics and supportive therapy, with a third-generation cephalosporin such as ceftriaxone as the preferred choice, and blood cultures obtained as part of that same urgent work-up. This boy is cardiovascularly stable with a near-normal GCS of 14/15, so the priority is not resuscitation of an already-compromised circulation but rapidly securing IV access, sending cultures, and starting broad-spectrum antibiotics before the sepsis has time to progress -- exactly the box\'s point that "blood cultures must be taken before administration" of antibiotics, so the diagnostic sample is not lost to the treatment that must not be delayed.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Urgent CT brain* would only be indicated if there were signs of raised intracranial pressure or a focal neurological deficit, neither of which this stem describes, and imaging must never delay antibiotics in suspected meningococcal sepsis. *20 mL/kg normal saline bolus* is the correct action for a shocked child, but this boy is explicitly cardiovascularly stable, so fluid resuscitation is not the immediate priority. *3 mL/kg 10% dextrose* is the standard bolus for symptomatic hypoglycaemia, which nothing in the stem suggests he has. *Vitamin K* treats or prevents bleeding from vitamin K deficiency, not a purpuric rash from suspected sepsis, and has no role in this presentation.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'A febrile child with petechiae and reduced consciousness who is haemodynamically stable is managed with immediate IV access, blood cultures and broad-spectrum antibiotics for suspected meningococcaemia -- not imaging, a fluid bolus, dextrose or vitamin K.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.612'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-19', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'A 3-year-old girl presents with 8 days of vomiting and diarrhea, now with reduced consciousness. Vitals: HR 170, RR 20, SBP 75 mmHg, CRT 5 sec. pH 6.9, base excess -18, glucose 2.2 mmol/L. What is the most likely clinical syndrome?',
  options:['Anaphylactic shock','Septicaemic shock','Cardiogenic shock','Hypovolaemic shock','Neurogenic shock'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> The history of diarrhea and vomiting, hypotension, tachycardia, prolonged capillary refill, and acidosis indicate severe hypovolemic shock due to fluid loss.\n\n---\n\n`4) Shock in pediatrics_.txt` lists gastroenteritis as one of the recognised causes of dehydration and shock, and describes decompensated (late) shock as including acidotic (Kussmaul) breathing and hypotension, in contrast to the tachycardia and delayed capillary refill of early, compensated shock. This girl has all of the late, decompensated features together: a very low systolic blood pressure of 75 mmHg, a markedly prolonged capillary refill of 5 seconds, a profound metabolic acidosis (pH 6.9, base excess -18), and a low glucose from prolonged poor intake -- all following 8 days of ongoing fluid loss from vomiting and diarrhoea. That history of sustained gastrointestinal losses, with no fever, rash or other infective source described, is the direct cause the box identifies: severe hypovolaemic shock, decompensated because the compensatory tachycardia and vasoconstriction have already been overwhelmed.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Anaphylactic shock* is a distributive shock triggered by an allergen exposure, with urticaria, angioedema or wheeze typically described -- none of which appears here, and there is no exposure history. *Septicaemic shock* is also distributive and usually accompanied by fever or a rash such as the non-blanching petechiae seen in `pedep-emg-2` and `pedep-emg-18` -- this stem gives a clear volume-loss history instead, with no infective red flags. *Cardiogenic shock* results from pump failure (arrhythmia, myocarditis, heart failure) rather than fluid loss, and nothing here suggests a primary cardiac problem. *Neurogenic shock* follows spinal cord injury with loss of vascular tone, which this history of prolonged vomiting and diarrhoea does not support.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Prolonged vomiting and diarrhoea with hypotension, tachycardia, severely prolonged capillary refill and profound metabolic acidosis is decompensated hypovolaemic shock, not anaphylactic, septicaemic, cardiogenic or neurogenic shock.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.614'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-20', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'A known epileptic patient in status epilepticus received rectal diazepam 10 minutes ago but continues to seize. IV access is now available and blood sugar is 5.5 mmol/L. What is the next most appropriate treatment?',
  options:['Intravenous lorazepam','Intravenous phenytoin','Intravenous thiopental','Rectal paraldehyde','Buccal midazolam'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Lorazepam is the next line of treatment in status epilepticus if benzodiazepines (like rectal diazepam) have failed and IV access is available. It is effective and has a longer duration of action than diazepam.\n\n---\n\n`54) seizures and epilepsy (1).txt` defines status epilepticus as five or more minutes of continuous seizure activity, or recurrent seizures without regaining baseline consciousness, and names buccal midazolam as the standard rescue therapy for a prolonged seizure -- but this patient has already had a first-line benzodiazepine (rectal diazepam) 10 minutes ago and has continued to seize throughout, so the reversible cause has already been checked (glucose 5.5 mmol/L is normal) and the question moves to the next step of the treatment ladder *(the specific stepwise algorithm after the first benzodiazepine dose -- second benzodiazepine if IV access is available, then a loading anticonvulsant, then rapid sequence induction -- is standard status epilepticus management and is not spelled out in the lecture cache beyond naming buccal midazolam as rescue therapy; not taken from the course material)*. With IV access now available, a second benzodiazepine given intravenously is the appropriate next step, and lorazepam is preferred over further rectal diazepam because it is at least as effective and has a longer duration of anticonvulsant action once given intravenously.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *IV phenytoin* and *IV thiopental* both belong to later rungs of the status epilepticus ladder -- a loading anticonvulsant and then anaesthesia/rapid sequence induction respectively -- used once benzodiazepines have failed a second time, not substituted for the second benzodiazepine dose itself. *Rectal paraldehyde* is an older rescue option for when IV access is unavailable; here IV access is explicitly available, making the intravenous route preferable. *Buccal midazolam* is appropriate as an initial rescue dose or where IV access cannot be obtained, but a route already available intravenously is used in preference to a second mucosal dose.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'When a first benzodiazepine fails to stop status epilepticus and IV access becomes available, IV lorazepam is the next step -- ahead of phenytoin, thiopental, rectal paraldehyde or a second mucosal dose.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.616'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-21', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'A previously well 5-year-old boy is brought in by ambulance to the emergency department with an acute onset of breathing difficulty. On arrival, he appears unwell, pale with audible stridor, and is sitting upright unable to speak. His temperature is 40°C. What is the first priority in this child' + String.fromCharCode(39) + 's management?',
  options:['Lie the child down','Take a throat swab','Obtain intravenous access','Give oral dexamethasone','Summon immediate anesthetic help'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> This presentation suggests epiglottitis, a life-threatening airway emergency. The first priority is to call for senior anesthetic help urgently to secure the airway in a controlled environment.\n\n---\n\n`specific bacterial infection.txt` describes epiglottitis as a Hib-associated emergency with rapid onset of high fever, severe sore throat and stridor, and specifically notes children adopt a tripod position -- leaning forward with the mouth open and jaw thrust out -- to keep the airway open; this boy is sitting upright, pale, stridulous, unable to speak and febrile at 40°C, matching that picture closely. The same source is explicit about the priority: "in cases of epiglottitis, the priority is securing the airway (often requiring intubation) before any other procedures," and lists difficulty breathing or swallowing as a sign requiring immediate emergency intervention. Because the swollen epiglottis can obstruct completely with very little provocation, any examination of the throat, repositioning, or upsetting the child risks precipitating total obstruction, so the correct first action is to summon senior anaesthetic help so the airway can be secured under controlled conditions, ideally in theatre, rather than attempting anything at the bedside first.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Lying the child down* removes the upright posture he has adopted specifically to keep his airway as open as possible, and forcing a change in position can precipitate complete obstruction. *Taking a throat swab* means examining or instrumenting the airway directly, which is the single most dangerous thing to do in suspected epiglottitis and can trigger fatal laryngospasm or obstruction. *Obtaining intravenous access* can distress the child, and distress itself can worsen airway obstruction, so it is deferred until the airway is secured in a controlled setting. *Oral dexamethasone* has a role in viral croup, a different and generally less acute stridor-causing illness, but treats inflammation rather than the imminent obstructive risk of epiglottitis and does nothing to secure the airway.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'A child with acute stridor, high fever, drooling/inability to speak and a tripod posture has suspected epiglottitis, managed by summoning senior anaesthetic help to secure the airway in a controlled setting -- never by examining the throat or upsetting the child.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.618'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-22', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'A 9-year-old boy in asystole is brought in by ambulance to the emergency department. The various members of the cardiac arrest team arrive and resuscitation begins. Which is the most important drug of choice?',
  options:['Adrenaline (epinephrine)','Sodium bicarbonate','Atropine','Amiodarone','Lidocaine'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> In pediatric cardiac arrest with asystole, the most important and first-line drug is adrenaline. It is administered every 3–5 minutes during CPR to improve chances of return of spontaneous circulation.\n\n---\n\n`3) Pediatric resuscitation.txt` teaches chest compressions and ventilation as the mechanical basis of cardiopulmonary resuscitation -- push hard, push fast, release completely, do not interrupt compressions unnecessarily -- and lists the reversible causes to hunt for during arrest as the 4 Hs and 4 Ts (hypoxia, hypovolaemia, hypo/hyperkalaemia and metabolic causes, hypothermia; tension pneumothorax, thrombosis, tamponade, toxins). Asystole is a non-shockable rhythm, so defibrillation plays no role, and the pharmacological mainstay throughout is adrenaline, given at the standard 3-5 minute interval the box describes, to increase coronary and cerebral perfusion pressure during compressions and improve the chance of return of spontaneous circulation while the reversible causes are sought and treated.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Sodium bicarbonate* is not a routine first-line arrest drug -- it is reserved for specific situations such as severe, refractory acidosis or hyperkalaemia, and indiscriminate use can worsen intracellular acidosis and shift the oxygen dissociation curve unfavourably, the same caution the DKA-cerebral-oedema literature applies to bicarbonate in a different context *(the general caution against routine bicarbonate in arrest is standard resuscitation teaching beyond what this slide set spells out -- not taken from the course material)*. *Atropine* was historically used for bradycardic arrest but is no longer part of standard asystole/PEA management in current paediatric protocols. *Amiodarone* and *Lidocaine* are antiarrhythmics reserved for shockable rhythms (ventricular fibrillation or pulseless ventricular tachycardia) that persist after defibrillation -- asystole is not shockable, so neither drug has a role here.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Adrenaline every 3-5 minutes is the mainstay drug in paediatric asystole -- bicarbonate, atropine and the antiarrhythmics amiodarone/lidocaine are not routine first-line agents for a non-shockable arrest rhythm.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.620'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-23', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'A 3-year-old girl is brought into the emergency department by ambulance with a 4-hour history of listlessness, high fever, and reduced responsiveness. Her temperature is 40.0°C, she has a heart rate of 170 beats per minute, a respiratory rate of 35 per minute, and saturations of 100% on 15 liters of high-flow oxygen. On examination, she is responsive to voice and maintaining her airway. Systemic examination is unremarkable; her capillary refill time is 4 seconds. What is the first priority in the management of this child?',
  options:['Intubation and ventilation','10 mL/kg bolus of normal saline','20 mL/kg bolus of normal saline','15 mL/kg packed red cells'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> This child shows signs of shock. The first priority is fluid resuscitation with a 20 mL/kg bolus of isotonic saline to restore perfusion and improve circulation.\n\n---\n\nApplying the same ABCDE framework `pedep-emg-13` through `pedep-emg-17` walk through: her airway is maintained and she is responsive to voice, and her oxygen saturation is already 100% on high-flow oxygen, so airway and breathing are being adequately supported. What remains abnormal is Circulation -- a heart rate of 170 bpm and a capillary refill time of 4 seconds (beyond the normal 2-second cut-off `4) Shock in pediatrics_.txt` uses to define delayed capillary refill) together mark early, compensated shock, most likely from the sepsis her fever and reduced responsiveness point toward. The same lecture sets the standard resuscitation bolus at 20 mL/kg of isotonic saline, matching the box and the volume `pedep-emg-7` calculates for Mohammed -- the priority is restoring circulating volume before it progresses to the decompensated, hypotensive stage.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Intubation and ventilation* is not indicated -- her airway is maintained, she is responsive to voice, and her saturations are already 100%, so there is no airway or breathing failure to intervene on. *10 mL/kg bolus of normal saline* under-doses the resuscitation bolus; the standard rate the lecture and box both give is 20 mL/kg, not half that. *15 mL/kg packed red cells* would be appropriate for significant blood loss or severe anaemia causing shock, not for a septic-picture shock with no bleeding history -- crystalloid, not blood product, is the first-line resuscitation fluid here.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'A febrile, tachycardic child with a prolonged capillary refill but a maintained airway and normal saturations is in early (compensated) shock, treated first with a 20 mL/kg isotonic saline bolus -- not intubation, an under-dosed bolus, or blood products.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.622'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-24', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'An 8-year-old girl is brought to the hospital following ingestion of a cereal bar containing peanuts. She is noted to have a widespread urticarial rash and swelling of the face and lips. She is finding it difficult to speak and there is widespread wheeze on auscultation. The single most important step in her management is which of the following?',
  options:['Intramuscular 1 microgram/kg adrenaline (epinephrine) (1:1000)','Intravenous adrenaline (epinephrine), 1 microgram/kg (1:10,000)','Intravenous hydrocortisone','Oxygen','Chlorphenamine'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> This is an acute anaphylactic reaction. Intramuscular adrenaline is the first and most important step to counteract life-threatening airway swelling and bronchospasm.\n\n---\n\nThis girl meets the WAO clinical criteria for anaphylaxis that `27)Anaphylaxsis.txt` sets out -- skin/mucosal involvement (urticaria, facial and lip swelling) plus respiratory compromise (difficulty speaking, widespread wheeze) after a known food-allergen exposure (peanut). The lecture states the main therapy for anaphylaxis is to "inject epinephrine (adrenaline) intramuscularly in the mid-outer thigh," so the *route* this question is really testing -- intramuscular, ahead of the intravenous, hydrocortisone, oxygen and antihistamine alternatives -- is correctly identified by option A, and `answer` is left exactly where the bank prints it, on that option.\n\n**The printed dose in the key option needs a flag.** Option A gives the IM adrenaline dose as "1 microgram/kg (1:1000)". **The usual IM adrenaline dose used in paediatric anaphylaxis is about 10 micrograms/kg of the 1:1000 solution** (or the equivalent fixed, age-banded doses used with an auto-injector) *(not taken from the course material -- `27)Anaphylaxsis.txt` names the IM route and the mid-outer-thigh site but prints no microgram/kg or milligram/kg figure anywhere, and no other cached peds lecture gives one either)*. The book\'s printed "1 microgram/kg" is therefore roughly ten-fold below the usual figure -- read carefully off the page image, the digit is an unambiguous "1", not "10", so it is transcribed exactly as printed rather than silently corrected. Per the standing rule for a defective source, the error is noted here, not disputed, and the answer is not moved: the question is testing the route (IM, not IV) and the drug (adrenaline), and on that test option A is still the single best answer among the five. Option B\'s dose, "1 microgram/kg (1:10,000)" for the IV route, is transcribed the same way and carries the same open flag, but since B is not the key, the discrepancy there is noted rather than adjudicated.\n\n**Why each remaining distractor fails.** *Intravenous hydrocortisone* is listed among the lecture\'s additional treatments for anaphylaxis, given alongside or after adrenaline, not instead of it, and steroids act too slowly to reverse acute bronchospasm and airway swelling. *Oxygen* is another additional treatment the lecture names, valuable but not the single most important step when airway and circulatory compromise from anaphylaxis itself needs immediate reversal. *Chlorphenamine*, an antihistamine, similarly sits among the supportive additional treatments -- it does not act fast enough or powerfully enough to be first-line for a reaction that has already progressed to respiratory compromise.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Anaphylaxis with respiratory compromise after a known food-allergen exposure is treated first with IM adrenaline; the book\'s printed dose of "1 microgram/kg" is roughly a tenth of the usual ~10 microgram/kg IM figure and is reproduced as printed, but the question is testing the route, so the key is left unmoved.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.624'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-25', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'A 12-year-old girl with known epilepsy is brought in by ambulance. She has been intubated for 15 minutes. Her parents administered buccal midazolam after 5 minutes as part of her rescue regimen. On arrival, tonic-clonic movements are ongoing. The ambulance crew has inserted a cannula and is giving high-flow oxygen via facemask. What is the next step in management?',
  options:['Intravenous lorazepam','Rectal diazepam','Rapid sequence induction','Loading dose of phenytoin','Intravenous phenobarbital'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> In ongoing status epilepticus, a second dose of benzodiazepine is indicated 10 minutes after the first. Since IV access is available, intravenous lorazepam is preferred over rectal diazepam due to faster and more reliable effect.\n\n---\n\nAs with `pedep-emg-20`, this girl has already had one benzodiazepine (buccal midazolam) and continues to seize with a duration well past the 5-minute mark `54) seizures and epilepsy (1).txt` uses to define status epilepticus, so the treatment ladder moves to its next rung. IV access has now been secured by the ambulance crew, so the second benzodiazepine dose is given intravenously rather than by a second mucosal or rectal route -- IV lorazepam, again preferred over rectal diazepam for its more reliable and longer-lasting effect once IV access exists, matching the box\'s reasoning and the same step `pedep-emg-20` works through with a different first-line agent.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Rectal diazepam* is a reasonable rescue route when IV access is unavailable, but here a cannula has already been placed, making the intravenous route the preferred choice for both speed and reliability. *Rapid sequence induction* and *IV phenobarbital* both belong to later rungs of the ladder -- used once benzodiazepines have failed on a second attempt, moving toward anaesthesia or a barbiturate for truly refractory status -- not substituted in before a second benzodiazepine has even been tried intravenously. *Loading dose of phenytoin* is the next anticonvulsant step after benzodiazepines have failed twice, again premature here since only one benzodiazepine dose (the buccal midazolam) has actually been given so far.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'With one benzodiazepine already given and IV access now available, a second benzodiazepine -- IV lorazepam in preference to a further rectal or buccal dose -- is the next step in ongoing status epilepticus, before phenytoin, phenobarbital or rapid sequence induction.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.626'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-26', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'A 3-month-old baby is found dead in a Moses basket sleeping on her back. She was born in July at 41 weeks to a mother who smoked during the pregnancy and afterward. Which of the following factors is the most likely to have influenced her sudden infant death?',
  options:['Maternal smoking','Sleeping in a ' + String.fromCharCode(39) + 'Moses basket' + String.fromCharCode(39), 'Supine sleeping position','Born in summer months','Post-term delivery'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Maternal smoking during and after pregnancy is the strongest modifiable risk factor associated with sudden infant death syndrome (SIDS). It significantly increases the risk due to its effect on infant arousal and respiratory control.\n\n---\n\n*As with `pedep-emg-12`, SIDS risk factors are not covered in the cached peds lecture material -- this entry is answered from outside knowledge and tagged throughout.* This stem is constructed so that the baby already has the protective factor the previous SIDS question (`pedep-emg-12`) identifies as most important -- she was sleeping supine -- yet still died suddenly, so the question is really asking which of the remaining, non-protective factors listed is the recognised risk factor. Maternal smoking during and after pregnancy is well established as the single strongest modifiable SIDS risk factor, thought to act by impairing infant arousal responses and respiratory control during sleep *(not taken from the course material)*. Because this baby\'s mother smoked both during the pregnancy and afterward, that exposure is present at every stage the mechanism is thought to act through.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Sleeping in a Moses basket* is a normal, unremarkable sleep surface and is not itself a recognised SIDS risk factor -- what matters is the sleeping position and the bedding used, not the specific type of bassinet *(not taken from the course material)*. *Supine sleeping position* is the protective factor, not a risk factor -- this baby was already sleeping on her back, which should have reduced her risk, making it the wrong answer to a question asking what "influenced" the death. *Born in summer months* is not an established SIDS risk factor; if anything, some data associates SIDS more with cooler months, and this stem gives no mechanism linking a July birth to risk *(not taken from the course material)*. *Post-term delivery* (41 weeks is at the upper end of term but not markedly post-term) is not a major recognised SIDS risk factor in the way prematurity is *(not taken from the course material)*.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Maternal smoking during and after pregnancy is the strongest modifiable SIDS risk factor, distinguished here from the protective supine-sleeping position and from non-risk features like bassinet type, season of birth or a near-term gestational age.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.628'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-28', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'A 3-year-old boy comes to the ER with dehydration and rapid breathing, following a short history of abdominal pain and vomiting. Blood glucose is 850 mg/dl and ketones ++ in urine. What is the most appropriate initial management?',
  options:['Start IV insulin infusion','SC rapid-acting insulin','IV bolus of normal saline','NaHCO3','Oxygen therapy'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> The first step in diabetic ketoacidosis is fluid resuscitation with 0.9% normal saline to restore circulation, before starting insulin.\n\n---\n\nThis boy\'s abdominal pain, vomiting, rapid (acidotic) breathing, a blood glucose of 850 mg/dl and ++ ketonuria together fit diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA). `51) DKA.txt` sets out fluid therapy as serving three goals in this order: restoring circulating volume first, then replacing the extracellular and intracellular fluid deficit, and only as a result of that improved perfusion does glomerular filtration recover enough to clear glucose and ketones from the blood. The same lecture lists starting insulin within the first hour of fluid treatment as a named risk factor for cerebral edema, the most serious complication of DKA, which can develop without warning symptoms -- so insulin is deliberately held back until fluid resuscitation with an isotonic crystalloid such as 0.9% normal saline has already begun. An IV bolus of normal saline is exactly that first step.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Starting an IV insulin infusion* and *subcutaneous rapid-acting insulin* both give insulin before fluids, which is the sequencing the lecture specifically flags as raising cerebral oedema risk -- and subcutaneous dosing is unreliable in a dehydrated, poorly perfused child regardless of timing. *NaHCO3* is bicarbonate, which the lecture states is generally contraindicated in DKA: it can cause a paradoxical fall in CNS pH and oxygenation, drives potassium into cells with resulting hypokalaemia, and shifts the oxygen dissociation curve leftward, reducing oxygen delivery to the tissues -- and bicarbonate use is itself listed among the cerebral oedema risk factors. *Oxygen therapy* has a supportive role but does not address the underlying volume depletion and metabolic derangement driving this boy\'s presentation, so it is not the priority initial step.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'In paediatric DKA, fluid resuscitation with IV 0.9% saline comes before insulin -- starting insulin in the first hour, and giving bicarbonate, both raise the risk of cerebral oedema.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.632'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-29', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'When you are called for a child CPR, how do you open an unresponsive victim' + String.fromCharCode(39) + 's airway?',
  options:['Use a one-way mouth guard','Tilt their head back.','Turn the victim on their side','Tilt their head back and lift the chin.'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> The correct method is the head tilt-chin lift (unless cervical spine injury suspected), to open the airway during resuscitation.\n\n---\n\n`3) Pediatric resuscitation.txt` places assessing the airway as the first element of the ABCDE approach, looking, listening and feeling for airway obstruction before moving on to breathing, but the lecture as cached does not itself name the specific hand manoeuvre used to open the airway *(the head tilt-chin lift technique and its cervical-spine caveat are standard resuscitation teaching -- not taken from the course material)*. With one hand on the forehead and two fingers under the bony part of the chin, the head is tilted back and the chin lifted, which moves the tongue and soft tissues of the pharynx away from the posterior pharyngeal wall and relieves the airway obstruction they would otherwise cause in an unresponsive victim; this manoeuvre is avoided or modified to a jaw thrust when cervical spine injury is suspected, since head tilt risks worsening a spinal injury.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *A one-way mouth guard* is a barrier device used to protect the rescuer during rescue breaths, not a manoeuvre that opens the airway itself. *Tilting the head back alone*, without the accompanying chin lift, does not reliably clear the tongue from the airway and is not the complete recognised technique. *Turning the victim onto their side* is the recovery position, used for a breathing, unresponsive victim once the airway is already clear -- it does not open an obstructed airway and is not part of the CPR sequence at this step.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'The head tilt-chin lift is the standard manoeuvre to open an unresponsive child' + String.fromCharCode(39) + 's airway, modified to a jaw thrust if cervical spine injury is suspected.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.634'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-31', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'According to shock, which of the following is correct?',
  options:['Anaphylaxis is due to maldistribution of fluids','Hypovolemic shock is due to increased cardiac output','Cardiogenic shock results from peripheral vasodilation','Septic shock is always caused by external blood loss'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Anaphylactic shock is a distributive shock where massive vasodilation causes fluid maldistribution. Hypovolemic shock is due to volume loss, cardiogenic is pump failure, and septic is not always external blood loss.\n\n---\n\n`4) Shock in pediatrics_.txt` classifies shock into four types: hypovolaemia, distributive shock (giving sepsis and anaphylaxis as examples), cardiogenic (arrhythmias, heart failure) and neurogenic (spinal cord injury). Anaphylaxis sits under distributive shock, in which the abnormality is not a lost volume of fluid but an abnormal distribution of the fluid that is already present -- the lecture names "abnormal vascular tone" as one of the mechanisms of haemodynamic decompensation, and in anaphylaxis this takes the form of widespread vasodilation that pools blood in the periphery and lets fluid leak out of the circulation, so the effective circulating volume falls even though total body fluid has not been lost the way it is in true hypovolaemia.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Hypovolaemic shock is due to increased cardiac output* reverses the mechanism: hypovolaemia is a fall in circulating volume, and cardiac output falls as a result rather than rising. *Cardiogenic shock results from peripheral vasodilation* also swaps the mechanism -- the lecture\'s cardiogenic category is myocardial dysfunction (arrhythmias, heart failure), a pump problem, not a vascular-tone problem, and it typically produces vasoconstriction rather than vasodilation as the body tries to compensate for the failing pump *(the specific compensatory vasoconstriction detail is standard shock physiology -- not taken from the course material)*. *Septic shock is always caused by external blood loss* is wrong on two counts: sepsis is listed as a distributive, not a hypovolaemic, cause of shock, and the word "always" excludes the fact that septic shock arises from the physiological response to infection rather than from bleeding.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Shock is classified as hypovolaemic, distributive (sepsis, anaphylaxis), cardiogenic (arrhythmias, heart failure) or neurogenic; anaphylaxis causes distributive shock through vasodilation and fluid maldistribution, not volume loss.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.638'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-32', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'Which of the following is true regarding capillary refill time (CRT)?',
  options:['It is affected by cold environment','It is assessed by pressing on the forehead','It is not prolonged in compensated (early) shock','It is considered prolonged if > 4 seconds'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> CRT is influenced by temperature and perfusion status. It is normally assessed on the sternum or fingertip and considered prolonged if >2–3 seconds, not only >4 seconds.\n\n---\n\n`4) Shock in pediatrics_.txt` lists delayed capillary refill (>2 seconds) among the clinical features of early, compensated shock, alongside tachycardia, tachypnoea, decreased skin turgor and mottled/pale/cold skin -- so CRT prolongation is expected early, not absent, in compensated shock. A cold examination room or cold limb slows peripheral perfusion independently of the child\'s circulating volume, which is exactly why ambient and limb temperature are recognised confounders of the test *(the specific "cold environment falsely prolongs CRT" mechanism is general clinical technique, not spelled out on this slide -- not taken from the course material)*.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Assessed by pressing on the forehead* is wrong technique -- the box itself states CRT is normally checked on the sternum or a fingertip, pressing until blanched and timing the return of colour; the forehead is not a standard site. *Not prolonged in compensated shock* is the opposite of what the lecture teaches: delayed CRT is one of the earliest, most reliable compensated-shock signs, not a late one. *Considered prolonged if >4 seconds* sets the threshold too high -- both the lecture (">2 s") and the box ("2–3 seconds") place the cut-off well below 4 seconds, so a child could be significantly shocked with a CRT the ">4 second" definition would still call normal.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Capillary refill time is delayed (>2–3 seconds) in early compensated shock, assessed at the sternum or fingertip, and is confounded by a cold environment -- not tested on the forehead and not held to a >4-second threshold.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.640'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-33', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'Roberto is a 2.5-year-old boy. He pulled a chip pan off the cooker and has been extensively burnt. He is rushed to the nearest Children’s Emergency Department. His airway, breathing and circulation are satisfactory. His burns are distributed on his body as shown. Most of the burnt area is now blistering and mottled in colour, with a few white areas. Intravenous analgesia is given. Roberto is much more settled following intravenous analgesia. From the list of possible management options below, which should be undertaken first?',
  options:['Commence intravenous 0.9% saline','Cover the burns with sterile dressings','Intravenous antibiotics','Intubation and artificial ventilation','Place affected areas in cold water'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> With extensive burns, the most urgent step is fluid resuscitation to prevent shock. Covering the burns is important, but fluids must be started first to maintain perfusion.\n\n---\n\nThe stem states plainly that Roberto\'s burns are extensive, that his airway, breathing and circulation are currently satisfactory, and that his skin is blistering and mottled with some white areas -- a description consistent with a mix of partial- and full-thickness burn, without stating what fraction of his body surface is involved. A burn wound this size loses fluid continuously through the damaged skin and by capillary leak into the surrounding tissue, and once that ongoing loss outstrips what his circulation can compensate for, his currently "satisfactory" ABC status will deteriorate into hypovolaemic shock. `4) Shock in pediatrics_.txt` names burns directly as one of the recognised causes of dehydration and shock, alongside gastroenteritis, sepsis and diabetic ketoacidosis, and teaches the same fluid-management priority for any child at risk of shock: resuscitation fluid comes first, ahead of the deficit-replacement and maintenance calculations that follow it. That is why starting an IV 0.9% saline infusion is the step that must happen before anything else on the list -- it is what keeps his circulation intact while the rest of his burn care is organised.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Covering the burns with sterile dressings* protects the wound from infection and heat loss and is genuinely part of burn care, but a dressing does nothing to replace the fluid volume already being lost -- it follows fluid resuscitation, not precedes it. *Intravenous antibiotics* are not given prophylactically for a fresh burn wound and would not address the acute risk of shock in any case. *Intubation and artificial ventilation* is unnecessary here since the stem specifically states airway and breathing are satisfactory, with no mention of airway burns, singed facial or nasal hair, or hoarseness that would raise concern for inhalational injury. *Placing the affected areas in cold water* is not correct first aid for an established, already-analgised burn of this extent -- cooling is a very early first-aid measure for a fresh, limited burn, and prolonged or delayed cold-water immersion of an extensive burn instead risks hypothermia, which compounds rather than treats the shock physiology this question is testing *(the cold-water-immersion caution for large burns specifically is general burns-management knowledge -- not taken from the course material, which names burns as a cause of shock but does not detail first-aid cooling technique)*.\n\n**⚠️ Within-bank reprint — p.561, and the shorter printing is the one that was dropped.** The endpoint file prints this question twice: in full here on p.642, with the mechanism, the age, the burn distribution and the appearance of the burnt skin, and again on p.561 cut down to “Roberto, a boy who suffered an extensive burn, is much more settled following intravenous analgesia. which should be undertaken first?”. The five options, their order and the key are identical in both. The fuller printing is kept, because the burn depth described here — blistering, mottled, with a few white areas — is what the reasoning actually rests on and the p.561 printing omits it entirely.\n\n⚠️ **The figure is not reproduced.** p.642 refers the reader to a body diagram showing the distribution of the burn (“as shown”) and that figure has not been cropped into this bank, so the printed percentage of body surface area is not available here. The reasoning above does not depend on it: an extensive burn of this description warrants fluid resuscitation whatever the exact figure, and the option list contains no percentage.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'An extensively burnt child with currently satisfactory airway, breathing and circulation is started on IV 0.9% saline first, because burns cause ongoing fluid loss that can progress to shock -- ahead of dressings, antibiotics, intubation or cold-water immersion.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.642 (an ABBREVIATED printing of the same question appears on p.561, cutting the vignette to a single sentence but keeping the same five options in the same order and the same key)'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-34', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'Which of following is most common cause of pediatric cardiopulmonary arrest?',
  options:['cardiac arrhythmias','metabolic anomalies','overwhelming infections','trauma','respiratory problem'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> In children, cardiac arrest usually follows prolonged hypoxia and respiratory failure, not primary cardiac disease. This makes early recognition and correction of respiratory issues critical.\n\n---\n\n`3) Pediatric resuscitation.txt` opens by asking exactly this question -- "Most common cause of cardiopulmonary arrest in children is……" -- and the whole structure of the lecture answers it without needing a labelled slide: the entire ABCDE assessment is built around airway and breathing being checked and treated before circulation, respiratory failure is defined and its causes (alveolar hypoventilation, V/Q mismatch, diffusion impairment) are worked through in detail, and the lecture states directly that "the key to management of the seriously ill child is early recognition and intervention to prevent respiratory or circulatory failure; once present they are difficult to reverse." Unlike adults, where a primary cardiac event (arrhythmia, ischaemia) is the leading cause of arrest, children\'s hearts are structurally normal in the overwhelming majority of cases -- what fails first is oxygenation or ventilation, and the heart stops only after a period of hypoxia and acidosis has already damaged it *(the specific epidemiological framing "respiratory failure is the most common pathway to paediatric arrest, not primary cardiac disease" is standard paediatric resuscitation teaching -- not spelled out as a numbered statistic in the lecture cache, though the deck\'s entire emphasis on airway/breathing-first assessment and the box\'s own wording both point to the same conclusion)*.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Cardiac arrhythmias* are the dominant arrest mechanism in adults with underlying coronary disease, but primary arrhythmic arrest is uncommon in structurally normal paediatric hearts. *Metabolic anomalies* and *overwhelming infections* can each precipitate deterioration and eventually arrest, but they typically do so by causing shock or respiratory compromise first, rather than being the most common final common pathway themselves. *Trauma* is an important cause of paediatric death but is not the single most common cause of cardiopulmonary arrest across the whole paediatric population the way respiratory failure is.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Paediatric cardiopulmonary arrest most commonly follows prolonged respiratory failure and hypoxia, not primary cardiac disease -- the reason the ABCDE approach secures airway and breathing before circulation.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.644'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-35', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'A 1-year-old girl is brought to the emergency room with a history of persistent vomiting and loose, watery stools. Which of the following would be a sign of uncompensated shock?',
  options:['Capillary refilling time greater than 3 sec.','Heart rate greater than 120 beats/min','Cold extremities','Respiratory rate greater than 40 breaths/min.','Low blood pressure.'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Children compensate with tachycardia and cool extremities; hypotension appears late and signals uncompensated (decompensated) shock. It is therefore the most ominous sign.\n\n---\n\n`4) Shock in pediatrics_.txt` splits the clinical features of shock into two explicit lists. Tachycardia, tachypnoea, decreased skin turgor, sunken eyes and fontanelle, delayed capillary refill (>2 seconds), mottled/pale/cold skin and a core-peripheral temperature gap (>4°C) are all grouped as signs of early, compensated shock -- the stage at which the child\'s circulation is still managing to defend blood pressure. Bradycardia, acidotic (Kussmaul) breathing, confusion or a depressed cerebral state, blue peripheries, absent urine output and hypotension are grouped as late, decompensated shock, and the lecture states directly that "early sign of shock is tachycardia, late sign of shock is low blood pressure," stressing that recognising the reversible, compensated stage matters precisely because decompensated shock, once blood pressure falls, may be irreversible. Low blood pressure is therefore the one option on this list that the lecture places specifically in the late/decompensated column, matching the box\'s framing of hypotension as "the most ominous sign."\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Capillary refill time greater than 3 seconds*, *heart rate greater than 120 beats/min*, *cold extremities* and *respiratory rate greater than 40 breaths/min* are all listed by the lecture as early, compensated shock signs -- the body\'s active attempt to defend perfusion to vital organs by redirecting blood flow away from the skin and speeding the heart and breathing rate. A dehydrated 1-year-old with ongoing vomiting and diarrhoea would be expected to show exactly these compensated signs well before blood pressure starts to fall, which is why only hypotension marks the transition into uncompensated, decompensated shock.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Low blood pressure is a late, decompensated-shock sign, distinct from tachycardia, delayed capillary refill, cold extremities and tachypnoea, which are all early, compensated-shock signs that the body still generates while defending blood pressure.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.646'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-36', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'Regarding resuscitative effort, the most important goal is:',
  options:['restoration of age-appropriate heart rate','appropriate movement of the chest wall','auscultation of equal breath sounds on both lungs','adequate oxygen delivery to the tissues'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'`3) Pediatric resuscitation.txt` sets out what "effective" bag-mask ventilation actually means during a resuscitation: "Use only the amount of force and tidal volume needed to make the chest rise. Avoid excessive volume or pressure." Chest rise is presented as the practical, bedside marker the rescuer watches for breath by breath -- it is the visible confirmation that each delivered breath is reaching and inflating the lungs, without over-distending them (which risks gastric insufflation and barotrauma) or under-inflating them (which fails to ventilate). This is the same idea the page\'s own illustration makes for the newborn specifically, captioning a crying newborn photograph with "Good Chest Wall Movement Is The Key Of Neonatal Resuscitation" -- chest wall movement is singled out as the single most important goal to watch for during resuscitative effort because it is the one sign available immediately, breath by breath, that ventilation is actually effective, before any of the downstream outcomes (heart rate, oxygenation, breath sounds) have had time to respond.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Restoration of age-appropriate heart rate* is a real target of resuscitation, but heart rate responds to effective ventilation with a lag -- it is a downstream consequence, not the immediate goal a rescuer checks with every breath. *Auscultation of equal breath sounds on both lungs* is a useful confirmatory check, particularly to exclude a mainstem intubation or a pneumothorax, but it is not itself the "most important goal" of the resuscitative effort -- it verifies air entry after the fact rather than guiding the rescuer\'s technique in real time. *Adequate oxygen delivery to the tissues* is the ultimate physiological aim of the whole resuscitation, but it depends on circulation and haemoglobin as well as ventilation, and cannot be observed directly at the bedside the way chest rise can -- the lecture\'s emphasis on watching the chest is precisely because it is the immediate, visible, moment-to-moment proxy for effective ventilation that a rescuer can act on.\n\nWritten for this bank — Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Good, visible chest wall movement -- not excessive force or volume -- is the immediate, breath-by-breath marker of effective bag-mask ventilation during resuscitation, watched for ahead of heart rate, breath sounds or tissue oxygenation, which all respond only after ventilation is already effective.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.648'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-37', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'A child weight 8kg presents has had vomiting and been off his feeds for 2 days, he presented with shock and needs a bolus of normal saline to treat his shock. Calculate volume of fluid you would give him',
  options:['40 ml','80 ml','320 ml','800 ml'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> This question actually came once before in the old exam format and caused some confusion. You multiply the weight by 10 or 20. So, if you find 80, it’s correct ✅, or if you find 160, that’s also correct ✅\n\n---\n\n⚠️ The box printed on this page is internally inconsistent with the page it sits on: it tells the reader that 160 ml (8 kg × 20 ml/kg) is "also correct," but 160 ml is not one of the four printed options here at all -- the choices are 40, 80, 320 and 800 ml, and the only figure among them that a 10 or 20 ml/kg calculation can reach is 80 ml (8 kg × 10 ml/kg). The highlighted key on the page is **b, 80 ml**, and that key stays exactly as printed. `4) Shock in pediatrics_.txt` gives the standard resuscitation-fluid rate as a bolus with a repeatable dose depending on response, and this bank\'s own explanation for the closely analogous 8 kg-infant bolus question elsewhere in this section works the arithmetic at 20 ml/kg = 160 ml -- so 20 ml/kg is the rate this course otherwise uses. Here, though, the printed options simply do not include 160 ml, so whatever the intended rate, 80 ml (the 10 ml/kg calculation) is the only option consistent with both the highlighted key and an available answer choice, and it is treated as correct on this page\'s own terms.\n\n**Why each distractor fails, and why the box\'s "also correct" 160 ml cannot be selected.** *40 ml* is 5 ml/kg, below either bolus rate discussed in the box. *320 ml* is 40 ml/kg, double the higher rate the box mentions, and matches the cumulative resuscitation-fluid volume the lecture associates with a child likely needing mechanical ventilatory support, not a single bolus. *800 ml* is 100 ml/kg, the full 24-hour maintenance-fluid figure, not a resuscitation bolus at all. *160 ml*, which the box calls acceptable, cannot be chosen for the simple reason that it is not printed as an option on this page -- an answer the exam does not offer cannot be selected regardless of what the explanation says elsewhere.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'The printed key on this page is 80 ml (8 kg x 10 ml/kg); the box\'s claim that 160 ml is "also correct" cannot apply here because 160 ml is not among the four printed options -- the key is recorded as printed, and the mismatch is the box\'s error, not the reader\'s.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.650'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-38', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'A 6-year-old girl presents with vomiting and abdominal pain. She is dehydrated, her blood glucose is 13 mmol/L, and her blood pH is 7.2. What is the most appropriate initial management?',
  options:['IV bolus of normal saline','IV infusion of normal saline','IV insulin infusion','Sodium bicarbonate administration'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> In a child with DKA (vomiting, abdominal pain, hyperglycemia, acidosis), the first priority is fluid resuscitation.\n\n---\n\n`51) DKA.txt` names the classical triad of diabetic ketoacidosis as hyperglycaemia (blood glucose >200 mg/dL, roughly >11 mmol/L), metabolic acidosis (pH <7.3, HCO3 <18) and ketonaemia/ketonuria, and lists vomiting, abdominal pain and dehydration among the clinical signs -- this girl\'s glucose of 13 mmol/L and pH of 7.2 both cross those thresholds, alongside vomiting, abdominal pain and dehydration, so DKA is the diagnosis the stem is testing. The lecture then sequences fluid management explicitly under "Fluids therapy": resuscitation fluids come first, followed by deficit-replacement fluids and maintenance fluids, and it separately lists "administration of insulin in the first hour of fluid treatment" as a recognised risk factor for cerebral oedema -- one of DKA\'s most feared complications in children. Both of these point the same way: a shocked or significantly dehydrated child with DKA is resuscitated with an IV bolus of 0.9% saline first, to restore circulating volume safely, before any other fluid or insulin therapy is started.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *IV infusion of normal saline* (as opposed to a bolus) describes ongoing deficit-replacement or maintenance fluid, which is exactly what the lecture places after the initial resuscitation bolus, not instead of it -- the acute step still comes first. *IV insulin infusion* is dangerous to start immediately: giving insulin before volume is restored, and too early in the fluid course, is one of the lecture\'s own listed risk factors for cerebral oedema, and insulin will also drive potassium into cells and worsen an unrecognised low serum potassium if given before fluids. *Sodium bicarbonate administration* is not part of routine DKA correction -- the acidosis in DKA resolves as fluids and insulin correct the underlying ketosis and dehydration, and bicarbonate is reserved, if used at all, for extreme, life-threatening acidosis rather than a pH of 7.2.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'A dehydrated child in DKA (hyperglycaemia, acidosis, vomiting, abdominal pain) is given an IV bolus of 0.9% saline first -- ahead of maintenance infusion, insulin (a cerebral-oedema risk if started too early) or bicarbonate.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.652'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-39', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'A child with obstructive shock may suffer the following:',
  options:['Sepsis','Anaphylaxis','Gastroenteritis with severe dehydration','Diabetic ketoacidosis','Tension pneumothorax'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Obstructive shock occurs when mechanical obstruction prevents venous return or cardiac output, as in tension pneumothorax or cardiac tamponade. Sepsis and dehydration cause distributive or hypovolemic shock, not obstructive.\n\n---\n\n`4) Shock in pediatrics_.txt` names four shock types by name -- hypovolaemia, distributive (sepsis, anaphylaxis), cardiogenic (arrhythmias, heart failure) and neurogenic (spinal cord injury) -- but does not use the label "obstructive shock" itself *(the obstructive-shock category and its two classic causes, tension pneumothorax and cardiac tamponade, are standard paediatric critical-care teaching -- not taken from the course material, which teaches the same physiology under a different name)*. That physiology is, however, taught elsewhere in the course: `3) Pediatric resuscitation.txt` lists the reversible "4H4T" causes of cardiac arrest, and two of the "T"s are tension pneumothorax and (cardiac) tamponade -- exactly the two mechanisms the box names for obstructive shock. Both work the same way: a physical obstruction (air under tension compressing the great veins and mediastinum, or fluid compressing the heart in its pericardial sac) mechanically prevents blood returning to, or being ejected from, the heart, dropping cardiac output despite a heart and circulating volume that are not themselves primarily diseased.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Sepsis* is the lecture\'s own example of distributive shock, caused by pathological vasodilation and capillary leak, not mechanical obstruction. *Anaphylaxis* is the other distributive-shock example the lecture gives, driven by histamine-mediated vasodilation and increased capillary permeability. *Gastroenteritis with severe dehydration* causes hypovolaemic shock through fluid loss, with a normally compliant, unobstructed circulation. *Diabetic ketoacidosis* likewise produces hypovolaemic shock through osmotic diuresis and vomiting-related fluid loss, not through any mechanical blockage of venous return or cardiac output.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Obstructive shock -- tension pneumothorax and cardiac tamponade mechanically blocking venous return or cardiac output -- is a distinct mechanism from the distributive shock of sepsis/anaphylaxis or the hypovolaemic shock of gastroenteritis/DKA.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.654'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-40', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'A term male infant is delivered vaginally to a 22-year-old mother. Immediately after birth he is noted to have a scaphoid abdomen, cyanosis, and respiratory distress. Heart sounds are heard on the right side of the chest, and the breath sounds seem to be diminished on the left side. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in his resuscitation?',
  options:['Administer IV bicarbonate.','Administer IV naloxone.','Initiate bag-and-mask intubation.','Initiate chest compressions immediately.','Intubate with an endotracheal tube.'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> This is congenital diaphragmatic hernia, where bag-mask ventilation can worsen gas insufflation of the stomach. Immediate intubation with ET tube is the safest airway management.\n\n---\n\n`9) Respiratory distress in newborn.txt` describes congenital diaphragmatic hernia (CDH) with the same three features this stem gives almost verbatim: "1. Severe, immediate respiratory distress, 2. Scaphoid (sunken) abdomen, 3. Heart sounds shifted to the right." In CDH, abdominal viscera herniate through the diaphragmatic defect into the chest, most often on the left, which is why this infant\'s heart sounds are displaced rightward (the heart is pushed over by the herniated bowel/stomach filling the left hemithorax) and why breath sounds are diminished on the affected left side, while the abdomen looks sunken because there is less bowel left in it to fill it out. Bag-mask ventilation forces air down the oesophagus into the stomach and bowel as well as into the lungs, and in CDH that herniated, already space-occupying bowel becomes further distended with air, compressing the already hypoplastic lung(s) even further and worsening the respiratory distress and the risk of a tension-physiology deterioration *(the specific "bag-mask distends the herniated bowel and worsens compression" mechanism is standard neonatal resuscitation teaching for CDH -- not spelled out on this slide, which gives the diagnostic triad but not the airway-management rationale)*. Endotracheal intubation delivers ventilation directly to the trachea and lungs without inflating the gut, which is why it is the safer next step.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *IV bicarbonate* has no role in the acute airway management of a newly delivered, distressed infant and does not address the underlying mechanical problem. *IV naloxone* treats opioid-induced respiratory depression, which nothing in this stem suggests. *Bag-and-mask intubation* is not a real combined technique, and bag-mask ventilation alone is specifically the manoeuvre CDH management is trying to avoid, for the reasons above. *Chest compressions* are not indicated -- the stem describes respiratory distress and diminished breath sounds, with no statement that the infant is bradycardic or pulseless, so airway management, not compressions, is the immediate next step.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'A newborn with scaphoid abdomen, respiratory distress and heart sounds displaced to the right has congenital diaphragmatic hernia, and is intubated directly rather than bag-mask ventilated, because positive-pressure mask breaths distend the herniated bowel and worsen lung compression.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.656'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-41', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'A 37-week gestation boy is born after an uncomplicated pregnancy to a 33-year-old mother. At birth he was lethargic and had an HR of 40. Oxygen was administered via bag and mask, and he was intubated; his HR remained at 40 beats/min. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?',
  options:['Administer IV bicarbonate.','Administer IV atropine.','Administer IV epinephrine.','Administer IV calcium chloride.','Begin chest compressions.'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> If the HR remains <60/min after effective ventilation and oxygen, chest compressions are the next step. Epinephrine is given if HR does not improve after compressions.\n\n---\n\n*The stepwise escalation this box describes -- effective positive-pressure ventilation first, chest compressions if the heart rate stays below 60/min despite that ventilation, and epinephrine only if the heart rate still does not respond to compressions -- is the standard neonatal resuscitation algorithm; the cached course material covers the general principles of paediatric resuscitation (ABCDE, when arrest is diagnosed, the 4H4T reversible causes) but does not itself spell out this specific neonatal HR-threshold sequence, so the escalation logic below is not taken from the course material.* This infant has already had the first two steps of the sequence performed correctly and in order -- bag-and-mask oxygen, then intubation when that alone was not enough -- and his heart rate has not responded; it remains at 40 beats/min, well below the 60/min threshold at which compressions become necessary despite ventilation already being effective (he is intubated, so ventilation delivery is no longer in doubt). The next step in the algorithm is therefore to begin chest compressions, coordinated with continued ventilation, to support circulation while the underlying cause of the bradycardia is addressed.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *IV bicarbonate* has no established role at this point in a neonatal resuscitation and does not treat bradycardia from inadequate perfusion. *IV atropine* is not part of the standard neonatal bradycardia algorithm, which is driven by ventilation and compressions rather than vagolytic drugs. *IV epinephrine* is reserved for a heart rate that remains below 60/min despite both effective ventilation and adequate chest compressions -- compressions have not yet been tried in this infant, so escalating straight to a drug skips a step. *IV calcium chloride* is used for specific indications such as hyperkalaemia or hypocalcaemia, neither of which this stem establishes, and is not a routine part of the bradycardia algorithm.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'A neonate with a heart rate persisting below 60/min despite effective bag-mask ventilation and intubation is given chest compressions next -- before epinephrine, which is reserved for a heart rate still not responding after compressions.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.658'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-42', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'A term female infant is born vaginally after an uncomplicated pregnancy. She appears normal but has respiratory distress when she stops crying. When crying she is pink; when not she makes vigorous respiratory efforts but becomes dusky. Which of the following is the likely explanation for her symptoms?',
  options:['Choanal atresia','Diaphragmatic hernia','Meconium aspiration','Neonatal narcosis'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Newborns are obligate nasal breathers. In bilateral choanal atresia, they turn pink while crying (mouth breathing) but become cyanotic and distressed when quiet (trying to breathe through blocked nares).\n\n---\n\n*Choanal atresia itself is not covered in the cached peds lecture material -- searched under "choanal atresia", "nasal obstruction" and "obligate nasal breather" with no relevant hits, so this entry is grounded in general neonatal knowledge and tagged throughout (not taken from the course material).* Newborn infants breathe almost exclusively through the nose for feeding and quiet respiration, because the combination of a relatively large tongue and a soft palate positioned close to the epiglottis makes coordinated mouth breathing difficult in the first weeks of life *(not taken from the course material)*. In bilateral choanal atresia, a bony or membranous septum obstructs the posterior nasal passages from birth. While the infant cries, she opens her mouth and breathes around the obstruction, so she oxygenates normally and looks pink; the moment she stops crying and reverts to trying to breathe through her nose, the fixed obstruction prevents airflow and she becomes distressed and dusky -- exactly the cyclical "pink-when-crying, blue-when-quiet" pattern this stem describes *(not taken from the course material)*.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Diaphragmatic hernia* (covered in `9) Respiratory distress in newborn.txt`) causes continuous respiratory distress with a scaphoid abdomen and shifted heart sounds -- it does not cycle with crying versus quiet breathing, because the mechanical compression from herniated bowel is constant regardless of breathing route. *Meconium aspiration* produces respiratory distress from airway obstruction and chemical pneumonitis that is also persistent, not one that resolves specifically when the infant cries and reappears when she is quiet. *Neonatal narcosis* (opioid-related respiratory depression from maternal analgesia) causes generalised hypoventilation and low tone, not a cyclical pattern tied to nasal versus oral airflow.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'A newborn who is pink while crying (mouth breathing) but dusky and distressed once quiet (obligate nasal breathing blocked) has bilateral choanal atresia, distinguished from diaphragmatic hernia and meconium aspiration by its cyclical, crying-dependent pattern.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.660'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-43', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'A 25-day-old female infant is brought to the emergency department for fever of 101°F (38.3°C) at home. The baby was born vaginally at full term and was appropriate for gestational age. Maternal GBS was negative. Apgar scores were 8 and 9. The mother noticed the baby has had decreased feeding over the previous few days and has been sleeping more. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial choice of antibiotics for this infant?',
  options:['Oral amoxicillin','Vancomycin','Ampicillin','Ampicillin and cefotaxime','Ampicillin and gentamicin'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> This patient may have late-onset bacterial infection, likely GBS; she should be admitted for sepsis evaluation and IV antibiotics. The best initial treatment in this age group is broad-spectrum antibiotics such as ampicillin and cefotaxime. If cultures are positive for GBS, antibiotic therapy can be narrowed to penicillin G.\n\n---\n\n`18) febrile child and life threatening infections.txt` states that all febrile children under 1 month old require admission and empiric IV antibiotic coverage for the most common neonatal pathogens -- this infant, at 25 days old with a fever of 38.3°C, falls squarely in that category regardless of a negative maternal GBS screen, because GBS status does not exclude late-onset infection acquired horizontally after birth. `11) Neonatal sepsis.txt` classifies infection presenting after 72 hours of life as late-onset sepsis (LONS), with organisms including GBS as well as coagulase-negative staphylococci and Staph. aureus, and lists decreased activity/feeding and lethargy among the non-specific clinical features that should trigger a sepsis workup. Because a 25-day-old with fever, poor feeding and increased sleepiness cannot be reliably assessed for meningitis at the bedside, and the neonatal sepsis lecture\'s own antibiotic guidance names ampicillin plus cefotaxime specifically for suspected meningitis (as opposed to ampicillin plus gentamicin for septicaemia/pneumonia without meningeal involvement), the combination that covers both possibilities -- systemic sepsis and CNS infection -- is chosen empirically until cultures return *(the specific decision to default to the meningitis-covering regimen whenever CNS involvement cannot be excluded at this age is standard neonatal practice -- the lecture gives the two regimens side by side but does not spell out this precise decision rule)*.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Oral amoxicillin* is inappropriate -- infants this age with signs of possible sepsis are never managed with oral antibiotics as outpatients. *Vancomycin* alone does not cover the Gram-negative organisms relevant to neonatal sepsis and is not first-line empiric monotherapy. *Ampicillin* alone omits a second agent needed for adequate Gram-negative and CNS coverage. *Ampicillin and gentamicin* is the lecture\'s own regimen for septicaemia/pneumonia without suspected meningitis, but gentamicin does not achieve reliable cerebrospinal fluid levels, so it is not preferred once meningitis cannot be excluded, as here.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'A febrile infant under 1 month with poor feeding and lethargy is admitted for a full sepsis evaluation and started on empiric ampicillin plus cefotaxime -- covering possible meningitis -- rather than the ampicillin-plus-gentamicin regimen reserved for septicaemia/pneumonia without CNS involvement.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.662'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-44', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'A 12-hour-old infant who has been feeding poorly becomes tachypneic with grunting. Which of the following initial tests has the lowest diagnostic yield?',
  options:['Chest radiograph','Complete blood count','Urine culture','Blood culture','Glucose level'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> In the first 24 hours, sepsis workup includes blood culture, CBC, glucose, and CXR, but urine culture is not useful since UTIs rarely present this early. It has the lowest diagnostic yield.\n\n---\n\n`11) Neonatal sepsis.txt` classifies infection presenting in the first 72 hours of life as early-onset sepsis (EONS), acquired vertically from the mother, and lists septicaemia/bloodstream infection, pneumonia, UTI, meningitis and joint/bone infection among the organs sepsis can affect, with "triple tabs" (blood culture, CSF analysis and culture, urine analysis and culture) named as part of the diagnostic workup overall. This infant, at 12 hours old with poor feeding and grunting/tachypnoea (both listed by the same lecture among the clinical features of neonatal sepsis, alongside "not doing well" and heart rate variability), is being worked up for EONS, and a chest radiograph, CBC, blood culture and glucose are all reasonable first-line tests for a septic-appearing newborn this early *(a chest film for the grunting/tachypnoea, CBC and blood culture for sepsis, and glucose because hypoglycaemia both mimics and complicates neonatal sepsis)*. Urine culture, though listed as part of the broader "triple tabs" panel, is a poor early test specifically because ascending or haematogenous urinary tract infection has not had time to establish itself by 12 hours of life -- UTI in the neonatal period typically reflects bacteraemic seeding of the urinary tract over the following days rather than something already detectable at birth-plus-half-a-day *(this specific age-related timing point for why urine culture has low yield in the first 24 hours is standard neonatology teaching -- not spelled out as an explicit timing rule in the lecture cache, though the lecture\'s own EONS/LONS split by the 72-hour mark supports the same reasoning)*.\n\n**Why each other option is not the answer.** *Chest radiograph* directly investigates the grunting and tachypnoea and has high yield for pneumonia or RDS. *Complete blood count* and *blood culture* are core components of any neonatal sepsis screen from birth onward. *Glucose level* is essential given how closely hypoglycaemia and sepsis overlap in presentation in this age group.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'In a 12-hour-old infant being worked up for possible sepsis, urine culture has the lowest diagnostic yield of the standard tests, because urinary tract infection has not had time to establish itself this early in life, unlike chest radiograph, CBC, blood culture or glucose.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.664'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-45', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'A term male is born via repeat cesarean section to a 30-year-old woman. Immediately after birth he has mild respiratory distress. Chest auscultation in the delivery room reveals clear breath sounds. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?',
  options:['Endotracheal intubation with direct suction.','Begin intravenous antibiotic therapy.','Deliver surfactant therapy.','Observe and administer supplemental oxygen as needed.','Bag-mask ventilation.'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> This is likely transient tachypnea of the newborn (TTN), common after C-section due to delayed lung fluid clearance. Management is supportive with oxygen and observation.\n\n---\n\n`9) Respiratory distress in newborn.txt` describes transient tachypnoea of the newborn (TTN, also called RDS type 2 or "wet lung") as the most common cause of neonatal respiratory distress, resulting from delayed clearance of fetal lung fluid by the pulmonary lymphatics, and names elective caesarean section specifically among its risk factors -- because labour itself helps trigger the adrenaline surge that switches lung fluid secretion off and starts its resorption, and a baby delivered by caesarean before or without labour misses some of that physiological trigger. The lecture describes TTN as mild to moderate respiratory distress starting within 2 hours of birth, with clear breath sounds on auscultation (unlike pneumonia or meconium aspiration, which typically show abnormal air entry or added sounds) and a regressive course: "TTN is alleviated by supplemental oxygen or CPAP and resolves in 3 days. Most cases need no active treatment." This term boy, delivered by repeat (i.e. planned) caesarean with mild distress and a clear chest, matches that picture closely, so supportive observation with oxygen as needed is the appropriate next step, without further invasive intervention.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Endotracheal intubation with direct suction* is the correct step for a non-vigorous infant with meconium-stained fluid, not a mildly distressed baby with clear breath sounds and no mention of meconium. *Intravenous antibiotic therapy* is not started reflexively for mild respiratory distress with clear auscultation and no other sepsis risk factors described. *Surfactant therapy* is reserved for respiratory distress syndrome from surfactant deficiency, which classically affects preterm infants and produces a fine reticulogranular chest X-ray with air bronchograms -- this is a term infant, and surfactant deficiency is not the mechanism of TTN. *Bag-mask ventilation* is an active resuscitation manoeuvre that this mildly distressed, clear-chested infant does not need; observation and supplemental oxygen are sufficient.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Mild respiratory distress with clear breath sounds in a term infant born by elective/repeat caesarean section is transient tachypnoea of the newborn, managed with observation and supplemental oxygen as needed -- not surfactant, antibiotics or active ventilation.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.666'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-46', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'A term male is born vaginally to a 22-year-old primigravida woman; the pregnancy was uncomplicated. Just prior to delivery, fetal bradycardia was noted, and at delivery thick meconium is found. The infant has hypotonia and bradycardia. Which of the following is the first step in resuscitation?',
  options:['Administration of epinephrine through endotracheal tube','Bag-mask ventilation','Endotracheal intubation with direct suction','Oxygen delivered by cannula in close proximity to the nares','Tracheostomy'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> In a depressed neonate with meconium, the airway should be suctioned immediately before providing ventilation. Bag-mask ventilation would push meconium deeper into the lungs.\n\n---\n\nThis infant carries the same features that make thick meconium a resuscitation problem rather than a benign finding: fetal bradycardia beforehand, and hypotonia and bradycardia at delivery, meaning he is not vigorous. `9) Respiratory distress in newborn.txt` explains why acute or chronic fetal hypoxia both stimulates fetal peristalsis and relaxes the anal sphincter -- causing meconium to pass into the amniotic fluid -- and stimulates fetal breathing movements that aspirate meconium-stained fluid into the airway, and it names the delivery-room management directly: "Oral and pharyngeal suction as soon as the head is delivered" followed by oxygen supply. For a non-vigorous, meconium-stained infant, that suctioning principle extends below the pharynx: direct laryngoscopy with endotracheal intubation and suction clears meconium sitting at or above the vocal cords before any positive-pressure breath is given, preventing it from being driven further down into the trachea and bronchi, where the lecture describes it causing complete obstruction with atelectasis, partial (ball-valve) obstruction with air trapping and pneumothorax risk, chemical pneumonitis and surfactant deactivation.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Epinephrine via endotracheal tube* is a drug step reserved for a heart rate that stays critically low despite effective ventilation and compressions -- it is not the first action, and no airway has yet been secured or cleared. *Bag-mask ventilation* is exactly what risks forcing meconium deeper into the airway in a non-vigorous, meconium-stained infant, which is why direct suction under laryngoscopy comes first. *Oxygen by cannula near the nares* does nothing to clear an obstructed or meconium-contaminated airway and is inadequate for a bradycardic, hypotonic infant needing active resuscitation. *Tracheostomy* is a surgical airway reserved for situations where the airway cannot be secured any other way -- it has no role as a first step in a routine, if urgent, delivery-room resuscitation.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'A non-vigorous, meconium-stained newborn has direct laryngoscopy with endotracheal suction before any positive-pressure breath, because bag-mask ventilation first risks driving meconium further into the airway.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.668'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-47', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'A newborn female is delivered by C-section to a 23-year-old mother after 29 weeks of gestation. She has poor respiratory effort at time of delivery with cyanosis, requiring resuscitation and eventually intubated. On examination in the delivery room, the infant continues to have subcostal retractions and is difficult to ventilate. What is the next BEST step in management?',
  options:['Obtain a chest x-ray.','Administer albuterol.','Administer surfactant.','Closely monitor clinically.','Obtain an echocardiogram (ECHO)'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Preterm infants often lack surfactant, leading to respiratory distress syndrome. Intubated infants with persistent distress benefit most from surfactant replacement.\n\n---\n\nAt 29 weeks\' gestation this infant is born well before surfactant synthesis is mature. `9) Respiratory distress in newborn.txt` states that surfactant synthesis begins at 24–28 weeks and is not mature until 35 weeks, and that RDS/hyaline membrane disease results from exactly this pulmonary immaturity and surfactant deficiency, with an incidence of 60–80% in infants under 28 weeks\' gestation. `7) Intro to neonatology.txt` lists RDS type 1 as the leading respiratory problem of prematurity and gives its treatment directly: "Surfactant administration (tracheal or nebulized), NCPAP, Mechanical ventilation (failure of CPAP)." This infant already has poor respiratory effort, cyanosis and subcostal retractions at birth, has needed intubation, and remains difficult to ventilate -- signs of ongoing severe RDS in an already-secured airway -- so the next best step is to give exogenous surfactant directly down the endotracheal tube she already has in place, replacing what her immature type II alveolar cells cannot yet produce enough of to keep her alveoli from collapsing on expiration.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Chest x-ray* would support the diagnosis (small lung volumes, fine reticulogranular ground-glass mottling with air bronchograms, per the lecture) but is a diagnostic step, not a treatment, and does not address her ongoing difficulty ventilating right now. *Albuterol* is a bronchodilator for reversible airway obstruction (asthma, bronchiolitis); RDS is a surfactant-deficiency and compliance problem, not bronchospasm, so albuterol would not help. *Close clinical monitoring* is inappropriate for an infant who is already intubated and difficult to ventilate -- this is an indication to act, not to watch. *Echocardiogram* is useful for assessing complications such as a patent ductus arteriosus later in the course, but it is not the immediate next step for acute, severe respiratory distress in a ventilated preterm infant.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'A 29-week preterm infant who is intubated but remains difficult to ventilate has surfactant-deficient RDS, and is treated with exogenous surfactant next -- ahead of imaging, bronchodilators, observation or echocardiography.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.670'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-48', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'Which of the following features makes sudden infant death syndrome (SIDS) likely as the cause of a sudden death?',
  options:['An infant found with a bulging fontanelle and facial bruise.','An 18-month-old girl who had a prior sibling that at 1 year of age also died suddenly and unexpectedly.','A 5-month-old infant with dysmorphic features and an enlarged heart found on postmortem examination.','A 3-month-old boy whose parents smoke in the home but were using a high-efficiency particulate arrestance (HEPA) air purifier in his room, a baby monitor, placing him on his side to sleep so he wouldn’t aspirate any refluxed formula, and using a special foam wedge pillow to keep him in that position.','All the above features make SIDS likely as the cause of a sudden death.'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'The other four options each point toward an alternative, more specific diagnosis rather than SIDS, which is by definition a diagnosis of exclusion made only once every other explanation has been ruled out on history, scene investigation and autopsy (not taken from the course material). A bulging fontanelle with a facial bruise raises suspicion of raised intracranial pressure and non-accidental trauma, not SIDS. A sibling who also died suddenly and unexpectedly around the same age raises concern for an inherited metabolic or cardiac channelopathy (for example a fatty-acid oxidation defect or long-QT syndrome) running in the family, which needs its own work-up rather than being labelled SIDS. Dysmorphic features with an enlarged heart on postmortem point to a structural or metabolic cardiomyopathy as the actual cause of death, again a specific diagnosis rather than SIDS.\n\nThe third infant, by contrast, has genuine SIDS risk factors and no alternative explanation on the history given: parental smoking in the home is one of the best-established modifiable SIDS risk factors, and although the parents have tried several precautions (an air purifier, a monitor), the sleep position itself works against them -- he is placed on his side and mechanically held there with a foam wedge, rather than laid supine on a firm, bare surface. Side and prone positioning, and any soft object or device used to maintain a non-supine position, are recognised SIDS risk factors regardless of the intention behind using them (not taken from the course material). "All of the above" is wrong because three of the five vignettes describe findings that point away from SIDS toward a specific alternative cause of death, so they cannot all equally support a SIDS diagnosis.\n\nWritten for this bank — Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'SIDS is a diagnosis of exclusion; unexplained sudden death with recognised risk factors (smoking exposure, non-supine sleep position, soft bedding/positioning devices) and no alternative finding on history or autopsy supports SIDS, while trauma signs, a similarly-affected sibling, or dysmorphism with structural heart disease point to a specific alternative diagnosis instead.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.672'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-49', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'A mother presents to the emergency room with her 6-month-old daughter late at night after she noticed her to be breathing fast for 1 minute, then seemed to stop breathing for 1 minute, and became limp, pale, and unresponsive. The mother attempted to give mouth-to-mouth breaths for a few seconds and her daughter then began to cry and her breathing and appearance normalized. Your next best step is to:',
  options:['Perform a thorough history and physical examination, obtain basic laboratory tests, and admit to the hospital for workup of an ALTE.','Reassure the mother that her infant looks healthy and because the symptoms have resolved, discharge home.','Perform a complete blood count (CBC), chest x-ray (CXR), and discharge home if all are normal.','Instruct the mother to follow-up with the pediatrician to get an apnea monitor and pulse oximeter so that she will know if similar symptoms occur again and if they are actually life threatening.','Tell the mother this was a near SIDS event and instruct her on measures to prevent SIDS..'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'This infant’s episode -- a brief change in breathing pattern followed by apnea, limpness, pallor and unresponsiveness, that resolved after stimulation -- is the classic description of an apparent life-threatening event (ALTE) (not taken from the course material). An ALTE is frightening to the observer precisely because it looks like it could have been fatal, but its causes span a wide range from benign (a vigorous reflux episode, a breath-holding-type response) to dangerous (seizure, an evolving infection such as sepsis or pertussis, a cardiac arrhythmia, an inborn error of metabolism, or an early presentation of airway obstruction). Because the underlying cause cannot be determined from a single resolved episode and a normal-appearing infant in the emergency department, the correct approach is admission for observation together with a thorough history, physical examination and basic laboratory work-up aimed at looking for these treatable causes, rather than assuming the event was benign.\n\nReassuring the mother and discharging home ignores that a resolved episode does not exclude a serious underlying cause, and sending a 6-month-old home without any evaluation after a true apnoeic, unresponsive episode is not safe. Doing a CBC and CXR alone and discharging if normal under-investigates the range of possible causes (a normal CBC and CXR do not exclude a seizure, an arrhythmia or a metabolic disorder) and again discharges before the infant has been observed through a period of risk. Arranging an apnoea monitor and pulse oximeter for home use as the next step defers the actual work-up to an outpatient follow-up visit, when the priority right now is inpatient evaluation and observation. Telling the mother this was "a near SIDS event" is not an accurate label for a resolved, witnessed episode with recovery -- ALTE and SIDS are related concepts but are not interchangeable, and offering SIDS-prevention counselling in place of a proper work-up skips the necessary evaluation of this specific event.\n\nWritten for this bank — Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'A resolved episode of apnoea, colour change and limpness (ALTE) in an infant is admitted for a thorough history, examination and basic laboratory work-up looking for treatable causes -- it is not reassured away, under-investigated, or deferred to outpatient monitoring alone.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.675'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-50', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'You are going to counsel parents of a newborn about prevention of SIDS. Which of the following statements about ways to reduce SIDS is accurate?',
  options:['Infants should sleep in the same bed as the parent or on their chest so they can be closely monitored for apnea.','Infants should sleep on their back on a firm mattress with no accompanying soft bedding or objects, including no devices advertised to maintain the sleep position.','Pacifiers should be avoided because they can obstruct the baby’s airflow during respiration.','Keep the infant dressed in several layers and covered with a heavy blanket.','Infants should be given acetaminophen before their scheduled vaccines in order to prevent an undetected febrile seizure and resulting SIDS.'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'The core, evidence-based SIDS-prevention message is supine sleeping on a firm, bare surface: the infant is placed on their back, on a firm mattress, with no pillows, loose blankets, bumpers, soft toys, or any commercial device marketed to hold the baby in a particular sleep position, since none of these devices has been shown to be safe and several have been linked to suffocation (not taken from the course material).\n\nBed-sharing or sleeping the infant prone on a parent’s chest is itself a recognised SIDS risk factor rather than a protective measure -- overlying, airway obstruction against soft adult bedding, and overheating are all more likely in that setting, so it is not recommended as a monitoring strategy. Pacifiers are not avoided for SIDS prevention; the opposite is true -- pacifier use during sleep is associated with a reduced risk of SIDS and is one of the recommended measures, not a hazard to avoid. Dressing the infant in several layers and adding a heavy blanket promotes overheating, which is itself a SIDS risk factor -- infants should be kept comfortably warm, not bundled to the point of overheating, and heavy loose bedding is specifically discouraged (this is also consistent with the firm, bare-sleep-surface principle above). Giving acetaminophen before routine vaccination to try to prevent an "undetected febrile seizure" causing SIDS is not a real or recommended SIDS-prevention measure; febrile seizures are not a recognised mechanism of SIDS, and pre-emptive antipyretic dosing around vaccination is not part of standard SIDS-reduction counselling.\n\nWritten for this bank — Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'SIDS-prevention counselling centres on supine sleep on a firm, bare mattress with no soft bedding or positioning devices; bed-sharing, prone/chest sleeping, overheating from heavy bedding, and avoiding pacifiers are all wrong directions, while pacifier use during sleep is actually protective.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.678'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-51', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'The investigation of an unexpected infant death includes a history, a postmortem examination, and which of the following?',
  options:['DNA studies','Maternal drug screen','Analysis of parental electrocardiograms','A death scene investigation','Stool studies'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Standard SIDS investigation includes history, autopsy, and examination of the death scene. This helps rule out neglect, trauma, or environmental hazards.\n\n---\n\nThe standard triad for investigating an unexpected infant death is a detailed history (including the events and circumstances leading up to the death), a full postmortem examination, and a death scene investigation -- visiting and examining the place where the infant was found, including the sleep surface, bedding, room temperature and any objects present. The scene investigation is what lets the investigating team distinguish a true SIDS death from an accidental cause (for example accidental suffocation against soft bedding, overlying during bed-sharing, or an entrapment hazard) and from a scene suggesting neglect or non-accidental injury, none of which the history or autopsy alone can fully establish.\n\nDNA studies, a maternal drug screen, parental ECG analysis and stool studies can each occasionally be pursued as *targeted, case-specific* investigations once a particular concern is raised (for example, a family history suggesting a channelopathy might prompt parental ECGs, or a suspicion of a metabolic disorder might prompt genetic testing), but none of these is part of the standard three-part work-up that is performed for every unexpected infant death; the death scene investigation is.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'The standard investigation of every unexpected infant death is history plus postmortem examination plus a death scene investigation -- targeted tests such as DNA studies, toxicology or parental ECGs are added only when a specific concern is raised, not performed routinely.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.681'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-52', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'A 36-week gestation infant is delivered via cesarean section because of macrosomia and fetal distress. The mother has class D pregestational diabetes (insulin dependent, with vascular disease); her hemoglobin A1C is 15% (normal 7%). This infant is at risk for hypocalcemia, cardiomyopathy, polycythemia, and which of the following?',
  options:['Congenital hip dislocation','Dacryostenosis','Respiratory distress syndrome','Hyperglycemia','Pneumothorax'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Hyperinsulinemia in the fetus delays surfactant production, predisposing IDM infants to RDS, despite being often macrosomic.\n\n---\n\nThis infant is an infant of a diabetic mother (IDM), born late-preterm (36 weeks) to a mother with poorly controlled, vasculopathic pregestational diabetes (HbA1c 15% against a normal of roughly 7%). `7) Intro to neonatology.txt` lists the recognised complications of being an IDM, and hypocalcemia, cardiomyopathy and polycythemia -- all three already named in the stem -- sit on that same list alongside RDS, fetal demise, macrosomia, congenital malformations, hyperbilirubinemia, hypoglycemia (not hyperglycemia) and renal vein thrombosis, so RDS completes a pattern the stem is already describing rather than introducing an unrelated risk. Chronic fetal hyperglycemia drives fetal hyperinsulinemia, and insulin antagonises the cortisol-driven maturation of type II pneumocytes, so surfactant production is delayed even in an infant who is large for dates -- which is why IDM infants remain at elevated risk of RDS despite being macrosomic rather than growth-restricted, and why macrosomia does not "protect" against prematurity-type lung immaturity here.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Congenital hip dislocation (developmental dysplasia of the hip)* is not part of the recognised IDM complication list; its own risk factors are breech presentation, female sex and family history, not maternal diabetes. *Dacryostenosis* (blocked nasolacrimal duct) is a common, benign newborn finding unrelated to maternal diabetes. *Hyperglycemia* is the wrong direction -- IDM infants are at risk of neonatal **hypo**glycemia after birth, once the maternally-driven hyperinsulinemia persists but the transplacental glucose supply is suddenly cut off, not hyperglycemia. *Pneumothorax* is a possible complication of RDS and its ventilatory management, but it is not itself one of the primary, named IDM risks the way hypocalcemia, cardiomyopathy, polycythemia and RDS are.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Infants of diabetic mothers are at risk of RDS despite often being macrosomic, because fetal hyperinsulinemia delays surfactant maturation -- alongside hypocalcemia, cardiomyopathy, polycythemia, hypoglycemia (not hyperglycemia) and congenital malformations.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.683'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-53', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'A 1-month-old boy has a fever of 102.7 °F (39.3 °C), is irritable, has diarrhea, and has not been eating well. On examination, he has an immobile, dull, and red TM that has pus behind it. Which of the following is the most appropriate course of action?',
  options:['Admission to the hospital with complete sepsis evaluation','Intramuscular ceftriaxone and close outpatient follow-up','Oral amoxicillin-clavulanate','Oral cefuroxime','High-dose oral amoxicillin'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Infants <2 months with fever and systemic illness require full sepsis workup and admission. Outpatient antibiotics are not safe in this age group.\n\n---\n\n`18) febrile child and life threatening infections.txt` states the rule directly: "All febrile children < 1 mo = admission ... give empiric IV antibiotic coverage for the most common neonatal pathogens." This infant is exactly one month old with a high fever, and beyond the fever he is also irritable, has diarrhoea, and has not been feeding well -- systemic signs that fit the lecture\'s red-flag list for a possible serious bacterial infection in a young infant, alongside the local finding of acute otitis media (an immobile, dull, red TM with pus behind it). In an infant this young, fever plus systemic symptoms cannot be safely attributed to the otitis media alone, because young infants with serious bacterial infection (including occult bacteraemia, meningitis or urinary tract infection) can present with non-specific findings like poor feeding and irritability, and a source found on exam (here, the ear) does not reliably exclude a concurrent invasive infection. That is why the standard of care in this age group is a full sepsis evaluation and hospital admission for empiric IV antibiotics, not treatment of the otitis media alone as an outpatient.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *IM ceftriaxone with outpatient follow-up* is a strategy sometimes used in carefully selected, well-appearing febrile infants who are somewhat older and meet low-risk criteria -- it is not appropriate for a 1-month-old with fever plus systemic symptoms, who falls inside the under-2-month age group the lecture requires admission for. *Oral amoxicillin-clavulanate*, *oral cefuroxime* and *high-dose oral amoxicillin* are all reasonable outpatient antibiotic choices for acute otitis media in an older, well-appearing child, but none of them provides the systemic sepsis coverage or the inpatient observation this febrile, systemically unwell 1-month-old needs, and oral outpatient antibiotics are explicitly not considered safe in this age group.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'A febrile infant under 2 months old with systemic symptoms is admitted for a full sepsis evaluation and empiric IV antibiotics regardless of an identified local source such as otitis media -- outpatient oral or single-dose IM therapy is not considered safe at this age.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.685'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-54', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'An 11-month-old infant appears unwell with sunken eyes and reduced skin turgor. Capillary refill time (CRT) was found to be 5 seconds and extremities were cold. Which of the following would be the first step in the proper management of this infant?',
  options:['Breastfeeding','Oral rehydration solution','IV normal saline','Oral antibiotic'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Any child presenting with manifestations of shock resuscitation with IV normal saline 20 ml/kg. Begin treatment by resuscitation with IV normal saline 20 ml/kg.\n\n---\n\n`4) Shock in pediatrics_.txt` sets out the early/compensated versus late/decompensated pattern of shock, and this infant already shows features from both ends of it: sunken eyes and reduced skin turgor (dehydration), and a capillary refill time of 5 seconds with cold extremities -- a markedly prolonged CRT and cold peripheries are signs of poor perfusion reaching into decompensated shock, not simple dehydration that can be corrected slowly by mouth. The lecture\'s fluid-management sequence starts with **resuscitation** fluid before deficit, maintenance or ongoing-loss replacement are even considered, precisely for a child presenting in shock like this one -- an isotonic crystalloid bolus given rapidly by the IV route to restore circulating volume and perfusion first.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Breastfeeding* and *oral rehydration solution* both rely on the gut to absorb fluid over time, which is far too slow for a child already showing a 5-second capillary refill and cold extremities -- oral routes are reserved for mild-to-moderate dehydration without shock, not for a child with signs of poor perfusion needing immediate resuscitation. *Oral antibiotic* does not address the immediate haemodynamic problem at all; there is no indication here that treats sepsis as the first priority over restoring circulating volume, and even where infection is suspected, resuscitation still comes before antibiotics in a shocked child.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'A child with signs reaching into decompensated shock (markedly prolonged capillary refill, cold extremities) is resuscitated first with an IV isotonic saline bolus -- oral rehydration and breastfeeding are for dehydration without shock, and antibiotics never come before restoring perfusion.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.687'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-56', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'Which of the following is the best intervention that effectively prevents the occurrence of sudden infant death syndrome (SIDS)?',
  options:['Feet to foot of cot','Keeping baby in parent’s room until 6 months of age','Keeping room cool to prevent overheating','Parents not smoking in the same room as infant','Supine sleeping'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> The best intervention that effectively prevents SIDS: Supine sleeping.\n\n---\n\nOf the several genuinely protective practices listed here -- positioning the infant "feet to foot" in the cot (so they cannot wriggle down under the covers), room-sharing with a parent for the first months of life, keeping the room at a comfortable temperature rather than overheated, and avoiding smoke exposure -- supine sleep positioning is the single intervention with the strongest and most consistently demonstrated effect on SIDS rates: the shift to placing infants on their backs to sleep (the "Back to Sleep" message) produced the largest measured falls in SIDS incidence of any single recommended measure once it was adopted (not taken from the course material). The other listed measures (safe positioning within the cot, room-sharing, avoiding overheating, avoiding smoke exposure) are also genuinely protective and are part of the same overall safe-sleep counselling, but none individually carries the same weight of effect as supine sleeping, which is why it is singled out as the single "best" intervention among these options.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Among the several genuinely protective safe-sleep practices, supine sleep positioning is the single intervention associated with the largest reduction in SIDS incidence.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.691'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-57', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'A 3-year-old girl is \'blue-lighted\' into the local emergency department with reduced consciousness. Her mother informs the paramedic that her daughter has been unwell for the last 8 days with vomiting and profuse diarrhea. On arrival, she is apyrexial, has a heart rate of 170 beats per minute and a respiratory rate of 20 per minute. Her systolic blood pressure is 75 mmHg and she has a capillary refill time of 5 seconds. She is barely responsive to pain. Resuscitation begins and a blood gas reveals a metabolic acidosis.What is the most likely clinical syndrome?',
  options:['Anaphylactic shock','Septicemic shock','Cardiogenic shock','Hypovolemic shock'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> The child has a history of prolonged diarrhea and vomiting with signs of poor perfusion (tachycardia, delayed capillary refill, hypotension). These findings are most consistent with hypovolemic shock from dehydration.\n\n---\n\n`4) Shock in pediatrics_.txt` lists hypovolaemia as one of the recognised types of paediatric shock, and this girl\'s history is a textbook run-up to it: 8 days of vomiting and profuse diarrhoea, producing ongoing large fluid losses long enough to exhaust compensation. Her findings on arrival match the late/decompensated end of the shock picture the lecture describes -- marked tachycardia, a capillary refill time of 5 seconds (markedly prolonged), a low systolic blood pressure for her age, and reduced consciousness (barely responsive to pain) -- with a metabolic acidosis on the blood gas reflecting poor tissue perfusion and anaerobic metabolism. She is also apyrexial, arguing against an infective/septic process as the driver. Taken together, a straightforward history of large gastrointestinal fluid losses plus decompensated shock physiology and no fever points to hypovolaemic shock from dehydration as the most likely syndrome.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Anaphylactic shock* is a distributive shock triggered by an allergen exposure, typically with urticaria, angioedema or wheeze -- none of which is described here, and there is no exposure history suggesting it. *Septicaemic shock* is also a form of distributive shock, but it would be expected to come with fever (or in some children, hypothermia) as a marker of infection; this girl is specifically noted to be apyrexial, and her history points to a clear non-infective mechanism (dehydration from vomiting and diarrhoea) instead. *Cardiogenic shock* arises from pump failure rather than volume loss, and there is no history here of cardiac disease, arrhythmia or a presentation pattern (such as poor feeding with hepatomegaly or gallop rhythm) that would suggest a primary cardiac cause; her presentation is fully explained by fluid loss.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'A prolonged history of vomiting and diarrhoea culminating in decompensated shock physiology (severe tachycardia, markedly prolonged capillary refill, hypotension, reduced consciousness, metabolic acidosis) without fever points to hypovolaemic shock from dehydration, not septic, anaphylactic or cardiogenic shock.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.693'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-58', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'A 3-year-old girl was at a birthday party. She suddenly developed swollen cheeks and lips and a widespread urticarial rash. She is rushed to the nearby general practice surgery, where it is noted that her breathing is very noisy. She is distressed and frightened. On auscultation, she has widespread wheeze. Which medication would you give first?',
  options:['Intramuscular antihistamine','Intravenous hydrocortisone','Oral antihistamine','Oral corticosteroid','Intramuscular adrenaline'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> First-line treatment for anaphylaxis is IM adrenaline. Antihistamines and steroids are supportive but not lifesaving.\n\n---\n\nThis girl has the picture of anaphylaxis, not simple urticaria: facial and lip swelling (angioedema) and a widespread urticarial rash are joined here by noisy breathing and widespread wheeze on auscultation, meaning the airway and breathing are already compromised, not just the skin. First-line treatment for anaphylaxis is prompt intramuscular adrenaline, given before any other drug, because adrenaline is the only agent among these options that acts quickly enough to reverse the laryngeal oedema and bronchospasm that are the immediately life-threatening problem; antihistamines and corticosteroids act too slowly and only address the cutaneous and delayed/biphasic components, not the acute airway and breathing compromise.\n\nThis case shares its five-option medication menu with `pedep-emg-10`, which asks the same question for a child with a widespread urticarial rash and facial swelling but with **no** mention of noisy breathing or wheeze -- skin and soft-tissue involvement only, with the airway and breathing unaffected. That is the discriminating token between the two cases: the presence of respiratory compromise (noisy breathing, wheeze) here means this is anaphylaxis with airway and breathing involvement, calling for immediate IM adrenaline; a rash and angioedema confined to the skin without respiratory or cardiovascular compromise does not meet the criteria for anaphylaxis and does not need adrenaline as the first drug.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *IM antihistamine*, *oral antihistamine* and *oral corticosteroid* are all supportive treatments for the cutaneous features of an allergic reaction, but none of them acts fast enough to reverse airway swelling or bronchospasm, so none can be given as the first drug once there is respiratory compromise. *IV hydrocortisone* is sometimes used as an adjunct in anaphylaxis management, but corticosteroids act over hours, not minutes, so they cannot substitute for adrenaline as the immediate, first-line treatment.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Anaphylaxis with airway or breathing compromise (noisy breathing, wheeze) — not just skin and soft-tissue involvement — is treated first with intramuscular adrenaline; antihistamines and corticosteroids are supportive, not lifesaving, and act too slowly to be given first.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.695'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-59', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'An 18-month-old boy presents with 3 days of vomiting and diarrhea. The vomiting has now settled, but he continues to have loose stools (he had about eight loose stools in the last 24 hours). His mucous membranes are dry, he has sunken eyes, and appears lethargic. His skin turgor is reduced. His extremities are warm and capillary refill time is normal. What is the NEXT step in management?',
  options:['Antipyretic/analgesia','Intravenous bolus of 20 mL/kg of normal saline','Nasogastric rehydration therapy','Oral antibiotic','Oral rehydration solution'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Children with diarrhea and moderate dehydration but no shock should be rehydrated with ORS. IV fluids are reserved for cases with shock (cold extremities, prolonged CRT, weak pulses).\n\n---\n\nThe deciding findings in this vignette are the extremities and the capillary refill time: this boy has clear signs of dehydration from three days of vomiting and diarrhoea -- dry mucous membranes, sunken eyes, lethargy and reduced skin turgor -- but his extremities are warm and his capillary refill time is normal, showing that his peripheral perfusion is still intact and he has not progressed into shock. The standard approach in a dehydrated but non-shocked child is to correct the fluid deficit by the oral route wherever it can be tolerated, using oral rehydration solution, reserving intravenous fluids for children whose perfusion findings (cold extremities, prolonged capillary refill, weak pulses, hypotension) show they have progressed into shock.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Antipyretic/analgesia* does not address his fluid deficit and there is no fever in the stem to treat. *IV bolus of 20 mL/kg normal saline* is the correct step once a child shows perfusion failure (cold extremities, prolonged capillary refill, weak pulses) — none of which this boy has, since his extremities are warm and his CRT is normal, so starting with an IV bolus would be treating a severity of illness he has not reached. *Nasogastric rehydration therapy* is sometimes used when a child cannot tolerate oral fluids by mouth (for example from persistent vomiting), but there is no indication in this stem that oral intake has failed — his vomiting has already settled — so there is no reason to bypass the oral route with a nasogastric tube. *Oral antibiotic* is not indicated on the basis of vomiting and diarrhoea alone without a finding pointing to a bacterial cause needing antimicrobial treatment; correcting the dehydration is the priority.\n\n**⚠️ Within-bank reprint — p.689, and the key letter is not the same on both pages.** The endpoint file prints this vignette twice inside its Emergencies section, word for word bar punctuation. p.697, the printing kept here, offers five options and keys oral rehydration solution last. p.689 offers four — the same list without nasogastric rehydration therapy, and with the intravenous option written simply as “IV normal saline” rather than as a 20 mL/kg bolus — and keys the same answer text second.\n\n**The answer text is identical on both pages; only its position moves.** That matters because a letter in this bank is positional, so the two printings would be marked differently by letter and identically by content. The five-option printing is kept: it is the fuller menu, and the nasogastric option it adds is the clinically interesting distractor — nasogastric rehydration is the step for a child who cannot keep oral fluids down, which this boy, whose vomiting has settled, can.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Dehydration with preserved peripheral perfusion (warm extremities, normal capillary refill) is corrected with oral rehydration solution before an IV bolus, nasogastric rehydration or antibiotics are considered; IV fluids are reserved for dehydration that has progressed to shock.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.697 (the same vignette is printed on p.689 with a four-option menu — the same options bar nasogastric rehydration therapy, and the same answer text sitting at a different position in the list)'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-60', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'A 1-year-old infant arrives to the emergency department with history of diarrhea and poor fluid intake since one day. Your quick examination reveals a lethargic child; his heart rate is 180 beats per minute, his respiratory rate is 30 breaths per minute, and his blood pressure is low for age. He has poor skin turgor, 5-second capillary refill, and cool extremities. Which of the following fluids is most appropriate management for his condition?',
  options:['IV Dextrose 5%','Oral rehydration solution','0.9% sodium chloride','Whole blood','IV sodium bicarbonate'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> The child has hypovolemic shock due to dehydration, best treated with IV isotonic saline bolus. Oral rehydration is insufficient in this emergency\n\n---\n\nThis infant\'s findings sit at the decompensated end of the shock spectrum described in `4) Shock in pediatrics_.txt`: marked tachycardia (180/min), a low blood pressure for age, poor skin turgor, a 5-second capillary refill and cool extremities, together with lethargy -- signs of poor perfusion reaching the point of hypotension, not simple dehydration that can wait for enteral correction. The lecture\'s sequence puts resuscitation fluid first in exactly this situation, given as an isotonic crystalloid -- 0.9% sodium chloride (normal saline) -- delivered rapidly by the IV route to restore circulating volume and perfusion before deficit or maintenance fluids are addressed.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *IV Dextrose 5%* is a hypotonic, glucose-containing fluid intended for maintenance fluid therapy or correcting hypoglycaemia, not for restoring intravascular volume in shock -- giving a hypotonic fluid rapidly in this setting risks worsening cerebral oedema and does not effectively expand the circulating volume the way an isotonic fluid does. *Oral rehydration solution* depends on gut absorption over time, which is far too slow for a child already hypotensive with a 5-second capillary refill and cool extremities; oral fluids are for dehydration without shock. *Whole blood* is reserved for haemorrhagic shock or significant blood loss, and there is nothing in this history (diarrhoea and poor intake) suggesting blood loss as the mechanism. *IV sodium bicarbonate* corrects severe, refractory metabolic acidosis in specific circumstances, but it is not a resuscitation fluid and does not restore circulating volume; it is not the first fluid given for hypovolaemic shock.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
  objective:'Decompensated hypovolaemic shock (tachycardia, hypotension, markedly prolonged capillary refill, cool extremities) is resuscitated with an isotonic crystalloid such as 0.9% sodium chloride, not with dextrose, oral fluids, blood, or bicarbonate.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.699'
},

{
  id:'pedep-emg-61', bank:'endpoint', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'emergencies',
  stem:'Which of the following statements concerning shock is TRUE?',
  options:['Dehydration due to gastroenteritis can cause shock due to maldistribution of fluid','Bradycardia occurs in compensated shock','Mottled, pale and cold skin is a sign of compensated shock','Anaphylaxis can cause shock due to hypovolemia','Decreased urine output is a sign of decompensated shock'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'The correct statement is that mottled, pale and cold skin is a sign of compensated shock. `4) Shock in pediatrics_.txt` sets out this same early/compensated versus late/decompensated framework, and the page prints it as a two-column reference table, reproduced here with the key placed against it:\n\n**Early (Compensated) shock** — Tachypnoea; Tachycardia; Decreased skin turgor; Sunken eyes and fontanelle; Delayed capillary refill (>2 sec); Mottled/pale/cold skin; Core-peripheral temperature gap (>4°C); Decreased urinary output.\n\n**Late (Decompensated) shock** — Acidotic (Kussmaul) breathing; Bradycardia; Confusion/depressed cerebral state; Blue peripheries; Absent urine output; Hypotension.\n\nMottled, pale, cold skin sits in the Early/Compensated column, which is why option C is the correct statement: in compensated shock the body is still able to maintain blood pressure by shunting blood away from the skin and splanchnic circulation toward the vital organs (brain, heart), which is exactly what produces cool, pale, mottled peripheries even while the child\'s blood pressure and mental state remain preserved.\n\n**Why each distractor fails.** *Dehydration due to gastroenteritis causing shock due to "maldistribution" of fluid* is wrong on mechanism -- gastroenteritis causes shock through simple volume loss (hypovolaemic shock, fluid actually lost from the body through vomiting and diarrhoea), not through maldistribution of fluid that is still present, which is instead the mechanism behind distributive shock (as in sepsis or anaphylaxis). *Bradycardia occurs in compensated shock* has it backwards: the table places tachycardia in the Early/Compensated column and bradycardia in the Late/Decompensated column -- a slowing heart rate in a shocked child is an ominous late sign, not a compensatory one; the heart rate rises first to maintain cardiac output, and only falls once compensation is failing. *Anaphylaxis can cause shock due to hypovolemia* misassigns the mechanism -- anaphylactic shock is a distributive shock, caused by widespread vasodilation and capillary leak rather than true loss of fluid from the body, so it is not classified as hypovolaemic. *Decreased urine output is a sign of decompensated shock* also misplaces its row -- the table lists decreased urinary output under Early/Compensated shock (a sign the kidneys are already being under-perfused as blood flow is redirected centrally) and reserves absent urine output for the Late/Decompensated column, so decreased output specifically belongs to the compensated stage, not the decompensated one.\n\nWritten for this bank — Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Compensated shock shows tachycardia, tachypnoea, delayed capillary refill, mottled/pale/cold skin and decreased urine output as the body centralises perfusion; decompensated shock adds bradycardia, hypotension, absent urine output, Kussmaul breathing and depressed consciousness once compensation fails. Gastroenteritis causes hypovolaemic shock through true fluid loss; anaphylaxis causes distributive shock through vasodilation, not hypovolaemia.',
  source:'Pediatrics endpoint part1.pdf p.701'
}
];
